{"data": [{"qa_pairs": [{"context": "No context provided", "question": "What is the primary mission of the FDIC?", "short_answers": ["Maintain stability", "Public confidence"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "How can you ensure that you are on a secure FDIC website?", "short_answers": ["Look for 'https://'", "Information is encrypted"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "What should you check before sharing sensitive information on a federal government website?", "short_answers": [".gov or .mil", "Official government site"], "wikipage": null}], "wikipages": [{"title": "AML Topic 1", "url": "https://www.fdic.gov/"}], "annotations": [{"knowledge": [], "long_answer": "The primary mission of the FDIC is to maintain stability and public confidence in the nation\u2019s financial system. To ensure you are on a secure FDIC website, look for the 'https://' in the URL, which indicates you are connecting to the official website and that any information you provide is encrypted and transmitted securely. Before sharing sensitive information on a federal government website, you should make sure the website ends in ."}, {"knowledge": [{"content": "The Federal Deposit Insurance Corporation (FDIC) is an independent agency created by the Congress to maintain stability and public confidence in the nation\u2019s financial system.", "wikipage": "AML Topic 1"}, {"content": "The https:// ensures that you are connecting to the official website and that any information you provide is encrypted and transmitted securely.", "wikipage": "AML Topic 1"}, {"content": "Federal government websites often end in .gov or .mil. Before sharing sensitive information, make sure you\u2019re on a federal government site.", "wikipage": "AML Topic 1"}], "long_answer": "The primary mission of the FDIC is to maintain stability and public confidence in the nation\u2019s financial system.\n\nTo ensure you are on a secure FDIC website, look for the 'https://' in the URL, which indicates you are connecting to the official website and that any information you provide is encrypted and transmitted securely.\n\nBefore sharing sensitive information on a federal government website, you should make sure the website ends in .gov or .mil, indicating it is an official government site."}], "sample_id": "aml-topic-1-1", "question": "What is the primary mission of the FDIC?", "docs": [{"id": "doc-aml-topic-1-1-2", "title": "AML Topic 1 Part 2", "text": "oggle submenu\nAbout\nThe Federal Deposit Insurance Corporation (FDIC) is an independent agency created by the Congress to maintain stability and public confidence in the nation\u2019s financial system. Learn about the FDIC\u2019s mission, leadership, history, career opportunities, and more.\nLearn More About the FDIC\nWhat We Do\nLeadership\nCareers\nInitiatives\nStrategic Plans\nFinancial Reports\nHistory\nGovernance\nOmbudsman\nDoing Business with the FDIC\nPRESS RELEASE / February 25, 2025\nFDIC-Insured Institutions", "score": 0.85, "summary": "oggle submenu About The Federal Deposit Insurance Corporation (FDIC) is an independent agency created by the Congress to maintain stability and public...", "extraction": "oggle submenu\nAbout\nThe Federal Deposit Insurance Corporation (FDIC) is an independent agency created by the Congress to maintain stability and public confidence in the nation\u2019s financial system. Lear"}], "answer": "The primary mission of the FDIC is to maintain stability and public confidence in the nation\u2019s financial system. To ensure you are on a secure FDIC website, look for the 'https://' in the URL, which indicates you are connecting to the official website and that any information you provide is encrypted and transmitted securely. Before sharing sensitive information on a federal government website, you should make sure the website ends in .", "output": "The FDIC's mission is to maintain stability and public confidence in the U. S. financial system [1].", "intermediate": ["[Retrieval]", "The FDIC's mission is to maintain stability and public confidence in the U.S. financial system."]}, {"qa_pairs": [{"context": "No context provided", "question": "What resources does the FDIC offer for bankers seeking information on laws and regulations?", "short_answers": ["Banker Resource Center", "Documentation of laws and regulations"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "How can consumers access financial education materials provided by the FDIC?", "short_answers": ["Consumer Resource Center", "Financial education materials"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "What type of research programs does the FDIC offer related to community banking?", "short_answers": ["Community Banking Research Program", "FDIC resources"], "wikipage": null}], "wikipages": [{"title": "AML Topic 1", "url": "https://www.fdic.gov/"}], "annotations": [{"knowledge": [], "long_answer": "The FDIC provides a Banker Resource Center that includes documentation of laws and regulations among other resources. Consumers can access financial education materials through the FDIC's Consumer Resource Center. The FDIC offers a Community Banking Research Program as part of its resources."}, {"knowledge": [{"content": "The FDIC provides a wealth of resources for consumers, bankers, analysts, and other stakeholders. Browse our collection of financial education materials, data tools, documentation of laws and regulations, information on important initiatives, and more.", "wikipage": "AML Topic 1"}, {"content": "The FDIC provides a wealth of resources for consumers, bankers, analysts, and other stakeholders. Browse our collection of financial education materials, data tools, documentation of laws and regulations, information on important initiatives, and more.", "wikipage": "AML Topic 1"}, {"content": "Additional FDIC Resources... Community Banking Research Program", "wikipage": "AML Topic 1"}], "long_answer": "The FDIC provides a Banker Resource Center that includes documentation of laws and regulations among other resources.\n\nConsumers can access financial education materials through the FDIC's Consumer Resource Center.\n\nThe FDIC offers a Community Banking Research Program as part of its resources."}], "sample_id": "aml-topic-1-2", "question": "What resources does the FDIC offer for bankers seeking information on laws and regulations?", "docs": [{"id": "doc-aml-topic-1-2-1", "title": "AML Topic 1 Part 1", "text": "ium Bank, Des Plaines, Ill. Assumes All Deposits of Pulaski Savings\u2026\nPODCASTS / January 06, 2025\nSmall Business Lending\nResources\nToggle submenu\nResources\nThe FDIC provides a wealth of resources for consumers, bankers, analysts, and other stakeholders. Browse our collection of financial education materials, data tools, documentation of laws and regulations, information on important initiatives, and more.\nAdditional FDIC Resources\nConsumer Resource Center\nBanker Resource Center\nDeposit Insurance", "score": 0.9, "summary": "ium Bank, Des Plaines, Ill. Assumes All Deposits of Pulaski Savings\u2026 PODCASTS / January 06, 2025 Small Business Lending Resources Toggle submenu Resou...", "extraction": "The FDIC provides a wealth of resources for consumers, bankers, analysts, and other stakeholders. Browse our collection of financial education materials, data tools, documentation of laws and regulations, information on important initiatives, and more."}], "answer": "The FDIC provides a Banker Resource Center that includes documentation of laws and regulations among other resources. Consumers can access financial education materials through the FDIC's Consumer Resource Center. The FDIC offers a Community Banking Research Program as part of its resources.", "output": "The FDIC provides a wealth of resources for consumers, bankers, analysts, and other stakeholders [1].", "intermediate": ["[Retrieval]", "The FDIC provides a wealth of resources for consumers, bankers, analysts, and other stakeholders."]}, {"qa_pairs": [{"context": "No context provided", "question": "What type of financial performance data does the FDIC provide?", "short_answers": ["Return on assets", "Net income"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "How does the FDIC keep the public informed about banking issues?", "short_answers": ["News announcements", "Policy changes"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "What are some of the resources and tools available through the FDIC for financial research?", "short_answers": ["Center for Financial Research", "Quarterly Banking Profile"], "wikipage": null}], "wikipages": [{"title": "AML Topic 1", "url": "https://www.fdic.gov/"}], "annotations": [{"knowledge": [], "long_answer": "The FDIC provides financial performance data such as return on assets and net income for FDIC-insured institutions. The FDIC keeps the public informed through regular updates such as news announcements, speeches, testimonies, and details on policy changes, conferences, and events. The FDIC offers resources and tools such as the Center for Financial Research, Consumer Research, the FDIC National Survey of Unbanked and Underbanked Households, and the Quarterly Banking Profile."}, {"knowledge": [{"content": "EVENT / February 25, 2025 FDIC-Insured Institutions Reported Return on Assets of 1.11 Percent and Net Inc\u2026 EVENT / December 12, 2024 FDIC-Insured Institutions Reported Net Income of $65.4 Billion EVENT / September 05, 2024 FDIC-Insured Institutions Reported Net Income of $71.5 Billion", "wikipage": "AML Topic 1"}, {"content": "The FDIC publishes regular updates on news and activities. Keep up with FDIC announcements, read speeches and testimony on the latest banking issues, learn about policy changes for banks, and get the details on upcoming conferences and events.", "wikipage": "AML Topic 1"}, {"content": "More FDIC Analysis Center for Financial Research Consumer Research FDIC National Survey of Unbanked and Underbanked Households Quarterly Banking Profile", "wikipage": "AML Topic 1"}], "long_answer": "The FDIC provides financial performance data such as return on assets and net income for FDIC-insured institutions.\n\nThe FDIC keeps the public informed through regular updates such as news announcements, speeches, testimonies, and details on policy changes, conferences, and events.\n\nThe FDIC offers resources and tools such as the Center for Financial Research, Consumer Research, the FDIC National Survey of Unbanked and Underbanked Households, and the Quarterly Banking Profile."}], "sample_id": "aml-topic-1-3", "question": "What type of financial performance data does the FDIC provide?", "docs": [{"id": "doc-aml-topic-1-3-1", "title": "AML Topic 1 Part 1", "text": "ers, and state banking performance data. Browse our extensive research tools and reports.\nMore FDIC Analysis\nCenter for Financial Research\nConsumer Research\nFDIC National Survey of Unbanked and Underbanked Households\nQuarterly Banking Profile\nFDIC Academic Challenge\nFDIC Banking Issues in Focus\nAnnual Risk Review\nEVENT / February 25, 2025\nFDIC-Insured Institutions Reported Return on Assets of 1.11 Percent and Net Inc\u2026\nEVENT / December 12, 2024\nFDIC-Insured Institutions Reported Net Income of $65", "score": 0.9, "summary": "ers, and state banking performance data. Browse our extensive research tools and reports. More FDIC Analysis Center for Financial Research Consumer Re...", "extraction": "EVENT / February 25, 2025 FDIC-Insured Institutions Reported Return on Assets of 1.11 Percent and Net Inc\u2026 EVENT / December 12, 2024 FDIC-Insured Institutions Reported Net Income of $65.4 Billion EVENT / September 05, 2024 FDIC-Insured Institutions Reported Net Income of $71.5 Billion"}], "answer": "The FDIC provides financial performance data such as return on assets and net income for FDIC-insured institutions. The FDIC keeps the public informed through regular updates such as news announcements, speeches, testimonies, and details on policy changes, conferences, and events. The FDIC offers resources and tools such as the Center for Financial Research, Consumer Research, the FDIC National Survey of Unbanked and Underbanked Households, and the Quarterly Banking Profile.", "output": "The FDIC provides financial performance data such as return on assets, net income, and total assets [1].", "intermediate": ["[Retrieval]", "The FDIC provides financial performance data such as return on assets, net income, and total assets."]}, {"qa_pairs": [{"context": "No context provided", "question": "What is the purpose of the FDIC's Money Smart program?", "short_answers": ["Financial education", "Enhance financial literacy"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "How does the FDIC ensure the security of information shared on its website?", "short_answers": ["Uses HTTPS", "Secure connection to .gov"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "What types of educational resources does the FDIC offer for different age groups?", "short_answers": ["Resources for adults and seniors", "Youth and children education"], "wikipage": null}], "wikipages": [{"title": "AML Topic 2", "url": "https://www.fdic.gov/espanol/educacion/glosario/"}], "annotations": [{"knowledge": [], "long_answer": "The FDIC's Money Smart program is designed to provide financial education to various groups, including adults, seniors, small businesses, and youth, to enhance their financial literacy and decision-making skills. The FDIC ensures the security of information shared on its website by using HTTPS, which indicates a secure connection to a . The FDIC offers educational resources tailored for different age groups through its Money Smart program, which includes resources for adults, seniors, small businesses, youth, and children."}, {"knowledge": [{"content": "Money Smart Program Community Teach Money Smart Money Smart for Older Adults Money Smart for Adults Money Smart for Small Business Money Smart for Youth", "wikipage": "AML Topic 2"}, {"content": "Secure .gov websites use HTTPS A https:// means you have securely connected to a .gov website. Share sensitive information only on official and secure websites.", "wikipage": "AML Topic 2"}, {"content": "Money Smart Program Community Teach Money Smart Money Smart for Older Adults Money Smart for Adults Money Smart for Small Businesses Money Smart for Youth News Money Smart for Kids", "wikipage": "AML Topic 2"}], "long_answer": "The FDIC's Money Smart program is designed to provide financial education to various groups, including adults, seniors, small businesses, and youth, to enhance their financial literacy and decision-making skills.\n\nThe FDIC ensures the security of information shared on its website by using HTTPS, which indicates a secure connection to a .gov website. This ensures that sensitive information is only shared on official and secure sites.\n\nThe FDIC offers educational resources tailored for different age groups through its Money Smart program, which includes resources for adults, seniors, small businesses, youth, and children."}], "sample_id": "aml-topic-2-1", "question": "What is the purpose of the FDIC's Money Smart program?", "docs": [{"id": "doc-aml-topic-2-1-2", "title": "AML Topic 2 Part 2", "text": "Deposit Insurance  \nConsumer News  \nAbout the FDIC  \nBreadcrumb  \nHome  \nSpanish  \nSpanish  \nGlossary of Financial Terms  \nShare This:  \nShare on Facebook  \nShare on X  \nShare on X  \nFollow the FDIC on LinkedIn  \nShare through email  \nPrint  \n\nWelcome to the FDIC  \nFinancial Education  \nBank Account  \nOverdraft Fees  \nDeposit Accounts  \nFind Your Bank  \nMoney Smart Program  \nCommunity  \nTeach Money Smart  \nMoney Smart for Older Adults  \nMoney Smart for Adults  \nMoney Smart for Small Businesses", "score": 0.85, "summary": "Deposit Insurance News for Consumers About the FDIC Breadcrumb Home Spanish Glossary of Financial Terms Share This: Sha...", "extraction": "Deposit Insurance  \nConsumer News  \nAbout the FDIC  \nBreadcrumb  \nHome  \nSpanish  \nGlossary of Financial Terms  \nShare This:  \nShare on Facebook  \nShare on X  \nShare on X  \nFollow the FDIC"}], "answer": "The FDIC's Money Smart program is designed to provide financial education to various groups, including adults, seniors, small businesses, and youth, to enhance their financial literacy and decision-making skills. The FDIC ensures the security of information shared on its website by using HTTPS, which indicates a secure connection to a . The FDIC offers educational resources tailored for different age groups through its Money Smart program, which includes resources for adults, seniors, small businesses, youth, and children.", "output": "Answer: The FDIC's Money Smart program is a financial education program that helps consumers learn about managing their money and avoiding financial fraud [1].", "intermediate": ["[Retrieval]", "Answer: The FDIC's Money Smart program is a financial education program that helps consumers learn about managing their money and avoiding financial fraud."]}, {"qa_pairs": [{"context": "No context provided", "question": "What is the role of an attorney in real estate transactions?", "short_answers": ["Drafting contracts", "Conducting title searches", "Handling closings"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "Who is considered a creditor in financial terms?", "short_answers": ["Lends money to debtors", "Issues credit cards"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "What distinguishes a financial asset from a physical asset?", "short_answers": ["Financial asset has monetary value", "Physical asset is tangible"], "wikipage": null}], "wikipages": [{"title": "AML Topic 2", "url": "https://www.fdic.gov/espanol/educacion/glosario/"}], "annotations": [{"knowledge": [], "long_answer": "In some real estate transactions, an attorney can perform functions such as drafting the real estate contract, conducting the title search, and handling the closing. A creditor is a person, company, or organization that lends money to a debtor. A financial asset has monetary value, such as a savings account balance or cash, while a physical asset is something tangible that you own and has value."}, {"knowledge": [{"content": "Lawyer: A licensed professional authorized to practice law. In some real estate transactions, a lawyer can perform functions such as drafting the real estate contract, conducting the title search, and carrying out the closing.", "wikipage": "AML Topic 2"}, {"content": "Creditor (or lender): a person, company, or organization that lends money to the debtor. It also refers to the company that issues credit cards.", "wikipage": "AML Topic 2"}, {"content": "Financial Asset: An asset with monetary value, such as the balance in a savings account, the amount of cash in your pocket, or the money in an investment fund. Physical Asset: Something you own that has value and can be seen and touched.", "wikipage": "AML Topic 2"}], "long_answer": "In some real estate transactions, an attorney can perform functions such as drafting the real estate contract, conducting the title search, and handling the closing.\n\nA creditor is a person, company, or organization that lends money to a debtor. It also refers to the company that issues credit cards.\n\nA financial asset has monetary value, such as a savings account balance or cash, while a physical asset is something tangible that you own and has value."}], "sample_id": "aml-topic-2-2", "question": "What is the role of an attorney in real estate transactions?", "docs": [{"id": "doc-aml-topic-2-2-2", "title": "AML Topic 2 Part 2", "text": "Terms\n\nA\n\nAttorney: A licensed professional authorized to practice law. In some real estate transactions, an attorney may perform functions such as drafting the real estate contract, conducting the title search, and executing the closing.\n\nA\n\nCreditor (or lender): A person, company, or organization that lends money to the debtor. It also refers to the company that issues credit cards.\n\nA\n\nAsset: Something you own that has value.\n\nFinancial asset: An asset with monetary value, such as the s", "score": 0.85, "summary": "Terms\n\nA\n\nAttorney: A licensed professional authorized to practice law. In some real estate transactions, an attorney may perform functions such as...", "extraction": "Terms\n\nA\n\nAttorney: A licensed professional authorized to practice law. In some real estate transactions, an attorney may perform functions such as drafting the real estate contract, conducting the title search, and overseeing the closing process."}], "answer": "In some real estate transactions, an attorney can perform functions such as drafting the real estate contract, conducting the title search, and handling the closing. A creditor is a person, company, or organization that lends money to a debtor. A financial asset has monetary value, such as a savings account balance or cash, while a physical asset is something tangible that you own and has value.", "output": "An attorney is a licensed professional authorized to practice law. In some real estate transactions, an attorney may perform functions such as drafting the real estate contract, conducting the title search, and executing the closing [1].", "intermediate": ["[Retrieval]", "An attorney is a licensed professional authorized to practice law.In some real estate transactions, an attorney may perform functions such as drafting the real estate contract, conducting the title search, and executing the closing."]}, {"qa_pairs": [{"context": "No context provided", "question": "What is an 'acuerdo por morosidad' and how does it affect federal student loans?", "short_answers": ["Temporarily stop payments", "Responsible for interest during forbearance"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "How does a 'reasonable accommodation' function in housing for individuals with disabilities?", "short_answers": ["Change in rules for disabilities", "Equal opportunity in housing"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "What does the term 'adjudicaci\u00f3n' refer to in the context of insurance claims?", "short_answers": ["Review of insurance claims", "Determines coverage by policy"], "wikipage": null}], "wikipages": [{"title": "AML Topic 2", "url": "https://www.fdic.gov/espanol/educacion/glosario/"}], "annotations": [{"knowledge": [], "long_answer": "An 'acuerdo por morosidad' allows you to temporarily stop making payments or reduce your monthly payment amount for a certain period if you meet specific requirements. A 'reasonable accommodation' in housing involves a change in rules, policies, practices, or services so that a person with a disability has the same opportunity to use and enjoy a housing unit or common space. In the context of insurance claims, 'adjudicaci\u00f3n' refers to the process of reviewing claims."}, {"knowledge": [{"content": "Delinquency Agreement: If you meet certain requirements, the delinquency agreement allows you to temporarily stop making payments or temporarily reduce the amount of your monthly payment for a specified period. For all types of federal student loans, you are responsible for paying the interest that accrues during a delinquency agreement period.", "wikipage": "AML Topic 2"}, {"content": "Reasonable Accommodation: In terms of housing, a reasonable accommodation is a change in rules, policies, practices, or services to ensure that a person with a disability has the same opportunity to use and enjoy a housing unit or common area.", "wikipage": "AML Topic 2"}, {"content": "Adjudication: When used in the context of insurance claims, adjudication is the process of reviewing claims. The insurance company determines if a loss is covered by its p", "wikipage": "AML Topic 2"}], "long_answer": "An 'acuerdo por morosidad' allows you to temporarily stop making payments or reduce your monthly payment amount for a certain period if you meet specific requirements. For all types of federal student loans, you are responsible for paying the interest that accrues during a period of forbearance.\n\nA 'reasonable accommodation' in housing involves a change in rules, policies, practices, or services so that a person with a disability has the same opportunity to use and enjoy a housing unit or common space.\n\nIn the context of insurance claims, 'adjudicaci\u00f3n' refers to the process of reviewing claims. The insurance company determines if a loss is covered by the policy."}], "sample_id": "aml-topic-2-3", "question": "What is an 'acuerdo por morosidad' and how does it affect federal student loans?", "docs": [{"id": "doc-aml-topic-2-3-2", "title": "AML Topic 2 Part 2", "text": "a specific period. For all types of federal student loans, you are responsible for paying the interest that accrues during a period of forbearance. More information at StudentAid.gov.\n\nA\nReasonable accommodation: In terms of housing, a reasonable accommodation is a change in rules, policies, practices, or services to ensure that a person with a disability has the same opportunity to use and enjoy a housing unit or common area.\nA\nAdjud", "score": 0.85, "summary": "a determined period. For all types of federal student loans, you are responsible for paying the interest that accrues during...", "extraction": "a specific period. For all types of federal student loans, you are responsible for paying the interest that accrues during a delinquency agreement period. More info"}], "answer": "An 'acuerdo por morosidad' allows you to temporarily stop making payments or reduce your monthly payment amount for a certain period if you meet specific requirements. A 'reasonable accommodation' in housing involves a change in rules, policies, practices, or services so that a person with a disability has the same opportunity to use and enjoy a housing unit or common space. In the context of insurance claims, 'adjudicaci\u00f3n' refers to the process of reviewing claims.", "output": "An \"acuerdo por morosidad\" is an agreement between a debtor and a creditor to repay a debt over time [1].", "intermediate": ["[Retrieval]", "An \"acuerdo por morosidad\" is an agreement between a debtor and a creditor to repay a debt over time."]}, {"qa_pairs": [{"context": "No context provided", "question": "What resources does the FDIC provide to help consumers open a bank account?", "short_answers": ["Resources to help open a bank account", "Guidance for unbanked households"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "How does the Money Smart program benefit individuals?", "short_answers": ["Improves financial skills", "Establishes positive banking relationships"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "What is the main focus of the FDIC's consumer protection efforts?", "short_answers": ["Informed financial decisions", "Protection against fraud and scams"], "wikipage": null}], "wikipages": [{"title": "AML Topic 3", "url": "https://www.fdic.gov/espanol/educacion-financiera"}], "annotations": [{"knowledge": [], "long_answer": "The FDIC provides resources to help consumers who are among the 5. The Money Smart program by the FDIC helps individuals of all ages improve their financial skills and establish positive banking relationships. The FDIC focuses on providing consumers with useful information to make informed financial decisions and protect themselves against fraud and financial scams."}, {"knowledge": [{"content": "If you are part of one of the 5.9 million households in the United States that still does not have a bank account and are looking to open one, there are resources available to assist you in this process.", "wikipage": "AML Topic 3"}, {"content": "The FDIC's Money Smart financial education program can help people of all ages improve their financial skills and establish positive banking relationships.", "wikipage": "AML Topic 3"}, {"content": "The FDIC recognizes the importance of providing consumers with useful information to help them make informed decisions about their money and to protect themselves against fraud and financial scams.", "wikipage": "AML Topic 3"}], "long_answer": "The FDIC provides resources to help consumers who are among the 5.9 million households in the United States without a bank account to open one. These resources include information and guidance on the process.\n\nThe Money Smart program by the FDIC helps individuals of all ages improve their financial skills and establish positive banking relationships.\n\nThe FDIC focuses on providing consumers with useful information to make informed financial decisions and protect themselves against fraud and financial scams."}], "sample_id": "aml-topic-3-2", "question": "What resources does the FDIC provide to help consumers open a bank account?", "docs": [{"id": "doc-aml-topic-3-2-1", "title": "AML Topic 3 Part 1", "text": "Privacy  \nGlossary of Financial Terms  \nContact Information  \nThe FDIC recognizes the importance of providing consumers with useful information to help them make informed decisions about their money and to protect themselves against fraud and financial scams. Here you will find information on specific banking topics, as well as links to other resources for more information.  \nEducation  \nOpen a Bank Account (#GetBanked)  \nMoney Smart Program  \nCredit and Loans  \nProtection for", "score": 0.9, "summary": "Privacy IAS Financial Terms Glossary Contact Information The FDIC recognizes the importance of providing consumers with useful information...", "extraction": "If you are part of one of the 5.9 million households in the United States that still does not have a bank account and are looking to open one, there are resources available to assist you in this process."}], "answer": "The FDIC provides resources to help consumers who are among the 5. The Money Smart program by the FDIC helps individuals of all ages improve their financial skills and establish positive banking relationships. The FDIC focuses on providing consumers with useful information to make informed financial decisions and protect themselves against fraud and financial scams.", "output": "Answer: The FDIC provides resources to help consumers open a bank account, such as the Money Smart Program, which provides information on managing money, credit and loans, and protection for consumers [1].", "intermediate": ["[Retrieval]", "Answer: The FDIC provides resources to help consumers open a bank account, such as the Money Smart Program, which provides information on managing money, credit and loans, and protection for consumers."]}, {"qa_pairs": [{"context": "No context provided", "question": "What resources does the FDIC provide to help consumers protect against financial fraud and scams?", "short_answers": ["Information on banking topics", "Links to other resources"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "How does the FDIC support financial education for different age groups?", "short_answers": ["Money Smart for different age groups", "Resources for seniors, adults, businesses, youth"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "What are some of the risks associated with technological services in banking, according to the FDIC?", "short_answers": ["Risks with technological services", "Resources to recognize and protect against risks"], "wikipage": null}], "wikipages": [{"title": "AML Topic 3", "url": "https://www.fdic.gov/espanol/educacion-financiera"}], "annotations": [{"knowledge": [], "long_answer": "The FDIC provides information on specific banking topics and links to other resources to help consumers make informed decisions about their money and protect themselves against financial fraud and scams. The FDIC offers the Money Smart program, which includes specific resources for seniors, adults, small businesses, and youth to enhance financial education across different age groups. While technological services in banking offer clear benefits such as speed and convenience, there are risks involved."}, {"knowledge": [{"content": "The FDIC recognizes the importance of providing consumers with useful information to help them make informed decisions about their money and to protect themselves against fraud and financial scams. Here you will find information on specific banking topics, as well as links to other resources for further information.", "wikipage": "AML Topic 3"}, {"content": "Teach Money Smart  \nMoney Smart for Older Adults  \nMoney Smart for Adults  \nMoney Smart for Small Businesses  \nMoney Smart for Youth  \nMoney Smart News for Kids", "wikipage": "AML Topic 3"}, {"content": "The benefits of technological services, such as speed and convenience for banking customers, are clear, but there are risks. These resources will help you recognize common risks and protect your finances.", "wikipage": "AML Topic 3"}], "long_answer": "The FDIC provides information on specific banking topics and links to other resources to help consumers make informed decisions about their money and protect themselves against financial fraud and scams.\n\nThe FDIC offers the Money Smart program, which includes specific resources for seniors, adults, small businesses, and youth to enhance financial education across different age groups.\n\nWhile technological services in banking offer clear benefits such as speed and convenience, there are risks involved. The FDIC provides resources to help recognize common risks and protect finances."}], "sample_id": "aml-topic-3-3", "question": "What resources does the FDIC provide to help consumers protect against financial fraud and scams?", "docs": [{"id": "doc-aml-topic-3-3-1", "title": "AML Topic 3 Part 1", "text": "oney Smart in your community\nTeach Money Smart\nMoney Smart for Older Adults\nMoney Smart for Adults\nMoney Smart for Small Businesses\nMoney Smart for Youth\nMoney Smart News for Kids\nBack to top\nCredit and Loans\nThe FDIC recognizes the importance of providing consumers with useful information to help them make informed decisions about their money and to protect themselves against fraud and financial scams. Here you will find information on specific banking topics, as well as", "score": 0.9, "summary": "oney Smart in your community Teach Money Smart Money Smart for Older Adults Money Smart for Adults Money Smart for Small Businesses Money Smart...", "extraction": "The FDIC recognizes the importance of providing consumers with useful information to help them make informed decisions about their money and to protect themselves against fraud and financial scams. Here you will find information on specific banking topics, as well as links to other resources for further information."}], "answer": "The FDIC provides information on specific banking topics and links to other resources to help consumers make informed decisions about their money and protect themselves against financial fraud and scams. The FDIC offers the Money Smart program, which includes specific resources for seniors, adults, small businesses, and youth to enhance financial education across different age groups. While technological services in banking offer clear benefits such as speed and convenience, there are risks involved.", "output": "Answer: The FDIC provides resources to help consumers protect against financial fraud and scams, including information on specific banking topics, such as avoiding and reporting fraud, and tips for protecting personal information [1].", "intermediate": ["[Retrieval]", "Answer: The FDIC provides resources to help consumers protect against financial fraud and scams, including information on specific banking topics, such as avoiding and reporting fraud, and tips for protecting personal information."]}, {"qa_pairs": [{"context": "No context provided", "question": "What is the maximum amount covered by FDIC insurance for deposits in a single bank?", "short_answers": ["$250,000", "FDIC-insured bank"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "How can I confirm if a bank is insured by the FDIC?", "short_answers": ["FDIC website", "Find FDIC-insured banks"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "What should I do if my bank fails and I have deposits over $250,000?", "short_answers": ["FDIC actions", "Bank failure steps"], "wikipage": null}], "wikipages": [{"title": "AML Topic 4", "url": "https://www.fdic.gov/espanol/deposito"}], "annotations": [{"knowledge": [], "long_answer": "The FDIC offers deposit insurance that automatically covers deposits up to at least $250,000 in each FDIC-insured bank. You can find information about whether a bank is insured by the FDIC on their website, which provides details on how to find FDIC-insured banks. The FDIC's frequently asked questions page provides information on the actions taken by the FDIC in the event of a bank failure, which can guide you on what steps to take if your deposits exceed the insured limit."}, {"knowledge": [{"content": "The FDIC offers deposit insurance to protect your money in the event of a bank failure. Your deposits are automatically insured for at least $250,000 at each FDIC-insured bank.", "wikipage": "AML Topic 4"}, {"content": "Our frequently asked questions page provides details about deposit insurance coverage, the actions taken by the FDIC in the event of a bank failure, how to find FDIC-insured banks, and more information.", "wikipage": "AML Topic 4"}, {"content": "Our frequently asked questions page provides details about deposit insurance coverage, the actions taken by the FDIC in the event of a bank failure, how to find FDIC-insured banks, and more information.", "wikipage": "AML Topic 4"}], "long_answer": "The FDIC offers deposit insurance that automatically covers deposits up to at least $250,000 in each FDIC-insured bank.\n\nYou can find information about whether a bank is insured by the FDIC on their website, which provides details on how to find FDIC-insured banks.\n\nThe FDIC's frequently asked questions page provides information on the actions taken by the FDIC in the event of a bank failure, which can guide you on what steps to take if your deposits exceed the insured limit."}], "sample_id": "aml-topic-4-1", "question": "What is the maximum amount covered by FDIC insurance for deposits in a single bank?", "docs": [{"id": "doc-aml-topic-4-1-2", "title": "AML Topic 4 Part 2", "text": "Deposit Insurance  \nConsumer News  \nAbout the FDIC  \nBreadcrumb  \nHome  \nSpanish  \nFDIC: Deposit Insurance  \nThe FDIC provides deposit insurance to protect your money in the event of a bank failure. Your deposits are automatically insured up to at least $250,000 at each FDIC-insured bank.  \nSee this page in English  \nLearn how deposit insurance works  \nFrequently Asked Questions  \nOur frequently asked questions page offers details about deposit insurance coverage.", "score": 0.85, "summary": "Deposit Insurance News for Consumers About the FDIC Breadcrumb Home Spanish FDIC: Deposit Insurance The FDIC provides deposit insurance for...", "extraction": "Deposit Insurance  \nConsumer News  \nAbout the FDIC  \nBreadcrumb  \nHome  \nSpanish  \nFDIC: Deposit Insurance  \nThe FDIC offers deposit insurance to protect your money in the event of a bank failure."}], "answer": "The FDIC offers deposit insurance that automatically covers deposits up to at least $250,000 in each FDIC-insured bank. You can find information about whether a bank is insured by the FDIC on their website, which provides details on how to find FDIC-insured banks. The FDIC's frequently asked questions page provides information on the actions taken by the FDIC in the event of a bank failure, which can guide you on what steps to take if your deposits exceed the insured limit.", "output": "Answer: The maximum amount covered by FDIC insurance for deposits in a single bank is $250,000 [1].", "intermediate": ["[Retrieval]", "Answer: The maximum amount covered by FDIC insurance for deposits in a single bank is $250,000."]}, {"qa_pairs": [{"context": "No context provided", "question": "How can I determine the coverage of my deposit insurance using FDIC resources?", "short_answers": ["Use the EDIE tool", "Calculate deposit insurance coverage"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "What should I do if I need assistance with my deposit insurance coverage?", "short_answers": ["Contact FDIC for assistance", "Call 1-877-275-3342"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "How can I stay informed about updates from the FDIC?", "short_answers": ["Subscribe with email", "Follow on social media"], "wikipage": null}], "wikipages": [{"title": "AML Topic 4", "url": "https://www.fdic.gov/espanol/deposito"}], "annotations": [{"knowledge": [], "long_answer": "You can use the FDIC's Electronic Deposit Insurance Estimator (EDIE), which is an easy-to-use online tool designed to calculate your deposit insurance coverage. If you need assistance with your deposit insurance coverage, you can contact the FDIC by calling 1-877-275-3342 for help in determining your coverage or for any specific questions about deposit insurance. You can stay informed about updates from the FDIC by subscribing with your email address to receive notifications or by following the FDIC on various social media platforms such as Facebook, X, Instagram, LinkedIn, Flickr, and YouTube."}, {"knowledge": [{"content": "The Electronic Deposit Insurance Estimator \u2013 known as EDIE \u2013 is a simple online tool that is easy to use for calculating your deposit insurance coverage.", "wikipage": "AML Topic 4"}, {"content": "Contact the FDIC Call 1-877-275-3342 to determine your coverage under deposit insurance or if you have any other specific questions about deposit insurance.", "wikipage": "AML Topic 4"}, {"content": "Stay Informed (English) Enter your email address Subscribe Follow the FDIC on Facebook Follow the FDIC on X Share on X Follow the FDIC on Instagram Follow the FDIC on LinkedIn Follow the FDIC on Flickr Follow the FDIC on YouTube", "wikipage": "AML Topic 4"}], "long_answer": "You can use the FDIC's Electronic Deposit Insurance Estimator (EDIE), which is an easy-to-use online tool designed to calculate your deposit insurance coverage.\n\nIf you need assistance with your deposit insurance coverage, you can contact the FDIC by calling 1-877-275-3342 for help in determining your coverage or for any specific questions about deposit insurance.\n\nYou can stay informed about updates from the FDIC by subscribing with your email address to receive notifications or by following the FDIC on various social media platforms such as Facebook, X, Instagram, LinkedIn, Flickr, and YouTube."}], "sample_id": "aml-topic-4-2", "question": "How can I determine the coverage of my deposit insurance using FDIC resources?", "docs": [{"id": "doc-aml-topic-4-2-2", "title": "AML Topic 4 Part 2", "text": "Contact the FDIC\nStay Informed\n(English)\nEnter your email address\nSubscribe\nFollow the FDIC on Facebook\nFollow the FDIC on X\nShare on X\nFollow the FDIC on Instagram\nFollow the FDIC on LinkedIn\nFollow the FDIC on Flickr\nFollow the FDIC on YouTube\nHow Can We Help You?\n(English)\nDefine \u201cI am a . . . \u201d to learn more specific information\nI am...\nBank Customer\nBanker\nConsumer\nSmall Business Owner\nAnalyst/Researcher/Student\nLegal Professional\nBank Regulator\nPr", "score": 0.85, "summary": "Contact the FDIC Stay Informed (English) Enter your email address Subscribe Follow the FDIC on Facebook Follow...", "extraction": "Contact the FDIC  \nStay Informed  \n(English)  \nEnter your email address  \nSubscribe  \nFollow the FDIC on Facebook  \nFollow the FDIC on X  \nShare on X  \nFollow the FDIC on Instagram"}], "answer": "You can use the FDIC's Electronic Deposit Insurance Estimator (EDIE), which is an easy-to-use online tool designed to calculate your deposit insurance coverage. If you need assistance with your deposit insurance coverage, you can contact the FDIC by calling 1-877-275-3342 for help in determining your coverage or for any specific questions about deposit insurance. You can stay informed about updates from the FDIC by subscribing with your email address to receive notifications or by following the FDIC on various social media platforms such as Facebook, X, Instagram, LinkedIn, Flickr, and YouTube.", "output": "Answer: To determine your deposit insurance coverage, you can use the FDIC's Deposit Insurance Calculator [1].", "intermediate": ["[Retrieval]", "Answer: To determine your deposit insurance coverage, you can use the FDIC's Deposit Insurance Calculator."]}, {"qa_pairs": [{"context": "No context provided", "question": "What are some of the educational programs offered by the FDIC for financial literacy?", "short_answers": ["Money Smart para Adultos Mayores", "Money Smart para Adultos", "Money Smart para Peque\u00f1os Negocios", "Money Smart para la juventud"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "How does the FDIC ensure the security of information shared on their website?", "short_answers": ["Secure .gov websites", "Uses HTTPS", "Share sensitive info only on official sites"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "What resources does the FDIC provide to help consumers manage overdraft fees?", "short_answers": ["Cargos por sobregiro", "Consumer news", "Financial education"], "wikipage": null}], "wikipages": [{"title": "AML Topic 5", "url": "https://www.fdic.gov/espanol/noticiasconsumidor"}], "annotations": [{"knowledge": [], "long_answer": "The FDIC offers several educational programs under the 'Money Smart' initiative, including 'Money Smart para Adultos Mayores', 'Money Smart para Adultos', 'Money Smart para Peque\u00f1os Negocios', and 'Money Smart para la juventud'. The FDIC ensures the security of information by using secure . The FDIC provides resources related to managing overdraft fees under the section 'Cargos por sobregiro' as part of their consumer news and financial education efforts."}, {"knowledge": [{"content": "Money Smart Program: Teach Money Smart, Money Smart for Older Adults, Money Smart for Adults, Money Smart for Small Businesses, Money Smart for Youth.", "wikipage": "AML Topic 5"}, {"content": "Secure .gov websites use HTTPS. An https:// means that you have securely connected to a .gov website. Share sensitive information only on official and secure websites.", "wikipage": "AML Topic 5"}, {"content": "Financial Education: Bank Account, Overdraft Fees, Deposit Accounts.", "wikipage": "AML Topic 5"}], "long_answer": "The FDIC offers several educational programs under the 'Money Smart' initiative, including 'Money Smart para Adultos Mayores', 'Money Smart para Adultos', 'Money Smart para Peque\u00f1os Negocios', and 'Money Smart para la juventud'.\n\nThe FDIC ensures the security of information by using secure .gov websites that employ HTTPS, which means users are connected securely to the site. It is advised to share sensitive information only on these official and secure websites.\n\nThe FDIC provides resources related to managing overdraft fees under the section 'Cargos por sobregiro' as part of their consumer news and financial education efforts."}], "sample_id": "aml-topic-5-1", "question": "What are some of the educational programs offered by the FDIC for financial literacy?", "docs": [{"id": "doc-aml-topic-5-1-2", "title": "AML Topic 5 Part 2", "text": "Deposit Insurance  \nConsumer News  \nAbout the FDIC  \nBreadcrumb  \nHome  \nSpanish  \nSpanish  \nConsumer News  \nShare This:  \nShare on Facebook  \nShare on X  \nShare on X  \nFollow the FDIC on LinkedIn  \nShare through email  \nPrint  \n\nWelcome to the FDIC  \nFinancial Education  \nBank Account  \nOverdraft Fees  \nDeposit Accounts  \nFind Your Bank  \nMoney Smart Program  \nCommunity  \nTeach Money Smart  \nMoney Smart for Older Adults  \nMoney Smart for Adults  \nMoney Smart for Small Businesses  \nMoney S", "score": 0.85, "summary": "Deposit Insurance Consumer News About the FDIC Breadcrumb Home Spanish Espa\u00f1ol Consumer News Share This: Share on Face...", "extraction": "Deposit Insurance  \nConsumer News  \nAbout the FDIC  \nBreadcrumb  \nHome  \nSpanish  \nSpanish  \nConsumer News  \nShare This:  \nShare on Facebook  \nShare on X  \nShare on X  \nFollow the FDIC on LinkedIn"}], "answer": "The FDIC offers several educational programs under the 'Money Smart' initiative, including 'Money Smart para Adultos Mayores', 'Money Smart para Adultos', 'Money Smart para Peque\u00f1os Negocios', and 'Money Smart para la juventud'. The FDIC ensures the security of information by using secure . The FDIC provides resources related to managing overdraft fees under the section 'Cargos por sobregiro' as part of their consumer news and financial education efforts.", "output": "Answer: The FDIC offers a variety of educational programs for financial literacy, including Money Smart for Adults, Money Smart for Older Adults, Money Smart for Small Businesses, and Teach Money Smart [1].", "intermediate": ["[Retrieval]", "Answer: The FDIC offers a variety of educational programs for financial literacy, including Money Smart for Adults, Money Smart for Older Adults, Money Smart for Small Businesses, and Teach Money Smart."]}, {"qa_pairs": [{"context": "No context provided", "question": "What should you consider before switching banks to ensure you choose the right account for your needs?", "short_answers": ["Understand your options", "Review practical tips"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "How can FDIC Consumer News help you become a more informed financial services user?", "short_answers": ["Provides practical information", "Offers useful tips and strategies"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "What resources does FDIC Consumer News offer to help manage your money effectively?", "short_answers": ["Helpful hints and advice", "Logical money management strategies"], "wikipage": null}], "wikipages": [{"title": "AML Topic 5", "url": "https://www.fdic.gov/espanol/noticiasconsumidor"}], "annotations": [{"knowledge": [], "long_answer": "Before switching banks, it's important to understand your options to ensure you obtain the account that suits your needs. FDIC Consumer News provides practical information to help individuals become more aware and informed users of financial services. FDIC Consumer News offers selected editions and articles that provide helpful hints, effective advice, and logical strategies for managing and stretching your money efficiently."}, {"knowledge": [{"content": "Are you thinking about switching banks? There is a lot to consider before moving to another financial institution. If you are considering leaving the bank where you currently have a checking or savings account, you need to understand your options. To ensure you get the account that suits your needs and perhaps avoid some stress, take a moment to review these tips.", "wikipage": "AML Topic 5"}, {"content": "FDIC Consumer News in Spanish provides practical information on how to become a more aware and informed user regarding financial services. The editions and selected articles offer helpful tips, effective advice, and logical strategies to protect and stretch the money you have worked hard to earn.", "wikipage": "AML Topic 5"}, {"content": "The selected editions and articles offer useful tips, effective advice, and logical strategies to protect and stretch the money you have worked hard to earn.", "wikipage": "AML Topic 5"}], "long_answer": "Before switching banks, it's important to understand your options to ensure you obtain the account that suits your needs. Reviewing practical tips and strategies can help you make an informed decision and potentially avoid stress.\n\nFDIC Consumer News provides practical information to help individuals become more aware and informed users of financial services. It offers useful tips, effective advice, and logical strategies to protect and maximize your hard-earned money.\n\nFDIC Consumer News offers selected editions and articles that provide helpful hints, effective advice, and logical strategies for managing and stretching your money efficiently."}], "sample_id": "aml-topic-5-2", "question": "What should you consider before switching banks to ensure you choose the right account for your needs?", "docs": [{"id": "doc-aml-topic-5-2-2", "title": "AML Topic 5 Part 2", "text": "and consider before switching to another financial institution. If you are thinking about leaving the bank where you currently have a checking or savings account, you should understand your options. To ensure you get the account that suits your needs and perhaps avoid some stress, take a moment to review these tips.\nView the full article\nList of articles from\nConsumer News\nClick on the arrows on the sides to sort in Ascending or Descending order\nArticle\nTopic\nDate\nIs your s", "score": 0.85, "summary": "Translate this text to English: and consider before switching to another financial institution. If you are thinking about leaving the bank where you currently have a checking or savings account...", "extraction": "and consider before switching to another financial institution. If you are thinking about leaving the bank where you currently have a checking or savings account, you need to understand your options. To ensure"}], "answer": "Before switching banks, it's important to understand your options to ensure you obtain the account that suits your needs. FDIC Consumer News provides practical information to help individuals become more aware and informed users of financial services. FDIC Consumer News offers selected editions and articles that provide helpful hints, effective advice, and logical strategies for managing and stretching your money efficiently.", "output": "Before switching banks to ensure you choose the right account for your needs, you should consider the fees, the location of the bank, the accessibility of the bank, the customer service of the bank, the account features, and the reputation of the bank [1].", "intermediate": ["[Retrieval]", "Before switching banks to ensure you choose the right account for your needs, you should consider the fees, the location of the bank, the accessibility of the bank, the customer service of the bank, the account features, and the reputation of the bank."]}, {"qa_pairs": [{"context": "No context provided", "question": "How can I ensure my money is insured by the FDIC?", "short_answers": ["Verify bank is FDIC member", "FDIC insures deposits up to legal limit"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "What should I be aware of to avoid falling victim to scammers and fake banks?", "short_answers": ["Be cautious of unsolicited communications", "Verify legitimacy of financial institutions"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "What steps can seniors take to protect themselves from fraud?", "short_answers": ["Stay informed about scams", "Be cautious with personal information"], "wikipage": null}], "wikipages": [{"title": "AML Topic 5", "url": "https://www.fdic.gov/espanol/noticiasconsumidor"}], "annotations": [{"knowledge": [], "long_answer": "To ensure your money is insured by the FDIC, you should verify that your bank is an FDIC member. To avoid scammers and fake banks, be cautious of unsolicited communications and verify the legitimacy of any financial institution before engaging in transactions. Seniors can protect themselves from fraud by staying informed about common scams targeting their age group, being cautious with personal information, and consulting with trusted family members or professionals before making financial decisions."}, {"knowledge": [{"content": "Is My Money Insured by the FDIC? Deposit Insurance August 2023", "wikipage": "AML Topic 5"}, {"content": "Scammers and Fake Banks Mobile Banking November 2023", "wikipage": "AML Topic 5"}, {"content": "Fraud Against the Elderly Seniors and Caregivers October 2023", "wikipage": "AML Topic 5"}], "long_answer": "To ensure your money is insured by the FDIC, you should verify that your bank is an FDIC member. The FDIC insures deposits in member banks up to the legal limit, providing protection in case the bank fails.\n\nTo avoid scammers and fake banks, be cautious of unsolicited communications and verify the legitimacy of any financial institution before engaging in transactions. Always check for official certifications and reviews.\n\nSeniors can protect themselves from fraud by staying informed about common scams targeting their age group, being cautious with personal information, and consulting with trusted family members or professionals before making financial decisions."}], "sample_id": "aml-topic-5-3", "question": "How can I ensure my money is insured by the FDIC?", "docs": [{"id": "doc-aml-topic-5-3-2", "title": "AML Topic 5 Part 2", "text": "Fake ores and banks\nMobile Banking\nOctober 2023\nFraud Against the Elderly\nSeniors and Caregivers\nSeptember 2023\nCredit Cards for Young Adults\nYoung Adults\nAugust 2023\nIs My Money Insured by the FDIC?\nDeposit Insurance\nJuly 2023\nThe Functioning and Importance of Real Estate Appraisals\nMortgages\nJune 2023\nDo You Need a Loan for Your Small Business?\nSmall Businesses\nMay 2023\nYour First Job?\nYoung Adults\nApril 2023\nFinancial Inclusion and Empowerment", "score": 0.85, "summary": "Mobile Banking October 2023 Fake Ores and Banks Fraud Against the Elderly Seniors and Caregivers September 2023 Credit Cards for Adul...", "extraction": "Fake gold and banks\nMobile banking\nOctober 2023\nFraud against the elderly\nSeniors and caregivers\nSeptember 2023\nCredit cards for young adults\nYoung adults\nAugust 2023\nIs my d"}], "answer": "To ensure your money is insured by the FDIC, you should verify that your bank is an FDIC member. To avoid scammers and fake banks, be cautious of unsolicited communications and verify the legitimacy of any financial institution before engaging in transactions. Seniors can protect themselves from fraud by staying informed about common scams targeting their age group, being cautious with personal information, and consulting with trusted family members or professionals before making financial decisions.", "output": "To ensure your money is insured by the FDIC, you should check the FDIC's website to see if your bank is insured [1].", "intermediate": ["[Retrieval]", "To ensure your money is insured by the FDIC, you should check the FDIC's website to see if your bank is insured."]}, {"qa_pairs": [{"context": "No context provided", "question": "What is the standard coverage amount provided by FDIC deposit insurance?", "short_answers": ["$250,000 per depositante", "Per insured bank", "Each account ownership category"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "Since when has the FDIC been insuring deposits, and has any depositor ever lost insured funds due to a bank failure?", "short_answers": ["Since January 1, 1934", "No depositor has lost insured funds"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "How many banks and savings institutions does the FDIC directly supervise and examine?", "short_answers": ["More than 5,000 banks", "Ensures safety and soundness"], "wikipage": null}], "wikipages": [{"title": "AML Topic 6", "url": "https://www.fdic.gov/espanol/acerca"}], "annotations": [{"knowledge": [], "long_answer": "The standard coverage amount provided by FDIC deposit insurance is $250,000 per depositante, per insured bank, for each account ownership category. The FDIC has been insuring deposits since January 1, 1934, and no depositor has lost a single cent of insured funds due to a bank failure since then. The FDIC directly supervises and examines more than 5,000 banks and savings institutions to ensure their operational safety and soundness."}, {"knowledge": [{"content": "The standard deposit insurance coverage is $250,000 per depositor, per insured bank for each account ownership category.", "wikipage": "AML Topic 6"}, {"content": "Since the inception of FDIC insurance on January 1, 1934, no depositor has lost a single cent of insured funds as a result of a bank failure.", "wikipage": "AML Topic 6"}, {"content": "The FDIC directly supervises and examines more than 5,000 banks and savings institutions to ensure soundness and safety in their operations.", "wikipage": "AML Topic 6"}], "long_answer": "The standard coverage amount provided by FDIC deposit insurance is $250,000 per depositante, per insured bank, for each account ownership category.\n\nThe FDIC has been insuring deposits since January 1, 1934, and no depositor has lost a single cent of insured funds due to a bank failure since then.\n\nThe FDIC directly supervises and examines more than 5,000 banks and savings institutions to ensure their operational safety and soundness."}], "sample_id": "aml-topic-6-3", "question": "What is the standard coverage amount provided by FDIC deposit insurance?", "docs": [{"id": "doc-aml-topic-6-3-1", "title": "AML Topic 6 Part 1", "text": "The text translates to:\n\nBank resolution is a process that allows the continuity of essential services of a financial institution when it is considered non-viable (or bankrupt); and\nIt serves as a \"judicial administrator\" of insolvent financial institutions.\nDeposit insurance\nThe standard coverage of deposit insurance is $250,000 per depositor, per insured bank for each account ownership category. Since the inception of FDIC insurance on January 1, 1934, no", "score": 0.9, "summary": "The text appears to be cut off, but I can translate the portion provided:\n\n\"The banking resolution is a process that allows the continuity of essential services of a financial institution when it is considered...\" \n\nIf you have more of the text, please provide it for a complete translation.", "extraction": "The standard deposit insurance coverage is $250,000 per depositor, per insured bank for each account ownership category."}], "answer": "The standard coverage amount provided by FDIC deposit insurance is $250,000 per depositante, per insured bank, for each account ownership category. The FDIC has been insuring deposits since January 1, 1934, and no depositor has lost a single cent of insured funds due to a bank failure since then. The FDIC directly supervises and examines more than 5,000 banks and savings institutions to ensure their operational safety and soundness.", "output": "The standard coverage amount provided by FDIC deposit insurance is $250,000 per depositor, per insured bank for each account ownership category [1].", "intermediate": ["[Retrieval]", "The standard coverage amount provided by FDIC deposit insurance is $250,000 per depositor, per insured bank for each account ownership category."]}, {"qa_pairs": [{"context": "No context provided", "question": "What types of resources does the FDIC provide for bankers?", "short_answers": ["Financial education materials", "Data tools", "Documentation of laws and regulations"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "Where can bankers find information on FDIC's laws and regulations?", "short_answers": ["Documentation of laws and regulations", "FDIC resources"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "Does the FDIC offer any international seminars and training?", "short_answers": ["International seminars", "Training"], "wikipage": null}], "wikipages": [{"title": "AML Topic 7", "url": "https://www.fdic.gov/consumer-resource-center"}], "annotations": [{"knowledge": [], "long_answer": "The FDIC provides a variety of resources for bankers, including financial education materials, data tools, documentation of laws and regulations, and information on important initiatives. Bankers can find information on FDIC's laws and regulations within the resources provided by the FDIC, which includes documentation of laws and regulations. Yes, the FDIC offers international seminars and training as part of its resources."}, {"knowledge": [{"content": "The FDIC provides a wealth of resources for consumers, bankers, analysts, and other stakeholders. Browse our collection of financial education materials, data tools, documentation of laws and regulations, information on important initiatives, and more.", "wikipage": "AML Topic 7"}, {"content": "The FDIC provides a wealth of resources for consumers, bankers, analysts, and other stakeholders. Browse our collection of financial education materials, data tools, documentation of laws and regulations, information on important initiatives, and more.", "wikipage": "AML Topic 7"}, {"content": "International Seminars and Training", "wikipage": "AML Topic 7"}], "long_answer": "The FDIC provides a variety of resources for bankers, including financial education materials, data tools, documentation of laws and regulations, and information on important initiatives.\n\nBankers can find information on FDIC's laws and regulations within the resources provided by the FDIC, which includes documentation of laws and regulations.\n\nYes, the FDIC offers international seminars and training as part of its resources."}], "sample_id": "aml-topic-7-2", "question": "What types of resources does the FDIC provide for bankers?", "docs": [{"id": "doc-aml-topic-7-2-1", "title": "AML Topic 7 Part 1", "text": ", Des Plaines, Ill. Assumes All Deposits of Pulaski Savings\u2026\nPODCASTS / January 06, 2025\nSmall Business Lending\nResources\nToggle submenu\nResources\nThe FDIC provides a wealth of resources for consumers, bankers, analysts, and other stakeholders. Browse our collection of financial education materials, data tools, documentation of laws and regulations, information on important initiatives, and more.\nAdditional FDIC Resources\nConsumer Resource Center\nBanker Resource Center\nDeposit Insurance\nSupervis", "score": 0.9, "summary": ", Des Plaines, Ill. Assumes All Deposits of Pulaski Savings\u2026 PODCASTS / January 06, 2025 Small Business Lending Resources Toggle submenu Resources The...", "extraction": "The FDIC provides a wealth of resources for consumers, bankers, analysts, and other stakeholders. Browse our collection of financial education materials, data tools, documentation of laws and regulations, information on important initiatives, and more."}], "answer": "The FDIC provides a variety of resources for bankers, including financial education materials, data tools, documentation of laws and regulations, and information on important initiatives. Bankers can find information on FDIC's laws and regulations within the resources provided by the FDIC, which includes documentation of laws and regulations. Yes, the FDIC offers international seminars and training as part of its resources.", "output": "Answer: The FDIC provides a wealth of resources for consumers, bankers, analysts, and other stakeholders [1].", "intermediate": ["[Retrieval]", "Answer: The FDIC provides a wealth of resources for consumers, bankers, analysts, and other stakeholders."]}, {"qa_pairs": [{"context": "No context provided", "question": "What is the purpose of the FDIC National Survey of Unbanked and Underbanked Households?", "short_answers": ["Insights into banking habits", "Focus on unbanked and underbanked households"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "How often does the FDIC publish the Quarterly Banking Profile?", "short_answers": ["Published quarterly", "Updates on banking industry performance"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "What type of information can be found in the Annual Risk Review by the FDIC?", "short_answers": ["Assessment of banking risks", "Economic and operational challenges"], "wikipage": null}], "wikipages": [{"title": "AML Topic 7", "url": "https://www.fdic.gov/consumer-resource-center"}], "annotations": [{"knowledge": [], "long_answer": "The FDIC National Survey of Unbanked and Underbanked Households aims to provide insights into the banking habits of households that do not have access to traditional banking services or are underserved by the banking industry. The FDIC publishes the Quarterly Banking Profile on a quarterly basis, providing updates and analysis on the banking industry's performance. The Annual Risk Review by the FDIC provides an assessment of the risks facing the banking industry, including economic, financial, and operational challenges that may affect banks."}, {"knowledge": [{"content": "FDIC National Survey of Unbanked and Underbanked Households", "wikipage": "AML Topic 7"}, {"content": "Quarterly Banking Profile", "wikipage": "AML Topic 7"}, {"content": "Annual Risk Review", "wikipage": "AML Topic 7"}], "long_answer": "The FDIC National Survey of Unbanked and Underbanked Households aims to provide insights into the banking habits of households that do not have access to traditional banking services or are underserved by the banking industry.\n\nThe FDIC publishes the Quarterly Banking Profile on a quarterly basis, providing updates and analysis on the banking industry's performance.\n\nThe Annual Risk Review by the FDIC provides an assessment of the risks facing the banking industry, including economic, financial, and operational challenges that may affect banks."}], "sample_id": "aml-topic-7-3", "question": "What is the purpose of the FDIC National Survey of Unbanked and Underbanked Households?", "docs": [{"id": "doc-aml-topic-7-3-1", "title": "AML Topic 7 Part 1", "text": "state banking performance data. Browse our extensive research tools and reports.\nMore FDIC Analysis\nCenter for Financial Research\nConsumer Research\nFDIC National Survey of Unbanked and Underbanked Households\nQuarterly Banking Profile\nFDIC Academic Challenge\nFDIC Banking Issues in Focus\nAnnual Risk Review\nEVENT / February 25, 2025\nFDIC-Insured Institutions Reported Return on Assets of 1.11 Percent and Net Inc\u2026\nEVENT / December 12, 2024\nFDIC-Insured Institutions Reported Net Income of $65.4 Billio", "score": 0.9, "summary": "state banking performance data. Browse our extensive research tools and reports. More FDIC Analysis Center for Financial Research Consumer Research FD...", "extraction": "FDIC National Survey of Unbanked and Underbanked Households"}], "answer": "The FDIC National Survey of Unbanked and Underbanked Households aims to provide insights into the banking habits of households that do not have access to traditional banking services or are underserved by the banking industry. The FDIC publishes the Quarterly Banking Profile on a quarterly basis, providing updates and analysis on the banking industry's performance. The Annual Risk Review by the FDIC provides an assessment of the risks facing the banking industry, including economic, financial, and operational challenges that may affect banks.", "output": "Answer: The FDIC National Survey of Unbanked and Underbanked Households is a survey conducted by the Federal Deposit Insurance Corporation (FDIC) to collect information on the level of banking activity among U. S. households [1].", "intermediate": ["[Retrieval]", "Answer: The FDIC National Survey of Unbanked and Underbanked Households is a survey conducted by the Federal Deposit Insurance Corporation (FDIC) to collect information on the level of banking activity among U.S. households."]}, {"qa_pairs": [{"context": "No context provided", "question": "What type of resources does the FDIC provide to help consumers make smart financial decisions?", "short_answers": ["News", "Educational tools"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "How does the FDIC contribute to financial education?", "short_answers": ["Strengthens financial education", "Protects consumers"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "What is the purpose of the FDIC's Spanish website page?", "short_answers": ["Provide information", "Keep consumers informed"], "wikipage": null}], "wikipages": [{"title": "AML Topic 8", "url": "https://www.fdic.gov/espanol?page=1"}], "annotations": [{"knowledge": [], "long_answer": "The FDIC provides news, resources, and educational tools to help consumers make smart decisions and protect their savings. Besides insuring deposits, the FDIC offers resources to strengthen financial education and protect consumers. The purpose of the FDIC's Spanish website page is to provide information and resources to keep consumers well informed."}, {"knowledge": [{"content": "The FDIC provides news, resources, and educational tools to help consumers make smart decisions and protect their savings \u2014including this webpage in Spanish.", "wikipage": "AML Topic 8"}, {"content": "In addition to insuring deposits, the FDIC offers resources to strengthen financial education and protect consumers.", "wikipage": "AML Topic 8"}, {"content": "Here you will find more information about the FDIC and some of the most popular resources available to keep you well informed.", "wikipage": "AML Topic 8"}], "long_answer": "The FDIC provides news, resources, and educational tools to help consumers make smart decisions and protect their savings.\n\nBesides insuring deposits, the FDIC offers resources to strengthen financial education and protect consumers.\n\nThe purpose of the FDIC's Spanish website page is to provide information and resources to keep consumers well informed."}], "sample_id": "aml-topic-8-1", "question": "What type of resources does the FDIC provide to help consumers make smart financial decisions?", "docs": [{"id": "doc-aml-topic-8-1-2", "title": "AML Topic 8 Part 2", "text": "Deposit\nConsumer News\nAbout the FDIC\nBreadcrumb\nHome\nWelcome to the FDIC\nThe FDIC provides news, resources, and educational tools to help consumers make smart decisions and protect their savings \u2014 including this website in Spanish.\n\nHere you will find more information about the FDIC and some of the most popular resources available to keep you well-informed.\nFinancial Education\nIn addition to insuring your deposits, the FDIC", "score": 0.85, "summary": "Deposit News for the Consumer About the FDIC Breadcrumb Home Welcome to the FDIC The FDIC provides news, resources, and educational tools...", "extraction": "Deposit\nConsumer News\nAbout the FDIC\nBreadcrumb\nHome\nWelcome to the FDIC\nThe FDIC provides news, resources, and educational tools to help consumers make dec"}], "answer": "The FDIC provides news, resources, and educational tools to help consumers make smart decisions and protect their savings. Besides insuring deposits, the FDIC offers resources to strengthen financial education and protect consumers. The purpose of the FDIC's Spanish website page is to provide information and resources to keep consumers well informed.", "output": "Answer: The FDIC provides news, resources, and educational tools to help consumers make smart decisions and protect their savings [1].", "intermediate": ["[Retrieval]", "Answer: The FDIC provides news, resources, and educational tools to help consumers make smart decisions and protect their savings."]}, {"qa_pairs": [{"context": "No context provided", "question": "What is the standard insurance coverage amount provided by the FDIC for depositors?", "short_answers": ["$250,000 per depositor", "per insured bank"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "How does the FDIC insurance apply if a bank fails?", "short_answers": ["Covers accounts up to the limit", "Includes principal and interest"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "What types of account ownership categories are covered under FDIC insurance?", "short_answers": ["Individual accounts", "Joint accounts", "Retirement accounts", "Revocable trust accounts"], "wikipage": null}], "wikipages": [{"title": "AML Topic 8", "url": "https://www.fdic.gov/espanol?page=1"}], "annotations": [{"knowledge": [], "long_answer": "The standard insurance coverage amount provided by the FDIC is $250,000 per depositor, per insured bank, for each category of account ownership. If a bank fails, the FDIC insurance covers the accounts of depositors at insured banks up to the insurance coverage limit, including both principal and interest up to the date of the bank's closure. The FDIC insurance covers various categories of account ownership including individual accounts, joint accounts, retirement accounts, and revocable trust accounts."}, {"knowledge": [{"content": "The standard deposit insurance amount is $250,000 per depositor, per insured bank for each account ownership category: individual accounts, joint accounts, retirement accounts, and revocable trust accounts.", "wikipage": "AML Topic 8"}, {"content": "In the unlikely event that your bank fails, FDIC insurance covers depositors' accounts at insured banks up to the insurance coverage limit, including principal and interest as of the bank's closing date.", "wikipage": "AML Topic 8"}, {"content": "The standard deposit insurance amount is $250,000 per depositor, per insured bank for each account ownership category: individual accounts, joint accounts, retirement accounts, and revocable trust accounts.", "wikipage": "AML Topic 8"}], "long_answer": "The standard insurance coverage amount provided by the FDIC is $250,000 per depositor, per insured bank, for each category of account ownership.\n\nIf a bank fails, the FDIC insurance covers the accounts of depositors at insured banks up to the insurance coverage limit, including both principal and interest up to the date of the bank's closure.\n\nThe FDIC insurance covers various categories of account ownership including individual accounts, joint accounts, retirement accounts, and revocable trust accounts."}], "sample_id": "aml-topic-8-2", "question": "What is the standard insurance coverage amount provided by the FDIC for depositors?", "docs": [{"id": "doc-aml-topic-8-2-1", "title": "AML Topic 8 Part 1", "text": "Compliance with federal consumer protection laws by FDIC-supervised banks.  \nLearn more  \nYour FDIC-insured deposits  \nIn the unlikely event that your bank fails, FDIC insurance covers depositors' accounts at insured banks up to the insurance coverage limit, including principal and interest up to the date of the bank's closure. The standard deposit insurance amount is $250,000 per depositor, per insured bank for each", "score": 0.9, "summary": "Compliance with federal consumer protection laws by banks supervised by the FDIC. Learn more Your insured deposits by...", "extraction": "The standard deposit insurance amount is $250,000 per depositor, per insured bank for each account ownership category: individual accounts, joint accounts, retirement accounts, and revocable trust accounts."}], "answer": "The standard insurance coverage amount provided by the FDIC is $250,000 per depositor, per insured bank, for each category of account ownership. If a bank fails, the FDIC insurance covers the accounts of depositors at insured banks up to the insurance coverage limit, including both principal and interest up to the date of the bank's closure. The FDIC insurance covers various categories of account ownership including individual accounts, joint accounts, retirement accounts, and revocable trust accounts.", "output": "Answer: The standard insurance coverage amount provided by the FDIC for depositors is $250,000 per depositor, per insured bank for each account ownership category [1].", "intermediate": ["[Retrieval]", "Answer: The standard insurance coverage amount provided by the FDIC for depositors is $250,000 per depositor, per insured bank for each account ownership category."]}, {"qa_pairs": [{"context": "No context provided", "question": "What is the role of the FDIC's Consumer Protection Program?", "short_answers": ["Respond to consumer inquiries", "Handle complaints objectively"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "How does the Community Affairs Program support the FDIC's mission?", "short_answers": ["Promote stability", "Enhance public confidence"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "How can individuals stay updated with FDIC activities?", "short_answers": ["Subscribe to email updates", "Follow on social media"], "wikipage": null}], "wikipages": [{"title": "AML Topic 8", "url": "https://www.fdic.gov/espanol?page=1"}], "annotations": [{"knowledge": [], "long_answer": "The FDIC's Consumer Protection Program aims to offer optimal service by responding to consumer inquiries and complaints in an objective, informed, professional, precise, and timely manner. The Community Affairs Program supports the FDIC's mission by promoting stability and public confidence in the nation's financial system. Individuals can stay updated with FDIC activities by subscribing to their email updates and following their social media accounts on platforms like Facebook, X, Instagram, LinkedIn, Flickr, and YouTube."}, {"knowledge": [{"content": "The Consumer Protection Center strives to provide optimal service by responding to consumer inquiries and complaints in an objective, informed, professional, accurate, and timely manner.", "wikipage": "AML Topic 8"}, {"content": "The Community Affairs Program supports the FDIC's mission to promote stability and public confidence in the nation's financial system.", "wikipage": "AML Topic 8"}, {"content": "Stay Informed... Enter your email address Subscribe Follow the FDIC on Facebook Follow the FDIC on X Share on X Follow the FDIC on Instagram Follow the FDIC on LinkedIn Follow the FDIC on Flickr Follow the FDIC on YouTube", "wikipage": "AML Topic 8"}], "long_answer": "The FDIC's Consumer Protection Program aims to offer optimal service by responding to consumer inquiries and complaints in an objective, informed, professional, precise, and timely manner.\n\nThe Community Affairs Program supports the FDIC's mission by promoting stability and public confidence in the nation's financial system.\n\nIndividuals can stay updated with FDIC activities by subscribing to their email updates and following their social media accounts on platforms like Facebook, X, Instagram, LinkedIn, Flickr, and YouTube."}], "sample_id": "aml-topic-8-3", "question": "What is the role of the FDIC's Consumer Protection Program?", "docs": [{"id": "doc-aml-topic-8-3-1", "title": "AML Topic 8 Part 1", "text": "The mission of the Federal Deposit Insurance Corporation (FDIC) is to maintain stability and public confidence in the nation's financial system.  \nLearn more.  \nConsumer Protection Program  \nThe Consumer Protection Center strives to provide optimal service by responding to consumer inquiries and complaints in an objective, informed, professional, accurate, and timely manner.  \nLearn more  \nCommunity Affairs Program and contact information  \nThe Community Affairs Program", "score": 0.9, "summary": "The text appears to be incomplete, but I can translate the portion provided:\n\n\"Insurance Corporation (FDIC, for its acronym in English) is to maintain stability and public confidence in the nation's financial system. A...\"\n\nIf you have more of the text, feel free to provide it for a complete translation.", "extraction": "The Consumer Protection Center strives to provide optimal service by responding to consumer inquiries and complaints in an objective, informed, professional, accurate, and timely manner."}], "answer": "The FDIC's Consumer Protection Program aims to offer optimal service by responding to consumer inquiries and complaints in an objective, informed, professional, precise, and timely manner. The Community Affairs Program supports the FDIC's mission by promoting stability and public confidence in the nation's financial system. Individuals can stay updated with FDIC activities by subscribing to their email updates and following their social media accounts on platforms like Facebook, X, Instagram, LinkedIn, Flickr, and YouTube.", "output": "The FDIC's Consumer Protection Program strives to provide optimal service by responding to consumer inquiries and complaints in an objective, informed, professional, accurate, and timely manner [1].", "intermediate": ["[Retrieval]", "The FDIC's Consumer Protection Program strives to provide optimal service by responding to consumer inquiries and complaints in an objective, informed, professional, accurate, and timely manner."]}, {"qa_pairs": [{"context": "No context provided", "question": "What is the significance of a website using HTTPS when opening a bank account?", "short_answers": ["Secure connection", "Share sensitive information safely"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "How can the FDIC's Money Smart program assist small businesses?", "short_answers": ["Financial education for small businesses", "Resources for small business owners"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "What resources does the FDIC provide for financial education?", "short_answers": ["Money Smart programs", "Resources for different age groups"], "wikipage": null}], "wikipages": [{"title": "AML Topic 9", "url": "https://www.fdic.gov/espanol/educacion/cuenta-bancaria"}], "annotations": [{"knowledge": [], "long_answer": "A website using HTTPS indicates that you are connected securely to a . The FDIC's Money Smart program includes a component specifically for small businesses, called 'Money Smart para Peque\u00f1os Negocios,' which is designed to provide financial education and resources to help small business owners manage their finances more effectively. The FDIC provides a variety of resources for financial education, including programs like Money Smart for different age groups and communities, such as Money Smart for Adults, Money Smart for Seniors, and Money Smart for Youth."}, {"knowledge": [{"content": "A https:// means you have securely connected to a .gov website. Share sensitive information only on official and secure websites.", "wikipage": "AML Topic 9"}, {"content": "Money Smart for Small Businesses", "wikipage": "AML Topic 9"}, {"content": "Money Smart Program, Money Smart for Older Adults, Money Smart for Adults, Money Smart for Small Businesses, Money Smart for Youth", "wikipage": "AML Topic 9"}], "long_answer": "A website using HTTPS indicates that you are connected securely to a .gov website. This ensures that any sensitive information you share is protected and is only done on official and secure websites.\n\nThe FDIC's Money Smart program includes a component specifically for small businesses, called 'Money Smart para Peque\u00f1os Negocios,' which is designed to provide financial education and resources to help small business owners manage their finances more effectively.\n\nThe FDIC provides a variety of resources for financial education, including programs like Money Smart for different age groups and communities, such as Money Smart for Adults, Money Smart for Seniors, and Money Smart for Youth."}], "sample_id": "aml-topic-9-1", "question": "What is the significance of a website using HTTPS when opening a bank account?", "docs": [{"id": "doc-aml-topic-9-1-1", "title": "AML Topic 9 Part 1", "text": "Open a Bank Account | FDIC.gov\nSkip to main content\nAn official website of the United States government\nOfficial websites use .gov\nA\n.gov\nwebsite belongs to an official organization of the United States Government.\nSecure .gov websites use HTTPS\nAn\nhttps://\nmeans you\u2019ve safely connected to a .gov website. Share sensitive information only on official, secure websites.\nSwitch to English\n|\nGlossary\nSearch FDIC.gov\nSearch\nFinancial Education\nSeg", "score": 0.9, "summary": "Open a Bank Account | FDIC.gov\n\nSkip to main content\n\nAn official website of the United States government\n\nOfficial websites use .gov", "extraction": "A https:// means you have securely connected to a .gov website. Share sensitive information only on official and secure websites."}], "answer": "A website using HTTPS indicates that you are connected securely to a . The FDIC's Money Smart program includes a component specifically for small businesses, called 'Money Smart para Peque\u00f1os Negocios,' which is designed to provide financial education and resources to help small business owners manage their finances more effectively. The FDIC provides a variety of resources for financial education, including programs like Money Smart for different age groups and communities, such as Money Smart for Adults, Money Smart for Seniors, and Money Smart for Youth.", "output": "HTTPS is a protocol that provides security and privacy for online communication [1].", "intermediate": ["[Retrieval]", "HTTPS is a protocol that provides security and privacy for online communication."]}, {"qa_pairs": [{"context": "No context provided", "question": "What resources are available for individuals who do not have a bank account and wish to open one?", "short_answers": ["Resources available for opening a bank account", "Help for unbanked households"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "How can opening a deposit account in an FDIC-insured institution benefit your financial goals?", "short_answers": ["Important step for financial goals", "Consumer protections provided"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "What tool does the FDIC provide to help users find insured banking institutions?", "short_answers": ["BankFind application", "Search for FDIC-insured banks"], "wikipage": null}], "wikipages": [{"title": "AML Topic 9", "url": "https://www.fdic.gov/espanol/educacion/cuenta-bancaria"}], "annotations": [{"knowledge": [], "long_answer": "There are resources available to help individuals who are part of the 5. Opening a deposit account in an FDIC-insured institution can be one of the most important steps to take to reach your financial goals. The FDIC provides an online application called 'BankFind' that helps users search within the FDIC's extensive database to find both current and former FDIC-insured banking institutions by name, institution certificate number, website, or entity location."}, {"knowledge": [{"content": "If you are part of one of the 5.9 million households in the United States that still does not have a bank account and are looking to open one, there are resources available to assist you in this process.", "wikipage": "AML Topic 9"}, {"content": "Opening a deposit account at an FDIC-insured financial institution can be one of the most important steps to take in order to achieve your financial goals. Below you will find information about the most common deposit accounts, the consumer protections provided for those accounts, as well as additional information.", "wikipage": "AML Topic 9"}, {"content": "Our online application \"BankFind\" helps users search within the extensive FDIC database, allowing them to find both current and former FDIC-insured banking institutions by name, institution certificate number, website, or entity location.", "wikipage": "AML Topic 9"}], "long_answer": "There are resources available to help individuals who are part of the 5.9 million households in the United States without a bank account to open one.\n\nOpening a deposit account in an FDIC-insured institution can be one of the most important steps to take to reach your financial goals. It provides consumer protections for these accounts.\n\nThe FDIC provides an online application called 'BankFind' that helps users search within the FDIC's extensive database to find both current and former FDIC-insured banking institutions by name, institution certificate number, website, or entity location."}], "sample_id": "aml-topic-9-2", "question": "What resources are available for individuals who do not have a bank account and wish to open one?", "docs": [{"id": "doc-aml-topic-9-2-1", "title": "AML Topic 9 Part 1", "text": "Mortgage\nPrivacy\nGlossary of Financial Terms\nContact Information\nIf you are part of one of the 5.9 million households in the United States that still does not have a bank account and are looking to open one, there are resources available to assist you in this process.\nDeposit Accounts\nOpening a deposit account at an FDIC-insured financial institution can be one of the most important steps to take in order to achieve your financial goals. Below you will find inf", "score": 0.9, "summary": "Mortgage Privacy Financial Terms Glossary Contact Information If you are part of one of the 5.9 million households in the United States...", "extraction": "If you are part of one of the 5.9 million households in the United States that still does not have a bank account and are looking to open one, there are resources available to assist you in this process."}], "answer": "There are resources available to help individuals who are part of the 5. Opening a deposit account in an FDIC-insured institution can be one of the most important steps to take to reach your financial goals. The FDIC provides an online application called 'BankFind' that helps users search within the FDIC's extensive database to find both current and former FDIC-insured banking institutions by name, institution certificate number, website, or entity location.", "output": "Answer: There are resources available for individuals who do not have a bank account and wish to open one [1].", "intermediate": ["[Retrieval]", "Answer: There are resources available for individuals who do not have a bank account and wish to open one."]}, {"qa_pairs": [{"context": "No context provided", "question": "What is the new maximum insurance coverage for trust account holders with five or more beneficiaries starting April 1, 2024?", "short_answers": ["$1,250,000", "April 1, 2024"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "Does the new insurance coverage limit apply to both new and existing trust accounts?", "short_answers": ["Yes", "new and existing accounts"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "How can depositors inquire about the changes to trust account insurance coverage?", "short_answers": ["Deposit Insurance Form", "call FDIC"], "wikipage": null}], "wikipages": [{"title": "AML Topic 10", "url": "https://www.fdic.gov/espanol/seguro-de-deposito-en-breve"}], "annotations": [{"knowledge": [], "long_answer": "Starting April 1, 2024, the maximum insurance coverage for a trust account holder with five or more beneficiaries is $1,250,000 per holder for all trust accounts maintained at the same bank. Yes, the new insurance coverage limit of $1,250,000 applies to both new and existing trust accounts for all deposit products, including CDs, regardless of the purchase or maturity date. Depositors can inquire about the changes to trust account insurance coverage by using the Deposit Insurance Form or by calling the FDIC at 1-877-275-3342 or 1-877-ASK-FDIC."}, {"knowledge": [{"content": "Starting April 1, 2024, the maximum insurance coverage for a trust account holder with five or more beneficiaries is $1,250,000 per holder for all trust accounts (including POD/ITF accounts, revocable and irrevocable trusts) maintained at the same bank.", "wikipage": "AML Topic 10"}, {"content": "This change in coverage applies to both new and existing trust accounts, for all deposit products, including CDs, regardless of the purchase or maturity date.", "wikipage": "AML Topic 10"}, {"content": "Questions? You can submit your inquiry using the Deposit Insurance Form. You can also call the FDIC at 1-877-275-3342 or 1-877-ASK-FDIC.", "wikipage": "AML Topic 10"}], "long_answer": "Starting April 1, 2024, the maximum insurance coverage for a trust account holder with five or more beneficiaries is $1,250,000 per holder for all trust accounts maintained at the same bank.\n\nYes, the new insurance coverage limit of $1,250,000 applies to both new and existing trust accounts for all deposit products, including CDs, regardless of the purchase or maturity date.\n\nDepositors can inquire about the changes to trust account insurance coverage by using the Deposit Insurance Form or by calling the FDIC at 1-877-275-3342 or 1-877-ASK-FDIC."}], "sample_id": "aml-topic-10-2", "question": "What is the new maximum insurance coverage for trust account holders with five or more beneficiaries starting April 1, 2024?", "docs": [{"id": "doc-aml-topic-10-2-2", "title": "AML Topic 10 Part 2", "text": "The beneficiaries as desired, however, the coverage limit will not exceed $1,250,000 starting April 1, 2024.  \nThis change in coverage applies to both new and existing trust accounts, for all deposit products, including CDs, regardless of the purchase or maturity date.  \nQuestions?  \nYou can submit your inquiry using the Deposit Insurance Form.  \nYou can also call the FDIC at 1-877-275-3342 or 1-877-ASK-FDIC.  \nDeposit Insurance", "score": 0.85, "summary": "The beneficiaries as they wish, however, the coverage limit will not exceed $1,250,000 starting from April 1, 2024. This change in coverage ...", "extraction": "The beneficiaries as desired, however, the coverage limit will not exceed $1,250,000 starting April 1, 2024. This change in coverage applies to both new trust accounts."}], "answer": "Starting April 1, 2024, the maximum insurance coverage for a trust account holder with five or more beneficiaries is $1,250,000 per holder for all trust accounts maintained at the same bank. Yes, the new insurance coverage limit of $1,250,000 applies to both new and existing trust accounts for all deposit products, including CDs, regardless of the purchase or maturity date. Depositors can inquire about the changes to trust account insurance coverage by using the Deposit Insurance Form or by calling the FDIC at 1-877-275-3342 or 1-877-ASK-FDIC.", "output": "The new maximum insurance coverage for trust account holders with five or more beneficiaries starting April 1, 2024 is $1,250,000 [1].", "intermediate": ["[Retrieval]", "The new maximum insurance coverage for trust account holders with five or more beneficiaries starting April 1, 2024 is $1,250,000."]}, {"qa_pairs": [{"context": "No context provided", "question": "What types of deposit accounts are insured by the FDIC?", "short_answers": ["Checking accounts", "NOW accounts", "Savings accounts", "MMDAs", "Certificates of deposit"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "Is FDIC deposit insurance available for all financial institutions?", "short_answers": ["Only FDIC-insured banks"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "What assurances does the FDIC provide to consumers about their deposits?", "short_answers": ["Backed by the U.S. government", "Deposits are safe"], "wikipage": null}], "wikipages": [{"title": "AML Topic 10", "url": "https://www.fdic.gov/espanol/seguro-de-deposito-en-breve"}], "annotations": [{"knowledge": [], "long_answer": "In FDIC-insured banks, the FDIC insures money in deposit products, which include checking accounts, NOW accounts (negotiable order of withdrawal), savings accounts, money market deposit accounts (MMDAs), and time deposits like certificates of deposit (CDs). FDIC deposit insurance is only available for money deposited in an FDIC-insured bank. The FDIC provides assurance that money deposited in an FDIC-insured institution is backed by the full faith and credit of the United States government, ensuring that the deposited money is safe."}, {"knowledge": [{"content": "In FDIC-insured banks, the FDIC insures money in deposit products, which include: Checking accounts, NOW accounts (negotiable order of withdrawal), Savings accounts, Money market deposit accounts (MMDA), Time deposits, such as certificates of deposit (CDs).", "wikipage": "AML Topic 10"}, {"content": "However, FDIC deposit insurance is only available for money deposited in an FDIC-insured bank.", "wikipage": "AML Topic 10"}, {"content": "When you see the official FDIC symbol, whether in person or online, you know that the financial institution is backed by the full faith and credit of the United States government, and that your deposited money is safe.", "wikipage": "AML Topic 10"}], "long_answer": "In FDIC-insured banks, the FDIC insures money in deposit products, which include checking accounts, NOW accounts (negotiable order of withdrawal), savings accounts, money market deposit accounts (MMDAs), and time deposits like certificates of deposit (CDs).\n\nFDIC deposit insurance is only available for money deposited in an FDIC-insured bank.\n\nThe FDIC provides assurance that money deposited in an FDIC-insured institution is backed by the full faith and credit of the United States government, ensuring that the deposited money is safe."}], "sample_id": "aml-topic-10-3", "question": "What types of deposit accounts are insured by the FDIC?", "docs": [{"id": "doc-aml-topic-10-3-2", "title": "AML Topic 10 Part 2", "text": "This brochure provides basic information about the types of insured accounts, coverage limits, and how the FDIC insures your money if your bank fails. For more detailed information about deposit insurance coverage, our brochure titled\nYour Insured Deposits\nmay be helpful to you.\nView brochure as PDF\nView brochure as PDF\n(English)\nWhat types of deposits are insured?\nIn FDIC-insured banks, the FDIC insures money in deposit products, which", "score": 0.85, "summary": "This brochure provides basic information about the types of insured accounts, coverage limits, and how the FDIC insures your money if your bank...", "extraction": "This brochure provides basic information about the types of insured accounts, coverage limits, and how the FDIC insures your money if your bank fails. For more detailed information"}], "answer": "In FDIC-insured banks, the FDIC insures money in deposit products, which include checking accounts, NOW accounts (negotiable order of withdrawal), savings accounts, money market deposit accounts (MMDAs), and time deposits like certificates of deposit (CDs). FDIC deposit insurance is only available for money deposited in an FDIC-insured bank. The FDIC provides assurance that money deposited in an FDIC-insured institution is backed by the full faith and credit of the United States government, ensuring that the deposited money is safe.", "output": "Answer: The FDIC insures money in deposit products, which include checking accounts, savings accounts, and money market deposit accounts [1].", "intermediate": ["[Retrieval]", "Answer: The FDIC insures money in deposit products, which include checking accounts, savings accounts, and money market deposit accounts."]}, {"qa_pairs": [{"context": "No context provided", "question": "What resources does the FDIC provide for bankers to ensure compliance with laws and regulations?", "short_answers": ["Banker Resource Center", "Documentation of laws and regulations", "Information on important initiatives"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "How does the FDIC support international banking education and training?", "short_answers": ["International Seminars", "Training programs"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "What kind of research does the FDIC conduct related to community banking?", "short_answers": ["Community Banking Research Program"], "wikipage": null}], "wikipages": [{"title": "AML Topic 11", "url": "https://www.fdic.gov/consumer-resource-center/2025-01/ahorrar-para-lo-inesperado-y-para-el-futuro"}], "annotations": [{"knowledge": [], "long_answer": "The FDIC offers a variety of resources for bankers, including a Banker Resource Center, documentation of laws and regulations, and information on important initiatives. The FDIC supports international banking education and training through its International Seminars and Training programs. The FDIC conducts research related to community banking through its Community Banking Research Program."}, {"knowledge": [{"content": "The FDIC provides a wealth of resources for consumers, bankers, analysts, and other stakeholders. Browse our collection of financial education materials, data tools, documentation of laws and regulations, information on important initiatives, and more.", "wikipage": "AML Topic 11"}, {"content": "Additional FDIC Resources... International Seminars and Training", "wikipage": "AML Topic 11"}, {"content": "Additional FDIC Resources... Community Banking Research Program", "wikipage": "AML Topic 11"}], "long_answer": "The FDIC offers a variety of resources for bankers, including a Banker Resource Center, documentation of laws and regulations, and information on important initiatives.\n\nThe FDIC supports international banking education and training through its International Seminars and Training programs.\n\nThe FDIC conducts research related to community banking through its Community Banking Research Program."}], "sample_id": "aml-topic-11-2", "question": "What resources does the FDIC provide for bankers to ensure compliance with laws and regulations?", "docs": [{"id": "doc-aml-topic-11-2-1", "title": "AML Topic 11 Part 1", "text": "025\nMillennium Bank, Des Plaines, Ill. Assumes All Deposits of Pulaski Savings\u2026\nPODCASTS / January 06, 2025\nSmall Business Lending\nResources\nToggle submenu\nResources\nThe FDIC provides a wealth of resources for consumers, bankers, analysts, and other stakeholders. Browse our collection of financial education materials, data tools, documentation of laws and regulations, information on important initiatives, and more.\nAdditional FDIC Resources\nConsumer Resource Center\nBanker Resource Center\nDeposit", "score": 0.9, "summary": "025 Millennium Bank, Des Plaines, Ill. Assumes All Deposits of Pulaski Savings\u2026 PODCASTS / January 06, 2025 Small Business Lending Resources Toggle su...", "extraction": "The FDIC provides a wealth of resources for consumers, bankers, analysts, and other stakeholders. Browse our collection of financial education materials, data tools, documentation of laws and regulations, information on important initiatives, and more."}], "answer": "The FDIC offers a variety of resources for bankers, including a Banker Resource Center, documentation of laws and regulations, and information on important initiatives. The FDIC supports international banking education and training through its International Seminars and Training programs. The FDIC conducts research related to community banking through its Community Banking Research Program.", "output": "Answer: The FDIC provides a wealth of resources for consumers, bankers, analysts, and other stakeholders [1].", "intermediate": ["[Retrieval]", "Answer: The FDIC provides a wealth of resources for consumers, bankers, analysts, and other stakeholders."]}, {"qa_pairs": [{"context": "No context provided", "question": "What type of data and reports does the FDIC provide for understanding banking performance?", "short_answers": ["working papers", "state banking performance data", "Quarterly Banking Profile"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "How can enterprises stay informed about the latest FDIC policies and banking issues?", "short_answers": ["FDIC announcements", "policy changes", "upcoming conferences"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "What are some of the ways the FDIC communicates its activities and updates to the public?", "short_answers": ["press releases", "conferences and events", "podcasts"], "wikipage": null}], "wikipages": [{"title": "AML Topic 11", "url": "https://www.fdic.gov/consumer-resource-center/2025-01/ahorrar-para-lo-inesperado-y-para-el-futuro"}], "annotations": [{"knowledge": [], "long_answer": "The FDIC provides working papers, state banking performance data, and various research tools and reports. Enterprises can stay informed about the latest FDIC policies and banking issues by keeping up with FDIC announcements, reading speeches and testimony, learning about policy changes for banks, and getting details on upcoming conferences and events through the FDIC's news updates. The FDIC communicates its activities and updates to the public through press releases, financial institution letters, conferences and events, board matters, media campaigns, speeches, statements, testimonies, podcasts, videos, opinion editorials, and policy fact sheets."}, {"knowledge": [{"content": "working papers, and state banking performance data. Browse our extensive research tools and reports. More FDIC Analysis Center for Financial Research Consumer Research FDIC National Survey of Unbanked and Underbanked Households Quarterly Banking Profile FDIC Academic Challenge FDIC Banking Issues in Focus Annual Risk Review", "wikipage": "AML Topic 11"}, {"content": "The FDIC publishes regular updates on news and activities. Keep up with FDIC announcements, read speeches and testimony on the latest banking issues, learn about policy changes for banks, and get the details on upcoming conferences and events.", "wikipage": "AML Topic 11"}, {"content": "Find More FDIC News Press Releases Financial Institution Letters Conferences & Events Board Matters Natural Disasters Media Campaigns Speeches, Statements & Testimonies Podcasts Videos Opinion Editorials Policy Fact Sheets", "wikipage": "AML Topic 11"}], "long_answer": "The FDIC provides working papers, state banking performance data, and various research tools and reports. These include the Quarterly Banking Profile, Annual Risk Review, and other analyses from the Center for Financial Research.\n\nEnterprises can stay informed about the latest FDIC policies and banking issues by keeping up with FDIC announcements, reading speeches and testimony, learning about policy changes for banks, and getting details on upcoming conferences and events through the FDIC's news updates.\n\nThe FDIC communicates its activities and updates to the public through press releases, financial institution letters, conferences and events, board matters, media campaigns, speeches, statements, testimonies, podcasts, videos, opinion editorials, and policy fact sheets."}], "sample_id": "aml-topic-11-3", "question": "What type of data and reports does the FDIC provide for understanding banking performance?", "docs": [{"id": "doc-aml-topic-11-3-1", "title": "AML Topic 11 Part 1", "text": "working papers, and state banking performance data. Browse our extensive research tools and reports.\nMore FDIC Analysis\nCenter for Financial Research\nConsumer Research\nFDIC National Survey of Unbanked and Underbanked Households\nQuarterly Banking Profile\nFDIC Academic Challenge\nFDIC Banking Issues in Focus\nAnnual Risk Review\nEVENT / February 25, 2025\nFDIC-Insured Institutions Reported Return on Assets of 1.11 Percent and Net Inc\u2026\nEVENT / December 12, 2024\nFDIC-Insured Institutions Reported Net In", "score": 0.9, "summary": "working papers, and state banking performance data. Browse our extensive research tools and reports. More FDIC Analysis Center for Financial Research ...", "extraction": "working papers, and state banking performance data. Browse our extensive research tools and reports. More FDIC Analysis Center for Financial Research Consumer Research FDIC National Survey of Unbanked and Underbanked Households Quarterly Banking Profile FDIC Academic Challenge FDIC Banking Issues in Focus Annual Risk Review"}], "answer": "The FDIC provides working papers, state banking performance data, and various research tools and reports. Enterprises can stay informed about the latest FDIC policies and banking issues by keeping up with FDIC announcements, reading speeches and testimony, learning about policy changes for banks, and getting details on upcoming conferences and events through the FDIC's news updates. The FDIC communicates its activities and updates to the public through press releases, financial institution letters, conferences and events, board matters, media campaigns, speeches, statements, testimonies, podcasts, videos, opinion editorials, and policy fact sheets.", "output": "Answer: The FDIC provides data and reports on banking performance, including the FDIC Quarterly Banking Profile, which provides data on the financial condition and performance of insured banks and thrifts, and the FDIC National Survey of Unbanked and Underbanked Households, which provides information on the prevalence and characteristics of households that are unbanked or underbanked [1].", "intermediate": ["[Retrieval]", "Answer: The FDIC provides data and reports on banking performance, including the FDIC Quarterly Banking Profile, which provides data on the financial condition and performance of insured banks and thrifts, and the FDIC National Survey of Unbanked and Underbanked Households, which provides information on the prevalence and characteristics of households that are unbanked or underbanked."]}, {"qa_pairs": [{"context": "No context provided", "question": "What resources does the FDIC provide for bankers to enhance their understanding of laws and regulations?", "short_answers": ["Documentation of laws and regulations", "Financial education materials"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "How does the FDIC support community banking research?", "short_answers": ["Community Banking Research Program"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "What type of educational resources does the FDIC offer for international stakeholders?", "short_answers": ["International seminars", "Training"], "wikipage": null}], "wikipages": [{"title": "AML Topic 12", "url": "https://www.fdic.gov/consumer-resource-center/2024-12/lo-que-debe-saber-sobre-las-tarjetas-de-regalo"}], "annotations": [{"knowledge": [], "long_answer": "The FDIC provides a variety of resources for bankers, including documentation of laws and regulations, financial education materials, data tools, and information on important initiatives. The FDIC supports community banking research through its Community Banking Research Program, which is part of the additional resources provided for stakeholders. The FDIC offers international seminars and training as part of its educational resources for international stakeholders."}, {"knowledge": [{"content": "The FDIC provides a wealth of resources for consumers, bankers, analysts, and other stakeholders. Browse our collection of financial education materials, data tools, documentation of laws and regulations, information on important initiatives, and more.", "wikipage": "AML Topic 12"}, {"content": "Community Banking Research Program", "wikipage": "AML Topic 12"}, {"content": "International Seminars and Training", "wikipage": "AML Topic 12"}], "long_answer": "The FDIC provides a variety of resources for bankers, including documentation of laws and regulations, financial education materials, data tools, and information on important initiatives.\n\nThe FDIC supports community banking research through its Community Banking Research Program, which is part of the additional resources provided for stakeholders.\n\nThe FDIC offers international seminars and training as part of its educational resources for international stakeholders."}], "sample_id": "aml-topic-12-2", "question": "What resources does the FDIC provide for bankers to enhance their understanding of laws and regulations?", "docs": [{"id": "doc-aml-topic-12-2-1", "title": "AML Topic 12 Part 1", "text": ", 2025\nMillennium Bank, Des Plaines, Ill. Assumes All Deposits of Pulaski Savings\u2026\nPODCASTS / January 06, 2025\nSmall Business Lending\nResources\nToggle submenu\nResources\nThe FDIC provides a wealth of resources for consumers, bankers, analysts, and other stakeholders. Browse our collection of financial education materials, data tools, documentation of laws and regulations, information on important initiatives, and more.\nAdditional FDIC Resources\nConsumer Resource Center\nBanker Resource Center\nDepo", "score": 0.9, "summary": ", 2025 Millennium Bank, Des Plaines, Ill. Assumes All Deposits of Pulaski Savings\u2026 PODCASTS / January 06, 2025 Small Business Lending Resources Toggle...", "extraction": "The FDIC provides a wealth of resources for consumers, bankers, analysts, and other stakeholders. Browse our collection of financial education materials, data tools, documentation of laws and regulations, information on important initiatives, and more."}], "answer": "The FDIC provides a variety of resources for bankers, including documentation of laws and regulations, financial education materials, data tools, and information on important initiatives. The FDIC supports community banking research through its Community Banking Research Program, which is part of the additional resources provided for stakeholders. The FDIC offers international seminars and training as part of its educational resources for international stakeholders.", "output": "Answer: The FDIC provides a wealth of resources for consumers, bankers, analysts, and other stakeholders [1].", "intermediate": ["[Retrieval]", "Answer: The FDIC provides a wealth of resources for consumers, bankers, analysts, and other stakeholders."]}, {"qa_pairs": [{"context": "No context provided", "question": "What kind of data does the FDIC provide in its Quarterly Banking Profile?", "short_answers": ["Banking performance data", "Return on assets", "Net income"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "How does the FDIC keep the public informed about banking issues and policy changes?", "short_answers": ["Regular updates", "Announcements", "Policy changes"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "What resources does the FDIC offer for understanding the challenges faced by unbanked and underbanked households?", "short_answers": ["FDIC National Survey", "Unbanked and underbanked households"], "wikipage": null}], "wikipages": [{"title": "AML Topic 12", "url": "https://www.fdic.gov/consumer-resource-center/2024-12/lo-que-debe-saber-sobre-las-tarjetas-de-regalo"}], "annotations": [{"knowledge": [], "long_answer": "The FDIC's Quarterly Banking Profile provides data on banking performance, including metrics such as return on assets and net income for FDIC-insured institutions. The FDIC keeps the public informed through regular updates on news and activities, which include announcements, speeches, testimony on banking issues, policy changes for banks, and details on conferences and events. The FDIC offers resources such as the FDIC National Survey of Unbanked and Underbanked Households, which provides insights into the challenges faced by these groups."}, {"knowledge": [{"content": "Quarterly Banking Profile for First Quarter 2024\nFDIC-Insured Institutions Reported Return on Assets of 1.11 Percent and Net Inc\u2026", "wikipage": "AML Topic 12"}, {"content": "The FDIC publishes regular updates on news and activities. Keep up with FDIC announcements, read speeches and testimony on the latest banking issues, learn about policy changes for banks, and get the details on upcoming conferences and events.", "wikipage": "AML Topic 12"}, {"content": "FDIC National Survey of Unbanked and Underbanked Households", "wikipage": "AML Topic 12"}], "long_answer": "The FDIC's Quarterly Banking Profile provides data on banking performance, including metrics such as return on assets and net income for FDIC-insured institutions. For instance, FDIC-insured institutions reported a return on assets of 1.11 percent in one of the reports.\n\nThe FDIC keeps the public informed through regular updates on news and activities, which include announcements, speeches, testimony on banking issues, policy changes for banks, and details on conferences and events.\n\nThe FDIC offers resources such as the FDIC National Survey of Unbanked and Underbanked Households, which provides insights into the challenges faced by these groups."}], "sample_id": "aml-topic-12-3", "question": "What kind of data does the FDIC provide in its Quarterly Banking Profile?", "docs": [{"id": "doc-aml-topic-12-3-1", "title": "AML Topic 12 Part 1", "text": "s, working papers, and state banking performance data. Browse our extensive research tools and reports.\nMore FDIC Analysis\nCenter for Financial Research\nConsumer Research\nFDIC National Survey of Unbanked and Underbanked Households\nQuarterly Banking Profile\nFDIC Academic Challenge\nFDIC Banking Issues in Focus\nAnnual Risk Review\nEVENT / February 25, 2025\nFDIC-Insured Institutions Reported Return on Assets of 1.11 Percent and Net Inc\u2026\nEVENT / December 12, 2024\nFDIC-Insured Institutions Reported Net", "score": 0.9, "summary": "s, working papers, and state banking performance data. Browse our extensive research tools and reports. More FDIC Analysis Center for Financial Resear...", "extraction": "Quarterly Banking Profile for First Quarter 2024\nFDIC-Insured Institutions Reported Return on Assets of 1.11 Percent and Net Inc\u2026"}], "answer": "The FDIC's Quarterly Banking Profile provides data on banking performance, including metrics such as return on assets and net income for FDIC-insured institutions. The FDIC keeps the public informed through regular updates on news and activities, which include announcements, speeches, testimony on banking issues, policy changes for banks, and details on conferences and events. The FDIC offers resources such as the FDIC National Survey of Unbanked and Underbanked Households, which provides insights into the challenges faced by these groups.", "output": "Answer: The FDIC provides data on the number of insured institutions, insured deposits, and the number of branches in its Quarterly Banking Profile [1].", "intermediate": ["[Retrieval]", "Answer: The FDIC provides data on the number of insured institutions, insured deposits, and the number of branches in its Quarterly Banking Profile."]}, {"qa_pairs": [{"context": "No context provided", "question": "What resources does the FDIC offer to bankers to help them with compliance and regulatory issues?", "short_answers": ["Banker Resource Center", "Deposit Insurance", "Supervision & Examinations"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "How can the FDIC's Community Banking Research Program benefit small banks?", "short_answers": ["Research and data", "Informed decisions", "Improve operations"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "What kind of educational materials does the FDIC provide to help stakeholders understand banking regulations?", "short_answers": ["Financial education materials", "Documentation of laws", "Important initiatives"], "wikipage": null}], "wikipages": [{"title": "AML Topic 13", "url": "https://www.fdic.gov/consumer-resource-center/2024-11/que-tanto-sabe-acerca-del-seguro-de-deposito"}], "annotations": [{"knowledge": [], "long_answer": "The FDIC offers a variety of resources for bankers, including a Banker Resource Center, information on deposit insurance, supervision and examinations, and documentation of laws and regulations. The FDIC's Community Banking Research Program provides research and data that can help small banks understand trends and challenges in the banking industry, allowing them to make informed decisions and improve their operations. The FDIC provides a collection of financial education materials that include documentation of laws and regulations, important initiatives, and more to help stakeholders understand banking regulations."}, {"knowledge": [{"content": "The FDIC provides a wealth of resources for consumers, bankers, analysts, and other stakeholders. Browse our collection of financial education materials, data tools, documentation of laws and regulations, information on important initiatives, and more. Additional FDIC Resources Consumer Resource Center Banker Resource Center Deposit Insurance Supervision & Examinations Laws & Regulations Resolutions Publications Forms Data Tools Community Banking Research Program International Seminars and Training", "wikipage": "AML Topic 13"}, {"content": "The FDIC provides a wealth of resources for consumers, bankers, analysts, and other stakeholders. Browse our collection of financial education materials, data tools, documentation of laws and regulations, information on important initiatives, and more. Additional FDIC Resources Consumer Resource Center Banker Resource Center Deposit Insurance Supervision & Examinations Laws & Regulations Resolutions Publications Forms Data Tools Community Banking Research Program International Seminars and Training", "wikipage": "AML Topic 13"}, {"content": "The FDIC provides a wealth of resources for consumers, bankers, analysts, and other stakeholders. Browse our collection of financial education materials, data tools, documentation of laws and regulations, information on important initiatives, and more.", "wikipage": "AML Topic 13"}], "long_answer": "The FDIC offers a variety of resources for bankers, including a Banker Resource Center, information on deposit insurance, supervision and examinations, and documentation of laws and regulations.\n\nThe FDIC's Community Banking Research Program provides research and data that can help small banks understand trends and challenges in the banking industry, allowing them to make informed decisions and improve their operations.\n\nThe FDIC provides a collection of financial education materials that include documentation of laws and regulations, important initiatives, and more to help stakeholders understand banking regulations."}], "sample_id": "aml-topic-13-2", "question": "What resources does the FDIC offer to bankers to help them with compliance and regulatory issues?", "docs": [{"id": "doc-aml-topic-13-2-1", "title": "AML Topic 13 Part 1", "text": ", 2025\nMillennium Bank, Des Plaines, Ill. Assumes All Deposits of Pulaski Savings\u2026\nPODCASTS / January 06, 2025\nSmall Business Lending\nResources\nToggle submenu\nResources\nThe FDIC provides a wealth of resources for consumers, bankers, analysts, and other stakeholders. Browse our collection of financial education materials, data tools, documentation of laws and regulations, information on important initiatives, and more.\nAdditional FDIC Resources\nConsumer Resource Center\nBanker Resource Center\nDepo", "score": 0.9, "summary": ", 2025 Millennium Bank, Des Plaines, Ill. Assumes All Deposits of Pulaski Savings\u2026 PODCASTS / January 06, 2025 Small Business Lending Resources Toggle...", "extraction": "The FDIC provides a wealth of resources for consumers, bankers, analysts, and other stakeholders. Browse our collection of financial education materials, data tools, documentation of laws and regulations, information on important initiatives, and more. Additional FDIC Resources Consumer Resource Center Banker Resource Center Deposit Insurance Supervision & Examinations Laws & Regulations Resolutions Publications Forms Data Tools Community Banking Research Program International Seminars and Training"}], "answer": "The FDIC offers a variety of resources for bankers, including a Banker Resource Center, information on deposit insurance, supervision and examinations, and documentation of laws and regulations. The FDIC's Community Banking Research Program provides research and data that can help small banks understand trends and challenges in the banking industry, allowing them to make informed decisions and improve their operations. The FDIC provides a collection of financial education materials that include documentation of laws and regulations, important initiatives, and more to help stakeholders understand banking regulations.", "output": "Answer: The FDIC provides a wealth of resources for consumers, bankers, analysts, and other stakeholders [1].", "intermediate": ["[Retrieval]", "Answer: The FDIC provides a wealth of resources for consumers, bankers, analysts, and other stakeholders."]}, {"qa_pairs": [{"context": "No context provided", "question": "What kind of reports and data does the FDIC provide regarding unbanked and underbanked households?", "short_answers": ["FDIC National Survey of Unbanked and Underbanked Households"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "How can I stay updated with the latest FDIC news and policy changes?", "short_answers": ["FDIC announcements", "Speeches and testimonies", "Policy changes", "Conferences and events"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "What is the reported return on assets for FDIC-insured institutions as of February 25, 2025?", "short_answers": ["Return on assets: 1.11 percent"], "wikipage": null}], "wikipages": [{"title": "AML Topic 13", "url": "https://www.fdic.gov/consumer-resource-center/2024-11/que-tanto-sabe-acerca-del-seguro-de-deposito"}], "annotations": [{"knowledge": [], "long_answer": "The FDIC provides the 'FDIC National Survey of Unbanked and Underbanked Households' as part of its consumer research tools. You can keep up with FDIC announcements by reading their regular updates, speeches, testimonies, and learning about policy changes for banks, as well as details on upcoming conferences and events. As of February 25, 2025, FDIC-insured institutions reported a return on assets of 1."}, {"knowledge": [{"content": "FDIC National Survey of Unbanked and Underbanked Households", "wikipage": "AML Topic 13"}, {"content": "The FDIC publishes regular updates on news and activities. Keep up with FDIC announcements, read speeches and testimony on the latest banking issues, learn about policy changes for banks, and get the details on upcoming conferences and events.", "wikipage": "AML Topic 13"}, {"content": "EVENT / February 25, 2025 FDIC-Insured Institutions Reported Return on Assets of 1.11 Percent", "wikipage": "AML Topic 13"}], "long_answer": "The FDIC provides the 'FDIC National Survey of Unbanked and Underbanked Households' as part of its consumer research tools.\n\nYou can keep up with FDIC announcements by reading their regular updates, speeches, testimonies, and learning about policy changes for banks, as well as details on upcoming conferences and events.\n\nAs of February 25, 2025, FDIC-insured institutions reported a return on assets of 1.11 percent."}], "sample_id": "aml-topic-13-3", "question": "What kind of reports and data does the FDIC provide regarding unbanked and underbanked households?", "docs": [{"id": "doc-aml-topic-13-3-1", "title": "AML Topic 13 Part 1", "text": "s, working papers, and state banking performance data. Browse our extensive research tools and reports.\nMore FDIC Analysis\nCenter for Financial Research\nConsumer Research\nFDIC National Survey of Unbanked and Underbanked Households\nQuarterly Banking Profile\nFDIC Academic Challenge\nFDIC Banking Issues in Focus\nAnnual Risk Review\nEVENT / February 25, 2025\nFDIC-Insured Institutions Reported Return on Assets of 1.11 Percent and Net Inc\u2026\nEVENT / December 12, 2024\nFDIC-Insured Institutions Reported Net", "score": 0.9, "summary": "s, working papers, and state banking performance data. Browse our extensive research tools and reports. More FDIC Analysis Center for Financial Resear...", "extraction": "FDIC National Survey of Unbanked and Underbanked Households"}], "answer": "The FDIC provides the 'FDIC National Survey of Unbanked and Underbanked Households' as part of its consumer research tools. You can keep up with FDIC announcements by reading their regular updates, speeches, testimonies, and learning about policy changes for banks, as well as details on upcoming conferences and events. As of February 25, 2025, FDIC-insured institutions reported a return on assets of 1.", "output": "The FDIC provides reports and data regarding unbanked and underbanked households in its National Survey of Unbanked and Underbanked Households [1].", "intermediate": ["[Retrieval]", "The FDIC provides reports and data regarding unbanked and underbanked households in its National Survey of Unbanked and Underbanked Households."]}, {"qa_pairs": [{"context": "No context provided", "question": "What is the primary purpose of the FDIC as described on their official website?", "short_answers": ["Maintain stability", "Public confidence in financial system"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "How can you ensure that you are securely connected to the FDIC's official website?", "short_answers": ["URL begins with 'https://'", "Connection is encrypted"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "How can you identify an official federal government website, such as the FDIC's site?", "short_answers": [".gov or .mil domain", "Identifies federal government sites"], "wikipage": null}], "wikipages": [{"title": "AML Topic 15", "url": "https://www.fdic.gov/consumer-resource-center/fdic-consumer-news-en-espanol"}], "annotations": [{"knowledge": [], "long_answer": "The primary purpose of the FDIC, as described on their official website, is to maintain stability and public confidence in the nation\u2019s financial system. You can ensure that you are securely connected to the FDIC's official website by checking that the URL begins with 'https://' which indicates that the connection is encrypted and secure. An official federal government website, such as the FDIC's site, can be identified by its domain, which often ends in ."}, {"knowledge": [{"content": "The Federal Deposit Insurance Corporation (FDIC) is an independent agency created by the Congress to maintain stability and public confidence in the nation\u2019s financial system.", "wikipage": "AML Topic 15"}, {"content": "The https:// ensures that you are connecting to the official website and that any information you provide is encrypted and transmitted securely.", "wikipage": "AML Topic 15"}, {"content": "Federal government websites often end in .gov or .mil. Before sharing sensitive information, make sure you\u2019re on a federal government site.", "wikipage": "AML Topic 15"}], "long_answer": "The primary purpose of the FDIC, as described on their official website, is to maintain stability and public confidence in the nation\u2019s financial system.\n\nYou can ensure that you are securely connected to the FDIC's official website by checking that the URL begins with 'https://' which indicates that the connection is encrypted and secure.\n\nAn official federal government website, such as the FDIC's site, can be identified by its domain, which often ends in .gov or .mil."}], "sample_id": "aml-topic-15-1", "question": "What is the primary purpose of the FDIC as described on their official website?", "docs": [{"id": "doc-aml-topic-15-1-2", "title": "AML Topic 15 Part 2", "text": "le submenu\nAbout\nThe Federal Deposit Insurance Corporation (FDIC) is an independent agency created by the Congress to maintain stability and public confidence in the nation\u2019s financial system. Learn about the FDIC\u2019s mission, leadership, history, career opportunities, and more.\nLearn More About the FDIC\nWhat We Do\nLeadership\nCareers\nInitiatives\nStrategic Plans\nFinancial Reports\nHistory\nGovernance\nOmbudsman\nDoing Business with the FDIC\nPRESS RELEASE / February 25, 2025\nFDIC-Insured Institutions Re", "score": 0.85, "summary": "le submenu About The Federal Deposit Insurance Corporation (FDIC) is an independent agency created by the Congress to maintain stability and public co...", "extraction": "le submenu\nAbout\nThe Federal Deposit Insurance Corporation (FDIC) is an independent agency created by the Congress to maintain stability and public confidence in the nation\u2019s financial system. Learn a"}], "answer": "The primary purpose of the FDIC, as described on their official website, is to maintain stability and public confidence in the nation\u2019s financial system. You can ensure that you are securely connected to the FDIC's official website by checking that the URL begins with 'https://' which indicates that the connection is encrypted and secure. An official federal government website, such as the FDIC's site, can be identified by its domain, which often ends in .", "output": "Answer: The FDIC's mission is to maintain stability and public confidence in the U. S. financial system [1].", "intermediate": ["[Retrieval]", "Answer: The FDIC's mission is to maintain stability and public confidence in the U.S. financial system."]}, {"qa_pairs": [{"context": "No context provided", "question": "What types of resources does the FDIC provide for stakeholders like consumers and bankers?", "short_answers": ["Financial education materials", "Data tools", "Laws and regulations documentation"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "Where can bankers find specific resources tailored for their needs on the FDIC website?", "short_answers": ["Banker Resource Center"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "What kind of research does the FDIC offer regarding the U.S. banking industry?", "short_answers": ["Quarterly banking profiles", "Working papers"], "wikipage": null}], "wikipages": [{"title": "AML Topic 15", "url": "https://www.fdic.gov/consumer-resource-center/fdic-consumer-news-en-espanol"}], "annotations": [{"knowledge": [], "long_answer": "The FDIC provides a variety of resources for consumers, bankers, analysts, and other stakeholders. Bankers can find specific resources tailored for their needs in the Banker Resource Center on the FDIC website. The FDIC offers research on the U."}, {"knowledge": [{"content": "The FDIC provides a wealth of resources for consumers, bankers, analysts, and other stakeholders. Browse our collection of financial education materials, data tools, documentation of laws and regulations, information on important initiatives, and more.", "wikipage": "AML Topic 15"}, {"content": "Additional FDIC Resources: Consumer Resource Center, Banker Resource Center, Deposit Insurance, Supervision & Examinations, Laws & Regulations, Resolutions, Publications, Forms, Data Tools, Community Banking Research Program, International Seminars and Training", "wikipage": "AML Topic 15"}, {"content": "The FDIC is proud to be a pre-eminent source of U.S. banking industry research, including quarterly banking profiles, working papers", "wikipage": "AML Topic 15"}], "long_answer": "The FDIC provides a variety of resources for consumers, bankers, analysts, and other stakeholders. These include financial education materials, data tools, documentation of laws and regulations, information on important initiatives, and more.\n\nBankers can find specific resources tailored for their needs in the Banker Resource Center on the FDIC website.\n\nThe FDIC offers research on the U.S. banking industry, including quarterly banking profiles and working papers."}], "sample_id": "aml-topic-15-2", "question": "What types of resources does the FDIC provide for stakeholders like consumers and bankers?", "docs": [{"id": "doc-aml-topic-15-2-1", "title": "AML Topic 15 Part 1", "text": "Bank, Des Plaines, Ill. Assumes All Deposits of Pulaski Savings\u2026\nPODCASTS / January 06, 2025\nSmall Business Lending\nResources\nToggle submenu\nResources\nThe FDIC provides a wealth of resources for consumers, bankers, analysts, and other stakeholders. Browse our collection of financial education materials, data tools, documentation of laws and regulations, information on important initiatives, and more.\nAdditional FDIC Resources\nConsumer Resource Center\nBanker Resource Center\nDeposit Insurance\nSupe", "score": 0.9, "summary": "Bank, Des Plaines, Ill. Assumes All Deposits of Pulaski Savings\u2026 PODCASTS / January 06, 2025 Small Business Lending Resources Toggle submenu Resources...", "extraction": "The FDIC provides a wealth of resources for consumers, bankers, analysts, and other stakeholders. Browse our collection of financial education materials, data tools, documentation of laws and regulations, information on important initiatives, and more."}], "answer": "The FDIC provides a variety of resources for consumers, bankers, analysts, and other stakeholders. Bankers can find specific resources tailored for their needs in the Banker Resource Center on the FDIC website. The FDIC offers research on the U.", "output": "The FDIC provides a wealth of resources for consumers, bankers, analysts, and other stakeholders [1].", "intermediate": ["[Retrieval]", "The FDIC provides a wealth of resources for consumers, bankers, analysts, and other stakeholders."]}, {"qa_pairs": [{"context": "No context provided", "question": "What resources does the FDIC offer for financial education?", "short_answers": ["Money Smart program", "Financial education resources"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "How can consumers find information about deposit insurance on the FDIC website?", "short_answers": ["Deposit insurance information", "FDIC website section"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "What topics are covered under the FDIC's financial education for youths?", "short_answers": ["Money Smart for Youth", "Financial education topics"], "wikipage": null}], "wikipages": [{"title": "AML Topic 16", "url": "https://www.fdic.gov/espanol/acerca-de-la-fdic"}], "annotations": [{"knowledge": [], "long_answer": "The FDIC offers a variety of financial education resources under the 'Money Smart' program, which includes Money Smart for Adults, Money Smart for Seniors, Money Smart for Small Businesses, and Money Smart for Youth. Consumers can find information about deposit insurance on the FDIC website under the section titled 'Seguro de dep\u00f3sito'. The FDIC's financial education for youths covers topics under the 'Money Smart para la juventud' program, which is part of the Money Smart initiative."}, {"knowledge": [{"content": "Money Smart Program  \nCommunity  \nTeach Money Smart  \nMoney Smart for Older Adults  \nMoney Smart for Adults  \nMoney Smart for Small Businesses  \nMoney Smart for Youth", "wikipage": "AML Topic 16"}, {"content": "Financial Education  \nDeposit Insurance", "wikipage": "AML Topic 16"}, {"content": "Money Smart for Youth  \nMoney Smart News for Kids", "wikipage": "AML Topic 16"}], "long_answer": "The FDIC offers a variety of financial education resources under the 'Money Smart' program, which includes Money Smart for Adults, Money Smart for Seniors, Money Smart for Small Businesses, and Money Smart for Youth.\n\nConsumers can find information about deposit insurance on the FDIC website under the section titled 'Seguro de dep\u00f3sito'.\n\nThe FDIC's financial education for youths covers topics under the 'Money Smart para la juventud' program, which is part of the Money Smart initiative."}], "sample_id": "aml-topic-16-1", "question": "What resources does the FDIC offer for financial education?", "docs": [{"id": "doc-aml-topic-16-1-1", "title": "AML Topic 16 Part 1", "text": "About the FDIC | FDIC.gov\nSkip to main content\nAn official website of the United States government\nOfficial websites use .gov\nA\n.gov\nwebsite belongs to an official organization of the United States Government.\nSecure .gov websites use HTTPS\nAn\nhttps://\nmeans you\u2019ve safely connected to a .gov website. Share sensitive information only on official, secure websites.\nSwitch to English\n|\nGlossary\nSearch FDIC.gov\nSearch\nFinancial Education\nInsurance of", "score": 0.9, "summary": "About the FDIC | FDIC.gov Skip to main content An official website of the United States government Official websites use .gov A site ...", "extraction": "Money Smart Program\nCommunity\nTeach Money Smart\nMoney Smart for Older Adults\nMoney Smart for Adults\nMoney Smart for Small Businesses\nMoney Smart for Youth"}], "answer": "The FDIC offers a variety of financial education resources under the 'Money Smart' program, which includes Money Smart for Adults, Money Smart for Seniors, Money Smart for Small Businesses, and Money Smart for Youth. Consumers can find information about deposit insurance on the FDIC website under the section titled 'Seguro de dep\u00f3sito'. The FDIC's financial education for youths covers topics under the 'Money Smart para la juventud' program, which is part of the Money Smart initiative.", "output": "Answer: The FDIC offers a variety of resources for financial education, including publications, videos, and online courses [1].", "intermediate": ["[Retrieval]", "Answer: The FDIC offers a variety of resources for financial education, including publications, videos, and online courses."]}, {"qa_pairs": [{"context": "No context provided", "question": "How does the FDIC fund its operations without receiving budget allocations from Congress?", "short_answers": ["Funded by premiums from banks", "No Congressional budget allocations"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "What are the main responsibilities of the FDIC in maintaining the stability of the financial system?", "short_answers": ["Insures deposits", "Supervises financial institutions", "Protects consumers", "Resolves large financial institutions"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "What prompted the creation of the FDIC in 1933?", "short_answers": ["Created in response to bank failures", "Established in 1933"], "wikipage": null}], "wikipages": [{"title": "AML Topic 16", "url": "https://www.fdic.gov/espanol/acerca-de-la-fdic"}], "annotations": [{"knowledge": [], "long_answer": "The FDIC funds its operations through the premiums paid by banks and savings associations to insure their deposits. The main responsibilities of the FDIC include insuring deposits, examining and supervising financial institutions to ensure their safety and soundness, and protecting consumers. The FDIC was created in 1933 in response to thousands of bank failures that occurred during the 1920s and early 1930s."}, {"knowledge": [{"content": "The FDIC does not receive budget allocations from Congress and is funded through the premiums paid by banks and savings associations to insure their deposits.", "wikipage": "AML Topic 16"}, {"content": "The mission of the Federal Deposit Insurance Corporation (FDIC) is to maintain stability and public confidence in the nation's financial system. To achieve this goal, the FDIC: Insures deposits, examines and supervises financial institutions to ensure their safety and soundness and to ensure consumer protection. It works to ensure that large and complex financial institutions can undergo a bank resolution, if necessary.", "wikipage": "AML Topic 16"}, {"content": "The FDIC is an independent agency of the United States federal government created in 1933 in response to thousands of bank failures that occurred in the 1920s and early 1930s.", "wikipage": "AML Topic 16"}], "long_answer": "The FDIC funds its operations through the premiums paid by banks and savings associations to insure their deposits.\n\nThe main responsibilities of the FDIC include insuring deposits, examining and supervising financial institutions to ensure their safety and soundness, and protecting consumers. Additionally, the FDIC works to ensure that large and complex financial institutions can be resolved if necessary.\n\nThe FDIC was created in 1933 in response to thousands of bank failures that occurred during the 1920s and early 1930s."}], "sample_id": "aml-topic-16-2", "question": "How does the FDIC fund its operations without receiving budget allocations from Congress?", "docs": [{"id": "doc-aml-topic-16-2-1", "title": "AML Topic 16 Part 1", "text": "Glossary of Financial Terms\nContact Information\nThe FDIC is an independent agency of the United States federal government created in 1933 in response to thousands of bank failures that occurred in the 1920s and early 1930s.\nThe FDIC does not receive budget allocations from Congress and is funded through the premiums paid by banks and savings associations to insure their deposits. The FDIC insures trillions of dollars in deposits cu", "score": 0.9, "summary": "Glossary of Financial Terms\n\nContact Information\n\nThe FDIC is an independent agency of the United States federal government created...", "extraction": "The FDIC does not receive budget allocations from Congress and is funded through the premiums paid by banks and savings associations to insure their deposits."}], "answer": "The FDIC funds its operations through the premiums paid by banks and savings associations to insure their deposits. The main responsibilities of the FDIC include insuring deposits, examining and supervising financial institutions to ensure their safety and soundness, and protecting consumers. The FDIC was created in 1933 in response to thousands of bank failures that occurred during the 1920s and early 1930s.", "output": "The FDIC is funded through the premiums paid by banks and savings associations to insure their deposits [1].", "intermediate": ["[Retrieval]", "The FDIC is funded through the premiums paid by banks and savings associations to insure their deposits."]}, {"qa_pairs": [{"context": "No context provided", "question": "What is the purpose of the FDIC's consumer protection program?", "short_answers": ["Educate and protect consumers", "Financial literacy initiatives"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "How does the FDIC ensure secure communication on its website?", "short_answers": ["HTTPS for secure connection", "Share information on secure sites"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "What educational resources does the FDIC offer for different age groups?", "short_answers": ["Money Smart program", "Resources for all age groups"], "wikipage": null}], "wikipages": [{"title": "AML Topic 17", "url": "https://www.fdic.gov/espanol/programa-de-proteccion-al-consumidor"}], "annotations": [{"knowledge": [], "long_answer": "The FDIC's consumer protection program is designed to educate and protect consumers regarding financial matters, including deposit insurance, overdraft fees, and financial literacy through initiatives like the Money Smart program. The FDIC ensures secure communication on its website by using HTTPS, which indicates a secure connection to a . The FDIC offers the Money Smart program, which includes tailored financial education resources for adults, seniors, small businesses, youth, and children."}, {"knowledge": [{"content": "Consumer Protection Program | FDIC.gov...Consumer Protection Program...Financial Education...Deposit Insurance...Overdraft Fees...Money Smart Program...", "wikipage": "AML Topic 17"}, {"content": "Secure .gov websites use HTTPS... An https:// means that you have securely connected to a .gov website. Share sensitive information only on official and secure websites.", "wikipage": "AML Topic 17"}, {"content": "Money Smart Program...Money Smart for Older Adults...Money Smart for Adults...Money Smart for Small Businesses...Money Smart for Youth...Money Smart News for Kids...", "wikipage": "AML Topic 17"}], "long_answer": "The FDIC's consumer protection program is designed to educate and protect consumers regarding financial matters, including deposit insurance, overdraft fees, and financial literacy through initiatives like the Money Smart program.\n\nThe FDIC ensures secure communication on its website by using HTTPS, which indicates a secure connection to a .gov site. Users are advised to share sensitive information only on official and secure sites.\n\nThe FDIC offers the Money Smart program, which includes tailored financial education resources for adults, seniors, small businesses, youth, and children."}], "sample_id": "aml-topic-17-1", "question": "What is the purpose of the FDIC's consumer protection program?", "docs": [{"id": "doc-aml-topic-17-1-1", "title": "AML Topic 17 Part 1", "text": "Consumer Protection Program | FDIC.gov\nSkip to main content\nAn official website of the United States government\nOfficial websites use .gov\nA .gov website belongs to an official United States Government organization.\nSecure .gov websites use HTTPS\nAn\nhttps://\nmeans you\u2019ve safely connected to a .gov website. Share sensitive information only on official, secure websites.\nSwitch to English\n|\nGlossary\nSearch FDIC.gov\nSearch\nFinancial Education", "score": 0.9, "summary": "Consumer Protection Program | FDIC.gov\n\nSkip to main content\n\nAn official website of the United States government\n\nOfficial websites...", "extraction": "Consumer Protection Program | FDIC.gov...Consumer Protection Program...Financial Education...Deposit Insurance...Overdraft Fees...Money Smart Program..."}], "answer": "The FDIC's consumer protection program is designed to educate and protect consumers regarding financial matters, including deposit insurance, overdraft fees, and financial literacy through initiatives like the Money Smart program. The FDIC ensures secure communication on its website by using HTTPS, which indicates a secure connection to a . The FDIC offers the Money Smart program, which includes tailored financial education resources for adults, seniors, small businesses, youth, and children.", "output": "Answer: The FDIC's consumer protection program's purpose is to educate consumers about their financial rights and responsibilities [1].", "intermediate": ["[Retrieval]", "Answer: The FDIC's consumer protection program's purpose is to educate consumers about their financial rights and responsibilities."]}, {"qa_pairs": [{"context": "No context provided", "question": "What is the role of the FDIC in consumer protection?", "short_answers": ["Insures deposits", "Examines and supervises banks"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "How can consumers contact the FDIC for inquiries?", "short_answers": ["Call 1-877-ASK-FDIC", "Available Monday to Saturday"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "What services are available for consumers with hearing impairments to contact the FDIC?", "short_answers": ["1-800-925-4618", "(703) 562-2289"], "wikipage": null}], "wikipages": [{"title": "AML Topic 17", "url": "https://www.fdic.gov/espanol/programa-de-proteccion-al-consumidor"}], "annotations": [{"knowledge": [], "long_answer": "The Federal Deposit Insurance Corporation (FDIC) plays a role in consumer protection by insuring deposits in banks and savings associations in the event that the institution fails. Consumers can contact the FDIC for inquiries through their toll-free call center at 1-877-ASK-FDIC (1-877-275-3342). Consumers with hearing impairments can contact the FDIC using the numbers 1-800-925-4618 or (703) 562-2289."}, {"knowledge": [{"content": "The FDIC insures deposits in banks and savings and loan associations in the event that the institution fails. The FDIC also examines and supervises state-chartered banks that are not members of the Federal Reserve System, promoting consumer confidence in the banking system.", "wikipage": "AML Topic 17"}, {"content": "Toll-Free Call Center: 1-877-ASK-FDIC (1-877-275-3342) Our hours are Monday to Friday, from 8 a.m. to 6 p.m. Eastern Time (except federal holidays), Saturday, from 8 a.m. to 1 p.m., and closed on Sunday.", "wikipage": "AML Topic 17"}, {"content": "People with hearing impairments: 1-800-925-4618 or (703) 562-2289", "wikipage": "AML Topic 17"}], "long_answer": "The Federal Deposit Insurance Corporation (FDIC) plays a role in consumer protection by insuring deposits in banks and savings associations in the event that the institution fails. Additionally, the FDIC examines and supervises state-regulated banks that are not members of the Federal Reserve System to promote consumer confidence in the banking system.\n\nConsumers can contact the FDIC for inquiries through their toll-free call center at 1-877-ASK-FDIC (1-877-275-3342). The call center operates Monday to Friday, from 8 a.m. to 6 p.m. Eastern Time (except federal holidays), Saturday from 8 a.m. to 1 p.m., and is closed on Sundays.\n\nConsumers with hearing impairments can contact the FDIC using the numbers 1-800-925-4618 or (703) 562-2289."}], "sample_id": "aml-topic-17-2", "question": "What is the role of the FDIC in consumer protection?", "docs": [{"id": "doc-aml-topic-17-2-1", "title": "AML Topic 17 Part 1", "text": "Cybernetics  \nMortgages  \nPrivacy  \nGlossary of Financial Terms  \nContact Information  \nThe Federal Deposit Insurance Corporation (FDIC), through its Consumer Protection Program, can answer many of your questions:  \nYour rights as a consumer  \nThe responsibilities of banks under federal consumer protection laws  \nYour insured deposits  \nOther regulatory agencies  \nYour rights as a consumer  \nThe FDIC insures deposits in banks and associations", "score": 0.9, "summary": "Cyber Privacy Mortgage Privacy Glossary of Financial Terms Contact Information The Federal Deposit Insurance Corporation (FDIC...", "extraction": "The FDIC insures deposits in banks and savings and loan associations in the event that the institution fails. The FDIC also examines and supervises state-chartered banks that are not members of the Federal Reserve System, promoting consumer confidence in the banking system."}], "answer": "The Federal Deposit Insurance Corporation (FDIC) plays a role in consumer protection by insuring deposits in banks and savings associations in the event that the institution fails. Consumers can contact the FDIC for inquiries through their toll-free call center at 1-877-ASK-FDIC (1-877-275-3342). Consumers with hearing impairments can contact the FDIC using the numbers 1-800-925-4618 or (703) 562-2289.", "output": "The FDIC's mission is to maintain stability and public confidence in the U. S. financial system [1].", "intermediate": ["[Retrieval]", "The FDIC's mission is to maintain stability and public confidence in the U.S. financial system."]}, {"qa_pairs": [{"context": "No context provided", "question": "What steps does the FDIC take to ensure banks comply with federal consumer protection laws?", "short_answers": ["Investigates consumer claims", "Responds to inquiries", "Educates consumers", "Supervises public perception", "Formulates policies"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "How does the FDIC educate consumers about their rights under federal consumer protection laws?", "short_answers": ["Dissemination of information", "Education programs"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "What is the typical response time if the FDIC decides not to contact the bank regarding a consumer complaint?", "short_answers": ["Response time is 14 days or less"], "wikipage": null}], "wikipages": [{"title": "AML Topic 17", "url": "https://www.fdic.gov/espanol/programa-de-proteccion-al-consumidor"}], "annotations": [{"knowledge": [], "long_answer": "The FDIC ensures compliance with federal consumer protection laws by investigating consumer claims related to supervised banks, responding to public and financial institution inquiries about consumer protection and lending equality, educating consumers about their rights, supervising public perception of consumer protection and banking issues, and participating in the formulation of related norms, policies, and procedures. The FDIC educates consumers about their rights under federal consumer protection laws through the dissemination of information and education programs that explain these rights and the relevant norms and laws. If the FDIC determines that it is not necessary to contact the bank regarding a consumer complaint, the typical response time is approximately 14 days or less."}, {"knowledge": [{"content": "...and ensure compliance with federal consumer protection laws by banks supervised by the FDIC, including those related to equal lending opportunities and unfair and deceptive practices. Our work includes: Investigating consumer claims related to the banks we supervise. Responding to inquiries from the public and financial institutions on consumer protection and equal lending opportunity issues. Educating consumers about their rights according to federal consumer protection standards and laws through dissemination. Monitoring public perception of consumer protection issues and the banking system, and identifying trends that could affect consumer rights and guarantees. Participating in the formulation of standards, policies, and procedures related to consumers.", "wikipage": "AML Topic 17"}, {"content": "The education of consumers about their rights in accordance with federal consumer protection standards and laws through their dissemination.", "wikipage": "AML Topic 17"}, {"content": "If after reviewing the issue we determine that it is not necessary to contact the person's bank, the final response time is approximately 14 days or less.", "wikipage": "AML Topic 17"}], "long_answer": "The FDIC ensures compliance with federal consumer protection laws by investigating consumer claims related to supervised banks, responding to public and financial institution inquiries about consumer protection and lending equality, educating consumers about their rights, supervising public perception of consumer protection and banking issues, and participating in the formulation of related norms, policies, and procedures.\n\nThe FDIC educates consumers about their rights under federal consumer protection laws through the dissemination of information and education programs that explain these rights and the relevant norms and laws.\n\nIf the FDIC determines that it is not necessary to contact the bank regarding a consumer complaint, the typical response time is approximately 14 days or less."}], "sample_id": "aml-topic-17-3", "question": "What steps does the FDIC take to ensure banks comply with federal consumer protection laws?", "docs": [{"id": "doc-aml-topic-17-3-1", "title": "AML Topic 17 Part 1", "text": "and ensure compliance with federal consumer protection laws by banks supervised by the FDIC, including those related to equal lending opportunities and unfair and deceptive practices. Our work includes:\nInvestigating consumer claims related to the banks we supervise.\nResponding to inquiries from the public and financial institutions on consumer protection and equal lending opportunity issues.", "score": 0.9, "summary": "...and ensure compliance with federal consumer protection laws by banks supervised by the FDIC, including those related...", "extraction": "...and ensure compliance with federal consumer protection laws by banks supervised by the FDIC, including those related to equal lending opportunities and unfair and deceptive practices. Our work includes: Investigating consumer claims related to the banks we supervise. Responding to inquiries from the public and financial institutions on consumer protection and equal lending opportunity issues. Educating consumers about their rights according to federal consumer protection standards and laws through dissemination. Monitoring public perception of consumer protection issues and the banking system, and identifying trends that could affect consumer rights and guarantees. Participating in the formulation of standards, policies, and procedures related to consumers."}], "answer": "The FDIC ensures compliance with federal consumer protection laws by investigating consumer claims related to supervised banks, responding to public and financial institution inquiries about consumer protection and lending equality, educating consumers about their rights, supervising public perception of consumer protection and banking issues, and participating in the formulation of related norms, policies, and procedures. The FDIC educates consumers about their rights under federal consumer protection laws through the dissemination of information and education programs that explain these rights and the relevant norms and laws. If the FDIC determines that it is not necessary to contact the bank regarding a consumer complaint, the typical response time is approximately 14 days or less.", "output": "Answer: The FDIC ensures banks comply with federal consumer protection laws by investigating consumer claims related to the banks they supervise, responding to inquiries from the public and financial institutions on consumer protection and equal lending opportunity issues, and enforcing compliance with consumer protection laws through the imposition of civil money penalties [1].", "intermediate": ["[Retrieval]", "Answer: The FDIC ensures banks comply with federal consumer protection laws by investigating consumer claims related to the banks they supervise, responding to inquiries from the public and financial institutions on consumer protection and equal lending opportunity issues, and enforcing compliance with consumer protection laws through the imposition of civil money penalties."]}, {"qa_pairs": [{"context": "No context provided", "question": "How does the FDIC support banks in addressing the banking needs of low and moderate-income communities?", "short_answers": ["Provides information and technical assistance", "Supports banks in serving low and moderate-income communities"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "What role does the FDIC play in fostering economic inclusion and community development?", "short_answers": ["Promotes economic inclusion", "Focuses on low and moderate-income consumers"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "In what way does the FDIC engage with various stakeholders to enhance community banking resources?", "short_answers": ["Convenes banks and community organizations", "Explores resources and promising practices"], "wikipage": null}], "wikipages": [{"title": "AML Topic 18", "url": "https://www.fdic.gov/espanol/programa-de-asuntos-comunitarios"}], "annotations": [{"knowledge": [], "long_answer": "The FDIC provides information and technical assistance to banks to help them address the banking and credit needs of the communities they serve, including those with low and moderate incomes. The FDIC promotes economic inclusion and community development initiatives aimed at low and moderate-income consumers and small businesses, ensuring they have safe and affordable access to deposit and credit services from FDIC-insured financial institutions. The FDIC convenes banks, state and local governments, and community organizations to explore resources and promising practices on a variety of topics, enhancing community banking resources."}, {"knowledge": [{"content": "It provides information and technical assistance to banks to help them respond to the banking and credit needs of the communities they serve, including low- and moderate-income individuals.", "wikipage": "AML Topic 18"}, {"content": "This program promotes economic inclusion and community development initiatives, which are aimed at low- and moderate-income (LMI) consumers and small businesses in particular, to safely and affordably expand access to deposit and credit services offered by FDIC-insured financial institutions.", "wikipage": "AML Topic 18"}, {"content": "It calls upon banks, state and local governments, and community organizations to explore resources and promising practices on a variety of topics.", "wikipage": "AML Topic 18"}], "long_answer": "The FDIC provides information and technical assistance to banks to help them address the banking and credit needs of the communities they serve, including those with low and moderate incomes.\n\nThe FDIC promotes economic inclusion and community development initiatives aimed at low and moderate-income consumers and small businesses, ensuring they have safe and affordable access to deposit and credit services from FDIC-insured financial institutions.\n\nThe FDIC convenes banks, state and local governments, and community organizations to explore resources and promising practices on a variety of topics, enhancing community banking resources."}], "sample_id": "aml-topic-18-2", "question": "How does the FDIC support banks in addressing the banking needs of low and moderate-income communities?", "docs": [{"id": "doc-aml-topic-18-2-2", "title": "AML Topic 18 Part 2", "text": "Translate this text to English: \"ma segura y asequible, poder ampliar el acceso a los servicios de dep\u00f3sito y cr\u00e9dito ofrecidos por instituciones financieras aseguradas por la FDIC. Para lograr este objetivo, la FDIC: Brinda informaci\u00f3n y asistencia t\u00e9cnica a los bancos para ayudarles a responder a las necesidades bancarias y crediticias de las comunidades a las que sirven, incluidas las personas de ingresos bajos y moderados. Convoca a bancos, gobiernos estatales y locales y a las organizaciones comunitarias para explorar recu\"\n\nTranslation: \"safer and more affordable, to expand access to deposit and credit services offered by FDIC-insured financial institutions. To achieve this objective, the FDIC: Provides information and technical assistance to banks to help them respond to the banking and credit needs of the communities they serve, including low- and moderate-income individuals. It convenes banks, state and local governments, and community organizations to explore reso\"", "score": 0.85, "summary": "\"More secure and affordable, to be able to expand access to deposit and credit services offered by FDIC-insured financial institutions. P...\"", "extraction": "Translate this text to English: \"more secure and affordable, to be able to expand access to deposit and credit services offered by FDIC-insured financial institutions. To achieve this goal, the FDIC: Provides inform\""}], "answer": "The FDIC provides information and technical assistance to banks to help them address the banking and credit needs of the communities they serve, including those with low and moderate incomes. The FDIC promotes economic inclusion and community development initiatives aimed at low and moderate-income consumers and small businesses, ensuring they have safe and affordable access to deposit and credit services from FDIC-insured financial institutions. The FDIC convenes banks, state and local governments, and community organizations to explore resources and promising practices on a variety of topics, enhancing community banking resources.", "output": "Answer: The FDIC provides information and technical assistance to banks to help them respond to the banking and credit needs of the communities they serve, including low- and moderate-income individuals [1].", "intermediate": ["[Retrieval]", "Answer: The FDIC provides information and technical assistance to banks to help them respond to the banking and credit needs of the communities they serve, including low- and moderate-income individuals."]}, {"qa_pairs": [{"context": "No context provided", "question": "How does the FDIC support financial institutions in serving low and moderate-income communities?", "short_answers": ["FDIC assists in strategy development", "Focus on credit, service, investment needs"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "What role does access to the conventional banking system play for low or moderate-income consumers?", "short_answers": ["Pathway to economic opportunities", "Helps manage needs and build wealth"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "Why is broad participation in the traditional financial system important?", "short_answers": ["Promotes stability and trust", "Banks offer fair and affordable services"], "wikipage": null}], "wikipages": [{"title": "AML Topic 18", "url": "https://www.fdic.gov/espanol/programa-de-asuntos-comunitarios"}], "annotations": [{"knowledge": [], "long_answer": "The FDIC supports financial institutions in serving low and moderate-income communities by helping them develop strategies that address the credit, service, and investment needs of these communities. Access to the conventional banking system provides an important pathway to economic opportunities for low or moderate-income consumers. Broad participation in the traditional financial system is important because it is essential for promoting stability and trust in the system."}, {"knowledge": [{"content": "The FDIC's Community Affairs staff is available to assist financial institutions in developing strategies that address the credit, service, and investment needs of their communities.", "wikipage": "AML Topic 18"}, {"content": "Access to the conventional banking system provides an important pathway to economic opportunities for consumers with low or moderate incomes and for small business owners.", "wikipage": "AML Topic 18"}, {"content": "The broad participation in the traditional financial system is an essential element for promoting stability and confidence in the system. Banks generate trust through their ongoing work to serve their communities by offering fair, safe, and affordable services.", "wikipage": "AML Topic 18"}], "long_answer": "The FDIC supports financial institutions in serving low and moderate-income communities by helping them develop strategies that address the credit, service, and investment needs of these communities. The FDIC's Community Affairs staff is available to assist in these efforts.\n\nAccess to the conventional banking system provides an important pathway to economic opportunities for low or moderate-income consumers. It helps them manage daily needs and build wealth over time to achieve future goals.\n\nBroad participation in the traditional financial system is important because it is essential for promoting stability and trust in the system. Banks contribute to this trust by continuously working to serve their communities with fair, safe, and affordable services."}], "sample_id": "aml-topic-18-3", "question": "How does the FDIC support financial institutions in serving low and moderate-income communities?", "docs": [{"id": "doc-aml-topic-18-3-1", "title": "AML Topic 18 Part 1", "text": "emerging markets and nonprofit training organizations. It supports pilot programs and partnerships to expand financial capacity and economic inclusion. Access to the conventional banking system provides an important pathway to economic opportunities for consumers with low or moderate incomes and for small business owners. Over time, establishing a successful relationship with a financial institution can help manage daily needs and", "score": 0.9, "summary": "Emerging networks and nonprofit training organizations. It supports pilot programs and partnerships to expand financial capacity and inc...", "extraction": "The FDIC's Community Affairs staff is available to assist financial institutions in developing strategies that address the credit, service, and investment needs of their communities."}], "answer": "The FDIC supports financial institutions in serving low and moderate-income communities by helping them develop strategies that address the credit, service, and investment needs of these communities. Access to the conventional banking system provides an important pathway to economic opportunities for low or moderate-income consumers. Broad participation in the traditional financial system is important because it is essential for promoting stability and trust in the system.", "output": "Answer: The FDIC supports financial institutions in serving low and moderate-income communities through its Community Reinvestment Act (CRA) program [1].", "intermediate": ["[Retrieval]", "Answer: The FDIC supports financial institutions in serving low and moderate-income communities through its Community Reinvestment Act (CRA) program."]}, {"qa_pairs": [{"context": "No context provided", "question": "What is the primary mission of the Federal Deposit Insurance Corporation (FDIC)?", "short_answers": ["Maintain stability", "Public confidence in financial system"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "How can I ensure that I am visiting the official FDIC website?", "short_answers": [".gov domain", "https:// for security"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "What recent press release topics were covered by the FDIC in early 2025?", "short_answers": ["Return on assets", "Withdrawal from Network of Central Banks"], "wikipage": null}], "wikipages": [{"title": "AML Topic 19", "url": "https://www.fdic.gov/contact"}], "annotations": [{"knowledge": [], "long_answer": "The primary mission of the FDIC is to maintain stability and public confidence in the nation\u2019s financial system. To ensure you are visiting the official FDIC website, look for the '. Recent press releases by the FDIC in early 2025 covered topics such as the reported return on assets of FDIC-insured institutions and the FDIC's withdrawal from the Network of Central Banks and Supervisors for Green."}, {"knowledge": [{"content": "The Federal Deposit Insurance Corporation (FDIC) is an independent agency created by the Congress to maintain stability and public confidence in the nation\u2019s financial system.", "wikipage": "AML Topic 19"}, {"content": "The .gov means it\u2019s official... The https:// ensures that you are connecting to the official website and that any information you provide is encrypted and transmitted securely.", "wikipage": "AML Topic 19"}, {"content": "PRESS RELEASE / February 25, 2025 FDIC-Insured Institutions Reported Return on Assets of 1.11 Percent and Ne\u2026 PRESS RELEASE / January 21, 2025 FDIC Withdraws from the Network of Central Banks and Supervisors for Green\u2026", "wikipage": "AML Topic 19"}], "long_answer": "The primary mission of the FDIC is to maintain stability and public confidence in the nation\u2019s financial system.\n\nTo ensure you are visiting the official FDIC website, look for the '.gov' domain and 'https://' in the URL, which indicates that the site is secure and any information you provide is encrypted.\n\nRecent press releases by the FDIC in early 2025 covered topics such as the reported return on assets of FDIC-insured institutions and the FDIC's withdrawal from the Network of Central Banks and Supervisors for Green."}], "sample_id": "aml-topic-19-1", "question": "What is the primary mission of the Federal Deposit Insurance Corporation (FDIC)?", "docs": [{"id": "doc-aml-topic-19-1-2", "title": "AML Topic 19 Part 2", "text": "out\nThe Federal Deposit Insurance Corporation (FDIC) is an independent agency created by the Congress to maintain stability and public confidence in the nation\u2019s financial system. Learn about the FDIC\u2019s mission, leadership, history, career opportunities, and more.\nLearn More About the FDIC\nWhat We Do\nLeadership\nCareers\nInitiatives\nStrategic Plans\nFinancial Reports\nHistory\nGovernance\nOmbudsman\nDoing Business with the FDIC\nPRESS RELEASE / February 25, 2025\nFDIC-Insured Institutions Reported Return", "score": 0.85, "summary": "out The Federal Deposit Insurance Corporation (FDIC) is an independent agency created by the Congress to maintain stability and public confidence in t...", "extraction": "out\nThe Federal Deposit Insurance Corporation (FDIC) is an independent agency created by the Congress to maintain stability and public confidence in the nation\u2019s financial system. Learn about the FDIC"}], "answer": "The primary mission of the FDIC is to maintain stability and public confidence in the nation\u2019s financial system. To ensure you are visiting the official FDIC website, look for the '. Recent press releases by the FDIC in early 2025 covered topics such as the reported return on assets of FDIC-insured institutions and the FDIC's withdrawal from the Network of Central Banks and Supervisors for Green.", "output": "Answer: The FDIC's mission is to maintain stability and public confidence in the U. S. financial system [1].", "intermediate": ["[Retrieval]", "Answer: The FDIC's mission is to maintain stability and public confidence in the U.S. financial system."]}, {"qa_pairs": [{"context": "No context provided", "question": "What is the return on assets reported by FDIC-insured institutions as of February 25, 2025?", "short_answers": ["1.11 percent", "Return on assets"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "How much net income did FDIC-insured institutions report on December 12, 2024?", "short_answers": ["$65.4 billion", "Net income"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "What was the net income reported by FDIC-insured institutions on September 5, 2024?", "short_answers": ["$71.5 billion", "Net income"], "wikipage": null}], "wikipages": [{"title": "AML Topic 19", "url": "https://www.fdic.gov/contact"}], "annotations": [{"knowledge": [], "long_answer": "FDIC-insured institutions reported a return on assets of 1. On December 12, 2024, FDIC-insured institutions reported a net income of $65. FDIC-insured institutions reported a net income of $71."}, {"knowledge": [{"content": "EVENT / February 25, 2025 FDIC-Insured Institutions Reported Return on Assets of 1.11 Percent and Net Inc\u2026", "wikipage": "AML Topic 19"}, {"content": "EVENT / December 12, 2024 FDIC-Insured Institutions Reported Net Income of $65.4 Billion", "wikipage": "AML Topic 19"}, {"content": "EVENT / September 05, 2024 FDIC-Insured Institutions Reported Net Income of $71.5 Billion", "wikipage": "AML Topic 19"}], "long_answer": "FDIC-insured institutions reported a return on assets of 1.11 percent as of February 25, 2025.\n\nOn December 12, 2024, FDIC-insured institutions reported a net income of $65.4 billion.\n\nFDIC-insured institutions reported a net income of $71.5 billion on September 5, 2024."}], "sample_id": "aml-topic-19-3", "question": "What is the return on assets reported by FDIC-insured institutions as of February 25, 2025?", "docs": [{"id": "doc-aml-topic-19-3-1", "title": "AML Topic 19 Part 1", "text": "anking performance data. Browse our extensive research tools and reports.\nMore FDIC Analysis\nCenter for Financial Research\nConsumer Research\nFDIC National Survey of Unbanked and Underbanked Households\nQuarterly Banking Profile\nFDIC Academic Challenge\nFDIC Banking Issues in Focus\nAnnual Risk Review\nEVENT / February 25, 2025\nFDIC-Insured Institutions Reported Return on Assets of 1.11 Percent and Net Inc\u2026\nEVENT / December 12, 2024\nFDIC-Insured Institutions Reported Net Income of $65.4 Billion\nEVENT", "score": 0.9, "summary": "anking performance data. Browse our extensive research tools and reports. More FDIC Analysis Center for Financial Research Consumer Research FDIC Nati...", "extraction": "EVENT / February 25, 2025 FDIC-Insured Institutions Reported Return on Assets of 1.11 Percent and Net Inc\u2026"}], "answer": "FDIC-insured institutions reported a return on assets of 1. On December 12, 2024, FDIC-insured institutions reported a net income of $65. FDIC-insured institutions reported a net income of $71.", "output": "As of February 25, 2025, FDIC-insured institutions reported a return on assets of 1. 11 percent and net income of $65. 4 billion [1].", "intermediate": ["[Retrieval]", "As of February 25, 2025, FDIC-insured institutions reported a return on assets of 1.11 percent and net income of $65.4 billion."]}, {"qa_pairs": [{"context": "No context provided", "question": "What types of resources does the FDIC provide for stakeholders such as bankers and analysts?", "short_answers": ["Financial education materials", "Data tools", "Documentation of laws and regulations"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "Where can bankers find specific resources provided by the FDIC?", "short_answers": ["Banker Resource Center"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "What research programs does the FDIC offer related to community banking?", "short_answers": ["Community Banking Research Program"], "wikipage": null}], "wikipages": [{"title": "AML Topic 20", "url": "https://www.fdic.gov/policies"}], "annotations": [{"knowledge": [], "long_answer": "The FDIC provides a variety of resources for stakeholders including financial education materials, data tools, documentation of laws and regulations, and information on important initiatives. Bankers can find specific resources in the Banker Resource Center provided by the FDIC. The FDIC offers the Community Banking Research Program as part of its resources."}, {"knowledge": [{"content": "The FDIC provides a wealth of resources for consumers, bankers, analysts, and other stakeholders. Browse our collection of financial education materials, data tools, documentation of laws and regulations, information on important initiatives, and more.", "wikipage": "AML Topic 20"}, {"content": "Additional FDIC Resources Consumer Resource Center Banker Resource Center Deposit Insurance Supervision & Examinations Laws & Regulations Resolutions Publications Forms Data Tools Community Banking Research Program International Seminars and Training", "wikipage": "AML Topic 20"}, {"content": "Additional FDIC Resources Consumer Resource Center Banker Resource Center Deposit Insurance Supervision & Examinations Laws & Regulations Resolutions Publications Forms Data Tools Community Banking Research Program International Seminars and Training", "wikipage": "AML Topic 20"}], "long_answer": "The FDIC provides a variety of resources for stakeholders including financial education materials, data tools, documentation of laws and regulations, and information on important initiatives.\n\nBankers can find specific resources in the Banker Resource Center provided by the FDIC.\n\nThe FDIC offers the Community Banking Research Program as par