s well as other resources like financial education materials, data tools, and information on important initiatives. Consumers can access information about deposit insurance through the FDIC's Consumer Resource Center, which is part of the FDIC's collection of resources. The FDIC offers financial education materials among its resources for stakeholders, which include consumers, bankers, analysts, and others."}, {"knowledge": [{"content": "The FDIC provides a wealth of resources for consumers, bankers, analysts, and other stakeholders. Browse our collection of financial education materials, data tools, documentation of laws and regulations, information on important initiatives, and more.", "wikipage": "AML Topic 144"}, {"content": "Additional FDIC Resources Consumer Resource Center Deposit Insurance Supervision & Examinations Laws & Regulations Resolutions Publications Forms Data Tools Community Banking Research Program International Seminars and Training", "wikipage": "AML Topic 144"}, {"content": "The FDIC provides a wealth of resources for consumers, bankers, analysts, and other stakeholders. Browse our collection of financial education materials, data tools, documentation of laws and regulations, information on important initiatives, and more.", "wikipage": "AML Topic 144"}], "long_answer": "The FDIC provides a Banker Resource Center, which includes documentation of laws and regulations, as well as other resources like financial education materials, data tools, and information on important initiatives.\n\nConsumers can access information about deposit insurance through the FDIC's Consumer Resource Center, which is part of the FDIC's collection of resources.\n\nThe FDIC offers financial education materials among its resources for stakeholders, which include consumers, bankers, analysts, and others."}], "sample_id": "aml-topic-144-2", "question": "What resources does the FDIC offer for bankers seeking guidance on regulations?", "docs": [{"id": "doc-aml-topic-144-2-1", "title": "AML Topic 144 Part 1", "text": "17, 2025\nMillennium Bank, Des Plaines, Ill. Assumes All Deposits of Pulaski Savings\u2026\nPODCASTS / January 06, 2025\nSmall Business Lending\nResources\nToggle submenu\nResources\nThe FDIC provides a wealth of resources for consumers, bankers, analysts, and other stakeholders. Browse our collection of financial education materials, data tools, documentation of laws and regulations, information on important initiatives, and more.\nAdditional FDIC Resources\nConsumer Resource Center\nBanker Resource Center\nDe", "score": 0.9, "summary": "17, 2025 Millennium Bank, Des Plaines, Ill. Assumes All Deposits of Pulaski Savings\u2026 PODCASTS / January 06, 2025 Small Business Lending Resources Togg...", "extraction": "The FDIC provides a wealth of resources for consumers, bankers, analysts, and other stakeholders. Browse our collection of financial education materials, data tools, documentation of laws and regulations, information on important initiatives, and more."}], "answer": "The FDIC provides a Banker Resource Center, which includes documentation of laws and regulations, as well as other resources like financial education materials, data tools, and information on important initiatives. Consumers can access information about deposit insurance through the FDIC's Consumer Resource Center, which is part of the FDIC's collection of resources. The FDIC offers financial education materials among its resources for stakeholders, which include consumers, bankers, analysts, and others.", "output": "Answer: The FDIC provides a wealth of resources for consumers, bankers, analysts, and other stakeholders [1].", "intermediate": ["[Retrieval]", "Answer: The FDIC provides a wealth of resources for consumers, bankers, analysts, and other stakeholders."]}, {"qa_pairs": [{"context": "No context provided", "question": "What type of research and reports does the FDIC provide for financial institutions?", "short_answers": ["Center for Financial Research", "Quarterly Banking Profile", "Annual Risk Review"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "How does the FDIC keep stakeholders informed about banking policy changes and issues?", "short_answers": ["Regular updates", "Policy changes", "Conferences and events"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "What financial performance data is reported by FDIC-insured institutions in their events?", "short_answers": ["Return on assets", "Net income figures"], "wikipage": null}], "wikipages": [{"title": "AML Topic 144", "url": "https://www.fdic.gov/news/fact-sheets/cra-final-rule-10-24-23.html"}], "annotations": [{"knowledge": [], "long_answer": "The FDIC provides a variety of research tools and reports, including those from the Center for Financial Research, consumer research, the FDIC National Survey of Unbanked and Underbanked Households, and the Quarterly Banking Profile. The FDIC keeps stakeholders informed through regular updates on news and activities. FDIC-insured institutions report various financial performance data, including return on assets and net income figures."}, {"knowledge": [{"content": "les, working papers, and state banking performance data. Browse our extensive research tools and reports. More FDIC Analysis Center for Financial Research Consumer Research FDIC National Survey of Unbanked and Underbanked Households Quarterly Banking Profile FDIC Academic Challenge FDIC Banking Issues in Focus Annual Risk Review", "wikipage": "AML Topic 144"}, {"content": "The FDIC publishes regular updates on news and activities. Keep up with FDIC announcements, read speeches and testimony on the latest banking issues, learn about policy changes for banks, and get the details on upcoming conferences and events.", "wikipage": "AML Topic 144"}, {"content": "EVENT / February 25, 2025 FDIC-Insured Institutions Reported Return on Assets of 1.11 Percent and Net Inc\u2026 EVENT / December 12, 2024 FDIC-Insured Institutions Reported Net Income of $65.4 Billion EVENT / September 05, 2024 FDIC-Insured Institutions Reported Net Income of $71.5 Billion", "wikipage": "AML Topic 144"}], "long_answer": "The FDIC provides a variety of research tools and reports, including those from the Center for Financial Research, consumer research, the FDIC National Survey of Unbanked and Underbanked Households, and the Quarterly Banking Profile. They also offer analysis through the FDIC Academic Challenge, FDIC Banking Issues in Focus, and the Annual Risk Review.\n\nThe FDIC keeps stakeholders informed through regular updates on news and activities. This includes announcements, speeches, and testimony on the latest banking issues, as well as details on policy changes for banks and upcoming conferences and events.\n\nFDIC-insured institutions report various financial performance data, including return on assets and net income figures. For example, they reported a return on assets of 1.11 percent and net income figures of $65.4 billion and $71.5 billion at different events."}], "sample_id": "aml-topic-144-3", "question": "What type of research and reports does the FDIC provide for financial institutions?", "docs": [{"id": "doc-aml-topic-144-3-1", "title": "AML Topic 144 Part 1", "text": "les, working papers, and state banking performance data. Browse our extensive research tools and reports.\nMore FDIC Analysis\nCenter for Financial Research\nConsumer Research\nFDIC National Survey of Unbanked and Underbanked Households\nQuarterly Banking Profile\nFDIC Academic Challenge\nFDIC Banking Issues in Focus\nAnnual Risk Review\nEVENT / February 25, 2025\nFDIC-Insured Institutions Reported Return on Assets of 1.11 Percent and Net Inc\u2026\nEVENT / December 12, 2024\nFDIC-Insured Institutions Reported N", "score": 0.9, "summary": "les, working papers, and state banking performance data. Browse our extensive research tools and reports. More FDIC Analysis Center for Financial Rese...", "extraction": "les, working papers, and state banking performance data. Browse our extensive research tools and reports. More FDIC Analysis Center for Financial Research Consumer Research FDIC National Survey of Unbanked and Underbanked Households Quarterly Banking Profile FDIC Academic Challenge FDIC Banking Issues in Focus Annual Risk Review"}], "answer": "The FDIC provides a variety of research tools and reports, including those from the Center for Financial Research, consumer research, the FDIC National Survey of Unbanked and Underbanked Households, and the Quarterly Banking Profile. The FDIC keeps stakeholders informed through regular updates on news and activities. FDIC-insured institutions report various financial performance data, including return on assets and net income figures.", "output": "Answer: The FDIC provides research and reports on banking topics such as consumer research, financial inclusion, and state banking performance data [1].", "intermediate": ["[Retrieval]", "Answer: The FDIC provides research and reports on banking topics such as consumer research, financial inclusion, and state banking performance data."]}, {"qa_pairs": [{"context": "No context provided", "question": "What kind of financial performance data does the FDIC provide in its reports?", "short_answers": ["Return on Assets", "Net Income"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "Where can I find the latest FDIC announcements and updates on banking issues?", "short_answers": ["FDIC news updates", "Policy changes"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "What are some of the tools and reports offered by the FDIC for analyzing banking performance?", "short_answers": ["Quarterly Banking Profile", "Annual Risk Review"], "wikipage": null}], "wikipages": [{"title": "AML Topic 145", "url": "https://www.fdic.gov/news/fact-sheets/interagency-overview-cra-final-rule-10-24-23.html"}], "annotations": [{"knowledge": [], "long_answer": "The FDIC provides data such as the Return on Assets and Net Income of FDIC-insured institutions. You can find the latest FDIC announcements and updates on banking issues in their regular news updates. The FDIC offers various tools and reports for analyzing banking performance, including the Quarterly Banking Profile, Annual Risk Review, and other research tools from the Center for Financial Research."}, {"knowledge": [{"content": "EVENT / February 25, 2025 FDIC-Insured Institutions Reported Return on Assets of 1.11 Percent and Net Inc\u2026 EVENT / December 12, 2024 FDIC-Insured Institutions Reported Net Income of $65.4 Billion EVENT / September 05, 2024 FDIC-Insured Institutions Reported Net Income of $71.5 Billion", "wikipage": "AML Topic 145"}, {"content": "The FDIC publishes regular updates on news and activities. Keep up with FDIC announcements, read speeches and testimony on the latest banking issues, learn about policy changes for banks, and get the details on upcoming conferences and events.", "wikipage": "AML Topic 145"}, {"content": "rly banking profiles, working papers, and state banking performance data. Browse our extensive research tools and reports. More FDIC Analysis Center for Financial Research Quarterly Banking Profile Annual Risk Review", "wikipage": "AML Topic 145"}], "long_answer": "The FDIC provides data such as the Return on Assets and Net Income of FDIC-insured institutions. For example, they reported a Return on Assets of 1.11 percent in February 2025 and net incomes of $65.4 billion and $71.5 billion in December 2024 and September 2024, respectively.\n\nYou can find the latest FDIC announcements and updates on banking issues in their regular news updates. This includes speeches, testimony on banking issues, policy changes, and details on upcoming conferences and events.\n\nThe FDIC offers various tools and reports for analyzing banking performance, including the Quarterly Banking Profile, Annual Risk Review, and other research tools from the Center for Financial Research."}], "sample_id": "aml-topic-145-3", "question": "What kind of financial performance data does the FDIC provide in its reports?", "docs": [{"id": "doc-aml-topic-145-3-1", "title": "AML Topic 145 Part 1", "text": "rly banking profiles, working papers, and state banking performance data. Browse our extensive research tools and reports.\nMore FDIC Analysis\nCenter for Financial Research\nConsumer Research\nFDIC National Survey of Unbanked and Underbanked Households\nQuarterly Banking Profile\nFDIC Academic Challenge\nFDIC Banking Issues in Focus\nAnnual Risk Review\nEVENT / February 25, 2025\nFDIC-Insured Institutions Reported Return on Assets of 1.11 Percent and Net Inc\u2026\nEVENT / December 12, 2024\nFDIC-Insured Instit", "score": 0.9, "summary": "rly banking profiles, working papers, and state banking performance data. Browse our extensive research tools and reports. More FDIC Analysis Center f...", "extraction": "EVENT / February 25, 2025 FDIC-Insured Institutions Reported Return on Assets of 1.11 Percent and Net Inc\u2026 EVENT / December 12, 2024 FDIC-Insured Institutions Reported Net Income of $65.4 Billion EVENT / September 05, 2024 FDIC-Insured Institutions Reported Net Income of $71.5 Billion"}], "answer": "The FDIC provides data such as the Return on Assets and Net Income of FDIC-insured institutions. You can find the latest FDIC announcements and updates on banking issues in their regular news updates. The FDIC offers various tools and reports for analyzing banking performance, including the Quarterly Banking Profile, Annual Risk Review, and other research tools from the Center for Financial Research.", "output": "Answer: The FDIC provides financial performance data in its reports, such as return on assets, net income, and total assets [1].", "intermediate": ["[Retrieval]", "Answer: The FDIC provides financial performance data in its reports, such as return on assets, net income, and total assets."]}, {"qa_pairs": [{"context": "No context provided", "question": "What is the effective date for the new Community Reinvestment Act regulations?", "short_answers": ["April 1, 2024", "First day of the first calendar quarter after 60 days"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "Who are the regulatory bodies involved in issuing the final rule on the Community Reinvestment Act?", "short_answers": ["FDIC", "Federal Reserve", "OCC"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "What are the main changes introduced in the final rule for the Community Reinvestment Act?", "short_answers": ["Updates on CRA activity qualification", "New metrics and quantitative standards"], "wikipage": null}], "wikipages": [{"title": "AML Topic 146", "url": "https://www.fdic.gov/news/board-matters/2023/2023-10-24-notice-dis-a-mem.pdf"}], "annotations": [{"knowledge": [], "long_answer": "The effective date for the new Community Reinvestment Act regulations is the first day of the first calendar quarter that begins at least 60 days after publication in the Federal Register, which is expected to be April 1, 2024. The regulatory bodies involved in issuing the final rule on the Community Reinvestment Act are the Federal Deposit Insurance Corporation (FDIC), the Board of Governors of the Federal Reserve System (Federal Reserve), and the Office of the Comptroller of the Currency (OCC). The main changes introduced in the final rule for the Community Reinvestment Act include updates on how CRA activities qualify for consideration, where CRA activities are considered, and how CRA activities are evaluated."}, {"knowledge": [{"content": "Staff recommends that the Federal Deposit Insurance Corporation\u2019s (FDIC) Board of Directors (Board) approve the attached final rule and authorize its publication in the Federal Register with an effective date of the first day of the first calendar quarter that begins at least 60 days after publication in the Federal Register, which staff expects will be April 1, 2024.", "wikipage": "AML Topic 146"}, {"content": "The final rule, to be issued jointly with the Board of Governors of the Federal Reserve System (Federal Reserve) and the Office of the Comptroller of the Currency (OCC), makes comprehensive regulatory amendments to the FDIC\u2019s CRA regulation implementing the Community Reinvestment Act of 1977 (CRA).", "wikipage": "AML Topic 146"}, {"content": "As explained in more detail below, the final rule updates how CRA activities qualify for consideration, where CRA activities are considered, and how CRA activities are evaluated. Among other things, the final rule includes a combination of new metrics and quantitative standards to help.", "wikipage": "AML Topic 146"}], "long_answer": "The effective date for the new Community Reinvestment Act regulations is the first day of the first calendar quarter that begins at least 60 days after publication in the Federal Register, which is expected to be April 1, 2024.\n\nThe regulatory bodies involved in issuing the final rule on the Community Reinvestment Act are the Federal Deposit Insurance Corporation (FDIC), the Board of Governors of the Federal Reserve System (Federal Reserve), and the Office of the Comptroller of the Currency (OCC).\n\nThe main changes introduced in the final rule for the Community Reinvestment Act include updates on how CRA activities qualify for consideration, where CRA activities are considered, and how CRA activities are evaluated. This includes the introduction of new metrics and quantitative standards."}], "sample_id": "aml-topic-146-1", "question": "What is the effective date for the new Community Reinvestment Act regulations?", "docs": [{"id": "doc-aml-topic-146-1-2", "title": "AML Topic 146 Part 2", "text": "at begins at least 60 days after publication in the Federal Register,\nwhich staff expects will be April 1, 2024. The final rule, to be issued jointly with the Board of Governors of the\nFederal Reserve System (Federal Reserve) and the Office of the Comptroller of the Currency (OCC), makes\ncomprehensive regulatory amendments to the FDIC\u2019s CRA regulation implementing the Community\nReinvestment Act of 1977 (CRA).1\nAs explained in more detail below, the final rule updates how CRA activities qualify f", "score": 0.85, "summary": "at begins at least 60 days after publication in the Federal Register, which staff expects will be April 1, 2024. The final rule, to be issued jointly ...", "extraction": "at begins at least 60 days after publication in the Federal Register,\nwhich staff expects will be April 1, 2024. The final rule, to be issued jointly with the Board of Governors of the\nFederal Reserve"}], "answer": "The effective date for the new Community Reinvestment Act regulations is the first day of the first calendar quarter that begins at least 60 days after publication in the Federal Register, which is expected to be April 1, 2024. The regulatory bodies involved in issuing the final rule on the Community Reinvestment Act are the Federal Deposit Insurance Corporation (FDIC), the Board of Governors of the Federal Reserve System (Federal Reserve), and the Office of the Comptroller of the Currency (OCC). The main changes introduced in the final rule for the Community Reinvestment Act include updates on how CRA activities qualify for consideration, where CRA activities are considered, and how CRA activities are evaluated.", "output": "Answer: The effective date for the new Community Reinvestment Act regulations is April 1, 2024 [1].", "intermediate": ["[Retrieval]", "Answer: The effective date for the new Community Reinvestment Act regulations is April 1, 2024."]}, {"qa_pairs": [{"context": "No context provided", "question": "How do examiners evaluate retail lending and community development financing for banks?", "short_answers": ["Separate tests", "Use of metrics and standards"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "What is the role of the qualitative aspect in the evaluation procedures for banks?", "short_answers": ["Central component", "Assessing performance context"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "Where can banks receive CRA credit for community development activities under the final rule?", "short_answers": ["Facility-based assessment areas", "Rural communities and Native Land Areas"], "wikipage": null}], "wikipages": [{"title": "AML Topic 146", "url": "https://www.fdic.gov/news/board-matters/2023/2023-10-24-notice-dis-a-mem.pdf"}], "annotations": [{"knowledge": [], "long_answer": "Examiners evaluate retail lending and community development financing in separate tests. The qualitative aspect of the current approach remains a central component of evaluation procedures in the final rule, particularly for assessing performance context, retail services, and community development services. The final rule expands where banks may receive CRA credit for community development activities, incentivizing banks to conduct such activities not only within the bank\u2019s facility-based assessment areas, but also in other areas, such as rural communities and Native Land Areas."}, {"knowledge": [{"content": "Examiners evaluate retail lending and community development financing in separate tests. The use of metrics and standards is intended to help achieve greater clarity, consistency, and transparency for banks and communities, while also calibrating expectations based on differences in local market conditions.", "wikipage": "AML Topic 146"}, {"content": "The qualitative aspect of the current approach remains a central component of evaluation procedures in the final rule, in particular for assessing performance context, retail services, and community development services.", "wikipage": "AML Topic 146"}, {"content": "The final rule also expands where banks may receive CRA credit for community development activities and is intended to incentivize banks to conduct such activities not only within the bank\u2019s facility-based assessment areas, but also in other areas, such as rural communities and Native Land Areas.", "wikipage": "AML Topic 146"}], "long_answer": "Examiners evaluate retail lending and community development financing in separate tests. The use of metrics and standards is intended to help achieve greater clarity, consistency, and transparency for banks and communities, while also calibrating expectations based on differences in local market conditions.\n\nThe qualitative aspect of the current approach remains a central component of evaluation procedures in the final rule, particularly for assessing performance context, retail services, and community development services.\n\nThe final rule expands where banks may receive CRA credit for community development activities, incentivizing banks to conduct such activities not only within the bank\u2019s facility-based assessment areas, but also in other areas, such as rural communities and Native Land Areas."}], "sample_id": "aml-topic-146-2", "question": "How do examiners evaluate retail lending and community development financing for banks?", "docs": [{"id": "doc-aml-topic-146-2-1", "title": "AML Topic 146 Part 1", "text": "examiners evaluate retail lending and\ncommunity development financing in separate tests. The use of metrics and standards is intended to help\nachieve greater clarity, consistency, and transparency for banks and communities, while also calibrating\nexpectations based on differences in local market conditions. The qualitative aspect of the current approach\nremains a central component of evaluation procedures in the final rule, in particular for assessing performance\ncontext, retail services, and co", "score": 0.9, "summary": "examiners evaluate retail lending and community development financing in separate tests. The use of metrics and standards is intended to help achieve ...", "extraction": "Examiners evaluate retail lending and community development financing in separate tests. The use of metrics and standards is intended to help achieve greater clarity, consistency, and transparency for banks and communities, while also calibrating expectations based on differences in local market conditions."}], "answer": "Examiners evaluate retail lending and community development financing in separate tests. The qualitative aspect of the current approach remains a central component of evaluation procedures in the final rule, particularly for assessing performance context, retail services, and community development services. The final rule expands where banks may receive CRA credit for community development activities, incentivizing banks to conduct such activities not only within the bank\u2019s facility-based assessment areas, but also in other areas, such as rural communities and Native Land Areas.", "output": "Answer: Examiners evaluate retail lending and community development financing in separate tests [1].", "intermediate": ["[Retrieval]", "Answer: Examiners evaluate retail lending and community development financing in separate tests."]}, {"qa_pairs": [{"context": "No context provided", "question": "What agencies are responsible for implementing the Community Reinvestment Act (CRA)?", "short_answers": ["FDIC", "Federal Reserve", "OCC", "OTS"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "When were the last major regulatory revisions to the CRA regulations completed?", "short_answers": ["1995"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "What actions did the Federal Reserve take in 2020 regarding the modernization of CRA regulations?", "short_answers": ["Published ANPR", "Solicited public input"], "wikipage": null}], "wikipages": [{"title": "AML Topic 146", "url": "https://www.fdic.gov/news/board-matters/2023/2023-10-24-notice-dis-a-mem.pdf"}], "annotations": [{"knowledge": [], "long_answer": "The agencies responsible for implementing the Community Reinvestment Act (CRA) are the FDIC, the Federal Reserve, the OCC, and the Office of Thrift Supervision (OTS). The last major regulatory revisions to the CRA regulations were completed in 1995. On October 19, 2020, the Federal Reserve published an Advanced Notice of Proposed Rulemaking (ANPR) to solicit public input regarding modernizing the Federal Reserve\u2019s CRA regulatory framework."}, {"knowledge": [{"content": "The agencies charged with implementing the statute\u2014the FDIC, the Federal Reserve, the OCC, and the Office of Thrift Supervision (OTS) (collectively, the Federal banking agencies)\u2014first issued joint regulations in 1978 to implement the statute.", "wikipage": "AML Topic 146"}, {"content": "The last major regulatory revisions were completed in 1995.", "wikipage": "AML Topic 146"}, {"content": "Moreover, on October 19, 2020, the Federal Reserve published an Advanced Notice of Proposed Rulemaking (ANPR) to solicit public input regarding modernizing the Federal Reserve\u2019s CRA regulatory an", "wikipage": "AML Topic 146"}], "long_answer": "The agencies responsible for implementing the Community Reinvestment Act (CRA) are the FDIC, the Federal Reserve, the OCC, and the Office of Thrift Supervision (OTS).\n\nThe last major regulatory revisions to the CRA regulations were completed in 1995.\n\nOn October 19, 2020, the Federal Reserve published an Advanced Notice of Proposed Rulemaking (ANPR) to solicit public input regarding modernizing the Federal Reserve\u2019s CRA regulatory framework."}], "sample_id": "aml-topic-146-3", "question": "What agencies are responsible for implementing the Community Reinvestment Act (CRA)?", "docs": [{"id": "doc-aml-topic-146-3-1", "title": "AML Topic 146 Part 1", "text": "it needs of their entire community,\nincluding low- and moderate-income neighborhoods.3\nThe agencies charged with implementing the statute\u2014the FDIC, the Federal Reserve, the OCC, and the\nOffice of Thrift Supervision (OTS) (collectively, the Federal banking agencies)\u2014first issued joint regulations in\n1978 to implement the statute.4 Since then, the Federal banking agencies (including the OTS until 2011) have\nrevised the CRA regulations several times and have provided guidance on the interpretation", "score": 0.9, "summary": "it needs of their entire community, including low- and moderate-income neighborhoods.3 The agencies charged with implementing the statute\u2014the FDIC, th...", "extraction": "The agencies charged with implementing the statute\u2014the FDIC, the Federal Reserve, the OCC, and the Office of Thrift Supervision (OTS) (collectively, the Federal banking agencies)\u2014first issued joint regulations in 1978 to implement the statute."}], "answer": "The agencies responsible for implementing the Community Reinvestment Act (CRA) are the FDIC, the Federal Reserve, the OCC, and the Office of Thrift Supervision (OTS). The last major regulatory revisions to the CRA regulations were completed in 1995. On October 19, 2020, the Federal Reserve published an Advanced Notice of Proposed Rulemaking (ANPR) to solicit public input regarding modernizing the Federal Reserve\u2019s CRA regulatory framework.", "output": "Answer: The agencies responsible for implementing the Community Reinvestment Act (CRA) are the Federal Deposit Insurance Corporation (FDIC), the Federal Reserve, the Office of the Comptroller of the Currency (OCC), and the Office of Thrift Supervision (OTS) [1].", "intermediate": ["[Retrieval]", "Answer: The agencies responsible for implementing the Community Reinvestment Act (CRA) are the Federal Deposit Insurance Corporation (FDIC), the Federal Reserve, the Office of the Comptroller of the Currency (OCC), and the Office of Thrift Supervision (OTS)."]}, {"qa_pairs": [{"context": "No context provided", "question": "What resources does the FDIC offer for bankers to enhance their understanding of financial regulations?", "short_answers": ["Banker Resource Center", "Financial education materials", "Data tools", "Laws and regulations"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "How can the FDIC's Community Banking Research Program benefit banking institutions?", "short_answers": ["Community Banking Research Program", "Insights on trends and challenges", "Aid in decision-making"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "What type of educational materials does the FDIC provide through its International Seminars and Training?", "short_answers": ["International seminars", "Training on banking regulations", "Supervision and examination practices"], "wikipage": null}], "wikipages": [{"title": "AML Topic 148", "url": "https://www.fdic.gov/news/speeches/2023/spoct2423c.html"}], "annotations": [{"knowledge": [], "long_answer": "The FDIC offers a Banker Resource Center, which includes a collection of financial education materials, data tools, documentation of laws and regulations, and information on important initiatives. The FDIC's Community Banking Research Program offers resources and insights that can help banking institutions understand trends and challenges in community banking, aiding in better decision-making and strategic planning. The FDIC provides international seminars and training that likely include educational materials on banking regulations, supervision, and examination practices to enhance global banking standards."}, {"knowledge": [{"content": "The FDIC provides a wealth of resources for consumers, bankers, analysts, and other stakeholders. Browse our collection of financial education materials, data tools, documentation of laws and regulations, information on important initiatives, and more.", "wikipage": "AML Topic 148"}, {"content": "Community Banking Research Program", "wikipage": "AML Topic 148"}, {"content": "International Seminars and Training", "wikipage": "AML Topic 148"}], "long_answer": "The FDIC offers a Banker Resource Center, which includes a collection of financial education materials, data tools, documentation of laws and regulations, and information on important initiatives.\n\nThe FDIC's Community Banking Research Program offers resources and insights that can help banking institutions understand trends and challenges in community banking, aiding in better decision-making and strategic planning.\n\nThe FDIC provides international seminars and training that likely include educational materials on banking regulations, supervision, and examination practices to enhance global banking standards."}], "sample_id": "aml-topic-148-2", "question": "What resources does the FDIC offer for bankers to enhance their understanding of financial regulations?", "docs": [{"id": "doc-aml-topic-148-2-1", "title": "AML Topic 148 Part 1", "text": "ary 17, 2025\nMillennium Bank, Des Plaines, Ill. Assumes All Deposits of Pulaski Savings\u2026\nPODCASTS / January 06, 2025\nSmall Business Lending\nResources\nToggle submenu\nResources\nThe FDIC provides a wealth of resources for consumers, bankers, analysts, and other stakeholders. Browse our collection of financial education materials, data tools, documentation of laws and regulations, information on important initiatives, and more.\nAdditional FDIC Resources\nConsumer Resource Center\nBanker Resource Cente", "score": 0.9, "summary": "ary 17, 2025 Millennium Bank, Des Plaines, Ill. Assumes All Deposits of Pulaski Savings\u2026 PODCASTS / January 06, 2025 Small Business Lending Resources ...", "extraction": "The FDIC provides a wealth of resources for consumers, bankers, analysts, and other stakeholders. Browse our collection of financial education materials, data tools, documentation of laws and regulations, information on important initiatives, and more."}], "answer": "The FDIC offers a Banker Resource Center, which includes a collection of financial education materials, data tools, documentation of laws and regulations, and information on important initiatives. The FDIC's Community Banking Research Program offers resources and insights that can help banking institutions understand trends and challenges in community banking, aiding in better decision-making and strategic planning. The FDIC provides international seminars and training that likely include educational materials on banking regulations, supervision, and examination practices to enhance global banking standards.", "output": "Answer: The FDIC provides a wealth of resources for consumers, bankers, analysts, and other stakeholders [1].", "intermediate": ["[Retrieval]", "Answer: The FDIC provides a wealth of resources for consumers, bankers, analysts, and other stakeholders."]}, {"qa_pairs": [{"context": "No context provided", "question": "What kind of research does the FDIC provide that might help us understand banking performance?", "short_answers": ["quarterly banking profiles", "working papers", "state banking performance data"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "How can we stay updated on the latest FDIC announcements and policy changes?", "short_answers": ["FDIC announcements", "policy changes", "conferences and events"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "What are some of the events where FDIC reports financial statistics of insured institutions?", "short_answers": ["February 25, 2025", "December 12, 2024", "September 05, 2024"], "wikipage": null}], "wikipages": [{"title": "AML Topic 150", "url": "https://www.fdic.gov/news/press-releases/2023/pr23086.html"}], "annotations": [{"knowledge": [], "long_answer": "The FDIC provides research including quarterly banking profiles, working papers, and state banking performance data. The FDIC publishes regular updates on news and activities. The FDIC reports financial statistics of insured institutions during events such as those on February 25, 2025, December 12, 2024, and September 05, 2024, where they reported on return on assets and net income."}, {"knowledge": [{"content": "research, including quarterly banking profiles, working papers, and state banking performance data. Browse our extensive research tools and reports.", "wikipage": "AML Topic 150"}, {"content": "The FDIC publishes regular updates on news and activities. Keep up with FDIC announcements, read speeches and testimony on the latest banking issues, learn about policy changes for banks, and get the details on upcoming conferences and events.", "wikipage": "AML Topic 150"}, {"content": "EVENT / February 25, 2025 FDIC-Insured Institutions Reported Return on Assets of 1.11 Percent and Net Inc\u2026 EVENT / December 12, 2024 FDIC-Insured Institutions Reported Net Income of $65.4 Billion EVENT / September 05, 2024 FDIC-Insured Institutions Reported Net Income of $71.5 Billion", "wikipage": "AML Topic 150"}], "long_answer": "The FDIC provides research including quarterly banking profiles, working papers, and state banking performance data. They also offer extensive research tools and reports.\n\nThe FDIC publishes regular updates on news and activities. You can keep up with FDIC announcements, read speeches and testimony on the latest banking issues, learn about policy changes for banks, and get details on upcoming conferences and events.\n\nThe FDIC reports financial statistics of insured institutions during events such as those on February 25, 2025, December 12, 2024, and September 05, 2024, where they reported on return on assets and net income."}], "sample_id": "aml-topic-150-3", "question": "What kind of research does the FDIC provide that might help us understand banking performance?", "docs": [{"id": "doc-aml-topic-150-3-1", "title": "AML Topic 150 Part 1", "text": "research, including quarterly banking profiles, working papers, and state banking performance data. Browse our extensive research tools and reports.\nMore FDIC Analysis\nCenter for Financial Research\nConsumer Research\nFDIC National Survey of Unbanked and Underbanked Households\nQuarterly Banking Profile\nFDIC Academic Challenge\nFDIC Banking Issues in Focus\nAnnual Risk Review\nEVENT / February 25, 2025\nFDIC-Insured Institutions Reported Return on Assets of 1.11 Percent and Net Inc\u2026\nEVENT / December 12", "score": 0.9, "summary": "research, including quarterly banking profiles, working papers, and state banking performance data. Browse our extensive research tools and reports. M...", "extraction": "research, including quarterly banking profiles, working papers, and state banking performance data. Browse our extensive research tools and reports."}], "answer": "The FDIC provides research including quarterly banking profiles, working papers, and state banking performance data. The FDIC publishes regular updates on news and activities. The FDIC reports financial statistics of insured institutions during events such as those on February 25, 2025, December 12, 2024, and September 05, 2024, where they reported on return on assets and net income.", "output": "Answer: The FDIC provides research that helps us understand banking performance, including quarterly banking profiles, working papers, and state banking performance data [1].", "intermediate": ["[Retrieval]", "Answer: The FDIC provides research that helps us understand banking performance, including quarterly banking profiles, working papers, and state banking performance data."]}, {"qa_pairs": [{"context": "No context provided", "question": "What resources does the FDIC offer for bankers to enhance their compliance knowledge?", "short_answers": ["Banker Resource Center", "financial education materials", "documentation of laws and regulations"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "How can stakeholders access FDIC's financial education materials and data tools?", "short_answers": ["financial education materials", "data tools", "resources for stakeholders"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "What kind of research programs does the FDIC offer to support community banking?", "short_answers": ["Community Banking Research Program", "FDIC support for community banking"], "wikipage": null}], "wikipages": [{"title": "AML Topic 151", "url": "https://www.fdic.gov/resources/bankers/community-reinvestment-act/cra-regional-contacts-list.html"}], "annotations": [{"knowledge": [], "long_answer": "The FDIC provides a Banker Resource Center as part of its resources, which includes financial education materials, documentation of laws and regulations, and information on important initiatives. Stakeholders can access the FDIC's financial education materials and data tools through their collection of resources provided for consumers, bankers, analysts, and other stakeholders. The FDIC offers a Community Banking Research Program as part of its resources to support community banking."}, {"knowledge": [{"content": "The FDIC provides a wealth of resources for consumers, bankers, analysts, and other stakeholders. Browse our collection of financial education materials, data tools, documentation of laws and regulations, information on important initiatives, and more. Additional FDIC Resources Banker Resource Center", "wikipage": "AML Topic 151"}, {"content": "The FDIC provides a wealth of resources for consumers, bankers, analysts, and other stakeholders. Browse our collection of financial education materials, data tools, documentation of laws and regulations, information on important initiatives, and more.", "wikipage": "AML Topic 151"}, {"content": "Additional FDIC Resources Community Banking Research Program", "wikipage": "AML Topic 151"}], "long_answer": "The FDIC provides a Banker Resource Center as part of its resources, which includes financial education materials, documentation of laws and regulations, and information on important initiatives.\n\nStakeholders can access the FDIC's financial education materials and data tools through their collection of resources provided for consumers, bankers, analysts, and other stakeholders.\n\nThe FDIC offers a Community Banking Research Program as part of its resources to support community banking."}], "sample_id": "aml-topic-151-2", "question": "What resources does the FDIC offer for bankers to enhance their compliance knowledge?", "docs": [{"id": "doc-aml-topic-151-2-1", "title": "AML Topic 151 Part 1", "text": "nk, Des Plaines, Ill. Assumes All Deposits of Pulaski Savings\u2026\nPODCASTS / January 06, 2025\nSmall Business Lending\nResources\nToggle submenu\nResources\nThe FDIC provides a wealth of resources for consumers, bankers, analysts, and other stakeholders. Browse our collection of financial education materials, data tools, documentation of laws and regulations, information on important initiatives, and more.\nAdditional FDIC Resources\nConsumer Resource Center\nBanker Resource Center\nDeposit Insurance\nSuperv", "score": 0.9, "summary": "nk, Des Plaines, Ill. Assumes All Deposits of Pulaski Savings\u2026 PODCASTS / January 06, 2025 Small Business Lending Resources Toggle submenu Resources T...", "extraction": "The FDIC provides a wealth of resources for consumers, bankers, analysts, and other stakeholders. Browse our collection of financial education materials, data tools, documentation of laws and regulations, information on important initiatives, and more. Additional FDIC Resources Banker Resource Center"}], "answer": "The FDIC provides a Banker Resource Center as part of its resources, which includes financial education materials, documentation of laws and regulations, and information on important initiatives. Stakeholders can access the FDIC's financial education materials and data tools through their collection of resources provided for consumers, bankers, analysts, and other stakeholders. The FDIC offers a Community Banking Research Program as part of its resources to support community banking.", "output": "Answer: The FDIC provides a wealth of resources for consumers, bankers, analysts, and other stakeholders [1].", "intermediate": ["[Retrieval]", "Answer: The FDIC provides a wealth of resources for consumers, bankers, analysts, and other stakeholders."]}, {"qa_pairs": [{"context": "No context provided", "question": "What resources does the FDIC provide for bankers to stay informed about regulations?", "short_answers": ["Banker Resource Center", "Laws and Regulations", "Supervision & Examinations"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "How can consumers access FDIC resources to learn more about deposit insurance?", "short_answers": ["Consumer Resource Center", "Deposit Insurance"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "What types of research programs does the FDIC offer to support community banking?", "short_answers": ["Community Banking Research Program"], "wikipage": null}], "wikipages": [{"title": "AML Topic 152", "url": "https://www.fdic.gov/resources/bankers/community-reinvestment-act/examination-schedule/"}], "annotations": [{"knowledge": [], "long_answer": "The FDIC offers a variety of resources for bankers, including a Banker Resource Center, documentation of laws and regulations, and supervision and examinations materials. Consumers can access FDIC resources through the Consumer Resource Center, which includes information about deposit insurance. The FDIC offers a Community Banking Research Program as part of its resources to support community banking."}, {"knowledge": [{"content": "The FDIC provides a wealth of resources for consumers, bankers, analysts, and other stakeholders. Browse our collection of financial education materials, data tools, documentation of laws and regulations, information on important initiatives, and more. Additional FDIC Resources Banker Resource Center Supervision & Examinations Laws & Regulations", "wikipage": "AML Topic 152"}, {"content": "The FDIC provides a wealth of resources for consumers, bankers, analysts, and other stakeholders. Additional FDIC Resources Consumer Resource Center Deposit Insurance", "wikipage": "AML Topic 152"}, {"content": "The FDIC provides a wealth of resources for consumers, bankers, analysts, and other stakeholders. Additional FDIC Resources Community Banking Research Program", "wikipage": "AML Topic 152"}], "long_answer": "The FDIC offers a variety of resources for bankers, including a Banker Resource Center, documentation of laws and regulations, and supervision and examinations materials.\n\nConsumers can access FDIC resources through the Consumer Resource Center, which includes information about deposit insurance.\n\nThe FDIC offers a Community Banking Research Program as part of its resources to support community banking."}], "sample_id": "aml-topic-152-2", "question": "What resources does the FDIC provide for bankers to stay informed about regulations?", "docs": [{"id": "doc-aml-topic-152-2-1", "title": "AML Topic 152 Part 1", "text": ", Des Plaines, Ill. Assumes All Deposits of Pulaski Savings\u2026\nPODCASTS / January 06, 2025\nSmall Business Lending\nResources\nToggle submenu\nResources\nThe FDIC provides a wealth of resources for consumers, bankers, analysts, and other stakeholders. Browse our collection of financial education materials, data tools, documentation of laws and regulations, information on important initiatives, and more.\nAdditional FDIC Resources\nConsumer Resource Center\nBanker Resource Center\nDeposit Insurance\nSupervis", "score": 0.9, "summary": ", Des Plaines, Ill. Assumes All Deposits of Pulaski Savings\u2026 PODCASTS / January 06, 2025 Small Business Lending Resources Toggle submenu Resources The...", "extraction": "The FDIC provides a wealth of resources for consumers, bankers, analysts, and other stakeholders. Browse our collection of financial education materials, data tools, documentation of laws and regulations, information on important initiatives, and more. Additional FDIC Resources Banker Resource Center Supervision & Examinations Laws & Regulations"}], "answer": "The FDIC offers a variety of resources for bankers, including a Banker Resource Center, documentation of laws and regulations, and supervision and examinations materials. Consumers can access FDIC resources through the Consumer Resource Center, which includes information about deposit insurance. The FDIC offers a Community Banking Research Program as part of its resources to support community banking.", "output": "Answer: The FDIC provides a wealth of resources for consumers, bankers, analysts, and other stakeholders [1].", "intermediate": ["[Retrieval]", "Answer: The FDIC provides a wealth of resources for consumers, bankers, analysts, and other stakeholders."]}, {"qa_pairs": [{"context": "No context provided", "question": "What are some of the resources provided by the FDIC for financial research?", "short_answers": ["Center for Financial Research", "Quarterly Banking Profile"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "How can one stay updated with the latest news and policy changes from the FDIC?", "short_answers": ["FDIC announcements", "Policy changes for banks"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "What types of events does the FDIC organize to engage with financial institutions and the public?", "short_answers": ["Conferences & Events", "Media Campaigns"], "wikipage": null}], "wikipages": [{"title": "AML Topic 153", "url": "https://www.fdic.gov/resources/bankers/community-reinvestment-act/monthly-list-of-examined-banks/"}], "annotations": [{"knowledge": [], "long_answer": "The FDIC provides various resources for financial research, including the Center for Financial Research, Consumer Research, the FDIC National Survey of Unbanked and Underbanked Households, and the Quarterly Banking Profile. To stay updated with the latest news and policy changes from the FDIC, individuals can follow their regular updates on news and activities. The FDIC organizes a variety of events to engage with financial institutions and the public, including conferences, events, and board matters."}, {"knowledge": [{"content": "iles, working papers, and state banking performance data. Browse our extensive research tools and reports. More FDIC Analysis Center for Financial Research Consumer Research FDIC National Survey of Unbanked and Underbanked Households Quarterly Banking Profile FDIC Academic Challenge FDIC Banking Issues in Focus Annual Risk Review", "wikipage": "AML Topic 153"}, {"content": "The FDIC publishes regular updates on news and activities. Keep up with FDIC announcements, read speeches and testimony on the latest banking issues, learn about policy changes for banks, and get the details on upcoming conferences and events.", "wikipage": "AML Topic 153"}, {"content": "Find More FDIC News Press Releases Financial Institution Letters Conferences & Events Board Matters Natural Disasters Media Campaigns Speeches, Statements & Testimonies Podcasts Videos Opinion Editorials", "wikipage": "AML Topic 153"}], "long_answer": "The FDIC provides various resources for financial research, including the Center for Financial Research, Consumer Research, the FDIC National Survey of Unbanked and Underbanked Households, and the Quarterly Banking Profile. They also conduct the FDIC Academic Challenge and publish the Annual Risk Review.\n\nTo stay updated with the latest news and policy changes from the FDIC, individuals can follow their regular updates on news and activities. This includes reading FDIC announcements, speeches, testimony on banking issues, and details on policy changes for banks. They also provide information on upcoming conferences and events.\n\nThe FDIC organizes a variety of events to engage with financial institutions and the public, including conferences, events, and board matters. They also provide information through media campaigns, speeches, statements, testimonies, podcasts, videos, and opinion editorials."}], "sample_id": "aml-topic-153-3", "question": "What are some of the resources provided by the FDIC for financial research?", "docs": [{"id": "doc-aml-topic-153-3-2", "title": "AML Topic 153 Part 2", "text": "Net Income of $65.4 Billion\nEVENT / September 05, 2024\nFDIC-Insured Institutions Reported Net Income of $71.5 Billion\nEVENT / May 29, 2024\nQuarterly Banking Profile for First Quarter 2024\nNews\nToggle submenu\nNews\nThe FDIC publishes regular updates on news and activities. Keep up with FDIC announcements, read speeches and testimony on the latest banking issues, learn about policy changes for banks, and get the details on upcoming conferences and events.\nFind More FDIC News\nPress Releases\nFinancia", "score": 0.85, "summary": "Net Income of $65.4 Billion EVENT / September 05, 2024 FDIC-Insured Institutions Reported Net Income of $71.5 Billion EVENT / May 29, 2024 Quarterly B...", "extraction": "Net Income of $65.4 Billion\nEVENT / September 05, 2024\nFDIC-Insured Institutions Reported Net Income of $71.5 Billion\nEVENT / May 29, 2024\nQuarterly Banking Profile for First Quarter 2024\nNews\nToggle "}], "answer": "The FDIC provides various resources for financial research, including the Center for Financial Research, Consumer Research, the FDIC National Survey of Unbanked and Underbanked Households, and the Quarterly Banking Profile. To stay updated with the latest news and policy changes from the FDIC, individuals can follow their regular updates on news and activities. The FDIC organizes a variety of events to engage with financial institutions and the public, including conferences, events, and board matters.", "output": "Answer: The FDIC provides resources for financial research including news, speeches, testimony, policy changes, conferences, and events [1].", "intermediate": ["[Retrieval]", "Answer: The FDIC provides resources for financial research including news, speeches, testimony, policy changes, conferences, and events."]}, {"qa_pairs": [{"context": "No context provided", "question": "What is the role of the Federal Deposit Insurance Corporation (FDIC) in the regulations mentioned in the Federal Register?", "short_answers": ["FDIC is involved via 12 CFR Part 345.", "Part of Community Reinvestment Act Regulations."], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "Which part of the regulations does the Office of the Comptroller of the Currency oversee?", "short_answers": ["Oversees 12 CFR Part 25.", "Managed by the Office of the Comptroller of the Currency."], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "What document identifier is associated with the Federal Reserve System's involvement in these regulations?", "short_answers": ["Regulation BB; Docket No. R-0822.", "Federal Reserve System's identifier."], "wikipage": null}], "wikipages": [{"title": "AML Topic 155", "url": "https://www.fdic.gov/regulations/community/community/crapreamb.txt"}], "annotations": [{"knowledge": [], "long_answer": "The Federal Deposit Insurance Corporation (FDIC) is involved in the regulations as outlined in 12 CFR Part 345, which is part of the Community Reinvestment Act Regulations and Home Mortgage Disclosure; Final Rules. The Office of the Comptroller of the Currency oversees the regulations under 12 CFR Part 25. The document identifier associated with the Federal Reserve System's involvement in these regulations is Regulation BB; Docket No."}, {"knowledge": [{"content": "FEDERAL DEPOSIT INSURANCE CORPORATION\n\n12 CFR Part 345", "wikipage": "AML Topic 155"}, {"content": "Office of the Comptroller of the Currency\n\n12 CFR Part 25", "wikipage": "AML Topic 155"}, {"content": "FEDERAL RESERVE SYSTEM\n\n12 CFR Part 228\n\n[Regulation BB; Docket No. R-0822]", "wikipage": "AML Topic 155"}], "long_answer": "The Federal Deposit Insurance Corporation (FDIC) is involved in the regulations as outlined in 12 CFR Part 345, which is part of the Community Reinvestment Act Regulations and Home Mortgage Disclosure; Final Rules.\n\nThe Office of the Comptroller of the Currency oversees the regulations under 12 CFR Part 25.\n\nThe document identifier associated with the Federal Reserve System's involvement in these regulations is Regulation BB; Docket No. R-0822."}], "sample_id": "aml-topic-155-1", "question": "What is the role of the Federal Deposit Insurance Corporation (FDIC) in the regulations mentioned in the Federal Register?", "docs": [{"id": "doc-aml-topic-155-1-2", "title": "AML Topic 155 Part 2", "text": "deral Deposit Insurance Corporation\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n12 CFR Part 345\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nDepartment of the Treasury\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nOffice of Thrift Supervision\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n12 CFR Part 563e\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nFederal Reserve System\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n12 CFR Part 203\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n_______________________________________________________________________\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nCommunity Reinvestment Act Regulations and Home Mortgage Disclosure; \r\n\r\nFinal Rules\r\n\r\n[[Page 22156]] \r\n\r\nDEPARTMENT OF THE TREASURY\r\n\r\nOffice of the Comptroller of the Currency\r\n\r\n12 CFR Par", "score": 0.85, "summary": "deral Deposit Insurance Corporation\r \r \r \r \r 12 CFR Part 345\r \r \r \r \r Department of the Treasury\r \r \r \r \r Office of Thrift Supervision\r \r \r \r \r 12 CFR...", "extraction": "deral Deposit Insurance Corporation\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n12 CFR Part 345\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nDepartment of the Treasury\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nOffice of Thrift Supervision\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n12 CFR Part 563e\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\nFederal Reserve System\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n"}], "answer": "The Federal Deposit Insurance Corporation (FDIC) is involved in the regulations as outlined in 12 CFR Part 345, which is part of the Community Reinvestment Act Regulations and Home Mortgage Disclosure; Final Rules. The Office of the Comptroller of the Currency oversees the regulations under 12 CFR Part 25. The document identifier associated with the Federal Reserve System's involvement in these regulations is Regulation BB; Docket No.", "output": "The FDIC is an independent agency created by Congress to maintain stability and public confidence in the nation's financial system by insuring deposits, examining and supervising financial institutions for compliance with applicable laws and regulations, and managing receiverships [1].", "intermediate": ["[Retrieval]", "The FDIC is an independent agency created by Congress to maintain stability and public confidence in the nation's financial system by insuring deposits, examining and supervising financial institutions for compliance with applicable laws and regulations, and managing receiverships."]}, {"qa_pairs": [{"context": "No context provided", "question": "What is the purpose of the Community Reinvestment Act (CRA) regulations?", "short_answers": ["Establish framework", "Assess institution's record"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "Which agencies are involved in amending the Community Reinvestment Act regulations?", "short_answers": ["OCC", "Board", "FDIC", "OTS"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "When were the joint notices of proposed rulemaking for the CRA regulations published?", "short_answers": ["December 21, 1993", "October 7, 1994"], "wikipage": null}], "wikipages": [{"title": "AML Topic 155", "url": "https://www.fdic.gov/regulations/community/community/crapreamb.txt"}], "annotations": [{"knowledge": [], "long_answer": "The purpose of the CRA regulations is to establish the framework and criteria by which the agencies assess an institution's record of helping. The agencies involved in amending the CRA regulations are the Office of the Comptroller of the Currency (OCC), the Board of Governors of the Federal Reserve System (Board), the Federal Deposit Insurance Corporation (FDIC), and the Office of Thrift Supervision (OTS). The joint notices of proposed rulemaking for the CRA regulations were published on December 21, 1993, and again on October 7, 1994."}, {"knowledge": [{"content": "The purpose of the CRA regulations is to establish the framework and criteria by which the agencies assess an institution's record of helping.", "wikipage": "AML Topic 155"}, {"content": "The OCC, Board, FDIC, and OTS, (collectively, the Federal financial supervisory agencies or agencies) are amending their regulations concerning the Community Reinvestment Act (CRA).", "wikipage": "AML Topic 155"}, {"content": "The agencies published a joint notice of proposed rulemaking on this issue on December 21, 1993 (1993 proposal) and again on October 7, 1994 (1994 proposal).", "wikipage": "AML Topic 155"}], "long_answer": "The purpose of the CRA regulations is to establish the framework and criteria by which the agencies assess an institution's record of helping.\n\nThe agencies involved in amending the CRA regulations are the Office of the Comptroller of the Currency (OCC), the Board of Governors of the Federal Reserve System (Board), the Federal Deposit Insurance Corporation (FDIC), and the Office of Thrift Supervision (OTS).\n\nThe joint notices of proposed rulemaking for the CRA regulations were published on December 21, 1993, and again on October 7, 1994."}], "sample_id": "aml-topic-155-2", "question": "What is the purpose of the Community Reinvestment Act (CRA) regulations?", "docs": [{"id": "doc-aml-topic-155-2-1", "title": "AML Topic 155 Part 1", "text": "-AB27\r\n\r\nDEPARTMENT OF THE TREASURY\r\n\r\nOffice of Thrift Supervision\r\n\r\n12 CFR Part 563e\r\n\r\n[Docket No. 95-72]\r\n\r\nRIN 1550-AA69\r\n\r\nFEDERAL RESERVE SYSTEM\r\n\r\n12 CFR Part 203\r\n\r\n \r\n\r\nCommunity Reinvestment Act Regulations\r\n\r\nAGENCIES: Office of the Comptroller of the Currency, Treasury (OCC); \r\n\r\nBoard of Governors of the Federal Reserve System (Board); Federal \r\n\r\nDeposit Insurance Corporation (FDIC); Office of Thrift Supervision, \r\n\r\nTreasury (OTS).\r\n\r\nACTION: Joint final rule.\r\n\r\n---------------", "score": 0.9, "summary": "-AB27\r \r DEPARTMENT OF THE TREASURY\r \r Office of Thrift Supervision\r \r 12 CFR Part 563e\r \r [Docket No. 95-72]\r \r RIN 1550-AA69\r \r FEDERAL RESERVE SYST...", "extraction": "The purpose of the CRA regulations is to establish the framework and criteria by which the agencies assess an institution's record of helping."}], "answer": "The purpose of the CRA regulations is to establish the framework and criteria by which the agencies assess an institution's record of helping. The agencies involved in amending the CRA regulations are the Office of the Comptroller of the Currency (OCC), the Board of Governors of the Federal Reserve System (Board), the Federal Deposit Insurance Corporation (FDIC), and the Office of Thrift Supervision (OTS). The joint notices of proposed rulemaking for the CRA regulations were published on December 21, 1993, and again on October 7, 1994.", "output": "The purpose of the Community Reinvestment Act (CRA) regulations is to encourage financial institutions to meet the credit needs of the communities in which they operate, including low- and moderate-income (LMI) neighborhoods, consistent with safe and sound operations [1].", "intermediate": ["[Retrieval]", "The purpose of the Community Reinvestment Act (CRA) regulations is to encourage financial institutions to meet the credit needs of the communities in which they operate, including low- and moderate-income (LMI) neighborhoods, consistent with safe and sound operations."]}, {"qa_pairs": [{"context": "No context provided", "question": "What is the main goal of the final rule mentioned in the document?", "short_answers": ["Emphasize performance", "Promote consistency", "Eliminate unnecessary burden"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "When did the joint rule become effective?", "short_answers": ["July 1, 1995"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "Who can be contacted for more information regarding the OCC's compliance?", "short_answers": ["Stephen M. Cross", "Matthew Roberts"], "wikipage": null}], "wikipages": [{"title": "AML Topic 155", "url": "https://www.fdic.gov/regulations/community/community/crapreamb.txt"}], "annotations": [{"knowledge": [], "long_answer": "The main goal of the final rule is to emphasize performance rather than process, promote consistency in evaluations, and eliminate unnecessary burden. The joint rule became effective on July 1, 1995. For more information regarding the OCC's compliance, Stephen M."}, {"knowledge": [{"content": "The final rule seeks to emphasize performance rather than process, to promote consistency in evaluations, and to eliminate unnecessary burden.", "wikipage": "AML Topic 155"}, {"content": "EFFECTIVE DATES: This joint rule is effective July 1, 1995...", "wikipage": "AML Topic 155"}, {"content": "OCC: Stephen M. Cross, Deputy Comptroller for Compliance, (202) 874-5216; or Matthew Roberts, Director, Community and Consumer Law Division, (202) 874-5750...", "wikipage": "AML Topic 155"}], "long_answer": "The main goal of the final rule is to emphasize performance rather than process, promote consistency in evaluations, and eliminate unnecessary burden.\n\nThe joint rule became effective on July 1, 1995.\n\nFor more information regarding the OCC's compliance, Stephen M. Cross, Deputy Comptroller for Compliance, or Matthew Roberts, Director, Community and Consumer Law Division, can be contacted."}], "sample_id": "aml-topic-155-3", "question": "What is the main goal of the final rule mentioned in the document?", "docs": [{"id": "doc-aml-topic-155-3-1", "title": "AML Topic 155 Part 1", "text": "g to meet the credit needs of its community, including low- and \r\n\r\nmoderate-income neighborhoods, consistent with safe and sound \r\n\r\noperations, and to provide that the agencies' assessment shall be taken \r\n\r\ninto account in reviewing certain applications.\r\n\r\n    The final rule seeks to emphasize performance rather than process, \r\n\r\nto promote consistency in evaluations, and to eliminate unnecessary \r\n\r\nburden. As compared to the 1993 and 1994 proposals, the final rule \r\n\r\nreduces recordkeeping", "score": 0.9, "summary": "g to meet the credit needs of its community, including low- and \r \r moderate-income neighborhoods, consistent with safe and sound \r \r operations, and ...", "extraction": "The final rule seeks to emphasize performance rather than process, to promote consistency in evaluations, and to eliminate unnecessary burden."}], "answer": "The main goal of the final rule is to emphasize performance rather than process, promote consistency in evaluations, and eliminate unnecessary burden. The joint rule became effective on July 1, 1995. For more information regarding the OCC's compliance, Stephen M.", "output": "Answer: The main goal of the final rule is to promote consistency in evaluations and eliminate unnecessary burden [1].", "intermediate": ["[Retrieval]", "Answer: The main goal of the final rule is to promote consistency in evaluations and eliminate unnecessary burden."]}, {"qa_pairs": [{"context": "No context provided", "question": "What is the purpose of the joint statement issued by the Federal Reserve, FDIC, and OCC on March 19, 2020?", "short_answers": ["Encourage meeting financial needs", "Address COVID-19 impact"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "Which organizations collaborated to issue the joint statement regarding the CRA consideration for activities in response to COVID-19?", "short_answers": ["Federal Reserve", "FDIC", "OCC"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "How are financial institutions encouraged to support communities affected by COVID-19 according to the March 19, 2020 statement?", "short_answers": ["Work with affected customers", "Focus on low- and moderate-income communities"], "wikipage": null}], "wikipages": [{"title": "AML Topic 159", "url": "https://www.fdic.gov/news/financial-institution-letters/2020/fil20019a.pdf"}], "annotations": [{"knowledge": [], "long_answer": "The joint statement issued by the Federal Reserve, FDIC, and OCC on March 19, 2020, aims to encourage financial institutions to meet the financial services needs of their customers and members in areas affected by COVID-19. The Board of Governors of the Federal Reserve System, the Federal Deposit Insurance Corporation (FDIC), and the Office of the Comptroller of the Currency (OCC) collaborated to issue the joint statement regarding the CRA consideration for activities in response to COVID-19. Financial institutions are encouraged to work with affected customers and communities, particularly those that are low- and moderate-income, in response to the challenges posed by COVID-19."}, {"knowledge": [{"content": "The Board of Governors of the Federal Reserve System (Federal Reserve), the Federal Deposit Insurance Corporation (FDIC), and the Office of the Comptroller of the Currency (OCC) (collectively, the agencies) recognize the potential for Coronavirus Disease (also referred to as COVID-19) to adversely affect the customers and operations of financial institutions. On March 9, 2020, the federal financial institution regulatory agencies and state bank regulators issued a statement to encourage financial institutions to meet the financial services needs of their customers and members in areas affected by COVID-19.", "wikipage": "AML Topic 159"}, {"content": "The Board of Governors of the Federal Reserve System (Federal Reserve), the Federal Deposit Insurance Corporation (FDIC), and the Office of the Comptroller of the Currency (OCC) (collectively, the agencies) recognize the potential for Coronavirus Disease (also referred to as COVID-19) to adversely affect the customers and operations of financial institutions.", "wikipage": "AML Topic 159"}, {"content": "Consistent with the March 9, 2020, statement and existing information that the agencies have provided in prior communications on disasters and national emergencies, the agencies encourage financial institutions to work with affected customers and communities, particularly those that are low- and moderate-income.", "wikipage": "AML Topic 159"}], "long_answer": "The joint statement issued by the Federal Reserve, FDIC, and OCC on March 19, 2020, aims to encourage financial institutions to meet the financial services needs of their customers and members in areas affected by COVID-19. It recognizes the potential adverse effects of COVID-19 on customers and operations of financial institutions.\n\nThe Board of Governors of the Federal Reserve System, the Federal Deposit Insurance Corporation (FDIC), and the Office of the Comptroller of the Currency (OCC) collaborated to issue the joint statement regarding the CRA consideration for activities in response to COVID-19.\n\nFinancial institutions are encouraged to work with affected customers and communities, particularly those that are low- and moderate-income, in response to the challenges posed by COVID-19."}], "sample_id": "aml-topic-159-1", "question": "What is the purpose of the joint statement issued by the Federal Reserve, FDIC, and OCC on March 19, 2020?", "docs": [{"id": "doc-aml-topic-159-1-2", "title": "AML Topic 159 Part 2", "text": "D-19) to adversely affect the customers and operations of financial institutions. On March\n9, 2020, the federal financial institution regulatory agencies and state bank regulators issued a\nstatement to encourage financial institutions to meet the financial services needs of their\ncustomers and members in areas affected by COVID-19.1\nConsistent with the March 9, 2020, statement and existing information that the agencies have\nprovided in prior communications on disasters and national emergencies,", "score": 0.85, "summary": "D-19) to adversely affect the customers and operations of financial institutions. On March 9, 2020, the federal financial institution regulatory agenc...", "extraction": "D-19) to adversely affect the customers and operations of financial institutions. On March\n9, 2020, the federal financial institution regulatory agencies and state bank regulators issued a\nstatement t"}], "answer": "The joint statement issued by the Federal Reserve, FDIC, and OCC on March 19, 2020, aims to encourage financial institutions to meet the financial services needs of their customers and members in areas affected by COVID-19. The Board of Governors of the Federal Reserve System, the Federal Deposit Insurance Corporation (FDIC), and the Office of the Comptroller of the Currency (OCC) collaborated to issue the joint statement regarding the CRA consideration for activities in response to COVID-19. Financial institutions are encouraged to work with affected customers and communities, particularly those that are low- and moderate-income, in response to the challenges posed by COVID-19.", "output": "Answer: The purpose of the joint statement issued by the Federal Reserve, FDIC, and OCC on March 19, 2020, is to encourage financial institutions to meet the financial services needs of their customers and members in areas affected by the COVID-19 pandemic [1].", "intermediate": ["[Retrieval]", "Answer: The purpose of the joint statement issued by the Federal Reserve, FDIC, and OCC on March 19, 2020, is to encourage financial institutions to meet the financial services needs of their customers and members in areas affected by the COVID-19 pandemic."]}, {"qa_pairs": [{"context": "No context provided", "question": "How does the Community Reinvestment Act (CRA) influence banking services for low- and moderate-income individuals?", "short_answers": ["CRA encourages services for low-income individuals", "Includes waiving fees and expanding credit"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "What fee waivers might be considered under the CRA to support communities during challenging times?", "short_answers": ["Waive ATM fees", "Waive overdraft and late payment fees"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "In what ways can financial institutions ease restrictions to assist customers according to CRA guidelines?", "short_answers": ["Ease check cashing restrictions", "Expand short-term credit availability"], "wikipage": null}], "wikipages": [{"title": "AML Topic 159", "url": "https://www.fdic.gov/news/financial-institution-letters/2020/fil20019a.pdf"}], "annotations": [{"knowledge": [], "long_answer": "The Community Reinvestment Act (CRA) encourages financial institutions to provide retail banking services and lending activities that are responsive to the needs of low- and moderate-income individuals. Under the CRA, financial institutions might consider waiving fees such as automated teller machine (ATM) fees for customers and non-customers, overdraft fees, late payment fees on credit cards and other loans, and early withdrawal penalties on time deposits to support communities during challenging times. Financial institutions can ease restrictions by allowing the cashing of out-of-state and non-customer checks, expanding the availability of short-term, unsecured credit products for creditworthy borrowers, and increasing credit card limits for creditworthy borrowers, all in line with CRA guidelines."}, {"knowledge": [{"content": "Pursuant to the Community Reinvestment Act (CRA), the agencies will favorably consider retail banking services and retail lending activities in a financial institution\u2019s assessment areas that are responsive to the needs of low- and moderate-income individuals, small businesses, and small farms affected by COVID-19 and that are consistent with safe and sound banking practices.", "wikipage": "AML Topic 159"}, {"content": "These activities may include, but are not limited to: Waiving certain fees, such as: Automated teller machine (ATM) fees for customers and non-customers, overdraft fees, late payment fees on credit cards and other loans, and early withdrawal penalties on time deposits;", "wikipage": "AML Topic 159"}, {"content": "These activities may include, but are not limited to: Easing restrictions on cashing out-of-state and non-customer checks; Expanding the availability of other short-term, unsecured credit products for creditworthy borrowers; Increasing credit card limits for creditworthy borrowers;", "wikipage": "AML Topic 159"}], "long_answer": "The Community Reinvestment Act (CRA) encourages financial institutions to provide retail banking services and lending activities that are responsive to the needs of low- and moderate-income individuals. These services should be consistent with safe and sound banking practices and may include waiving certain fees, easing restrictions on check cashing, expanding short-term credit products, and increasing credit card limits for creditworthy borrowers.\n\nUnder the CRA, financial institutions might consider waiving fees such as automated teller machine (ATM) fees for customers and non-customers, overdraft fees, late payment fees on credit cards and other loans, and early withdrawal penalties on time deposits to support communities during challenging times.\n\nFinancial institutions can ease restrictions by allowing the cashing of out-of-state and non-customer checks, expanding the availability of short-term, unsecured credit products for creditworthy borrowers, and increasing credit card limits for creditworthy borrowers, all in line with CRA guidelines."}], "sample_id": "aml-topic-159-2", "question": "How does the Community Reinvestment Act (CRA) influence banking services for low- and moderate-income individuals?", "docs": [{"id": "doc-aml-topic-159-2-1", "title": "AML Topic 159 Part 1", "text": "stent with\nsafe and sound banking practices and applicable laws, including consumer protection laws\u2014\nserve the long-term interests of these communities and the financial system.\nWorking with Customers. Pursuant to the Community Reinvestment Act (CRA), the agencies\nwill favorably consider retail banking services and retail lending activities in a financial\ninstitution\u2019s assessment areas that are responsive to the needs of low- and moderate-income\nindividuals, small businesses, and small farms aff", "score": 0.9, "summary": "stent with safe and sound banking practices and applicable laws, including consumer protection laws\u2014 serve the long-term interests of these communitie...", "extraction": "Pursuant to the Community Reinvestment Act (CRA), the agencies will favorably consider retail banking services and retail lending activities in a financial institution\u2019s assessment areas that are responsive to the needs of low- and moderate-income individuals, small businesses, and small farms affected by COVID-19 and that are consistent with safe and sound banking practices."}], "answer": "The Community Reinvestment Act (CRA) encourages financial institutions to provide retail banking services and lending activities that are responsive to the needs of low- and moderate-income individuals. Under the CRA, financial institutions might consider waiving fees such as automated teller machine (ATM) fees for customers and non-customers, overdraft fees, late payment fees on credit cards and other loans, and early withdrawal penalties on time deposits to support communities during challenging times. Financial institutions can ease restrictions by allowing the cashing of out-of-state and non-customer checks, expanding the availability of short-term, unsecured credit products for creditworthy borrowers, and increasing credit card limits for creditworthy borrowers, all in line with CRA guidelines.", "output": "Answer: The CRA encourages banks to meet the credit needs of the communities they serve, including low- and moderate-income neighborhoods [1].", "intermediate": ["[Retrieval]", "Answer: The CRA encourages banks to meet the credit needs of the communities they serve, including low- and moderate-income neighborhoods."]}, {"qa_pairs": [{"context": "No context provided", "question": "How can financial institutions support customers with limited branch access during COVID-19?", "short_answers": ["Alternative service options", "Limited branch access"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "What payment accommodations can be offered to borrowers affected by COVID-19?", "short_answers": ["Defer or skip payments", "Extend payment due date"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "Will financial institutions face criticism for modifying loan terms for customers affected by COVID-19?", "short_answers": ["No examiner criticism", "CRA consideration"], "wikipage": null}], "wikipages": [{"title": "AML Topic 159", "url": "https://www.fdic.gov/news/financial-institution-letters/2020/fil20019a.pdf"}], "annotations": [{"knowledge": [], "long_answer": "Financial institutions can support customers by providing alternative service options to those who have limited ability to access branches. Institutions can offer payment accommodations such as allowing borrowers to defer or skip payments or extending the payment due date to avoid delinquencies and negative credit bureau reporting. No, prudent efforts to modify the terms on new or existing loans for affected low- and moderate-income customers, small businesses, and small farms will receive CRA consideration and not be subject to examiner criticism."}, {"knowledge": [{"content": "Providing alternative service options to customers in light of limited ability to access branches;", "wikipage": "AML Topic 159"}, {"content": "Offering payment accommodations, such as allowing borrowers to defer or skip payments or extending the payment due date, which would avoid delinquencies and negative credit bureau reporting, caused by COVID-19-related issues.", "wikipage": "AML Topic 159"}, {"content": "The agencies emphasize that prudent efforts to modify the terms on new or existing loans for affected low- and moderate-income customers, small businesses, and small farms will receive CRA consideration and not be subject to examiner criticism.", "wikipage": "AML Topic 159"}], "long_answer": "Financial institutions can support customers by providing alternative service options to those who have limited ability to access branches.\n\nInstitutions can offer payment accommodations such as allowing borrowers to defer or skip payments or extending the payment due date to avoid delinquencies and negative credit bureau reporting.\n\nNo, prudent efforts to modify the terms on new or existing loans for affected low- and moderate-income customers, small businesses, and small farms will receive CRA consideration and not be subject to examiner criticism."}], "sample_id": "aml-topic-159-3", "question": "How can financial institutions support customers with limited branch access during COVID-19?", "docs": [{"id": "doc-aml-topic-159-3-1", "title": "AML Topic 159 Part 1", "text": "www.fdic.gov/news/news/press/2020/pr20025.html (FDIC); https://www.occ.gov/news-issuances/news-\nreleases/2020/nr-ia-2020-30.html (OCC).\n\u2022 Providing alternative service options to customers in light of limited ability to access\nbranches; and\n\u2022 Offering payment accommodations, such as allowing borrowers to defer or skip\npayments or extending the payment due date, which would avoid delinquencies and\nnegative credit bureau reporting, caused by COVID-19-related issues.\nThe agencies emphasize that pru", "score": 0.9, "summary": "www.fdic.gov/news/news/press/2020/pr20025.html (FDIC); https://www.occ.gov/news-issuances/news- releases/2020/nr-ia-2020-30.html (OCC). \u2022 Providing al...", "extraction": "Providing alternative service options to customers in light of limited ability to access branches;"}], "answer": "Financial institutions can support customers by providing alternative service options to those who have limited ability to access branches. Institutions can offer payment accommodations such as allowing borrowers to defer or skip payments or extending the payment due date to avoid delinquencies and negative credit bureau reporting. No, prudent efforts to modify the terms on new or existing loans for affected low- and moderate-income customers, small businesses, and small farms will receive CRA consideration and not be subject to examiner criticism.", "output": "Answer: The FDIC has provided guidance to financial institutions on how to support customers with limited branch access during the COVID-19 pandemic [1].", "intermediate": ["[Retrieval]", "Answer: The FDIC has provided guidance to financial institutions on how to support customers with limited branch access during the COVID-19 pandemic."]}, {"qa_pairs": [{"context": "No context provided", "question": "What are some organizational structures of Community Development Financial Institutions (CDFIs) mentioned in the FDIC document?", "short_answers": ["CDFI Banks", "CDFI Credit Unions", "CDFI Loan Funds", "CDFI Venture Capital Funds"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "What financing approaches are outlined for CDFIs in the FDIC document?", "short_answers": ["Equity", "Debt", "Deposits"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "How does the FDIC suggest banks evaluate potential CDFI partners?", "short_answers": ["Credit and performance review", "Sources of information for analysis"], "wikipage": null}], "wikipages": [{"title": "AML Topic 161", "url": "https://www.fdic.gov/resources/bankers/community-reinvestment-act/documents/strategies_for_community_banks_to_develop_partnerships_with_cdfis_2014.pdf"}], "annotations": [{"knowledge": [], "long_answer": "The FDIC document mentions several organizational structures for CDFIs, including CDFI Banks, CDFI Credit Unions, CDFI Loan Funds, and CDFI Venture Capital Funds. The FDIC document outlines three financing approaches for CDFIs: Equity, Debt, and Deposits. The FDIC suggests that banks evaluate potential CDFI partners by conducting a credit and performance review and utilizing various sources of information for analysis."}, {"knowledge": [{"content": "II. CDFI ORGANIZATIONAL STRUCTURES 7 A. CDFI Banks 8 B. CDFI Credit Unions 9 C. CDFI Loan Funds 10 D. CDFI Venture Capital Funds 11", "wikipage": "AML Topic 161"}, {"content": "III.FINANCING APPROACHES 13 A. Equity 13 B. Debt 15 C. Deposits 16", "wikipage": "AML Topic 161"}, {"content": "IV. EVALUATION OF BANK/CDFI PARTNERSHIP OPPORTUNITIES 17 A. Identifying Potential CDFI Partners 17 B. Evaluating Potential Partners 18 1. Credit and Performance Review 18 2. Sources of Information for Analysis 18", "wikipage": "AML Topic 161"}], "long_answer": "The FDIC document mentions several organizational structures for CDFIs, including CDFI Banks, CDFI Credit Unions, CDFI Loan Funds, and CDFI Venture Capital Funds.\n\nThe FDIC document outlines three financing approaches for CDFIs: Equity, Debt, and Deposits.\n\nThe FDIC suggests that banks evaluate potential CDFI partners by conducting a credit and performance review and utilizing various sources of information for analysis."}], "sample_id": "aml-topic-161-1", "question": "What are some organizational structures of Community Development Financial Institutions (CDFIs) mentioned in the FDIC document?", "docs": [{"id": "doc-aml-topic-161-1-1", "title": "AML Topic 161 Part 1", "text": "Strategies for\nCommunity Banks\nto Develop Partnerships with\nCommunity Development\nFinancial Institutions\nMarch 2014\n1\nACKNOWLEDGEMENTS\nThe FDIC acknowledges the expertise and critical review received by Paul Ginger, District\nCommunity Affairs Officer at the Office of the Comptroller of the Currency and U.S.\nDepartment of the Treasury CDFI Fund staff.\nI\nII\nCONTENTS\nEXECUTIVE SUMMARY 1\nI. INTRODUCTION 5\nII. CDFI ORGANIZATIONAL STRUCTURES 7\nA. CDFI Banks 8\nB. CDFI Credit Unions 9\nC. CDFI Loan Funds", "score": 0.9, "summary": "Strategies for Community Banks to Develop Partnerships with Community Development Financial Institutions March 2014 1 ACKNOWLEDGEMENTS The FDIC acknow...", "extraction": "II. CDFI ORGANIZATIONAL STRUCTURES 7 A. CDFI Banks 8 B. CDFI Credit Unions 9 C. CDFI Loan Funds 10 D. CDFI Venture Capital Funds 11"}], "answer": "The FDIC document mentions several organizational structures for CDFIs, including CDFI Banks, CDFI Credit Unions, CDFI Loan Funds, and CDFI Venture Capital Funds. The FDIC document outlines three financing approaches for CDFIs: Equity, Debt, and Deposits. The FDIC suggests that banks evaluate potential CDFI partners by conducting a credit and performance review and utilizing various sources of information for analysis.", "output": "Answer: The FDIC document mentions that some organizational structures of Community Development Financial Institutions (CDFIs) are CDFI banks, CDFI credit unions, and CDFI loan funds [1].", "intermediate": ["[Retrieval]", "Answer: The FDIC document mentions that some organizational structures of Community Development Financial Institutions (CDFIs) are CDFI banks, CDFI credit unions, and CDFI loan funds."]}, {"qa_pairs": [{"context": "No context provided", "question": "How can banks provide equity capital to Community Development Financial Institutions (CDFIs)?", "short_answers": ["Traditional equity instruments", "Equity capital provision"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "What are some methods banks can use to enhance the lending flexibility of CDFIs?", "short_answers": ["Equity-equivalent investments", "Enhance lending flexibility"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "In what ways can banks collaborate with CDFIs for loan participations?", "short_answers": ["Loan participations", "Bank-CDFI collaboration"], "wikipage": null}], "wikipages": [{"title": "AML Topic 161", "url": "https://www.fdic.gov/resources/bankers/community-reinvestment-act/documents/strategies_for_community_banks_to_develop_partnerships_with_cdfis_2014.pdf"}], "annotations": [{"knowledge": [], "long_answer": "Banks can provide equity capital to CDFIs by using traditional equity instruments as outlined in the case examples of partnership options. Banks can enhance the lending flexibility of CDFIs by providing equity-equivalent investments. Banks can collaborate with CDFIs in loan participations as part of their partnership options."}, {"knowledge": [{"content": "A. Providing Equity Capital Using Traditional Equity Instruments 38", "wikipage": "AML Topic 161"}, {"content": "B. Providing Equity-Equivalent Investments to Enhance CDFIs\u2019 Lending Flexibility 41", "wikipage": "AML Topic 161"}, {"content": "H. Collaborating with CDFIs in Loan Participations 52", "wikipage": "AML Topic 161"}], "long_answer": "Banks can provide equity capital to CDFIs by using traditional equity instruments as outlined in the case examples of partnership options.\n\nBanks can enhance the lending flexibility of CDFIs by providing equity-equivalent investments.\n\nBanks can collaborate with CDFIs in loan participations as part of their partnership options."}], "sample_id": "aml-topic-161-2", "question": "How can banks provide equity capital to Community Development Financial Institutions (CDFIs)?", "docs": [{"id": "doc-aml-topic-161-2-1", "title": "AML Topic 161 Part 1", "text": "ments 29\nB. CRA Consideration of Bank/CDFI Partnerships 31\nC. CRA Consideration of CDFI Investments and Lending Activities 33\nD. CRA Consideration of CDFI Partnerships and Community Development Services 34\nE. CRA Consideration for Investments in CDFIs that Serve Areas Broader\nthan a Bank\u2019s Assessment Areas 34\nF. CRA Consideration for Investments in Minority- or Women-Owned Financial\nInstitutions and Low-Income Credit Unions 35\nVI. CASE EXAMPLES OF PARTNERSHIP OPTIONS 37\nA. Providing Equity Capit", "score": 0.9, "summary": "ments 29 B. CRA Consideration of Bank/CDFI Partnerships 31 C. CRA Consideration of CDFI Investments and Lending Activities 33 D. CRA Consideration of ...", "extraction": "A. Providing Equity Capital Using Traditional Equity Instruments 38"}], "answer": "Banks can provide equity capital to CDFIs by using traditional equity instruments as outlined in the case examples of partnership options. Banks can enhance the lending flexibility of CDFIs by providing equity-equivalent investments. Banks can collaborate with CDFIs in loan participations as part of their partnership options.", "output": "Answer: Banks can provide equity capital to CDFIs through the CDFI Fund's Capital Programs [1].", "intermediate": ["[Retrieval]", "Answer: Banks can provide equity capital to CDFIs through the CDFI Fund's Capital Programs."]}, {"qa_pairs": [{"context": "No context provided", "question": "How does the FDIC support community banks in enhancing their Community Reinvestment Act (CRA) performance?", "short_answers": ["FDIC conducts outreach activities", "Enhance CRA performance"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "What types of financial institutions qualify as Community Development Financial Institutions (CDFIs)?", "short_answers": ["Banks", "Credit unions", "Loan funds", "Venture capital funds"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "What role do CDFIs play in the financial services system?", "short_answers": ["Specialize in underserved markets", "Provide credit to difficult-to-serve borrowers"], "wikipage": null}], "wikipages": [{"title": "AML Topic 161", "url": "https://www.fdic.gov/resources/bankers/community-reinvestment-act/documents/strategies_for_community_banks_to_develop_partnerships_with_cdfis_2014.pdf"}], "annotations": [{"knowledge": [], "long_answer": "The FDIC conducts outreach activities to help banks enhance their Community Reinvestment Act (CRA) performance and promote community development activities. CDFIs include banks and bank holding companies, as well as credit unions, loan funds, and venture capital funds. CDFIs fill a niche in the nation's financial services system by specializing in providing credit to borrowers and communities that may be difficult for traditional banks to serve."}, {"knowledge": [{"content": "In support of that mission, the FDIC conducts an array of outreach activities to help banks enhance Community Reinvestment Act (CRA) performance and promote community development activities.", "wikipage": "AML Topic 161"}, {"content": "CDFIs include banks and bank holding companies, as well as credit unions, loan funds, and venture capital funds.", "wikipage": "AML Topic 161"}, {"content": "CDFIs fill a niche in the nation\u2019s financial services system by specializing in providing credit to borrowers and communities that may be difficult for traditional banks to serve.", "wikipage": "AML Topic 161"}], "long_answer": "The FDIC conducts outreach activities to help banks enhance their Community Reinvestment Act (CRA) performance and promote community development activities.\n\nCDFIs include banks and bank holding companies, as well as credit unions, loan funds, and venture capital funds.\n\nCDFIs fill a niche in the nation's financial services system by specializing in providing credit to borrowers and communities that may be difficult for traditional banks to serve."}], "sample_id": "aml-topic-161-3", "question": "How does the FDIC support community banks in enhancing their Community Reinvestment Act (CRA) performance?", "docs": [{"id": "doc-aml-topic-161-3-1", "title": "AML Topic 161 Part 1", "text": "onfidence in the nation\u2019s financial system by:\ninsuring deposits, examining and supervising financial institutions for safety and soundness\nand consumer protection, and managing receiverships. In support of that mission, the FDIC\nconducts an array of outreach activities to help banks enhance Community Reinvestment Act\n(CRA) performance and promote community development activities. The purpose of this guide\nis to provide information that will help community banks identify and evaluate opportuniti", "score": 0.9, "summary": "onfidence in the nation\u2019s financial system by: insuring deposits, examining and supervising financial institutions for safety and soundness and consum...", "extraction": "In support of that mission, the FDIC conducts an array of outreach activities to help banks enhance Community Reinvestment Act (CRA) performance and promote community development activities."}], "answer": "The FDIC conducts outreach activities to help banks enhance their Community Reinvestment Act (CRA) performance and promote community development activities. CDFIs include banks and bank holding companies, as well as credit unions, loan funds, and venture capital funds. CDFIs fill a niche in the nation's financial services system by specializing in providing credit to borrowers and communities that may be difficult for traditional banks to serve.", "output": "Answer: The FDIC supports community banks in enhancing their CRA performance through a variety of initiatives, including technical assistance, training, and outreach [1].", "intermediate": ["[Retrieval]", "Answer: The FDIC supports community banks in enhancing their CRA performance through a variety of initiatives, including technical assistance, training, and outreach."]}, {"qa_pairs": [{"context": "No context provided", "question": "What type of data is included in the FDIC's Quarterly Banking Profile?", "short_answers": ["Financial performance of FDIC-insured institutions", "Return on assets and net income data"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "How does the FDIC keep the public informed about policy changes for banks?", "short_answers": ["Regular updates", "News announcements and policy fact sheets"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "What are some of the research tools and reports provided by the FDIC?", "short_answers": ["Center for Financial Research", "FDIC National Survey of Unbanked and Underbanked Households"], "wikipage": null}], "wikipages": [{"title": "AML Topic 163", "url": "https://www.fdic.gov/banker-resource-center/community-reinvestment-act-cra"}], "annotations": [{"knowledge": [], "long_answer": "The FDIC's Quarterly Banking Profile includes data on the financial performance of FDIC-insured institutions, such as return on assets and net income. The FDIC keeps the public informed about policy changes for banks through regular updates, including news announcements, speeches, testimony, and policy fact sheets. The FDIC provides a variety of research tools and reports, including the Center for Financial Research, Consumer Research, FDIC National Survey of Unbanked and Underbanked Households, and the Annual Risk Review."}, {"knowledge": [{"content": "Quarterly Banking Profile for First Quarter 2024", "wikipage": "AML Topic 163"}, {"content": "The FDIC publishes regular updates on news and activities. Keep up with FDIC announcements, read speeches and testimony on the latest banking issues, learn about policy changes for banks, and get the details on upcoming conferences and events.", "wikipage": "AML Topic 163"}, {"content": "More FDIC Analysis Center for Financial Research Consumer Research FDIC National Survey of Unbanked and Underbanked Households Annual Risk Review", "wikipage": "AML Topic 163"}], "long_answer": "The FDIC's Quarterly Banking Profile includes data on the financial performance of FDIC-insured institutions, such as return on assets and net income.\n\nThe FDIC keeps the public informed about policy changes for banks through regular updates, including news announcements, speeches, testimony, and policy fact sheets.\n\nThe FDIC provides a variety of research tools and reports, including the Center for Financial Research, Consumer Research, FDIC National Survey of Unbanked and Underbanked Households, and the Annual Risk Review."}], "sample_id": "aml-topic-163-3", "question": "What type of data is included in the FDIC's Quarterly Banking Profile?", "docs": [{"id": "doc-aml-topic-163-3-1", "title": "AML Topic 163 Part 1", "text": "ers, and state banking performance data. Browse our extensive research tools and reports.\nMore FDIC Analysis\nCenter for Financial Research\nConsumer Research\nFDIC National Survey of Unbanked and Underbanked Households\nQuarterly Banking Profile\nFDIC Academic Challenge\nFDIC Banking Issues in Focus\nAnnual Risk Review\nEVENT / February 25, 2025\nFDIC-Insured Institutions Reported Return on Assets of 1.11 Percent and Net Inc\u2026\nEVENT / December 12, 2024\nFDIC-Insured Institutions Reported Net Income of $65", "score": 0.9, "summary": "ers, and state banking performance data. Browse our extensive research tools and reports. More FDIC Analysis Center for Financial Research Consumer Re...", "extraction": "Quarterly Banking Profile for First Quarter 2024"}], "answer": "The FDIC's Quarterly Banking Profile includes data on the financial performance of FDIC-insured institutions, such as return on assets and net income. The FDIC keeps the public informed about policy changes for banks through regular updates, including news announcements, speeches, testimony, and policy fact sheets. The FDIC provides a variety of research tools and reports, including the Center for Financial Research, Consumer Research, FDIC National Survey of Unbanked and Underbanked Households, and the Annual Risk Review.", "output": "Answer: The FDIC's Quarterly Banking Profile includes data on the financial condition, performance, and risk profile of FDIC-insured institutions [1].", "intermediate": ["[Retrieval]", "Answer: The FDIC's Quarterly Banking Profile includes data on the financial condition, performance, and risk profile of FDIC-insured institutions."]}, {"qa_pairs": [{"context": "No context provided", "question": "What kind of data and reports can enterprises access from the FDIC for banking performance analysis?", "short_answers": ["state banking performance data", "FDIC National Survey", "Quarterly Banking Profile", "Annual Risk Review"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "How can enterprises stay informed about FDIC announcements and policy changes?", "short_answers": ["FDIC news", "policy changes", "speeches and testimony"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "What resources does the FDIC offer for understanding the impact of natural disasters on banking institutions?", "short_answers": ["press releases", "financial institution letters", "policy fact sheets"], "wikipage": null}], "wikipages": [{"title": "AML Topic 164", "url": "https://www.fdic.gov/laws-and-regulations/fdic-law-regulations-related-acts"}], "annotations": [{"knowledge": [], "long_answer": "Enterprises can access various data and reports from the FDIC for banking performance analysis, including state banking performance data, FDIC National Survey of Unbanked and Underbanked Households, Quarterly Banking Profile, and the Annual Risk Review. Enterprises can stay informed about FDIC announcements and policy changes by keeping up with FDIC news, reading speeches and testimony on the latest banking issues, and learning about policy changes for banks through FDIC's regular updates. The FDIC offers resources such as press releases, financial institution letters, and policy fact sheets to help understand the impact of natural disasters on banking institutions."}, {"knowledge": [{"content": "papers, and state banking performance data. Browse our extensive research tools and reports. More FDIC Analysis Center for Financial Research Consumer Research FDIC National Survey of Unbanked and Underbanked Households Quarterly Banking Profile FDIC Academic Challenge FDIC Banking Issues in Focus Annual Risk Review", "wikipage": "AML Topic 164"}, {"content": "The FDIC publishes regular updates on news and activities. Keep up with FDIC announcements, read speeches and testimony on the latest banking issues, learn about policy changes for banks, and get the details on upcoming conferences and events.", "wikipage": "AML Topic 164"}, {"content": "Find More FDIC News Press Releases Financial Institution Letters Conferences & Events Board Matters Natural Disasters Media Campaigns Speeches, Statements & Testimonies Podcasts Videos Opinion Editorials Policy Fact Sheets", "wikipage": "AML Topic 164"}], "long_answer": "Enterprises can access various data and reports from the FDIC for banking performance analysis, including state banking performance data, FDIC National Survey of Unbanked and Underbanked Households, Quarterly Banking Profile, and the Annual Risk Review.\n\nEnterprises can stay informed about FDIC announcements and policy changes by keeping up with FDIC news, reading speeches and testimony on the latest banking issues, and learning about policy changes for banks through FDIC's regular updates.\n\nThe FDIC offers resources such as press releases, financial institution letters, and policy fact sheets to help understand the impact of natural disasters on banking institutions."}], "sample_id": "aml-topic-164-3", "question": "What kind of data and reports can enterprises access from the FDIC for banking performance analysis?", "docs": [{"id": "doc-aml-topic-164-3-1", "title": "AML Topic 164 Part 1", "text": "papers, and state banking performance data. Browse our extensive research tools and reports.\nMore FDIC Analysis\nCenter for Financial Research\nConsumer Research\nFDIC National Survey of Unbanked and Underbanked Households\nQuarterly Banking Profile\nFDIC Academic Challenge\nFDIC Banking Issues in Focus\nAnnual Risk Review\nEVENT / February 25, 2025\nFDIC-Insured Institutions Reported Return on Assets of 1.11 Percent and Net Inc\u2026\nEVENT / December 12, 2024\nFDIC-Insured Institutions Reported Net Income of", "score": 0.9, "summary": "papers, and state banking performance data. Browse our extensive research tools and reports. More FDIC Analysis Center for Financial Research Consumer...", "extraction": "papers, and state banking performance data. Browse our extensive research tools and reports. More FDIC Analysis Center for Financial Research Consumer Research FDIC National Survey of Unbanked and Underbanked Households Quarterly Banking Profile FDIC Academic Challenge FDIC Banking Issues in Focus Annual Risk Review"}], "answer": "Enterprises can access various data and reports from the FDIC for banking performance analysis, including state banking performance data, FDIC National Survey of Unbanked and Underbanked Households, Quarterly Banking Profile, and the Annual Risk Review. Enterprises can stay informed about FDIC announcements and policy changes by keeping up with FDIC news, reading speeches and testimony on the latest banking issues, and learning about policy changes for banks through FDIC's regular updates. The FDIC offers resources such as press releases, financial institution letters, and policy fact sheets to help understand the impact of natural disasters on banking institutions.", "output": "Answer: Enterprises can access data and reports such as the FDIC's Quarterly Banking Profile, which provides detailed financial data on individual banks and bank groups, as well as the FDIC's National Survey of Unbanked and Underbanked Households, which provides information on the level of banking activity among households in the United States [1].", "intermediate": ["[Retrieval]", "Answer: Enterprises can access data and reports such as the FDIC's Quarterly Banking Profile, which provides detailed financial data on individual banks and bank groups, as well as the FDIC's National Survey of Unbanked and Underbanked Households, which provides information on the level of banking activity among households in the United States."]}, {"qa_pairs": [{"context": "No context provided", "question": "What resources does the FDIC provide for bankers to enhance their financial education?", "short_answers": ["Financial education materials", "Data tools", "Laws and regulations documentation"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "How can bankers access information on FDIC's laws and regulations?", "short_answers": ["Documentation of laws and regulations", "Available for stakeholders"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "Does the FDIC offer any specific resources for community banking research?", "short_answers": ["Community Banking Research Program", "FDIC resources"], "wikipage": null}], "wikipages": [{"title": "AML Topic 170", "url": "https://www.fdic.gov/laws-and-regulations/economic-growth-and-regulatory-paperwork-reduction-act-1996"}], "annotations": [{"knowledge": [], "long_answer": "The FDIC offers a collection of financial education materials, data tools, documentation of laws and regulations, and information on important initiatives for bankers. Bankers can access information on FDIC's laws and regulations through the FDIC's resources, which include documentation of laws and regulations available for stakeholders. Yes, the FDIC provides resources specifically for community banking research as part of its comprehensive resource offerings."}, {"knowledge": [{"content": "The FDIC provides a wealth of resources for consumers, bankers, analysts, and other stakeholders. Browse our collection of financial education materials, data tools, documentation of laws and regulations, information on important initiatives, and more.", "wikipage": "AML Topic 170"}, {"content": "The FDIC provides a wealth of resources for consumers, bankers, analysts, and other stakeholders. Browse our collection of financial education materials, data tools, documentation of laws and regulations, information on important initiatives, and more.", "wikipage": "AML Topic 170"}, {"content": "Additional FDIC Resources... Community Banking Research Program", "wikipage": "AML Topic 170"}], "long_answer": "The FDIC offers a collection of financial education materials, data tools, documentation of laws and regulations, and information on important initiatives for bankers.\n\nBankers can access information on FDIC's laws and regulations through the FDIC's resources, which include documentation of laws and regulations available for stakeholders.\n\nYes, the FDIC provides resources specifically for community banking research as part of its comprehensive resource offerings."}], "sample_id": "aml-topic-170-2", "question": "What resources does the FDIC provide for bankers to enhance their financial education?", "docs": [{"id": "doc-aml-topic-170-2-1", "title": "AML Topic 170 Part 1", "text": "ASE / January 17, 2025\nMillennium Bank, Des Plaines, Ill. Assumes All Deposits of Pulaski Savings\u2026\nPODCASTS / January 06, 2025\nSmall Business Lending\nResources\nToggle submenu\nResources\nThe FDIC provides a wealth of resources for consumers, bankers, analysts, and other stakeholders. Browse our collection of financial education materials, data tools, documentation of laws and regulations, information on important initiatives, and more.\nAdditional FDIC Resources\nConsumer Resource Center\nBanker Reso", "score": 0.9, "summary": "ASE / January 17, 2025 Millennium Bank, Des Plaines, Ill. Assumes All Deposits of Pulaski Savings\u2026 PODCASTS / January 06, 2025 Small Business Lending ...", "extraction": "The FDIC provides a wealth of resources for consumers, bankers, analysts, and other stakeholders. Browse our collection of financial education materials, data tools, documentation of laws and regulations, information on important initiatives, and more."}], "answer": "The FDIC offers a collection of financial education materials, data tools, documentation of laws and regulations, and information on important initiatives for bankers. Bankers can access information on FDIC's laws and regulations through the FDIC's resources, which include documentation of laws and regulations available for stakeholders. Yes, the FDIC provides resources specifically for community banking research as part of its comprehensive resource offerings.", "output": "Answer: The FDIC provides a wealth of resources for consumers, bankers, analysts, and other stakeholders [1].", "intermediate": ["[Retrieval]", "Answer: The FDIC provides a wealth of resources for consumers, bankers, analysts, and other stakeholders."]}, {"qa_pairs": [{"context": "No context provided", "question": "What resources does the FDIC provide for bankers seeking information on laws and regulations?", "short_answers": ["Banker Resource Center", "Documentation of laws and regulations"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "Where can consumers find educational materials and tools provided by the FDIC?", "short_answers": ["Consumer Resource Center", "Educational materials and tools"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "What type of research does the FDIC provide regarding the U.S. banking industry?", "short_answers": ["Quarterly banking profiles", "Working papers"], "wikipage": null}], "wikipages": [{"title": "AML Topic 175", "url": "https://www.fdic.gov/laws-and-regulations/public-comments-fdic"}], "annotations": [{"knowledge": [], "long_answer": "The FDIC offers a Banker Resource Center that includes documentation of laws and regulations, among other resources. Consumers can find educational materials and tools in the Consumer Resource Center provided by the FDIC. The FDIC provides research including quarterly banking profiles and working papers as part of its analysis of the U."}, {"knowledge": [{"content": "The FDIC provides a wealth of resources for consumers, bankers, analysts, and other stakeholders. Browse our collection of financial education materials, data tools, documentation of laws and regulations, information on important initiatives, and more.", "wikipage": "AML Topic 175"}, {"content": "Additional FDIC Resources: Consumer Resource Center", "wikipage": "AML Topic 175"}, {"content": "The FDIC is proud to be a pre-eminent source of U.S. banking industry research, including quarterly banking profiles, working papers.", "wikipage": "AML Topic 175"}], "long_answer": "The FDIC offers a Banker Resource Center that includes documentation of laws and regulations, among other resources.\n\nConsumers can find educational materials and tools in the Consumer Resource Center provided by the FDIC.\n\nThe FDIC provides research including quarterly banking profiles and working papers as part of its analysis of the U.S. banking industry."}], "sample_id": "aml-topic-175-2", "question": "What resources does the FDIC provide for bankers seeking information on laws and regulations?", "docs": [{"id": "doc-aml-topic-175-2-1", "title": "AML Topic 175 Part 1", "text": "ank, Des Plaines, Ill. Assumes All Deposits of Pulaski Savings\u2026\nPODCASTS / January 06, 2025\nSmall Business Lending\nResources\nToggle submenu\nResources\nThe FDIC provides a wealth of resources for consumers, bankers, analysts, and other stakeholders. Browse our collection of financial education materials, data tools, documentation of laws and regulations, information on important initiatives, and more.\nAdditional FDIC Resources\nConsumer Resource Center\nBanker Resource Center\nDeposit Insurance\nSuper", "score": 0.9, "summary": "ank, Des Plaines, Ill. Assumes All Deposits of Pulaski Savings\u2026 PODCASTS / January 06, 2025 Small Business Lending Resources Toggle submenu Resources ...", "extraction": "The FDIC provides a wealth of resources for consumers, bankers, analysts, and other stakeholders. Browse our collection of financial education materials, data tools, documentation of laws and regulations, information on important initiatives, and more."}], "answer": "The FDIC offers a Banker Resource Center that includes documentation of laws and regulations, among other resources. Consumers can find educational materials and tools in the Consumer Resource Center provided by the FDIC. The FDIC provides research including quarterly banking profiles and working papers as part of its analysis of the U.", "output": "Answer: The FDIC provides a wealth of resources for consumers, bankers, analysts, and other stakeholders [1].", "intermediate": ["[Retrieval]", "Answer: The FDIC provides a wealth of resources for consumers, bankers, analysts, and other stakeholders."]}, {"qa_pairs": [{"context": "No context provided", "question": "What is the purpose of the Customer Identification Program (CIP) Rule for FDIC-supervised institutions?", "short_answers": ["Risk-based verification procedures", "Identify true customer identity"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "What minimum identifying information must a bank collect from a customer under the CIP Rule before opening an account?", "short_answers": ["Name", "Date of birth", "Address", "Identification number"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "Why is a written Customer Identification Program important for banks?", "short_answers": ["Required for AML/CFT program", "Appropriate for bank's risk profile"], "wikipage": null}], "wikipages": [{"title": "AML Topic 183", "url": "https://www.fdic.gov/news/financial-institution-letters/2024/fil24015a.pdf"}], "annotations": [{"knowledge": [], "long_answer": "The purpose of the Customer Identification Program (CIP) Rule is to require banks to implement a program that includes risk-based verification procedures. Under the CIP Rule, a bank must collect at a minimum the customer's name, date of birth (for an individual), address, and identification number before opening an account. A written Customer Identification Program is important for banks because it is a required component of their Anti-Money Laundering/Countering the Financing of Terrorism (AML/CFT) program."}, {"knowledge": [{"content": "A written CIP is required as part of a bank\u2019s Anti-Money Laundering/Countering the Financing of Terrorism (AML/CFT) program and must be appropriate for the bank\u2019s risk profile, complexity, and business activities. The CIP Rule requires a bank to implement a program that includes risk-based verification procedures that enable the bank to form a reasonable belief that it knows the true identity of its customers.", "wikipage": "AML Topic 183"}, {"content": "These procedures must specify the identifying information that a bank must obtain from each customer prior to opening an account. These procedures must include collecting, at a minimum, the customer\u2019s name, date of birth (for an individual), address, and iden.", "wikipage": "AML Topic 183"}, {"content": "A written CIP is required as part of a bank\u2019s Anti-Money Laundering/Countering the Financing of Terrorism (AML/CFT) program and must be appropriate for the bank\u2019s risk profile, complexity, and business activities.", "wikipage": "AML Topic 183"}], "long_answer": "The purpose of the Customer Identification Program (CIP) Rule is to require banks to implement a program that includes risk-based verification procedures. These procedures enable the bank to form a reasonable belief that it knows the true identity of its customers, which is part of the bank\u2019s Anti-Money Laundering/Countering the Financing of Terrorism (AML/CFT) program.\n\nUnder the CIP Rule, a bank must collect at a minimum the customer's name, date of birth (for an individual), address, and identification number before opening an account.\n\nA written Customer Identification Program is important for banks because it is a required component of their Anti-Money Laundering/Countering the Financing of Terrorism (AML/CFT) program. It must be appropriate for the bank\u2019s risk profile, complexity, and business activities, ensuring that the bank can verify the true identity of its customers effectively."}], "sample_id": "aml-topic-183-1", "question": "What is the purpose of the Customer Identification Program (CIP) Rule for FDIC-supervised institutions?", "docs": [{"id": "doc-aml-topic-183-1-2", "title": "AML Topic 183 Part 2", "text": "(collectively \u201cbanks\u201d). A written2 CIP is required as part of a\nbank\u2019s Anti-Money Laundering/Countering the Financing of Terrorism (AML/CFT) program\nand must be appropriate for the bank\u2019s risk profile, complexity, and business activities.\nThe CIP Rule requires a bank to implement a program that includes risk-based verification\nprocedures that enable the bank to form a reasonable belief that it knows the true identity of its\ncustomers.3 These procedures must specify the identifying information t", "score": 0.85, "summary": "(collectively \u201cbanks\u201d). A written2 CIP is required as part of a bank\u2019s Anti-Money Laundering/Countering the Financing of Terrorism (AML/CFT) program a...", "extraction": "(collectively \u201cbanks\u201d). A written2 CIP is required as part of a\nbank\u2019s Anti-Money Laundering/Countering the Financing of Terrorism (AML/CFT) program\nand must be appropriate for the bank\u2019s risk profile"}], "answer": "The purpose of the Customer Identification Program (CIP) Rule is to require banks to implement a program that includes risk-based verification procedures. Under the CIP Rule, a bank must collect at a minimum the customer's name, date of birth (for an individual), address, and identification number before opening an account. A written Customer Identification Program is important for banks because it is a required component of their Anti-Money Laundering/Countering the Financing of Terrorism (AML/CFT) program.", "output": "Answer: The CIP Rule requires a bank to implement a program that includes risk-based verification procedures that enable the bank to form a reasonable belief that it knows the true identity of its customers [1].", "intermediate": ["[Retrieval]", "Answer: The CIP Rule requires a bank to implement a program that includes risk-based verification procedures that enable the bank to form a reasonable belief that it knows the true identity of its customers."]}, {"qa_pairs": [{"context": "No context provided", "question": "What identification information is required from a U.S. person before opening an account?", "short_answers": ["Taxpayer Identification Number (TIN)"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "What identification documents are acceptable for a non-U.S. person when opening an account?", "short_answers": ["TIN", "Passport number and country of issuance", "Alien identification card number", "Government-issued document with photo"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "Is it permissible to collect an abbreviated form of identification data under the CIP Rule?", "short_answers": ["No abbreviated data collection", "Full TIN required"], "wikipage": null}], "wikipages": [{"title": "AML Topic 183", "url": "https://www.fdic.gov/news/financial-institution-letters/2024/fil24015a.pdf"}], "annotations": [{"knowledge": [], "long_answer": "For a U. For a non-U. No, the CIP Rule does not allow for an abbreviated collection of any data element."}, {"knowledge": [{"content": "For a U.S. person, the identification number must be a taxpayer identification number (TIN).", "wikipage": "AML Topic 183"}, {"content": "For a non-U.S. person, one or more of the following is required: a TIN; passport number and country of issuance, alien identification card number; or number and country of issuance of any other government-issued document evidencing nationality or residence and bearing a photograph or similar safeguard.", "wikipage": "AML Topic 183"}, {"content": "Additionally, the CIP Rule does not allow for an abbreviated collection of any data element. For example, the full TIN must be collected from the customer prior to opening the account.", "wikipage": "AML Topic 183"}], "long_answer": "For a U.S. person, the identification number required before opening an account is a taxpayer identification number (TIN).\n\nFor a non-U.S. person, acceptable identification documents include a TIN, passport number and country of issuance, alien identification card number, or any other government-issued document evidencing nationality or residence and bearing a photograph or similar safeguard.\n\nNo, the CIP Rule does not allow for an abbreviated collection of any data element. For instance, the full TIN must be collected from the customer prior to opening the account."}], "sample_id": "aml-topic-183-2", "question": "What identification information is required from a U.S. person before opening an account?", "docs": [{"id": "doc-aml-topic-183-2-1", "title": "AML Topic 183 Part 1", "text": "tification\nnumber.4 For a U.S. person, the identification number must be a taxpayer identification number\n(TIN). For a non-U.S. person, one or more of the following is required: a TIN; passport number\nand country of issuance, alien identification card number; or number and country of issuance of\nany other government-issued document evidencing nationality or residence and bearing a\nphotograph or similar safeguard.\nSummary\nThe customer information5 is required prior to account opening whether the", "score": 0.9, "summary": "tification number.4 For a U.S. person, the identification number must be a taxpayer identification number (TIN). For a non-U.S. person, one or more of...", "extraction": "For a U.S. person, the identification number must be a taxpayer identification number (TIN)."}], "answer": "For a U. For a non-U. No, the CIP Rule does not allow for an abbreviated collection of any data element.", "output": "For a U. S. person, the identification number must be a taxpayer identification number (TIN) [1].", "intermediate": ["[Retrieval]", "For a U.S. person, the identification number must be a taxpayer identification number (TIN)."]}, {"qa_pairs": [{"context": "No context provided", "question": "What documentation is required when opening an account for a foreign business without an identification number?", "short_answers": ["Alternative government-issued documentation", "Certifying existence of the business"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "What are the methods a bank can use to verify a customer's identity under its Customer Identification Program (CIP)?", "short_answers": ["Documentary methods", "Non-documentary methods", "Combination of both"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "Can a bank rely on another financial institution to perform its Customer Identification Program (CIP) procedures?", "short_answers": ["Rely on another financial institution", "Procedures of the bank\u2019s CIP"], "wikipage": null}], "wikipages": [{"title": "AML Topic 183", "url": "https://www.fdic.gov/news/financial-institution-letters/2024/fil24015a.pdf"}], "annotations": [{"knowledge": [], "long_answer": "When opening an account for a foreign business or enterprise that does not have an identification number, the bank must request alternative government-issued documentation certifying the existence of the business or enterprise. A bank\u2019s CIP must contain procedures for verifying the identity of the customer and describe when the bank will verify customer identity through documentary methods, non-documentary methods, or a combination of both. A bank\u2019s CIP may include procedures specifying when a bank will rely on the performance by another financial institution of the procedures of the bank\u2019s CIP with respect to any customer of the bank that is opening, or has opened, an account or has established a similar formal banking or business relationship."}, {"knowledge": [{"content": "When opening an account for a foreign business or enterprise that does not have an identification number, the bank must request alternative government-issued documentation certifying the existence of the business or enterprise.", "wikipage": "AML Topic 183"}, {"content": "A bank\u2019s CIP must also contain procedures for verifying the identity of the customer and describe when the bank will verify customer identity through documentary methods, non-documentary methods, or a combination of both.", "wikipage": "AML Topic 183"}, {"content": "A bank\u2019s CIP may include procedures specifying when a bank will rely on the performance by another financial institution of the procedures of the bank\u2019s CIP with respect to any customer of the bank that is opening, or has opened, an account or has established a similar formal banking or business relationship.", "wikipage": "AML Topic 183"}], "long_answer": "When opening an account for a foreign business or enterprise that does not have an identification number, the bank must request alternative government-issued documentation certifying the existence of the business or enterprise.\n\nA bank\u2019s CIP must contain procedures for verifying the identity of the customer and describe when the bank will verify customer identity through documentary methods, non-documentary methods, or a combination of both.\n\nA bank\u2019s CIP may include procedures specifying when a bank will rely on the performance by another financial institution of the procedures of the bank\u2019s CIP with respect to any customer of the bank that is opening, or has opened, an account or has established a similar formal banking or business relationship."}], "sample_id": "aml-topic-183-3", "question": "What documentation is required when opening an account for a foreign business without an identification number?", "docs": [{"id": "doc-aml-topic-183-3-2", "title": "AML Topic 183 Part 2", "text": "ank must\nrequest alternative government-issued documentation certifying the existence of the business or enterprise.\n5 31 CFR 1020.220(a)(2)(i)(A)\n6 31 CFR 1020.220(a)(2)(i)(C).\n1\nA bank\u2019s CIP must also contain procedures for verifying the identity of the customer and\ndescribe when the bank will verify customer identity through documentary methods, non-\ndocumentary methods, or a combination of both.7\nA bank\u2019s CIP may include procedures specifying when a bank will rely8 on the performance by\nanot", "score": 0.85, "summary": "ank must request alternative government-issued documentation certifying the existence of the business or enterprise. 5 31 CFR 1020.220(a)(2)(i)(A) 6 3...", "extraction": "ank must\nrequest alternative government-issued documentation certifying the existence of the business or enterprise.\n5 31 CFR 1020.220(a)(2)(i)(A)\n6 31 CFR 1020.220(a)(2)(i)(C).\n1\nA bank\u2019s CIP must al"}], "answer": "When opening an account for a foreign business or enterprise that does not have an identification number, the bank must request alternative government-issued documentation certifying the existence of the business or enterprise. A bank\u2019s CIP must contain procedures for verifying the identity of the customer and describe when the bank will verify customer identity through documentary methods, non-documentary methods, or a combination of both. A bank\u2019s CIP may include procedures specifying when a bank will rely on the performance by another financial institution of the procedures of the bank\u2019s CIP with respect to any customer of the bank that is opening, or has opened, an account or has established a similar formal banking or business relationship.", "output": "Answer: A bank's CIP must include procedures specifying when a bank will rely on the performance by an individual customer or a customer's representative of a CIP [1].", "intermediate": ["[Retrieval]", "Answer: A bank's CIP must include procedures specifying when a bank will rely on the performance by an individual customer or a customer's representative of a CIP."]}, {"qa_pairs": [{"context": "No context provided", "question": "What type of educational resources does the FDIC provide for stakeholders?", "short_answers": ["Financial education materials", "Data tools", "Documentation of laws and regulations", "Information on initiatives"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "Where can bankers find FDIC resources specifically designed for them?", "short_answers": ["Banker Resource Center"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "What kind of data tools does the FDIC offer?", "short_answers": ["Data tools for banking industry analysis"], "wikipage": null}], "wikipages": [{"title": "AML Topic 185", "url": "https://www.fdic.gov/news/financial-institution-letters/2005/fil3405.html"}], "annotations": [{"knowledge": [], "long_answer": "The FDIC provides a wealth of resources for consumers, bankers, analysts, and other stakeholders, including financial education materials, data tools, documentation of laws and regulations, and information on important initiatives. Bankers can find resources specifically designed for them in the Banker Resource Center provided by the FDIC. The FDIC offers data tools as part of its resources for stakeholders, which help in accessing and analyzing banking industry data."}, {"knowledge": [{"content": "The FDIC provides a wealth of resources for consumers, bankers, analysts, and other stakeholders. Browse our collection of financial education materials, data tools, documentation of laws and regulations, information on important initiatives, and more.", "wikipage": "AML Topic 185"}, {"content": "Additional FDIC Resources Consumer Resource Center Banker Resource Center Deposit Insurance Supervision & Examinations Laws & Regulations Resolutions Publications Forms Data Tools Community Banking Research Program International Seminars and Training", "wikipage": "AML Topic 185"}, {"content": "The FDIC provides a wealth of resources for consumers, bankers, analysts, and other stakeholders. Browse our collection of financial education materials, data tools, documentation of laws and regulations, information on important initiatives, and more.", "wikipage": "AML Topic 185"}], "long_answer": "The FDIC provides a wealth of resources for consumers, bankers, analysts, and other stakeholders, including financial education materials, data tools, documentation of laws and regulations, and information on important initiatives.\n\nBankers can find resources specifically designed for them in the Banker Resource Center provided by the FDIC.\n\nThe FDIC offers data tools as part of its resources for stakeholders, which help in accessing and analyzing banking industry data."}], "sample_id": "aml-topic-185-2", "question": "What type of educational resources does the FDIC provide for stakeholders?", "docs": [{"id": "doc-aml-topic-185-2-1", "title": "AML Topic 185 Part 1", "text": "Green\u2026\nPRESS RELEASE / January 17, 2025\nMillennium Bank, Des Plaines, Ill. Assumes All Deposits of Pulaski Savings\u2026\nPODCASTS / January 06, 2025\nSmall Business Lending\nResources\nToggle submenu\nResources\nThe FDIC provides a wealth of resources for consumers, bankers, analysts, and other stakeholders. Browse our collection of financial education materials, data tools, documentation of laws and regulations, information on important initiatives, and more.\nAdditional FDIC Resources\nConsumer Resource C", "score": 0.9, "summary": "Green\u2026 PRESS RELEASE / January 17, 2025 Millennium Bank, Des Plaines, Ill. Assumes All Deposits of Pulaski Savings\u2026 PODCASTS / January 06, 2025 Small ...", "extraction": "The FDIC provides a wealth of resources for consumers, bankers, analysts, and other stakeholders. Browse our collection of financial education materials, data tools, documentation of laws and regulations, information on important initiatives, and more."}], "answer": "The FDIC provides a wealth of resources for consumers, bankers, analysts, and other stakeholders, including financial education materials, data tools, documentation of laws and regulations, and information on important initiatives. Bankers can find resources specifically designed for them in the Banker Resource Center provided by the FDIC. The FDIC offers data tools as part of its resources for stakeholders, which help in accessing and analyzing banking industry data.", "output": "Answer: The FDIC provides a wealth of resources for consumers, bankers, analysts, and other stakeholders [1].", "intermediate": ["[Retrieval]", "Answer: The FDIC provides a wealth of resources for consumers, bankers, analysts, and other stakeholders."]}, {"qa_pairs": [{"context": "No context provided", "question": "What is the purpose of the interagency guidance issued on March 21, 2016, regarding prepaid cards?", "short_answers": ["Guidance on CIP requirements for prepaid cards", "Issued on March 21, 2016"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "Which agencies are involved in publishing the guidance on customer identification programs for prepaid cards?", "short_answers": ["FRB, FDIC, NCUA, OCC, FinCEN"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "To what types of prepaid cards does the CIP guidance apply?", "short_answers": ["Prepaid cards by third-party managers", "Cards for wages, healthcare, benefits"], "wikipage": null}], "wikipages": [{"title": "AML Topic 187", "url": "https://www.fdic.gov/sites/default/files/2024-03/fil16021a.pdf"}], "annotations": [{"knowledge": [], "long_answer": "The purpose of the interagency guidance issued on March 21, 2016, is to provide issuing banks with information on applying the customer identification program (CIP) requirements to prepaid cards, as part of the joint regulations implementing the CIP requirements of the USA PATRIOT Act. The agencies involved in publishing the guidance are the Board of Governors of the Federal Reserve System (FRB), the Federal Deposit Insurance Corporation (FDIC), the National Credit Union Administration (NCUA), the Office of the Comptroller of the Currency (OCC), and the U. The CIP guidance applies to prepaid cards that are sold and distributed by third-party program managers, as well as cards used to provide employee wages, healthcare, and government benefits."}, {"knowledge": [{"content": "The Board of Governors of the Federal Reserve System (FRB), the Federal Deposit Insurance Corporation (FDIC), the National Credit Union Administration (NCUA), the Office of the Comptroller of the Currency (OCC), and the U.S. Department of Treasury\u2019s Financial Crimes Enforcement Network (FinCEN) (collectively, the Agencies), are publishing this guidance to issuing banks1 on the application of the joint regulations implementing the customer identification program (CIP) requirements set forth in Section 326 of the USA PATRIOT Act2 (referred to in this guidance as the \u201cCIP rule\u201d)3 to their prepaid cards.", "wikipage": "AML Topic 187"}, {"content": "The Board of Governors of the Federal Reserve System (FRB), the Federal Deposit Insurance Corporation (FDIC), the National Credit Union Administration (NCUA), the Office of the Comptroller of the Currency (OCC), and the U.S. Department of Treasury\u2019s Financial Crimes Enforcement Network (FinCEN) (collectively, the Agencies), are publishing this guidance...", "wikipage": "AML Topic 187"}, {"content": "Prepaid cards include those that are sold and distributed by third-party program managers, 4 as well as cards that are used to provide employee wages, healthcare, and government benefits.", "wikipage": "AML Topic 187"}], "long_answer": "The purpose of the interagency guidance issued on March 21, 2016, is to provide issuing banks with information on applying the customer identification program (CIP) requirements to prepaid cards, as part of the joint regulations implementing the CIP requirements of the USA PATRIOT Act.\n\nThe agencies involved in publishing the guidance are the Board of Governors of the Federal Reserve System (FRB), the Federal Deposit Insurance Corporation (FDIC), the National Credit Union Administration (NCUA), the Office of the Comptroller of the Currency (OCC), and the U.S. Department of Treasury\u2019s Financial Crimes Enforcement Network (FinCEN).\n\nThe CIP guidance applies to prepaid cards that are sold and distributed by third-party program managers, as well as cards used to provide employee wages, healthcare, and government benefits."}], "sample_id": "aml-topic-187-1", "question": "What is the purpose of the interagency guidance issued on March 21, 2016, regarding prepaid cards?", "docs": [{"id": "doc-aml-topic-187-1-2", "title": "AML Topic 187 Part 2", "text": "ice of the\nComptroller of the Currency (OCC), and the U.S. Department of Treasury\u2019s Financial Crimes\nEnforcement Network (FinCEN) (collectively, the Agencies), are publishing this guidance to\nissuing banks1 on the application of the joint regulations implementing the customer\nidentification program (CIP) requirements set forth in Section 326 of the USA PATRIOT Act2\n(referred to in this guidance as the \u201cCIP rule\u201d)3 to their prepaid cards. Prepaid cards include\nthose that are sold and distributed", "score": 0.85, "summary": "ice of the Comptroller of the Currency (OCC), and the U.S. Department of Treasury\u2019s Financial Crimes Enforcement Network (FinCEN) (collectively, the A...", "extraction": "ice of the\nComptroller of the Currency (OCC), and the U.S. Department of Treasury\u2019s Financial Crimes\nEnforcement Network (FinCEN) (collectively, the Agencies), are publishing this guidance to\nissuing "}], "answer": "The purpose of the interagency guidance issued on March 21, 2016, is to provide issuing banks with information on applying the customer identification program (CIP) requirements to prepaid cards, as part of the joint regulations implementing the CIP requirements of the USA PATRIOT Act. The agencies involved in publishing the guidance are the Board of Governors of the Federal Reserve System (FRB), the Federal Deposit Insurance Corporation (FDIC), the National Credit Union Administration (NCUA), the Office of the Comptroller of the Currency (OCC), and the U. The CIP guidance applies to prepaid cards that are sold and distributed by third-party program managers, as well as cards used to provide employee wages, healthcare, and government benefits.", "output": "Answer: The purpose of the interagency guidance issued on March 21, 2016, regarding prepaid cards is to provide guidance to financial institutions on the application of the joint regulations implementing the customer identification program (CIP) requirements set forth in Section 326 of the USA PATRIOT Act to their prepaid cards [1].", "intermediate": ["[Retrieval]", "Answer: The purpose of the interagency guidance issued on March 21, 2016, regarding prepaid cards is to provide guidance to financial institutions on the application of the joint regulations implementing the customer identification program (CIP) requirements set forth in Section 326 of the USA PATRIOT Act to their prepaid cards."]}, {"qa_pairs": [{"context": "No context provided", "question": "What is the role of an issuing bank in the context of prepaid cards?", "short_answers": ["Authorizes prepaid card use", "Name on prepaid card", "Commercial bank or similar"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "Who is responsible for designing and managing a prepaid card program?", "short_answers": ["Third-party program manager", "Designs and manages program", "Contracts with bank"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "What are the regulatory references for issuing banks handling prepaid cards?", "short_answers": ["31 CFR 1010.100(d)", "31 USC 5318(l)", "12 CFR 21.21", "12 CFR 326"], "wikipage": null}], "wikipages": [{"title": "AML Topic 187", "url": "https://www.fdic.gov/sites/default/files/2024-03/fil16021a.pdf"}], "annotations": [{"knowledge": [], "long_answer": "The issuing bank is the entity that authorizes the use of the prepaid card. A third-party program manager is responsible for designing, managing, and operating a prepaid card program. The regulatory references for issuing banks handling prepaid cards include 31 CFR 1010."}, {"knowledge": [{"content": "The term \u201cissuing bank\u201d used in this guidance means the bank that authorizes use of the prepaid card. Usually the issuing bank is the bank that has its name printed on the back of a prepaid card. A bank includes any commercial bank, savings association, or credit union, and branch of a foreign bank.", "wikipage": "AML Topic 187"}, {"content": "For purposes of this guidance, a third-party program manager is a company that designs, manages and operates a prepaid card program and contracts with a bank to issue prepaid cards under the program and to process transactions conducted using those cards. The third-party program manager also provides customer service and card distribution (sales).", "wikipage": "AML Topic 187"}, {"content": "See 31 CFR 1010.100(d). 2 31 USC 5318(l). 3 68 Federal Register (FR) 25090 (May 9, 2003) codified at 31 CFR 1020.220 (Treasury); 12 CFR 21.21 (OCC); 12 CFR 208.62(b)(2) and 211.24(j)(2) (FRB); 12 CFR 326 (FDIC); and 12 CFR 748.2 (NCUA).", "wikipage": "AML Topic 187"}], "long_answer": "The issuing bank is the entity that authorizes the use of the prepaid card. It is usually the bank that has its name printed on the back of the prepaid card. An issuing bank can be a commercial bank, savings association, credit union, or a branch of a foreign bank.\n\nA third-party program manager is responsible for designing, managing, and operating a prepaid card program. They contract with a bank to issue prepaid cards under the program and process transactions conducted using those cards. They also provide customer service and card distribution.\n\nThe regulatory references for issuing banks handling prepaid cards include 31 CFR 1010.100(d) for the definition of a bank, and other regulations such as 31 USC 5318(l), 31 CFR 1020.220, 12 CFR 21.21, 12 CFR 208.62(b)(2) and 211.24(j)(2), 12 CFR 326, and 12 CFR 748.2."}], "sample_id": "aml-topic-187-2", "question": "What is the role of an issuing bank in the context of prepaid cards?", "docs": [{"id": "doc-aml-topic-187-2-1", "title": "AML Topic 187 Part 1", "text": "ders of certain prepaid cards issued by the bank.5\n1 The term \u201cissuing bank\u201d used in this guidance means the bank that authorizes use of the prepaid card. Usually the\nissuing bank is the bank that has its name printed on the back of a prepaid card. A bank includes any commercial\nbank, savings association, or credit union, and branch of a foreign bank. See 31 CFR 1010.100(d).\n2 31 USC 5318(l).\n3 68 Federal Register (FR) 25090 (May 9, 2003) codified at 31 CFR 1020.220 (Treasury); 12 CFR 21.21 (OCC", "score": 0.9, "summary": "ders of certain prepaid cards issued by the bank.5 1 The term \u201cissuing bank\u201d used in this guidance means the bank that authorizes use of the prepaid c...", "extraction": "The term \u201cissuing bank\u201d used in this guidance means the bank that authorizes use of the prepaid card. Usually the issuing bank is the bank that has its name printed on the back of a prepaid card. A bank includes any commercial bank, savings association, or credit union, and branch of a foreign bank."}], "answer": "The issuing bank is the entity that authorizes the use of the prepaid card. A third-party program manager is responsible for designing, managing, and operating a prepaid card program. The regulatory references for issuing banks handling prepaid cards include 31 CFR 1010.", "output": "Answer: The role of an issuing bank in the context of prepaid cards is to authorize use of the prepaid card [1].", "intermediate": ["[Retrieval]", "Answer: The role of an issuing bank in the context of prepaid cards is to authorize use of the prepaid card."]}, {"qa_pairs": [{"context": "No context provided", "question": "What are some common uses of prepaid cards by individuals and corporations?", "short_answers": ["Withdrawing cash", "Paying bills", "Purchasing goods and services"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "How do prepaid cards compare to traditional payment mechanisms?", "short_answers": ["Similar to checks", "Similar to debit and credit cards"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "In what ways do employers utilize prepaid cards for employee compensation?", "short_answers": ["Provide wages", "Provide compensation or benefits"], "wikipage": null}], "wikipages": [{"title": "AML Topic 187", "url": "https://www.fdic.gov/sites/default/files/2024-03/fil16021a.pdf"}], "annotations": [{"knowledge": [], "long_answer": "Prepaid cards are used by individuals and corporations for a variety of functions, including withdrawing cash at ATMs, paying bills, purchasing goods and services, and transferring funds to other cardholders. Prepaid cards can perform many of the same functions as traditional payment mechanisms like checks, debit cards tied to bank accounts, or credit cards. Employers use prepaid cards to provide wages and other compensation or benefits to their employees."}, {"knowledge": [{"content": "Prepaid cards have become mainstream financial products, widely used by individuals, corporations, and other private sector entities, as well as state, federal and local governments. General purpose prepaid cards can be used at multiple, unaffiliated merchants and can allow cardholders to perform a variety of functions, including those that have traditionally been conducted using other payment mechanisms, such as checks, debit cards tied to bank accounts, or credit cards. These functions include withdrawing cash at automated teller machines (ATMs), paying bills, purchasing goods and services, and transferring funds to other cardholders and receiving funds transfers. Employers use prepaid cards to provide wages and other compensation or benefits.", "wikipage": "AML Topic 187"}, {"content": "General purpose prepaid cards can be used at multiple, unaffiliated merchants and can allow cardholders to perform a variety of functions, including those that have traditionally been conducted using other payment mechanisms, such as checks, debit cards tied to bank accounts, or credit cards.", "wikipage": "AML Topic 187"}, {"content": "Employers use prepaid cards to provide wages and other compensation or benefits, such as pre-tax benefits.", "wikipage": "AML Topic 187"}], "long_answer": "Prepaid cards are used by individuals and corporations for a variety of functions, including withdrawing cash at ATMs, paying bills, purchasing goods and services, and transferring funds to other cardholders. They are also used by employers to provide wages and other compensation or benefits.\n\nPrepaid cards can perform many of the same functions as traditional payment mechanisms like checks, debit cards tied to bank accounts, or credit cards. They allow cardholders to withdraw cash, pay bills, purchase goods and services, and transfer funds to other cardholders.\n\nEmployers use prepaid cards to provide wages and other compensation or benefits to their employees. This can include pre-tax benefits or other forms of compensation."}], "sample_id": "aml-topic-187-3", "question": "What are some common uses of prepaid cards by individuals and corporations?", "docs": [{"id": "doc-aml-topic-187-3-2", "title": "AML Topic 187 Part 2", "text": "ividuals,\ncorporations, and other private sector entities, as well as state, federal and local governments.6\nGeneral purpose prepaid cards can be used at multiple, unaffiliated merchants and can allow\ncardholders to perform a variety of functions, including those that have traditionally been\nconducted using other payment mechanisms, such as checks, debit cards tied to bank accounts,\nor credit cards.7 These functions include withdrawing cash at automated teller machines (ATMs),\npaying bills, purc", "score": 0.85, "summary": "ividuals, corporations, and other private sector entities, as well as state, federal and local governments.6 General purpose prepaid cards can be used...", "extraction": "ividuals,\ncorporations, and other private sector entities, as well as state, federal and local governments.6\nGeneral purpose prepaid cards can be used at multiple, unaffiliated merchants and can allow"}], "answer": "Prepaid cards are used by individuals and corporations for a variety of functions, including withdrawing cash at ATMs, paying bills, purchasing goods and services, and transferring funds to other cardholders. Prepaid cards can perform many of the same functions as traditional payment mechanisms like checks, debit cards tied to bank accounts, or credit cards. Employers use prepaid cards to provide wages and other compensation or benefits to their employees.", "output": "Answer: Common uses of prepaid cards by individuals and corporations include payroll, incentives, and rewards, gift cards, and general purpose reloadable cards [1].", "intermediate": ["[Retrieval]", "Answer: Common uses of prepaid cards by individuals and corporations include payroll, incentives, and rewards, gift cards, and general purpose reloadable cards."]}, {"qa_pairs": [{"context": "No context provided", "question": "What is the purpose of the exemption granted by the Federal Banking Agencies regarding customer identification program requirements?", "short_answers": ["Facilitate insurance purchases", "Exemption from CIP requirements"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "Which entities are involved in granting the exemption from the customer identification program requirements?", "short_answers": ["OCC", "Federal Reserve", "FDIC", "NCUA", "FinCEN"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "What specific section of the USA PATRIOT Act does the exemption relate to?", "short_answers": ["Section 326", "31 U.S.C. \u00a7 5318(l)"], "wikipage": null}], "wikipages": [{"title": "AML Topic 188", "url": "https://www.fdic.gov/news/financial-institution-letters/2020/fil20095a.pdf"}], "annotations": [{"knowledge": [], "long_answer": "The exemption is granted to facilitate purchases of property and casualty insurance policies by allowing banks and their subsidiaries to extend loans without adhering to the customer identification program requirements set by section 326 of the USA PATRIOT Act. The entities involved are the Office of the Comptroller of the Currency (OCC), the Board of Governors of the Federal Reserve System (Federal Reserve), the Federal Deposit Insurance Corporation (FDIC), and the National Credit Union Administration (NCUA), collectively known as the Federal Banking Agencies, with the concurrence of the Financial Crimes Enforcement Network (FinCEN). The exemption relates to section 326 of the USA PATRIOT Act, specifically under 31 U."}, {"knowledge": [{"content": "Order granting an exemption from customer identification program requirements implementing section 326 of the USA PATRIOT Act, 31 U.S.C. \u00a7 5318(l), for loans extended by banks (and their subsidiaries) subject to the jurisdiction of the Federal Banking Agencies to all customers to facilitate purchases of property and casualty insurance policies.", "wikipage": "AML Topic 188"}, {"content": "By ORDER, under the authority set forth in 31 C.F.R. \u00a7 1020.220(b) implementing section 326(a) of the USA PATRIOT Act, 31 U.S.C. \u00a7 5318(l)(5), the Office of the Comptroller of the Currency (OCC), the Board of Governors of the Federal Reserve System (Federal Reserve), the Federal Deposit Insurance Corporation (FDIC), and the National Credit Union Administration (NCUA), collectively the Federal Banking Agencies (FBAs), with the concurrence of the Financial Crimes Enforcement Network (FinCEN), hereby grant an exemption...", "wikipage": "AML Topic 188"}, {"content": "Order granting an exemption from customer identification program requirements implementing section 326 of the USA PATRIOT Act, 31 U.S.C. \u00a7 5318(l)...", "wikipage": "AML Topic 188"}], "long_answer": "The exemption is granted to facilitate purchases of property and casualty insurance policies by allowing banks and their subsidiaries to extend loans without adhering to the customer identification program requirements set by section 326 of the USA PATRIOT Act.\n\nThe entities involved are the Office of the Comptroller of the Currency (OCC), the Board of Governors of the Federal Reserve System (Federal Reserve), the Federal Deposit Insurance Corporation (FDIC), and the National Credit Union Administration (NCUA), collectively known as the Federal Banking Agencies, with the concurrence of the Financial Crimes Enforcement Network (FinCEN).\n\nThe exemption relates to section 326 of the USA PATRIOT Act, specifically under 31 U.S.C. \u00a7 5318(l)."}], "sample_id": "aml-topic-188-1", "question": "What is the purpose of the exemption granted by the Federal Banking Agencies regarding customer identification program requirements?", "docs": [{"id": "doc-aml-topic-188-1-1", "title": "AML Topic 188 Part 1", "text": "ORDER\nOrder granting an exemption from customer identification program requirements\nimplementing section 326 of the USA PATRIOT Act, 31 U.S.C. \u00a7 5318(l), for loans\nextended by banks (and their subsidiaries) subject to the jurisdiction of the Federal\nBanking Agencies to all customers to facilitate purchases of property and casualty\ninsurance policies.\nIssue Date: October 5, 2020\nBy ORDER, under the authority set forth in 31 C.F.R. \u00a7 1020.220(b) implementing\nsection 326(a) of the USA PATRIOT Act,", "score": 0.9, "summary": "ORDER Order granting an exemption from customer identification program requirements implementing section 326 of the USA PATRIOT Act, 31 U.S.C. \u00a7 5318(...", "extraction": "Order granting an exemption from customer identification program requirements implementing section 326 of the USA PATRIOT Act, 31 U.S.C. \u00a7 5318(l), for loans extended by banks (and their subsidiaries) subject to the jurisdiction of the Federal Banking Agencies to all customers to facilitate purchases of property and casualty insurance policies."}], "answer": "The exemption is granted to facilitate purchases of property and casualty insurance policies by allowing banks and their subsidiaries to extend loans without adhering to the customer identification program requirements set by section 326 of the USA PATRIOT Act. The entities involved are the Office of the Comptroller of the Currency (OCC), the Board of Governors of the Federal Reserve System (Federal Reserve), the Federal Deposit Insurance Corporation (FDIC), and the National Credit Union Administration (NCUA), collectively known as the Federal Banking Agencies, with the concurrence of the Financial Crimes Enforcement Network (FinCEN). The exemption relates to section 326 of the USA PATRIOT Act, specifically under 31 U.", "output": "The purpose of the exemption granted by the Federal Banking Agencies regarding customer identification program requirements is to facilitate purchases of property and casualty insurance policies [1].", "intermediate": ["[Retrieval]", "The purpose of the exemption granted by the Federal Banking Agencies regarding customer identification program requirements is to facilitate purchases of property and casualty insurance policies."]}, {"qa_pairs": [{"context": "No context provided", "question": "What are premium finance loans in the context of the provided document?", "short_answers": ["Premium finance loans", "Loans related to insurance policies"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "Which regulatory bodies are involved in the CIP rules as per the document?", "short_answers": ["FinCEN", "OCC", "Federal Reserve", "FDIC", "NCUA"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "What types of insurance products does the ORDER not apply to according to the document?", "short_answers": ["Life insurance policies", "Annuity contracts", "Other insurance products"], "wikipage": null}], "wikipages": [{"title": "AML Topic 188", "url": "https://www.fdic.gov/news/financial-institution-letters/2020/fil20095a.pdf"}], "annotations": [{"knowledge": [], "long_answer": "Premium finance loans refer to loans related to insurance policies, specifically mentioned in the context of the document. The regulatory bodies involved in the CIP rules include FinCEN, the OCC, the Federal Reserve, the FDIC, and the NCUA. The ORDER does not apply to life insurance policies, annuity contracts, or any other insurance product with certain features."}, {"knowledge": [{"content": "insurance policies4 (hereinafter referred to as premium finance loans or premium finance lending).", "wikipage": "AML Topic 188"}, {"content": "1 31 C.F.R. \u00a7 1020.220 (FinCEN); 12 C.F.R. \u00a7 21.21(c)(2) (OCC); 12 C.F.R. \u00a7\u00a7 208.63(b)(2) and 211.24(j)(2) (Federal Reserve); 12 C.F.R. \u00a7 326.8(b)(2) (FDIC); and 12 C.F.R. \u00a7 748.2(b)(2) (NCUA) (collectively, the CIP rules).", "wikipage": "AML Topic 188"}, {"content": "4 This ORDER does not apply to life insurance policies, annuity contracts or any other insurance product with features of cas", "wikipage": "AML Topic 188"}], "long_answer": "Premium finance loans refer to loans related to insurance policies, specifically mentioned in the context of the document.\n\nThe regulatory bodies involved in the CIP rules include FinCEN, the OCC, the Federal Reserve, the FDIC, and the NCUA.\n\nThe ORDER does not apply to life insurance policies, annuity contracts, or any other insurance product with certain features."}], "sample_id": "aml-topic-188-2", "question": "What are premium finance loans in the context of the provided document?", "docs": [{"id": "doc-aml-topic-188-2-1", "title": "AML Topic 188 Part 1", "text": "y\ninsurance policies4 (hereinafter referred to as premium finance loans or premium finance\nlending).\nBackground\nBy letter dated September 20, 2016, and by letter dated March 27, 2017, a consortium of\nbanks5 submitted a request to the FBAs and FinCEN for an exemption or interpretation\n1 31 C.F.R. \u00a7 1020.220 (FinCEN); 12 C.F.R. \u00a7 21.21(c)(2) (OCC); 12 C.F.R. \u00a7\u00a7 208.63(b)(2) and\n211.24(j)(2) (Federal Reserve); 12 C.F.R. \u00a7 326.8(b)(2) (FDIC); and 12 C.F.R. \u00a7 748.2(b)(2) (NCUA)\n(collectively, the CIP", "score": 0.9, "summary": "y insurance policies4 (hereinafter referred to as premium finance loans or premium finance lending). Background By letter dated September 20, 2016, an...", "extraction": "insurance policies4 (hereinafter referred to as premium finance loans or premium finance lending)."}], "answer": "Premium finance loans refer to loans related to insurance policies, specifically mentioned in the context of the document. The regulatory bodies involved in the CIP rules include FinCEN, the OCC, the Federal Reserve, the FDIC, and the NCUA. The ORDER does not apply to life insurance policies, annuity contracts, or any other insurance product with certain features.", "output": "In the context of the provided document, premium finance loans are a type of loan that is secured by the borrowers' insurance policies [1].", "intermediate": ["[Retrieval]", "In the context of the provided document, premium finance loans are a type of loan that is secured by the borrowers' insurance policies."]}, {"qa_pairs": [{"context": "No context provided", "question": "What is the significance of the exemption granted by the FBAs and FinCEN regarding CIP rules for insurance premium finance lending?", "short_answers": ["Exemption from CIP for premium finance lending", "Low risk of money laundering"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "Which financial institutions were involved in the consortium related to the CIP rule exemption for insurance premium finance lending?", "short_answers": ["Pacific Enterprise Bank", "BankDirect Capital Finance", "Premium Assignment Corporation", "First Insurance Funding Corp.", "AFCO Credit Corporation", "Metabank"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "Why did the Request Letters argue against applying CIP rules to insurance premium finance lending?", "short_answers": ["Low risk of money laundering", "No need for CIP rules"], "wikipage": null}], "wikipages": [{"title": "AML Topic 188", "url": "https://www.fdic.gov/news/financial-institution-letters/2020/fil20095a.pdf"}], "annotations": [{"knowledge": [], "long_answer": "The exemption granted by the FBAs and FinCEN means that banks and their subsidiaries, subject to the FBAs' jurisdiction, are not required to apply the Customer Identification Program (CIP) rules to loans extended to commercial customers specifically for premium finance lending. The consortium involved several financial institutions, including Pacific Enterprise Bank, BankDirect Capital Finance (a Division of Texas Capital Bank, N. The Request Letters argued against applying CIP rules to insurance premium finance lending because they asserted that this activity presents a low risk of money laundering."}, {"knowledge": [{"content": "On September 27, 2018, the FBAs and FinCEN issued an Order granting an exemption from the requirements of the CIP rules implementing section 326 of the USA PATRIOT Act, 31 U.S.C. \u00a7 5318(l), for loans extended by banks (and their subsidiaries) subject to the FBAs\u2019 jurisdiction only to commercial customers to facilitate premium finance lending.", "wikipage": "AML Topic 188"}, {"content": "The consortium included the following banks: Pacific Enterprise Bank; BankDirect Capital Finance, LLC \u2013 BankDirect Capital Finance, a Division of Texas Capital Bank, N.A.; Premium Assignment Corporation, a subsidiary of SunTrust Bank; First Insurance Funding Corp.; AFCO Credit Corporation \u2013 AFCO Acceptance Corporation- Prime Rate Premium Finance Corp., subsidiaries of Branch Banking & Trust Company (BB&T); and Metabank (its AFS/IBEX division).", "wikipage": "AML Topic 188"}, {"content": "The Request Letters asserted that there is no need to apply the CIP rules to insurance premium finance lending because this activity presents a low risk of money laundering.", "wikipage": "AML Topic 188"}], "long_answer": "The exemption granted by the FBAs and FinCEN means that banks and their subsidiaries, subject to the FBAs' jurisdiction, are not required to apply the Customer Identification Program (CIP) rules to loans extended to commercial customers specifically for premium finance lending. This decision was based on the assessment that insurance premium finance lending presents a low risk of money laundering.\n\nThe consortium involved several financial institutions, including Pacific Enterprise Bank, BankDirect Capital Finance (a Division of Texas Capital Bank, N.A.), Premium Assignment Corporation (a subsidiary of SunTrust Bank), First Insurance Funding Corp., AFCO Credit Corporation (subsidiaries of Branch Banking & Trust Company), and Metabank (its AFS/IBEX division).\n\nThe Request Letters argued against applying CIP rules to insurance premium finance lending because they asserted that this activity presents a low risk of money laundering."}], "sample_id": "aml-topic-188-3", "question": "What is the significance of the exemption granted by the FBAs and FinCEN regarding CIP rules for insurance premium finance lending?", "docs": [{"id": "doc-aml-topic-188-3-2", "title": "AML Topic 188 Part 2", "text": "of the CIP rules to insurance premium finance lending (the\n\u201cRequest Letters\u201d).6 The Request Letters asserted that there is no need to apply the CIP\nrules to insurance premium finance lending because this activity presents a low risk of\nmoney laundering. On September 27, 2018, the FBAs and FinCEN issued an Order\ngranting an exemption from the requirements of the CIP rules implementing section 326\nof the USA PATRIOT Act, 31 U.S.C. \u00a7 5318(l), for loans extended by banks (and their\nsubsidiaries) su", "score": 0.85, "summary": "of the CIP rules to insurance premium finance lending (the \u201cRequest Letters\u201d).6 The Request Letters asserted that there is no need to apply the CIP ru...", "extraction": "of the CIP rules to insurance premium finance lending (the\n\u201cRequest Letters\u201d).6 The Request Letters asserted that there is no need to apply the CIP\nrules to insurance premium finance lending because t"}], "answer": "The exemption granted by the FBAs and FinCEN means that banks and their subsidiaries, subject to the FBAs' jurisdiction, are not required to apply the Customer Identification Program (CIP) rules to loans extended to commercial customers specifically for premium finance lending. The consortium involved several financial institutions, including Pacific Enterprise Bank, BankDirect Capital Finance (a Division of Texas Capital Bank, N. The Request Letters argued against applying CIP rules to insurance premium finance lending because they asserted that this activity presents a low risk of money laundering.", "output": "Answer: The FBAs and FinCEN granted an exemption from the requirements of the CIP rules implementing section 326 of the USA PATRIOT Act, 31 U. S. C. \u00a7 5318(l), for loans extended by banks (and their subsidiaries) subject to their supervision [1].", "intermediate": ["[Retrieval]", "Answer: The FBAs and FinCEN granted an exemption from the requirements of the CIP rules implementing section 326 of the USA PATRIOT Act, 31 U.S.C. \u00a7 5318(l), for loans extended by banks (and their subsidiaries) subject to their supervision."]}, {"qa_pairs": [{"context": "No context provided", "question": "What are the key stages in the third-party relationship life cycle for community banks?", "short_answers": ["Planning", "Due Diligence and Third-Party Selection", "Contract Negotiation"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "What is the importance of due diligence in third-party selection for community banks?", "short_answers": ["Ensure standards", "Mitigate risks"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "Why is contract negotiation a critical step in managing third-party risks for community banks?", "short_answers": ["Defines relationship terms", "Mitigates risks"], "wikipage": null}], "wikipages": [{"title": "AML Topic 189", "url": "https://www.fdic.gov/resources/bankers/third-party-relationships/third-party-risk-management-guide.pdf"}], "annotations": [{"knowledge": [], "long_answer": "The key stages in the third-party relationship life cycle for community banks include Planning, Due Diligence and Third-Party Selection, and Contract Negotiation. Due diligence is crucial in third-party selection for community banks as it ensures that the selected third parties meet the necessary standards and do not pose significant risks to the bank. Contract negotiation is a critical step in managing third-party risks for community banks because it defines the terms of the relationship, including responsibilities, performance standards, and compliance requirements, which help in mitigating risks."}, {"knowledge": [{"content": "Third-Party Relationship Life Cycle  . . . . . . . . . . . . . .  . . .  . . . . .  . . .  . . . . .  . . .  . . . 4\nPlanning  . . . . . . . . . . . . . .  .  . . .  . . . . . .  .  . . . . . . .  .  . . . . . . .  .  . . . . . . . .  . . . . . . . . 5\nDue Diligence and Third-Party Selection  . . . . . . . . . . . . . .  . . .  . . . . .  . . .  . . . . .  . . .  . . . 8\nContract Negotiation .  . . . . . . . . . . . . . . .  . . . . . .  . .  . . . . .  .", "wikipage": "AML Topic 189"}, {"content": "Due Diligence and Third-Party Selection  . . . . . . . . . . . . . .  . . .  . . . . .  . . .  . . . . .  . . .  . . . 8", "wikipage": "AML Topic 189"}, {"content": "Contract Negotiation .  . . . . . . . . . . . . . . .  . . . . . .  . .  . . . . .  .", "wikipage": "AML Topic 189"}], "long_answer": "The key stages in the third-party relationship life cycle for community banks include Planning, Due Diligence and Third-Party Selection, and Contract Negotiation.\n\nDue diligence is crucial in third-party selection for community banks as it ensures that the selected third parties meet the necessary standards and do not pose significant risks to the bank.\n\nContract negotiation is a critical step in managing third-party risks for community banks because it defines the terms of the relationship, including responsibilities, performance standards, and compliance requirements, which help in mitigating risks."}], "sample_id": "aml-topic-189-1", "question": "What are the key stages in the third-party relationship life cycle for community banks?", "docs": [{"id": "doc-aml-topic-189-1-2", "title": "AML Topic 189 Part 2", "text": ". . . . . . . . . .  . . . . .  . .  . . . . . . .  .  . . . . . . .  .  . . . . . . .  . . . . . . . . 2\nRisk Management  . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . .  . . .  . . . . . .  .  . . . . . . .  .  . . . . . . .  .  . . . . . . . . 3\nThird-Party Relationship Life Cycle  . . . . . . . . . . . . . .  . . .  . . . . .  . . .  . . . . .  . . .  . . . 4\nPlanning  . . . . . . . . . . . . . . .  .  . . .  . . . . . .  .  . . . . . . .  .  . . . . . . .  .  . . . . . . . .  . . . . . . . . 5\nDue Diligen", "score": 0.85, "summary": ". . . . . . . . . .  . . . . .  . .  . . . . . . .  .  . . . . . . .  .  . . . . . . .  . . . . . . . . 2 Risk Management  . . . . . . . . . . . . . ....", "extraction": ". . . . . . . . . .  . . . . .  . .  . . . . . . .  .  . . . . . . .  .  . . . . . . .  . . . . . . . . 2\nRisk Management  . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . .  . . .  . . . . . .  .  . . . . . . .  .  . ."}], "answer": "The key stages in the third-party relationship life cycle for community banks include Planning, Due Diligence and Third-Party Selection, and Contract Negotiation. Due diligence is crucial in third-party selection for community banks as it ensures that the selected third parties meet the necessary standards and do not pose significant risks to the bank. Contract negotiation is a critical step in managing third-party risks for community banks because it defines the terms of the relationship, including responsibilities, performance standards, and compliance requirements, which help in mitigating risks.", "output": "Answer: The key stages in the third-party relationship life cycle for community banks are: Planning, Due Diligence, Monitoring, and Termination [1].", "intermediate": ["[Retrieval]", "Answer: The key stages in the third-party relationship life cycle for community banks are: Planning, Due Diligence, Monitoring, and Termination."]}, {"qa_pairs": [{"context": "No context provided", "question": "What are some benefits for community banks in engaging with third parties?", "short_answers": ["Access to new technologies", "Risk-management tools", "Human capital", "New delivery channels", "Products and services", "New markets"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "What is a potential downside of community banks relying on third parties?", "short_answers": ["Reduced operational control", "Introduction of new risks"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "Why do community banks need to manage third-party risk carefully?", "short_answers": ["Reduced operational control", "Introduction of new risks", "Effective competition and response"], "wikipage": null}], "wikipages": [{"title": "AML Topic 189", "url": "https://www.fdic.gov/resources/bankers/third-party-relationships/third-party-risk-management-guide.pdf"}], "annotations": [{"knowledge": [], "long_answer": "Community banks engage with third parties to access new technologies, risk-management tools, human capital, delivery channels, products, services, and markets. A community bank\u2019s reliance on third parties reduces its direct operational control over activities and may introduce new risks. Managing third-party risk is crucial for community banks because their reliance on third parties can reduce direct operational control and introduce new risks, which could affect their ability to effectively compete and respond to changes in the financial services landscape."}, {"knowledge": [{"content": "Community banks engage with third parties to compete in and respond to an evolving financial services landscape. Third-party relationships can offer community banks access to new technologies, risk-management tools, human capital, delivery channels, products, services, and markets.", "wikipage": "AML Topic 189"}, {"content": "A community bank\u2019s reliance on third parties, however, reduces its direct operational control over activities and may introduce new risks.", "wikipage": "AML Topic 189"}, {"content": "Community banks engage with third parties to compete in and respond to an evolving financial services landscape. A community bank\u2019s reliance on third parties, however, reduces its direct operational control over activities and may introduce new risks.", "wikipage": "AML Topic 189"}], "long_answer": "Community banks engage with third parties to access new technologies, risk-management tools, human capital, delivery channels, products, services, and markets. These relationships help them compete in and respond to an evolving financial services landscape.\n\nA community bank\u2019s reliance on third parties reduces its direct operational control over activities and may introduce new risks.\n\nManaging third-party risk is crucial for community banks because their reliance on third parties can reduce direct operational control and introduce new risks, which could affect their ability to effectively compete and respond to changes in the financial services landscape."}], "sample_id": "aml-topic-189-2", "question": "What are some benefits for community banks in engaging with third parties?", "docs": [{"id": "doc-aml-topic-189-2-2", "title": "AML Topic 189 Part 2", "text": ".  .  . . . . . . .  .  . . . . . . .  .  . . . . . . . .  . . . . . . . 23\nGovernment Resources for Community Banking Organizations\u2019\nThird-Party Risk Management  . . . . . . . . . . . . . .  . . .  . . . . .  . . .  . . . . .  . . .  . . . . .  . . .  . 23\n1\nIntroduction\nCommunity banks engage with third parties to compete in and respond to an evolving financial\nservices landscape. Third-party relationships can offer community banks access to new technolo-\ngies, risk-management tools, human ca", "score": 0.85, "summary": ".  .  . . . . . . .  .  . . . . . . .  .  . . . . . . . .  . . . . . . . 23 Government Resources for Community Banking Organizations\u2019 Third-Party Risk...", "extraction": ".  .  . . . . . . .  .  . . . . . . .  .  . . . . . . . .  . . . . . . . 23\nGovernment Resources for Community Banking Organizations\u2019\nThird-Party Risk Management  . . . . . . . . . . . . . .  . . .  ."}], "answer": "Community banks engage with third parties to access new technologies, risk-management tools, human capital, delivery channels, products, services, and markets. A community bank\u2019s reliance on third parties reduces its direct operational control over activities and may introduce new risks. Managing third-party risk is crucial for community banks because their reliance on third parties can reduce direct operational control and introduce new risks, which could affect their ability to effectively compete and respond to changes in the financial services landscape.", "output": "Answer: Some benefits for community banks in engaging with third parties include access to new technologies, risk-management tools, and human capital [1].", "intermediate": ["[Retrieval]", "Answer: Some benefits for community banks in engaging with third parties include access to new technologies, risk-management tools, and human capital."]}, {"qa_pairs": [{"context": "No context provided", "question": "What responsibilities does a community bank have when engaging third-party services in terms of compliance?", "short_answers": ["Retains compliance responsibility", "Must operate safely and soundly"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "How should community banks manage the risks associated with third-party relationships?", "short_answers": ["Identify, assess, monitor, control risks", "Ensure compliance with laws"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "Can a community bank delegate its risk management responsibilities to a third party?", "short_answers": ["Cannot delegate full responsibility", "Must employ effective practices"], "wikipage": null}], "wikipages": [{"title": "AML Topic 189", "url": "https://www.fdic.gov/resources/bankers/third-party-relationships/third-party-risk-management-guide.pdf"}], "annotations": [{"knowledge": [], "long_answer": "When a community bank engages third-party services, it retains the responsibility to operate in a safe and sound manner and to comply with applicable legal and regulatory requirements, including consumer protection laws and regulations. Community banks should appropriately identify, assess, monitor, and control the risks associated with third-party relationships to ensure activities are performed in a safe and sound manner and in compliance with applicable laws and regulations. A community bank may engage an external party to conduct aspects of its third-party risk management; however, the bank cannot abrogate its responsibility to employ effective risk-management practices."}, {"knowledge": [{"content": "Engaging a third party does not diminish or remove a bank\u2019s responsibility to operate in a safe and sound manner and to comply with applicable legal and regulatory requirements, including consumer protection laws and regulations, just as if the bank were to perform the service or activity itself.", "wikipage": "AML Topic 189"}, {"content": "Due to the varied risks associated with third-party relationships, it is important for community banks to appropriately identify, assess, monitor, and control these risks as well as ensure that activities are performed in a safe and sound manner and in compliance with applicable laws and regulations.", "wikipage": "AML Topic 189"}, {"content": "A community bank may engage an external party to conduct aspects of its third-party risk management. However, the bank cannot abrogate its responsibility to employ effective risk-management practices.", "wikipage": "AML Topic 189"}], "long_answer": "When a community bank engages third-party services, it retains the responsibility to operate in a safe and sound manner and to comply with applicable legal and regulatory requirements, including consumer protection laws and regulations. Engaging a third party does not diminish or remove these responsibilities.\n\nCommunity banks should appropriately identify, assess, monitor, and control the risks associated with third-party relationships to ensure activities are performed in a safe and sound manner and in compliance with applicable laws and regulations.\n\nA community bank may engage an external party to conduct aspects of its third-party risk management; however, the bank cannot abrogate its responsibility to employ effective risk-management practices."}], "sample_id": "aml-topic-189-3", "question": "What responsibilities does a community bank have when engaging third-party services in terms of compliance?", "docs": [{"id": "doc-aml-topic-189-3-2", "title": "AML Topic 189 Part 2", "text": "ect\nconsumers (such as fair lending laws and prohibitions against unfair, deceptive, or abusive acts\nor practices) and those addressing financial crimes (such as fraud and money laundering).\nEngaging a third party does not diminish or remove a bank\u2019s responsibility to operate in a safe\nand sound manner and to comply with applicable legal and regulatory requirements, including\nconsumer protection laws and regulations, just as if the bank were to perform the service or\nactivity itself. A community", "score": 0.85, "summary": "ect consumers (such as fair lending laws and prohibitions against unfair, deceptive, or abusive acts or practices) and those addressing financial crim...", "extraction": "ect\nconsumers (such as fair lending laws and prohibitions against unfair, deceptive, or abusive acts\nor practices) and those addressing financial crimes (such as fraud and money laundering).\nEngaging "}], "answer": "When a community bank engages third-party services, it retains the responsibility to operate in a safe and sound manner and to comply with applicable legal and regulatory requirements, including consumer protection laws and regulations. Community banks should appropriately identify, assess, monitor, and control the risks associated with third-party relationships to ensure activities are performed in a safe and sound manner and in compliance with applicable laws and regulations. A community bank may engage an external party to conduct aspects of its third-party risk management; however, the bank cannot abrogate its responsibility to employ effective risk-management practices.", "output": "Answer: A community bank has the responsibility to ensure that third-party services they engage in comply with all applicable laws and regulations [1].", "intermediate": ["[Retrieval]", "Answer: A community bank has the responsibility to ensure that third-party services they engage in comply with all applicable laws and regulations."]}, {"qa_pairs": [{"context": "No context provided", "question": "What type of data and research does the FDIC provide to help understand banking performance?", "short_answers": ["Quarterly banking profiles", "State banking performance data", "FDIC National Survey of Unbanked and Underbanked Households"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "How can one stay updated with the latest FDIC announcements and policy changes for banks?", "short_answers": ["FDIC announcements", "Policy changes", "Conferences and events"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "What are some of the events where FDIC-insured institutions reported their financial performance?", "short_answers": ["Return on assets of 1.11 percent", "Net income of $65.4 billion", "Net income of $71.5 billion"], "wikipage": null}], "wikipages": [{"title": "AML Topic 191", "url": "https://www.fdic.gov/news/financial-institution-letters/2023/fil23067.html"}], "annotations": [{"knowledge": [], "long_answer": "The FDIC provides industry research including quarterly banking profiles, working papers, and state banking performance data. To stay updated with the latest FDIC announcements and policy changes for banks, one can follow the FDIC's regular updates on news and activities, read their speeches and testimony on the latest banking issues, and learn about policy changes. Some events where FDIC-insured institutions reported their financial performance include the report of return on assets of 1."}, {"knowledge": [{"content": "ndustry research, including quarterly banking profiles, working papers, and state banking performance data. Browse our extensive research tools and reports. More FDIC Analysis Center for Financial Research Consumer Research FDIC National Survey of Unbanked and Underbanked Households Quarterly Banking Profile FDIC Academic Challenge FDIC Banking Issues in Focus Annual Risk Review", "wikipage": "AML Topic 191"}, {"content": "The FDIC publishes regular updates on news and activities. Keep up with FDIC announcements, read speeches and testimony on the latest banking issues, learn about policy changes for banks, and get the details on upcoming conferences and events.", "wikipage": "AML Topic 191"}, {"content": "EVENT / February 25, 2025 FDIC-Insured Institutions Reported Return on Assets of 1.11 Percent and Net Inc\u2026 EVENT / December 12, 2024 FDIC-Insured Institutions Reported Net Income of $65.4 Billion EVENT / September 05, 2024 FDIC-Insured Institutions Reported Net Income of $71.5 Billion", "wikipage": "AML Topic 191"}], "long_answer": "The FDIC provides industry research including quarterly banking profiles, working papers, and state banking performance data. They also offer extensive research tools and reports such as the FDIC National Survey of Unbanked and Underbanked Households, Quarterly Banking Profile, and Annual Risk Review.\n\nTo stay updated with the latest FDIC announcements and policy changes for banks, one can follow the FDIC's regular updates on news and activities, read their speeches and testimony on the latest banking issues, and learn about policy changes. Additionally, details on upcoming conferences and events are available.\n\nSome events where FDIC-insured institutions reported their financial performance include the report of return on assets of 1.11 percent on February 25, 2025, a net income of $65.4 billion on December 12, 2024, and a net income of $71.5 billion on September 05, 2024."}], "sample_id": "aml-topic-191-3", "question": "What type of data and research does the FDIC provide to help understand banking performance?", "docs": [{"id": "doc-aml-topic-191-3-2", "title": "AML Topic 191 Part 2", "text": "ember 12, 2024\nFDIC-Insured Institutions Reported Net Income of $65.4 Billion\nEVENT / September 05, 2024\nFDIC-Insured Institutions Reported Net Income of $71.5 Billion\nEVENT / May 29, 2024\nQuarterly Banking Profile for First Quarter 2024\nNews\nToggle submenu\nNews\nThe FDIC publishes regular updates on news and activities. Keep up with FDIC announcements, read speeches and testimony on the latest banking issues, learn about policy changes for banks, and get the details on upcoming conferences and e", "score": 0.85, "summary": "ember 12, 2024 FDIC-Insured Institutions Reported Net Income of $65.4 Billion EVENT / September 05, 2024 FDIC-Insured Institutions Reported Net Income...", "extraction": "ember 12, 2024\nFDIC-Insured Institutions Reported Net Income of $65.4 Billion\nEVENT / September 05, 2024\nFDIC-Insured Institutions Reported Net Income of $71.5 Billion\nEVENT / May 29, 2024\nQuarterly B"}], "answer": "The FDIC provides industry research including quarterly banking profiles, working papers, and state banking performance data. To stay updated with the latest FDIC announcements and policy changes for banks, one can follow the FDIC's regular updates on news and activities, read their speeches and testimony on the latest banking issues, and learn about policy changes. Some events where FDIC-insured institutions reported their financial performance include the report of return on assets of 1.", "output": "Answer: The FDIC provides data and research to help understand banking performance, including the FDIC Quarterly Banking Profile, which provides a detailed analysis of the performance of insured banks and thrifts, and the FDIC Summary of Deposits, which provides detailed information on the size and composition of deposits at insured institutions [1].", "intermediate": ["[Retrieval]", "Answer: The FDIC provides data and research to help understand banking performance, including the FDIC Quarterly Banking Profile, which provides a detailed analysis of the performance of insured banks and thrifts, and the FDIC Summary of Deposits, which provides detailed information on the size and composition of deposits at insured institutions."]}, {"qa_pairs": [{"context": "No context provided", "question": "What approach does the FDIC recommend for assessing customer relationships?", "short_answers": ["Risk-based approach", "Assess individual customer relationships"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "To whom does the FDIC's statement on providing banking services apply?", "short_answers": ["FDIC-supervised institutions", "Institutions with assets under $1 billion"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "What role does the FDIC see for insured depository institutions in their communities?", "short_answers": ["Serve communities", "Importance of services provided"], "wikipage": null}], "wikipages": [{"title": "AML Topic 192", "url": "https://www.fdic.gov/news/financial-institution-letters/2015/fil15005.pdf"}], "annotations": [{"knowledge": [], "long_answer": "The FDIC recommends that institutions take a risk-based approach in assessing individual customer relationships rather than declining to provide banking services to entire categories of customers. The FDIC's statement applies to all FDIC-supervised institutions, specifically those with total assets under $1 billion. The FDIC encourages insured depository institutions to serve their communities and recognizes the importance of the services they provide."}, {"knowledge": [{"content": "The FDIC is issuing this statement to encourage institutions to take a risk-based approach in assessing individual customer relationships rather than declining to provide banking services to entire categories of customers.", "wikipage": "AML Topic 192"}, {"content": "Statement of Applicability to Institutions with Total Assets Under $1 Billion: This Financial Institution Letter applies to all FDIC-supervised institutions.", "wikipage": "AML Topic 192"}, {"content": "The FDIC encourages insured depository institutions to serve their communities and recognizes the importance of services they provide.", "wikipage": "AML Topic 192"}], "long_answer": "The FDIC recommends that institutions take a risk-based approach in assessing individual customer relationships rather than declining to provide banking services to entire categories of customers.\n\nThe FDIC's statement applies to all FDIC-supervised institutions, specifically those with total assets under $1 billion.\n\nThe FDIC encourages insured depository institutions to serve their communities and recognizes the importance of the services they provide."}], "sample_id": "aml-topic-192-1", "question": "What approach does the FDIC recommend for assessing customer relationships?", "docs": [{"id": "doc-aml-topic-192-1-1", "title": "AML Topic 192 Part 1", "text": "Financial Institution Letter\nFIL-5-2015\nFederal Deposit Insurance Corporation January 28, 2015\n550 17th Street NW, Washington, D.C. 20429-9990\nStatement on Providing Banking Services\nCorrected on February 12, 2015, to correct the telephone number for the FDIC\u2019s Inspector General.\nSummary: The FDIC is issuing this statement to encourage institutions to take a risk-based approach in\nassessing individual customer relationships rather than declining to provide banking services to entire\ncategories o", "score": 0.9, "summary": "Financial Institution Letter FIL-5-2015 Federal Deposit Insurance Corporation January 28, 2015 550 17th Street NW, Washington, D.C. 20429-9990 Stateme...", "extraction": "The FDIC is issuing this statement to encourage institutions to take a risk-based approach in assessing individual customer relationships rather than declining to provide banking services to entire categories of customers."}], "answer": "The FDIC recommends that institutions take a risk-based approach in assessing individual customer relationships rather than declining to provide banking services to entire categories of customers. The FDIC's statement applies to all FDIC-supervised institutions, specifically those with total assets under $1 billion. The FDIC encourages insured depository institutions to serve their communities and recognizes the importance of the services they provide.", "output": "Answer: The FDIC recommends a risk-based approach for assessing customer relationships [1].", "intermediate": ["[Retrieval]", "Answer: The FDIC recommends a risk-based approach for assessing customer relationships."]}, {"qa_pairs": [{"context": "No context provided", "question": "What is expected of financial institutions regarding risk assessment of individual customers?", "short_answers": ["Assess risks on a case-by-case basis", "Implement controls based on risk"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "Where can financial institutions access FDIC Financial Institution Letters (FILs)?", "short_answers": ["Access FILs online at FDIC's website", "Paper copies available from FDIC's Public Information Center"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "How can one subscribe to receive FDIC Financial Institution Letters electronically?", "short_answers": ["Visit FDIC subscription page", "Subscribe electronically"], "wikipage": null}], "wikipages": [{"title": "AML Topic 192", "url": "https://www.fdic.gov/news/financial-institution-letters/2015/fil15005.pdf"}], "annotations": [{"knowledge": [], "long_answer": "Institutions are expected to assess the risks posed by an individual customer on a case-by-case basis and to implement controls to manage the relationship commensurate with the risks associated with each customer. FDIC Financial Institution Letters (FILs) can be accessed from the FDIC's website at https://www. To receive FDIC Financial Institution Letters electronically, one can visit http://www."}, {"knowledge": [{"content": "Institutions are expected to assess the risks posed by an individual customer on a case-by-case basis and to implement controls to manage the relationship commensurate with the risks associated with each customer.", "wikipage": "AML Topic 192"}, {"content": "FDIC Financial Institution Letters (FILs) may be accessed from the FDIC's Web site at https://www.fdic.gov/news/news/financial/2015/. Paper copies may be obtained through the FDIC's Public Information Center, 3501 Fairfax Drive, E-1002, Arlington, VA 22226 (1-877-275-3342 or 703-562-2200).", "wikipage": "AML Topic 192"}, {"content": "To receive FILs electronically, please visit http://www.fdic.gov/about/subscriptions/fil.html.", "wikipage": "AML Topic 192"}], "long_answer": "Institutions are expected to assess the risks posed by an individual customer on a case-by-case basis and to implement controls to manage the relationship commensurate with the risks associated with each customer.\n\nFDIC Financial Institution Letters (FILs) can be accessed from the FDIC's website at https://www.fdic.gov/news/news/financial/2015/. Paper copies may also be obtained through the FDIC's Public Information Center.\n\nTo receive FDIC Financial Institution Letters electronically, one can visit http://www.fdic.gov/about/subscriptions/fil.html."}], "sample_id": "aml-topic-192-2", "question": "What is expected of financial institutions regarding risk assessment of individual customers?", "docs": [{"id": "doc-aml-topic-192-2-1", "title": "AML Topic 192 Part 1", "text": ": categories of customers without regard to the risks\nRisk Management presented by an individual customer or the bank\u2019s ability\nCustomer Due Diligence to manage the risk.\n\uf0b7 Individual customers within broader customer categories\npresent varying degrees of risk.\nContact:\n\uf0b7 Institutions are expected to assess the risks posed by an\nSuzy Gardner, Senior Examination Specialist, at\nbgardner@fdic.gov or (202) 898-3640 individual customer on a case-by-case basis and to\nimplement controls to manage the r", "score": 0.9, "summary": ": categories of customers without regard to the risks Risk Management presented by an individual customer or the bank\u2019s ability Customer Due Diligence...", "extraction": "Institutions are expected to assess the risks posed by an individual customer on a case-by-case basis and to implement controls to manage the relationship commensurate with the risks associated with each customer."}], "answer": "Institutions are expected to assess the risks posed by an individual customer on a case-by-case basis and to implement controls to manage the relationship commensurate with the risks associated with each customer. FDIC Financial Institution Letters (FILs) can be accessed from the FDIC's website at https://www. To receive FDIC Financial Institution Letters electronically, one can visit http://www.", "output": "Answer: Institutions are expected to assess the risks posed by an individual customer on a case-by-case basis and to implement controls to manage the risk [1].", "intermediate": ["[Retrieval]", "Answer: Institutions are expected to assess the risks posed by an individual customer on a case-by-case basis and to implement controls to manage the risk."]}, {"qa_pairs": [{"context": "No context provided", "question": "How does the FDIC suggest financial institutions handle customer relationships in terms of risk management?", "short_answers": ["Risk-based approach", "Assess individual customer relationships"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "Are financial institutions prohibited from providing services to certain customer categories if they can manage the associated risks?", "short_answers": ["Not prohibited", "Manage and mitigate risks"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "What concerns might financial institutions have regarding compliance with the Bank Secrecy Act (BSA)?", "short_answers": ["Hesitant due to BSA compliance", "Fear of non-compliance"], "wikipage": null}], "wikipages": [{"title": "AML Topic 192", "url": "https://www.fdic.gov/news/financial-institution-letters/2015/fil15005.pdf"}], "annotations": [{"knowledge": [], "long_answer": "The FDIC encourages financial institutions to take a risk-based approach in assessing individual customer relationships rather than declining to provide banking services to entire categories of customers. No, financial institutions that can properly manage customer relationships and effectively mitigate risks are neither prohibited nor discouraged from providing services to any category of customer accounts or individual customers operating in compliance with applicable state and federal law. Some financial institutions may be hesitant to provide certain types of banking services due to concerns that they will be unable to comply with the associated requirements of the Bank Secrecy Act (BSA)."}, {"knowledge": [{"content": "Accordingly, the FDIC encourages institutions to take a risk-based approach in assessing individual customer relationships rather than declining to provide banking services to entire categories of customers, without regard to the risks presented by an individual customer or the financial institution\u2019s ability to manage the risk.", "wikipage": "AML Topic 192"}, {"content": "Financial institutions that can properly manage customer relationships and effectively mitigate risks are neither prohibited nor discouraged from providing services to any category of customer accounts or individual customer operating in compliance with applicable state and federal law.", "wikipage": "AML Topic 192"}, {"content": "The FDIC is aware that some institutions may be hesitant to provide certain types of banking services due to concerns that they will be unable to comply with the associated requirements of the Bank Secrecy Act (BSA).", "wikipage": "AML Topic 192"}], "long_answer": "The FDIC encourages financial institutions to take a risk-based approach in assessing individual customer relationships rather than declining to provide banking services to entire categories of customers. This means evaluating the risks presented by individual customers and the institution\u2019s ability to manage those risks, rather than making blanket decisions about entire customer categories.\n\nNo, financial institutions that can properly manage customer relationships and effectively mitigate risks are neither prohibited nor discouraged from providing services to any category of customer accounts or individual customers operating in compliance with applicable state and federal law.\n\nSome financial institutions may be hesitant to provide certain types of banking services due to concerns that they will be unable to comply with the associated requirements of the Bank Secrecy Act (BSA). This indicates a fear of not being able to detect and report all potentially illicit activities."}], "sample_id": "aml-topic-192-3", "question": "How does the FDIC suggest financial institutions handle customer relationships in terms of risk management?", "docs": [{"id": "doc-aml-topic-192-3-1", "title": "AML Topic 192 Part 1", "text": "stitutions to serve their communities and recognizes\nthe importance of the services they provide. Individual customers within broader customer\ncategories present varying degrees of risk. Accordingly, the FDIC encourages institutions to take\na risk-based approach in assessing individual customer relationships rather than declining to\nprovide banking services to entire categories of customers, without regard to the risks presented\nby an individual customer or the financial institution\u2019s ability to", "score": 0.9, "summary": "stitutions to serve their communities and recognizes the importance of the services they provide. Individual customers within broader customer categor...", "extraction": "Accordingly, the FDIC encourages institutions to take a risk-based approach in assessing individual customer relationships rather than declining to provide banking services to entire categories of customers, without regard to the risks presented by an individual customer or the financial institution\u2019s ability to manage the risk."}], "answer": "The FDIC encourages financial institutions to take a risk-based approach in assessing individual customer relationships rather than declining to provide banking services to entire categories of customers. No, financial institutions that can properly manage customer relationships and effectively mitigate risks are neither prohibited nor discouraged from providing services to any category of customer accounts or individual customers operating in compliance with applicable state and federal law. Some financial institutions may be hesitant to provide certain types of banking services due to concerns that they will be unable to comply with the associated requirements of the Bank Secrecy Act (BSA).", "output": "Answer: The FDIC suggests financial institutions handle customer relationships in terms of risk management by taking a risk-based approach in assessing individual customer relationships rather than declining to provide banking services to entire categories of customers [1].", "intermediate": ["[Retrieval]", "Answer: The FDIC suggests financial institutions handle customer relationships in terms of risk management by taking a risk-based approach in assessing individual customer relationships rather than declining to provide banking services to entire categories of customers."]}, {"qa_pairs": [{"context": "No context provided", "question": "What type of financial performance data is available from the FDIC for insured institutions?", "short_answers": ["Return on assets", "Net income"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "How can I stay updated with the latest news and activities from the FDIC?", "short_answers": ["Regular updates", "Announcements", "Speeches", "Policy changes"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "What resources does the FDIC provide for understanding banking issues and risks?", "short_answers": ["Quarterly Banking Profile", "Annual Risk Review", "Banking Issues in Focus"], "wikipage": null}], "wikipages": [{"title": "AML Topic 193", "url": "https://www.fdic.gov/news/financial-institution-letters/2023/fil23029.html"}], "annotations": [{"knowledge": [], "long_answer": "The FDIC provides data on the return on assets and net income for FDIC-insured institutions. You can stay updated with FDIC news and activities by following their regular updates, which include announcements, speeches, testimony on banking issues, policy changes, and details on upcoming conferences and events. The FDIC offers various resources including the Quarterly Banking Profile, Annual Risk Review, and reports on banking issues in focus to help understand banking issues and risks."}, {"knowledge": [{"content": "FDIC-Insured Institutions Reported Return on Assets of 1.11 Percent and Net Income of $65.4 Billion... FDIC-Insured Institutions Reported Net Income of $71.5 Billion", "wikipage": "AML Topic 193"}, {"content": "The FDIC publishes regular updates on news and activities. Keep up with FDIC announcements, read speeches and testimony on the latest banking issues, learn about policy changes for banks, and get the details on upcoming conferences and events.", "wikipage": "AML Topic 193"}, {"content": "Quarterly Banking Profile... FDIC Banking Issues in Focus... Annual Risk Review", "wikipage": "AML Topic 193"}], "long_answer": "The FDIC provides data on the return on assets and net income for FDIC-insured institutions. For example, institutions reported a return on assets of 1.11 percent and net incomes of $65.4 billion and $71.5 billion in different quarters.\n\nYou can stay updated with FDIC news and activities by following their regular updates, which include announcements, speeches, testimony on banking issues, policy changes, and details on upcoming conferences and events.\n\nThe FDIC offers various resources including the Quarterly Banking Profile, Annual Risk Review, and reports on banking issues in focus to help understand banking issues and risks."}], "sample_id": "aml-topic-193-3", "question": "What type of financial performance data is available from the FDIC for insured institutions?", "docs": [{"id": "doc-aml-topic-193-3-1", "title": "AML Topic 193 Part 1", "text": "erly banking profiles, working papers, and state banking performance data. Browse our extensive research tools and reports.\nMore FDIC Analysis\nCenter for Financial Research\nConsumer Research\nFDIC National Survey of Unbanked and Underbanked Households\nQuarterly Banking Profile\nFDIC Academic Challenge\nFDIC Banking Issues in Focus\nAnnual Risk Review\nEVENT / February 25, 2025\nFDIC-Insured Institutions Reported Return on Assets of 1.11 Percent and Net Inc\u2026\nEVENT / December 12, 2024\nFDIC-Insured Insti", "score": 0.9, "summary": "erly banking profiles, working papers, and state banking performance data. Browse our extensive research tools and reports. More FDIC Analysis Center ...", "extraction": "FDIC-Insured Institutions Reported Return on Assets of 1.11 Percent and Net Income of $65.4 Billion... FDIC-Insured Institutions Reported Net Income of $71.5 Billion"}], "answer": "The FDIC provides data on the return on assets and net income for FDIC-insured institutions. You can stay updated with FDIC news and activities by following their regular updates, which include announcements, speeches, testimony on banking issues, policy changes, and details on upcoming conferences and events. The FDIC offers various resources including the Quarterly Banking Profile, Annual Risk Review, and reports on banking issues in focus to help understand banking issues and risks.", "output": "Answer: The FDIC provides financial performance data for insured institutions in the form of quarterly and annual reports, which include information such as net income, total assets, and capital [1].", "intermediate": ["[Retrieval]", "Answer: The FDIC provides financial performance data for insured institutions in the form of quarterly and annual reports, which include information such as net income, total assets, and capital."]}, {"qa_pairs": [{"context": "No context provided", "question": "What resources does the FDIC provide for understanding unbanked and underbanked households?", "short_answers": ["FDIC National Survey of Unbanked and Underbanked Households", "Consumer Research"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "How can I stay informed about the latest banking issues and policy changes according to the FDIC?", "short_answers": ["FDIC announcements", "speeches and testimonies"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "What type of financial performance data does the FDIC report on?", "short_answers": ["Return on assets", "Net income"], "wikipage": null}], "wikipages": [{"title": "AML Topic 196", "url": "https://www.fdic.gov/news/financial-institution-letters/2022/fil22016.html"}], "annotations": [{"knowledge": [], "long_answer": "The FDIC provides the 'FDIC National Survey of Unbanked and Underbanked Households' as a resource to understand the banking status of these households. You can stay informed about the latest banking issues and policy changes by keeping up with FDIC announcements, reading speeches and testimonies, and learning about policy changes for banks. The FDIC reports on financial performance data such as the return on assets and net income of FDIC-insured institutions."}, {"knowledge": [{"content": "Consumer Research\nFDIC National Survey of Unbanked and Underbanked Households", "wikipage": "AML Topic 196"}, {"content": "The FDIC publishes regular updates on news and activities. Keep up with FDIC announcements, read speeches and testimony on the latest banking issues, learn about policy changes for banks, and get the details on upcoming conferences and events.", "wikipage": "AML Topic 196"}, {"content": "EVENT / February 25, 2025\nFDIC-Insured Institutions Reported Return on Assets of 1.11 Percent and Net Inc\u2026\nEVENT / December 12, 2024\nFDIC-Insured Institutions Reported Net Income of $65.4 Billion\nEVENT / September 05, 2024\nFDIC-Insured Institutions Reported Net Income of $71.5 Billion", "wikipage": "AML Topic 196"}], "long_answer": "The FDIC provides the 'FDIC National Survey of Unbanked and Underbanked Households' as a resource to understand the banking status of these households.\n\nYou can stay informed about the latest banking issues and policy changes by keeping up with FDIC announcements, reading speeches and testimonies, and learning about policy changes for banks.\n\nThe FDIC reports on financial performance data such as the return on assets and net income of FDIC-insured institutions."}], "sample_id": "aml-topic-196-3", "question": "What resources does the FDIC provide for understanding unbanked and underbanked households?", "docs": [{"id": "doc-aml-topic-196-3-1", "title": "AML Topic 196 Part 1", "text": "profiles, working papers, and state banking performance data. Browse our extensive research tools and reports.\nMore FDIC Analysis\nCenter for Financial Research\nConsumer Research\nFDIC National Survey of Unbanked and Underbanked Households\nQuarterly Banking Profile\nFDIC Academic Challenge\nFDIC Banking Issues in Focus\nAnnual Risk Review\nEVENT / February 25, 2025\nFDIC-Insured Institutions Reported Return on Assets of 1.11 Percent and Net Inc\u2026\nEVENT / December 12, 2024\nFDIC-Insured Institutions Repor", "score": 0.9, "summary": "profiles, working papers, and state banking performance data. Browse our extensive research tools and reports. More FDIC Analysis Center for Financial...", "extraction": "Consumer Research\nFDIC National Survey of Unbanked and Underbanked Households"}], "answer": "The FDIC provides the 'FDIC National Survey of Unbanked and Underbanked Households' as a resource to understand the banking status of these households. You can stay informed about the latest banking issues and policy changes by keeping up with FDIC announcements, reading speeches and testimonies, and learning about policy changes for banks. The FDIC reports on financial performance data such as the return on assets and net income of FDIC-insured institutions.", "output": "Answer: The FDIC provides resources for understanding unbanked and underbanked households, including the FDIC National Survey of Unbanked and Underbanked Households, which provides data on the number and characteristics of households that are unbanked or underbanked, and the FDIC's Consumer Research page, which provides research on consumer finance topics, including access to financial services [1].", "intermediate": ["[Retrieval]", "Answer: The FDIC provides resources for understanding unbanked and underbanked households, including the FDIC National Survey of Unbanked and Underbanked Households, which provides data on the number and characteristics of households that are unbanked or underbanked, and the FDIC's Consumer Research page, which provides research on consumer finance topics, including access to financial services."]}, {"qa_pairs": [{"context": "No context provided", "question": "What are some of the key risks associated with crypto-assets that banking organizations should be aware of?", "short_answers": ["fraud and scams", "legal uncertainties", "misleading representations"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "Why is there a concern about legal uncertainties in the crypto-asset sector for banking organizations?", "short_answers": ["custody practices", "redemptions", "ownership rights"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "How might crypto-asset companies misrepresent their services to banking organizations?", "short_answers": ["inaccurate representations", "misleading disclosures"], "wikipage": null}], "wikipages": [{"title": "AML Topic 197", "url": "https://www.fdic.gov/news/press-releases/2023/pr23002a.pdf"}], "annotations": [{"knowledge": [], "long_answer": "Banking organizations should be aware of several key risks associated with crypto-assets, including the risk of fraud and scams among crypto-asset sector participants, legal uncertainties related to custody practices, redemptions, and ownership rights, and inaccurate or misleading representations and disclosures by crypto-asset companies. There is concern about legal uncertainties in the crypto-asset sector for banking organizations because these uncertainties relate to custody practices, redemptions, and ownership rights, some of which are currently the subject of legal processes and proceedings. Crypto-asset companies might misrepresent their services to banking organizations by providing inaccurate or misleading representations and disclosures, including misrepresentations regarding federal deposit insurance."}, {"knowledge": [{"content": "These events highlight a number of key risks associated with crypto-assets and crypto-asset sector participants that banking organizations should be aware of, including: \u2022 Risk of fraud and scams among crypto-asset sector participants. \u2022 Legal uncertainties related to custody practices, redemptions, and ownership rights, some of which are currently the subject of legal processes and proceedings. \u2022 Inaccurate or misleading representations and disclosures by crypto-asset companies.", "wikipage": "AML Topic 197"}, {"content": "Legal uncertainties related to custody practices, redemptions, and ownership rights, some of which are currently the subject of legal processes and proceedings.", "wikipage": "AML Topic 197"}, {"content": "Inaccurate or misleading representations and disclosures by crypto-asset companies, including misrepresentations regarding federal deposit insurance.", "wikipage": "AML Topic 197"}], "long_answer": "Banking organizations should be aware of several key risks associated with crypto-assets, including the risk of fraud and scams among crypto-asset sector participants, legal uncertainties related to custody practices, redemptions, and ownership rights, and inaccurate or misleading representations and disclosures by crypto-asset companies.\n\nThere is concern about legal uncertainties in the crypto-asset sector for banking organizations because these uncertainties relate to custody practices, redemptions, and ownership rights, some of which are currently the subject of legal processes and proceedings.\n\nCrypto-asset companies might misrepresent their services to banking organizations by providing inaccurate or misleading representations and disclosures, including misrepresentations regarding federal deposit insurance."}], "sample_id": "aml-topic-197-1", "question": "What are some of the key risks associated with crypto-assets that banking organizations should be aware of?", "docs": [{"id": "doc-aml-topic-197-1-1", "title": "AML Topic 197 Part 1", "text": "Board of Governors of the Federal Reserve System\nFederal Deposit Insurance Corporation\nOffice of the Comptroller of the Currency\nJanuary 3, 2023\nJoint Statement on Crypto-Asset Risks to Banking Organizations\nThe Board of Governors of the Federal Reserve System (Federal Reserve), the Federal Deposit\nInsurance Corporation (FDIC), and the Office of the Comptroller of the Currency (OCC)\n(collectively, the agencies) are issuing the following statement on crypto-asset1 risks to banking\norganizations.", "score": 0.9, "summary": "Board of Governors of the Federal Reserve System Federal Deposit Insurance Corporation Office of the Comptroller of the Currency January 3, 2023 Joint...", "extraction": "These events highlight a number of key risks associated with crypto-assets and crypto-asset sector participants that banking organizations should be aware of, including: \u2022 Risk of fraud and scams among crypto-asset sector participants. \u2022 Legal uncertainties related to custody practices, redemptions, and ownership rights, some of which are currently the subject of legal processes and proceedings. \u2022 Inaccurate or misleading representations and disclosures by crypto-asset companies."}], "answer": "Banking organizations should be aware of several key risks associated with crypto-assets, including the risk of fraud and scams among crypto-asset sector participants, legal uncertainties related to custody practices, redemptions, and ownership rights, and inaccurate or misleading representations and disclosures by crypto-asset companies. There is concern about legal uncertainties in the crypto-asset sector for banking organizations because these uncertainties relate to custody practices, redemptions, and ownership rights, some of which are currently the subject of legal processes and proceedings. Crypto-asset companies might misrepresent their services to banking organizations by providing inaccurate or misleading representations and disclosures, including misrepresentations regarding federal deposit insurance.", "output": "Answer: The key risks associated with crypto-assets that banking organizations should be aware of include operational, credit, market, liquidity, and legal and regulatory risks [1].", "intermediate": ["[Retrieval]", "Answer: The key risks associated with crypto-assets that banking organizations should be aware of include operational, credit, market, liquidity, and legal and regulatory risks."]}, {"qa_pairs": [{"context": "No context provided", "question": "What are some potential impacts of significant volatility in crypto-asset markets on banking organizations?", "short_answers": ["Impacts on deposit flows", "Volatility in crypto markets"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "How might stablecoins create risks for banking organizations?", "short_answers": ["Run risk of stablecoins", "Deposit outflows risk"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "What are the risks associated with interconnections among crypto-asset participants?", "short_answers": ["Contagion risk", "Concentration risks"], "wikipage": null}], "wikipages": [{"title": "AML Topic 197", "url": "https://www.fdic.gov/news/press-releases/2023/pr23002a.pdf"}], "annotations": [{"knowledge": [], "long_answer": "Significant volatility in crypto-asset markets can lead to potential impacts on deposit flows associated with crypto-asset companies. Stablecoins are susceptible to run risk, which can create potential deposit outflows for banking organizations that hold stablecoin reserves. Interconnections among crypto-asset participants can lead to contagion risk and concentration risks for banking organizations due to opaque lending, investing, funding, service, and operational arrangements."}, {"knowledge": [{"content": "\u2022 Significant volatility in crypto-asset markets, the effects of which include potential impacts on deposit flows associated with crypto-asset companies.", "wikipage": "AML Topic 197"}, {"content": "\u2022 Susceptibility of stablecoins to run risk, creating potential deposit outflows for banking organizations that hold stablecoin reserves.", "wikipage": "AML Topic 197"}, {"content": "\u2022 Contagion risk within the crypto-asset sector resulting from interconnections among certain crypto-asset participants, including through opaque lending, investing, funding, service, and operational arrangements. These interconnections may also present concentration risks for banking organizations with exposures to the crypto-asset sector.", "wikipage": "AML Topic 197"}], "long_answer": "Significant volatility in crypto-asset markets can lead to potential impacts on deposit flows associated with crypto-asset companies.\n\nStablecoins are susceptible to run risk, which can create potential deposit outflows for banking organizations that hold stablecoin reserves.\n\nInterconnections among crypto-asset participants can lead to contagion risk and concentration risks for banking organizations due to opaque lending, investing, funding, service, and operational arrangements."}], "sample_id": "aml-topic-197-2", "question": "What are some potential impacts of significant volatility in crypto-asset markets on banking organizations?", "docs": [{"id": "doc-aml-topic-197-2-1", "title": "AML Topic 197 Part 1", "text": "tices that\nmay be unfair, deceptive, or abusive, contributing to significant harm to retail and\ninstitutional investors, customers, and counterparties.\n\u2022 Significant volatility in crypto-asset markets, the effects of which include potential impacts\non deposit flows associated with crypto-asset companies.\n\u2022 Susceptibility of stablecoins to run risk, creating potential deposit outflows for banking\norganizations that hold stablecoin reserves.\n\u2022 Contagion risk within the crypto-asset sector resultin", "score": 0.9, "summary": "tices that may be unfair, deceptive, or abusive, contributing to significant harm to retail and institutional investors, customers, and counterparties...", "extraction": "\u2022 Significant volatility in crypto-asset markets, the effects of which include potential impacts on deposit flows associated with crypto-asset companies."}], "answer": "Significant volatility in crypto-asset markets can lead to potential impacts on deposit flows associated with crypto-asset companies. Stablecoins are susceptible to run risk, which can create potential deposit outflows for banking organizations that hold stablecoin reserves. Interconnections among crypto-asset participants can lead to contagion risk and concentration risks for banking organizations due to opaque lending, investing, funding, service, and operational arrangements.", "output": "Answer: Potential impacts of significant volatility in crypto-asset markets on banking organizations include deposit flows associated with crypto-asset companies, susceptibility of stablecoins to run risk, creating potential deposit outflows for banking organizations that hold stablecoin reserves, and contagion risk within the crypto-asset sector resulting in deposit outflows and losses on banking organizations' investments [1].", "intermediate": ["[Retrieval]", "Answer: Potential impacts of significant volatility in crypto-asset markets on banking organizations include deposit flows associated with crypto-asset companies, susceptibility of stablecoins to run risk, creating potential deposit outflows for banking organizations that hold stablecoin reserves, and contagion risk within the crypto-asset sector resulting in deposit outflows and losses on banking organizations' investments."]}, {"qa_pairs": [{"context": "No context provided", "question": "What measures are agencies taking to address risks from the crypto-asset sector in the banking system?", "short_answers": ["Supervising banking organizations", "Carefully reviewing proposals", "Building knowledge and understanding"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "How do the agencies view the involvement of banking organizations with crypto-assets?", "short_answers": ["Careful and cautious approach", "Significant risks from recent failures"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "Are banking organizations prohibited from providing services related to crypto-assets?", "short_answers": ["Not prohibited", "Not discouraged"], "wikipage": null}], "wikipages": [{"title": "AML Topic 197", "url": "https://www.fdic.gov/news/press-releases/2023/pr23002a.pdf"}], "annotations": [{"knowledge": [], "long_answer": "The agencies are supervising banking organizations that may be exposed to risks from the crypto-asset sector and are carefully reviewing any proposals from these organizations to engage in activities involving crypto-assets. The agencies take a careful and cautious approach to current or proposed crypto-asset-related activities and exposures at each banking organization due to the significant risks highlighted by recent failures of several large crypto-asset companies. Banking organizations are neither prohibited nor discouraged from providing banking services related to crypto-assets."}, {"knowledge": [{"content": "The agencies are supervising banking organizations that may be exposed to risks stemming from the crypto-asset sector and carefully reviewing any proposals from banking organizations to engage in activities that involve crypto-assets. Through the agencies\u2019 case-by-case approaches to date, the agencies continue to build knowledge, expertise, and understanding of the risks crypto-assets may pose to banking organizations, their customers, and the broader U.S. financial system.", "wikipage": "AML Topic 197"}, {"content": "Given the significant risks highlighted by recent failures of several large crypto-asset companies, the agencies continue to take a careful and cautious approach related to current or proposed crypto-asset-related activities and exposures at each banking organization.", "wikipage": "AML Topic 197"}, {"content": "Banking organizations are neither prohibited nor discouraged from providing banking servi", "wikipage": "AML Topic 197"}], "long_answer": "The agencies are supervising banking organizations that may be exposed to risks from the crypto-asset sector and are carefully reviewing any proposals from these organizations to engage in activities involving crypto-assets. They are building knowledge and understanding of the risks posed by crypto-assets through a case-by-case approach.\n\nThe agencies take a careful and cautious approach to current or proposed crypto-asset-related activities and exposures at each banking organization due to the significant risks highlighted by recent failures of several large crypto-asset companies.\n\nBanking organizations are neither prohibited nor discouraged from providing banking services related to crypto-assets."}], "sample_id": "aml-topic-197-3", "question": "What measures are agencies taking to address risks from the crypto-asset sector in the banking system?", "docs": [{"id": "doc-aml-topic-197-3-1", "title": "AML Topic 197 Part 1", "text": "ities; and vulnerabilities related to cyber-attacks, outages, lost or\ntrapped assets, and illicit finance.\nIt is important that risks related to the crypto-asset sector that cannot be mitigated or controlled\ndo not migrate to the banking system. The agencies are supervising banking organizations that\nmay be exposed to risks stemming from the crypto-asset sector and carefully reviewing any\n1 By \u201ccrypto-asset,\u201d the agencies refer generally to any digital asset implemented using cryptographic techn", "score": 0.9, "summary": "ities; and vulnerabilities related to cyber-attacks, outages, lost or trapped assets, and illicit finance. It is important that risks related to the c...", "extraction": "The agencies are supervising banking organizations that may be exposed to risks stemming from the crypto-asset sector and carefully reviewing any proposals from banking organizations to engage in activities that involve crypto-assets. Through the agencies\u2019 case-by-case approaches to date, the agencies continue to build knowledge, expertise, and understanding of the risks crypto-assets may pose to banking organizations, their customers, and the broader U.S. financial system."}], "answer": "The agencies are supervising banking organizations that may be exposed to risks from the crypto-asset sector and are carefully reviewing any proposals from these organizations to engage in activities involving crypto-assets. The agencies take a careful and cautious approach to current or proposed crypto-asset-related activities and exposures at each banking organization due to the significant risks highlighted by recent failures of several large crypto-asset companies. Banking organizations are neither prohibited nor discouraged from providing banking services related to crypto-assets.", "output": "The agencies are supervising banking organizations that may be exposed to risks stemming from the crypto-asset sector and carefully reviewing any such exposures [1].", "intermediate": ["[Retrieval]", "The agencies are supervising banking organizations that may be exposed to risks stemming from the crypto-asset sector and carefully reviewing any such exposures."]}, {"qa_pairs": [{"context": "No context provided", "question": "What concerns does the FDIC have regarding crypto assets offered by insured banks?", "short_answers": ["Consumer confusion", "Risks with non-bank entities"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "How might inaccurate representations about FDIC deposit insurance by non-banks affect customers?", "short_answers": ["Customer confusion", "Mistaken belief of protection"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "What confusion might customers have about the application of FDIC deposit insurance?", "short_answers": ["Confusion about insurance application", "Protection up to $250,000"], "wikipage": null}], "wikipages": [{"title": "AML Topic 198", "url": "https://www.fdic.gov/news/financial-institution-letters/2022/fil22035b.pdf"}], "annotations": [{"knowledge": [], "long_answer": "The FDIC is concerned about the risks of consumer confusion or harm arising from crypto assets offered by, through, or in connection with insured depository institutions. Inaccurate representations about deposit insurance by non-banks, including crypto companies, may confuse the non-bank\u2019s customers and cause those customers to mistakenly believe they are protected against any type of loss. Customers can be confused about when FDIC deposit insurance applies."}, {"knowledge": [{"content": "The FDIC is concerned about the risks of consumer confusion or harm arising from crypto assets offered by, through, or in connection with insured depository institutions (insured banks). Risks are elevated when a non-bank entity offers crypto assets to the non-bank\u2019s customers, while also offering an insured bank\u2019s deposit products.", "wikipage": "AML Topic 198"}, {"content": "Inaccurate representations about deposit insurance by non-banks, including crypto companies, may confuse the non-bank\u2019s customers and cause those customers to mistakenly believe they are protected against any type of loss.", "wikipage": "AML Topic 198"}, {"content": "More broadly, customers can be confused about: When FDIC deposit insurance applies. In the unlikely event of an insured-bank failure, the FDIC protects depositors of insured banks against the loss of their deposits, up to at least $250,000.", "wikipage": "AML Topic 198"}], "long_answer": "The FDIC is concerned about the risks of consumer confusion or harm arising from crypto assets offered by, through, or in connection with insured depository institutions. These risks are elevated when a non-bank entity offers crypto assets to the non-bank\u2019s customers while also offering an insured bank\u2019s deposit products.\n\nInaccurate representations about deposit insurance by non-banks, including crypto companies, may confuse the non-bank\u2019s customers and cause those customers to mistakenly believe they are protected against any type of loss.\n\nCustomers can be confused about when FDIC deposit insurance applies. In the unlikely event of an insured-bank failure, the FDIC protects depositors of insured banks against the loss of their deposits, up to at least $250,000."}], "sample_id": "aml-topic-198-1", "question": "What concerns does the FDIC have regarding crypto assets offered by insured banks?", "docs": [{"id": "doc-aml-topic-198-1-1", "title": "AML Topic 198 Part 1", "text": "Federal Deposit\nInsurance Corporation\nADVISORY\nAdvisory to FDIC-Insured Institutions Regarding FDIC Deposit Insurance and Dealings\nwith Crypto Companies\nRISKS AND CONCERNS\n\u2022 The FDIC is concerned about the risks of consumer confusion or harm arising from\ncrypto assets offered by, through, or in connection with insured depository institutions\n(insured banks). Risks are elevated when a non-bank entity offers crypto assets to the\nnon-bank\u2019s customers, while also offering an insured bank\u2019s deposit p", "score": 0.9, "summary": "Federal Deposit Insurance Corporation ADVISORY Advisory to FDIC-Insured Institutions Regarding FDIC Deposit Insurance and Dealings with Crypto Compani...", "extraction": "The FDIC is concerned about the risks of consumer confusion or harm arising from crypto assets offered by, through, or in connection with insured depository institutions (insured banks). Risks are elevated when a non-bank entity offers crypto assets to the non-bank\u2019s customers, while also offering an insured bank\u2019s deposit products."}], "answer": "The FDIC is concerned about the risks of consumer confusion or harm arising from crypto assets offered by, through, or in connection with insured depository institutions. Inaccurate representations about deposit insurance by non-banks, including crypto companies, may confuse the non-bank\u2019s customers and cause those customers to mistakenly believe they are protected against any type of loss. Customers can be confused about when FDIC deposit insurance applies.", "output": "Answer: The FDIC is concerned about the risks of consumer confusion or harm arising from crypto assets offered by, through, or in connection with insured depository institutions (insured banks) [1].", "intermediate": ["[Retrieval]", "Answer: The FDIC is concerned about the risks of consumer confusion or harm arising from crypto assets offered by, through, or in connection with insured depository institutions (insured banks)."]}, {"qa_pairs": [{"context": "No context provided", "question": "What types of accounts are covered by FDIC deposit insurance?", "short_answers": ["Checking accounts", "Savings accounts"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "Are crypto assets protected by FDIC insurance?", "short_answers": ["Crypto assets not insured", "Non-deposit products not covered"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "What risks can arise from misrepresentations about FDIC insurance by crypto companies?", "short_answers": ["Customer confusion", "Liquidity risk to banks"], "wikipage": null}], "wikipages": [{"title": "AML Topic 198", "url": "https://www.fdic.gov/news/financial-institution-letters/2022/fil22035b.pdf"}], "annotations": [{"knowledge": [], "long_answer": "FDIC deposit insurance covers deposit products offered by insured banks, such as checking accounts and savings accounts. No, FDIC insurance does not apply to non-deposit products, such as crypto assets. Misrepresentations about the nature and scope of deposit insurance can lead to customer confusion, which may cause consumers with insured-bank relationships to move funds."}, {"knowledge": [{"content": "FDIC deposit insurance covers deposit products offered by insured banks, such as checking accounts and savings accounts.", "wikipage": "AML Topic 198"}, {"content": "Deposit insurance does not apply to non-deposit products, such as stocks, bonds, money market mutual funds, securities, commodities, or crypto assets.", "wikipage": "AML Topic 198"}, {"content": "In addition to potential consumer harm, customer confusion can lead to legal risks for banks if a crypto company, or other third-party partner of an insured bank with whom they are dealing, makes misrepresentations about the nature and scope of deposit insurance. Moreover, misrepresentations and customer confusion could cause concerned consumers with insured-bank relationships to move funds, which could result in liquidity risk to banks and in turn, could potentially result in earnings and capital risks.", "wikipage": "AML Topic 198"}], "long_answer": "FDIC deposit insurance covers deposit products offered by insured banks, such as checking accounts and savings accounts.\n\nNo, FDIC insurance does not apply to non-deposit products, such as crypto assets.\n\nMisrepresentations about the nature and scope of deposit insurance can lead to customer confusion, which may cause consumers with insured-bank relationships to move funds. This could result in liquidity risk to banks and potentially lead to earnings and capital risks."}], "sample_id": "aml-topic-198-2", "question": "What types of accounts are covered by FDIC deposit insurance?", "docs": [{"id": "doc-aml-topic-198-2-1", "title": "AML Topic 198 Part 1", "text": "deposit insurance after an\ninsured bank fails. FDIC insurance does not protect a non-bank\u2019s customers\nagainst the default, insolvency, or bankruptcy of any non-bank entity, including\ncrypto custodians, exchanges, brokers, wallet providers, or other entities that\nappear to mimic banks but are not, called \u201cneobanks.\u201d\nWhat products are FDIC insured. FDIC deposit insurance covers deposit products\no\noffered by insured banks, such as checking accounts and savings accounts.\nDeposit insurance does not a", "score": 0.9, "summary": "deposit insurance after an insured bank fails. FDIC insurance does not protect a non-bank\u2019s customers against the default, insolvency, or bankruptcy o...", "extraction": "FDIC deposit insurance covers deposit products offered by insured banks, such as checking accounts and savings accounts."}], "answer": "FDIC deposit insurance covers deposit products offered by insured banks, such as checking accounts and savings accounts. No, FDIC insurance does not apply to non-deposit products, such as crypto assets. Misrepresentations about the nature and scope of deposit insurance can lead to customer confusion, which may cause consumers with insured-bank relationships to move funds.", "output": "Answer: FDIC deposit insurance covers deposit products offered by insured banks, such as checking accounts and savings accounts [1].", "intermediate": ["[Retrieval]", "Answer: FDIC deposit insurance covers deposit products offered by insured banks, such as checking accounts and savings accounts."]}, {"qa_pairs": [{"context": "No context provided", "question": "What steps should insured banks take to manage risks associated with third-party relationships, specifically with crypto companies?", "short_answers": ["Confirm and monitor crypto companies", "Address misrepresentations"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "How can non-bank entities like crypto companies reduce consumer confusion regarding FDIC insurance?", "short_answers": ["State they are not an insured bank", "Identify insured bank(s)", "Communicate crypto assets are not FDIC-insured"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "What communication practices should insured banks adopt to minimize customer confusion about deposit and non-deposit products?", "short_answers": ["Ensure clear and conspicuous communications", "Minimize confusion about deposit and non-deposit products"], "wikipage": null}], "wikipages": [{"title": "AML Topic 198", "url": "https://www.fdic.gov/news/financial-institution-letters/2022/fil22035b.pdf"}], "annotations": [{"knowledge": [], "long_answer": "Insured banks should confirm and monitor that crypto companies do not misrepresent the availability of deposit insurance. Non-bank entities should clearly and conspicuously state that they are not an insured bank, identify the insured bank(s) where customer funds may be held, and communicate that crypto assets are not FDIC-insured products and may lose value. Insured banks should ensure communications related to deposit insurance are clear and conspicuous, particularly when involved with non-bank entities offering both deposit products and non-deposit products like crypto assets."}, {"knowledge": [{"content": "In their dealings with crypto companies, insured banks should confirm and monitor that these companies do not misrepresent the availability of deposit insurance in order to measure and control risks to the bank, and should take appropriate action to address such misrepresentations.", "wikipage": "AML Topic 198"}, {"content": "Non-bank entities, such as crypto companies, that advertise or offer FDIC-insured products in relationships with insured banks could reduce consumer confusion by clearly, conspicuously: (a) stating that they are not an insured bank; (b) identifying the insured bank(s) where any customer funds may be held on deposit; and (c) communicating that crypto assets are not FDIC-insured products and may lose value.", "wikipage": "AML Topic 198"}, {"content": "Communications related to deposit insurance need to be clear and conspicuous. Non-bank entities, such as crypto companies, that advertise or offer FDIC-insured products in relationships with insured banks could reduce consumer confusion by clearly, conspicuously: (a) stating that they are not an insured bank; (b) identifying the insured bank(s) where any customer funds may be held on deposit; and (c) communicating that crypto assets are not FDIC-insured products and may lose value.", "wikipage": "AML Topic 198"}], "long_answer": "Insured banks should confirm and monitor that crypto companies do not misrepresent the availability of deposit insurance. They need to measure and control risks to the bank and take appropriate action to address any misrepresentations.\n\nNon-bank entities should clearly and conspicuously state that they are not an insured bank, identify the insured bank(s) where customer funds may be held, and communicate that crypto assets are not FDIC-insured products and may lose value.\n\nInsured banks should ensure communications related to deposit insurance are clear and conspicuous, particularly when involved with non-bank entities offering both deposit products and non-deposit products like crypto assets."}], "sample_id": "aml-topic-198-3", "question": "What steps should insured banks take to manage risks associated with third-party relationships, specifically with crypto companies?", "docs": [{"id": "doc-aml-topic-198-3-1", "title": "AML Topic 198 Part 1", "text": "AND GOVERNANCE CONSIDERATIONS\n\u2022 Insured banks need to be aware of how FDIC insurance operates and need to assess,\nmanage, and control risks arising from all third-party relationships, including those with\ncrypto companies.\n\u2022 In their dealings with crypto companies, insured banks should confirm and monitor that\nthese companies do not misrepresent the availability of deposit insurance in order to\nmeasure and control risks to the bank, and should take appropriate action to address such\nmisrepresent", "score": 0.9, "summary": "AND GOVERNANCE CONSIDERATIONS \u2022 Insured banks need to be aware of how FDIC insurance operates and need to assess, manage, and control risks arising fr...", "extraction": "In their dealings with crypto companies, insured banks should confirm and monitor that these companies do not misrepresent the availability of deposit insurance in order to measure and control risks to the bank, and should take appropriate action to address such misrepresentations."}], "answer": "Insured banks should confirm and monitor that crypto companies do not misrepresent the availability of deposit insurance. Non-bank entities should clearly and conspicuously state that they are not an insured bank, identify the insured bank(s) where customer funds may be held, and communicate that crypto assets are not FDIC-insured products and may lose value. Insured banks should ensure communications related to deposit insurance are clear and conspicuous, particularly when involved with non-bank entities offering both deposit products and non-deposit products like crypto assets.", "output": "Insured banks should take steps to manage risks associated with third-party relationships, specifically with crypto companies, by confirming and monitoring that these companies do not misrepresent the availability of deposit insurance, and should take appropriate action to address such misrepresentation [1].", "intermediate": ["[Retrieval]", "Insured banks should take steps to manage risks associated with third-party relationships, specifically with crypto companies, by confirming and monitoring that these companies do not misrepresent the availability of deposit insurance, and should take appropriate action to address such misrepresentation."]}, {"qa_pairs": [{"context": "No context provided", "question": "What kind of research reports does the FDIC provide to help understand banking performance?", "short_answers": ["banking profiles", "Quarterly Banking Profile", "Annual Risk Review"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "How can I stay updated with the latest FDIC news and activities?", "short_answers": ["regular updates", "press releases", "conferences and events"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "What are some of the recent financial performance metrics reported by FDIC-insured institutions?", "short_answers": ["1.11% return on assets", "$65.4 billion net income", "$71.5 billion net income"], "wikipage": null}], "wikipages": [{"title": "AML Topic 199", "url": "https://www.fdic.gov/news/financial-institution-letters/2023/fil23001.html"}], "annotations": [{"knowledge": [], "long_answer": "The FDIC provides several research tools and reports to help understand banking performance, including banking profiles, working papers, and state banking performance data. To stay updated with the latest FDIC news and activities, you can follow their regular updates, read speeches and testimony on banking issues, learn about policy changes for banks, and get details on upcoming conferences and events. Recent financial performance metrics reported by FDIC-insured institutions include a return on assets of 1."}, {"knowledge": [{"content": "banking profiles, working papers, and state banking performance data. Browse our extensive research tools and reports. More FDIC Analysis Center for Financial Research Consumer Research FDIC National Survey of Unbanked and Underbanked Households Quarterly Banking Profile FDIC Academic Challenge FDIC Banking Issues in Focus Annual Risk Review", "wikipage": "AML Topic 199"}, {"content": "The FDIC publishes regular updates on news and activities. Keep up with FDIC announcements, read speeches and testimony on the latest banking issues, learn about policy changes for banks, and get the details on upcoming conferences and events. Find More FDIC News Press Releases Financial Institution Letters Conferences & Events", "wikipage": "AML Topic 199"}, {"content": "EVENT / February 25, 2025 FDIC-Insured Institutions Reported Return on Assets of 1.11 Percent and Net Inc\u2026 EVENT / December 12, 2024 FDIC-Insured Institutions Reported Net Income of $65.4 Billion EVENT / September 05, 2024 FDIC-Insured Institutions Reported Net Income of $71.5 Billion", "wikipage": "AML Topic 199"}], "long_answer": "The FDIC provides several research tools and reports to help understand banking performance, including banking profiles, working papers, and state banking performance data. They also offer consumer research, the FDIC National Survey of Unbanked and Underbanked Households, the Quarterly Banking Profile, and the Annual Risk Review.\n\nTo stay updated with the latest FDIC news and activities, you can follow their regular updates, read speeches and testimony on banking issues, learn about policy changes for banks, and get details on upcoming conferences and events. They also offer press releases, financial institution letters, and media campaigns.\n\nRecent financial performance metrics reported by FDIC-insured institutions include a return on assets of 1.11 percent and net income figures of $65.4 billion and $71.5 billion for different periods."}], "sample_id": "aml-topic-199-3", "question": "What kind of research reports does the FDIC provide to help understand banking performance?", "docs": [{"id": "doc-aml-topic-199-3-1", "title": "AML Topic 199 Part 1", "text": "banking profiles, working papers, and state banking performance data. Browse our extensive research tools and reports.\nMore FDIC Analysis\nCenter for Financial Research\nConsumer Research\nFDIC National Survey of Unbanked and Underbanked Households\nQuarterly Banking Profile\nFDIC Academic Challenge\nFDIC Banking Issues in Focus\nAnnual Risk Review\nEVENT / February 25, 2025\nFDIC-Insured Institutions Reported Return on Assets of 1.11 Percent and Net Inc\u2026\nEVENT / December 12, 2024\nFDIC-Insured Institutio", "score": 0.9, "summary": "banking profiles, working papers, and state banking performance data. Browse our extensive research tools and reports. More FDIC Analysis Center for F...", "extraction": "banking profiles, working papers, and state banking performance data. Browse our extensive research tools and reports. More FDIC Analysis Center for Financial Research Consumer Research FDIC National Survey of Unbanked and Underbanked Households Quarterly Banking Profile FDIC Academic Challenge FDIC Banking Issues in Focus Annual Risk Review"}], "answer": "The FDIC provides several research tools and reports to help understand banking performance, including banking profiles, working papers, and state banking performance data. To stay updated with the latest FDIC news and activities, you can follow their regular updates, read speeches and testimony on banking issues, learn about policy changes for banks, and get details on upcoming conferences and events. Recent financial performance metrics reported by FDIC-insured institutions include a return on assets of 1.", "output": "The FDIC provides research reports to help understand banking performance, such as the Quarterly Banking Profile, which provides a detailed analysis of the performance of FDIC-insured institutions, and the FDIC National Survey of Unbanked and Underbanked Households, which provides information on the number and characteristics of households that do not have a bank account or have an account but use it infrequently [1].", "intermediate": ["[Retrieval]", "The FDIC provides research reports to help understand banking performance, such as the Quarterly Banking Profile, which provides a detailed analysis of the performance of FDIC-insured institutions, and the FDIC National Survey of Unbanked and Underbanked Households, which provides information on the number and characteristics of households that do not have a bank account or have an account but use it infrequently."]}, {"qa_pairs": [{"context": "No context provided", "question": "What role does the Secretary of the Treasury play in establishing AML/CFT priorities?", "short_answers": ["Establish AML/CFT priorities", "Consult with various agencies"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "Who published the first national AML/CFT Priorities according to the AML Act?", "short_answers": ["FinCEN", "Published first AML/CFT Priorities"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "Which organizations are involved in the consultation process for establishing AML/CFT priorities?", "short_answers": ["Attorney General", "Federal and State regulators", "National security agencies"], "wikipage": null}], "wikipages": [{"title": "AML Topic 200", "url": "https://www.fdic.gov/news/financial-institution-letters/2021/fil21046a.pdf"}], "annotations": [{"knowledge": [], "long_answer": "The Secretary of the Treasury is responsible for establishing and making public the priorities for anti-money laundering and countering the financing of terrorism policy, in consultation with the Attorney General, Federal functional regulators, relevant State financial regulators, and relevant national security agencies. The U. The organizations involved in the consultation process for establishing AML/CFT priorities include the Attorney General, Federal functional regulators, relevant State financial regulators, and relevant national security agencies."}, {"knowledge": [{"content": "The Anti-Money Laundering Act of 2020 (the \u201cAML Act\u201d) requires the Secretary of the Treasury, in consultation with the Attorney General, Federal functional regulators, relevant State financial regulators, and relevant national security agencies, to establish and make public priorities for anti-money laundering and countering the financing of terrorism policy (AML/CFT Priorities).", "wikipage": "AML Topic 200"}, {"content": "Accordingly, the U.S. Department of the Treasury\u2019s Financial Crimes Enforcement Network (FinCEN) published these first national AML/CFT Priorities today in consultation with the parties as set out in the AML Act.", "wikipage": "AML Topic 200"}, {"content": "The Anti-Money Laundering Act of 2020 (the \u201cAML Act\u201d) requires the Secretary of the Treasury, in consultation with the Attorney General, Federal functional regulators, relevant State financial regulators, and relevant national security agencies, to establish and make public priorities for anti-money laundering and countering the financing of terrorism policy (AML/CFT Priorities).", "wikipage": "AML Topic 200"}], "long_answer": "The Secretary of the Treasury is responsible for establishing and making public the priorities for anti-money laundering and countering the financing of terrorism policy, in consultation with the Attorney General, Federal functional regulators, relevant State financial regulators, and relevant national security agencies.\n\nThe U.S. Department of the Treasury\u2019s Financial Crimes Enforcement Network (FinCEN) published the first national AML/CFT Priorities.\n\nThe organizations involved in the consultation process for establishing AML/CFT priorities include the Attorney General, Federal functional regulators, relevant State financial regulators, and relevant national security agencies."}], "sample_id": "aml-topic-200-1", "question": "What role does the Secretary of the Treasury play in establishing AML/CFT priorities?", "docs": [{"id": "doc-aml-topic-200-1-2", "title": "AML Topic 200 Part 2", "text": "e Attorney General, Federal functional regulators,2 relevant\nState financial regulators, and relevant national security agencies, to establish and make\npublic priorities for anti-money laundering and countering the financing of terrorism policy\n(AML/CFT Priorities).3 Accordingly, the U.S. Department of the Treasury\u2019s Financial Crimes\nEnforcement Network (FinCEN) published these first national AML/CFT Priorities today\nin consultation with the parties as set out in the AML Act. As a result of this", "score": 0.85, "summary": "e Attorney General, Federal functional regulators,2 relevant State financial regulators, and relevant national security agencies, to establish and mak...", "extraction": "e Attorney General, Federal functional regulators,2 relevant\nState financial regulators, and relevant national security agencies, to establish and make\npublic priorities for anti-money laundering and "}], "answer": "The Secretary of the Treasury is responsible for establishing and making public the priorities for anti-money laundering and countering the financing of terrorism policy, in consultation with the Attorney General, Federal functional regulators, relevant State financial regulators, and relevant national security agencies. The U. The organizations involved in the consultation process for establishing AML/CFT priorities include the Attorney General, Federal functional regulators, relevant State financial regulators, and relevant national security agencies.", "output": "The Secretary of the Treasury plays a role in establishing AML/CFT priorities by consulting with relevant parties, such as the Attorney General, Federal functional regulators, State financial regulators, and national security agencies, to establish and make public priorities for anti-money laundering and countering the financing of terrorism policy [1].", "intermediate": ["[Retrieval]", "The Secretary of the Treasury plays a role in establishing AML/CFT priorities by consulting with relevant parties, such as the Attorney General, Federal functional regulators, State financial regulators, and national security agencies, to establish and make public priorities for anti-money laundering and countering the financing of terrorism policy."]}, {"qa_pairs": [{"context": "No context provided", "question": "What is the purpose of the recent statement issued by federal banking agencies and FinCEN regarding AML/CFT Priorities?", "short_answers": ["Provide clarity on AML/CFT Priorities", "No immediate change to BSA requirements"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "Will the publication of the AML/CFT Priorities change the current BSA requirements for banks immediately?", "short_answers": ["No immediate change", "BSA requirements remain the same"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "What actions are the FBAs planning regarding the BSA regulations in light of the AML/CFT Priorities?", "short_answers": ["FBAs plan to revise BSA regulations", "Incorporate AML/CFT Priorities"], "wikipage": null}], "wikipages": [{"title": "AML Topic 200", "url": "https://www.fdic.gov/news/financial-institution-letters/2021/fil21046a.pdf"}], "annotations": [{"knowledge": [], "long_answer": "The statement aims to provide clarity for banks on the AML/CFT Priorities. No, the publication of the AML/CFT Priorities does not immediately change the current BSA requirements or supervisory expectations for banks. The FBAs plan to revise their BSA regulations, as necessary, to address how the AML/CFT Priorities will be incorporated into banks\u2019 BSA requirements."}, {"knowledge": [{"content": "cy (collectively, the \u201cfederal banking agencies\u201d or \u201cFBAs\u201d), State bank and credit union regulators,4 and FinCEN are issuing this statement to provide clarity for banks5 on these AML/CFT Priorities. Today\u2019s publication of the AML/CFT Priorities does not create an immediate change to Bank Secrecy Act (BSA) requirements or supervisory expectations for banks.", "wikipage": "AML Topic 200"}, {"content": "Today\u2019s publication of the AML/CFT Priorities does not create an immediate change to Bank Secrecy Act (BSA) requirements or supervisory expectations for banks.", "wikipage": "AML Topic 200"}, {"content": "Although not required by the AML Act, the FBAs plan to revise their BSA regulations, as necessary, to address how the AML/CFT Priorities will be incorporated into banks\u2019 BSA requirements.", "wikipage": "AML Topic 200"}], "long_answer": "The statement aims to provide clarity for banks on the AML/CFT Priorities. It does not create an immediate change to Bank Secrecy Act (BSA) requirements or supervisory expectations for banks.\n\nNo, the publication of the AML/CFT Priorities does not immediately change the current BSA requirements or supervisory expectations for banks.\n\nThe FBAs plan to revise their BSA regulations, as necessary, to address how the AML/CFT Priorities will be incorporated into banks\u2019 BSA requirements."}], "sample_id": "aml-topic-200-2", "question": "What is the purpose of the recent statement issued by federal banking agencies and FinCEN regarding AML/CFT Priorities?", "docs": [{"id": "doc-aml-topic-200-2-1", "title": "AML Topic 200 Part 1", "text": "cy (collectively, the \u201cfederal banking agencies\u201d or \u201cFBAs\u201d), State bank and credit\nunion regulators,4 and FinCEN are issuing this statement to provide clarity for banks5 on\nthese AML/CFT Priorities.\nToday\u2019s publication of the AML/CFT Priorities does not create an immediate change to\nBank Secrecy Act (BSA) requirements or supervisory expectations for banks. The AML Act\nrequires that, within 180 days of the establishment of the AML/CFT Priorities, FinCEN (in\nconsultation with Federal functional re", "score": 0.9, "summary": "cy (collectively, the \u201cfederal banking agencies\u201d or \u201cFBAs\u201d), State bank and credit union regulators,4 and FinCEN are issuing this statement to provide...", "extraction": "cy (collectively, the \u201cfederal banking agencies\u201d or \u201cFBAs\u201d), State bank and credit union regulators,4 and FinCEN are issuing this statement to provide clarity for banks5 on these AML/CFT Priorities. Today\u2019s publication of the AML/CFT Priorities does not create an immediate change to Bank Secrecy Act (BSA) requirements or supervisory expectations for banks."}], "answer": "The statement aims to provide clarity for banks on the AML/CFT Priorities. No, the publication of the AML/CFT Priorities does not immediately change the current BSA requirements or supervisory expectations for banks. The FBAs plan to revise their BSA regulations, as necessary, to address how the AML/CFT Priorities will be incorporated into banks\u2019 BSA requirements.", "output": "Answer: The purpose of the recent statement issued by federal banking agencies and FinCEN regarding AML/CFT Priorities is to provide clarity for banks on these AML/CFT Priorities [1].", "intermediate": ["[Retrieval]", "Answer: The purpose of the recent statement issued by federal banking agencies and FinCEN regarding AML/CFT Priorities is to provide clarity for banks on these AML/CFT Priorities."]}, {"qa_pairs": [{"context": "No context provided", "question": "Are banks currently required to incorporate AML/CFT Priorities into their risk-based BSA compliance programs?", "short_answers": ["Not required until final regulations", "Effective date pending"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "What preparatory steps can banks take regarding AML/CFT Priorities before the final regulations are published?", "short_answers": ["Assess product and service risks", "Evaluate customer and geographic risks"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "What entities are included in the definition of 'bank' under the Bank Secrecy Act regulations?", "short_answers": ["Includes banks, credit unions", "Includes savings associations, foreign banks"], "wikipage": null}], "wikipages": [{"title": "AML Topic 200", "url": "https://www.fdic.gov/news/financial-institution-letters/2021/fil21046a.pdf"}], "annotations": [{"knowledge": [], "long_answer": "Banks are not required to incorporate the AML/CFT Priorities into their risk-based BSA compliance programs until the effective date of the final revised regulations. Banks may wish to start considering how they will incorporate the AML/CFT Priorities into their risk-based BSA compliance programs by assessing the potential related risks associated with the products and services they offer, the customers they serve, and the geographic areas in which they operate. The term 'bank' includes each agent, agency, branch, or office within the United States of banks, credit unions, savings associations, and foreign banks."}, {"knowledge": [{"content": "Banks are not required to incorporate the AML/CFT Priorities into their risk-based BSA compliance programs until the effective date of the final revised regulations.", "wikipage": "AML Topic 200"}, {"content": "Nevertheless, in preparation for any new requirements when those final rules are published, banks may wish to start considering how they will incorporate the AML/CFT Priorities into their risk-based BSA compliance programs, such as by assessing the potential related risks associated with the products and services they offer, the customers they serve, and the geographic areas in which they operate.", "wikipage": "AML Topic 200"}, {"content": "The term \u201cbank\u201d used here is as defined in Bank Secrecy Act regulations at 31 C.F.R. \u00a7 1010.100(d) and includes each agent, agency, branch, or office within the United States of banks, credit unions, savings associations, and foreign banks.", "wikipage": "AML Topic 200"}], "long_answer": "Banks are not required to incorporate the AML/CFT Priorities into their risk-based BSA compliance programs until the effective date of the final revised regulations.\n\nBanks may wish to start considering how they will incorporate the AML/CFT Priorities into their risk-based BSA compliance programs by assessing the potential related risks associated with the products and services they offer, the customers they serve, and the geographic areas in which they operate.\n\nThe term 'bank' includes each agent, agency, branch, or office within the United States of banks, credit unions, savings associations, and foreign banks."}], "sample_id": "aml-topic-200-3", "question": "Are banks currently required to incorporate AML/CFT Priorities into their risk-based BSA compliance programs?", "docs": [{"id": "doc-aml-topic-200-3-2", "title": "AML Topic 200 Part 2", "text": "ns, and foreign banks.\n6. 31 U.S.C. \u00a7 5318(h)(4)(D) (as amended by AML Act \u00a7 6101(b)(2)(C)).\n1\nBanks are not required to incorporate the AML/CFT Priorities into their risk-based BSA\ncompliance programs until the effective date of the final revised regulations. Nevertheless,\nin preparation for any new requirements when those final rules are published, banks may\nwish to start considering how they will incorporate the AML/CFT Priorities into their risk-\nbased BSA compliance programs, such as by ass", "score": 0.85, "summary": "ns, and foreign banks. 6. 31 U.S.C. \u00a7 5318(h)(4)(D) (as amended by AML Act \u00a7 6101(b)(2)(C)). 1 Banks are not required to incorporate the AML/CFT Prior...", "extraction": "ns, and foreign banks.\n6. 31 U.S.C. \u00a7 5318(h)(4)(D) (as amended by AML Act \u00a7 6101(b)(2)(C)).\n1\nBanks are not required to incorporate the AML/CFT Priorities into their risk-based BSA\ncompliance program"}], "answer": "Banks are not required to incorporate the AML/CFT Priorities into their risk-based BSA compliance programs until the effective date of the final revised regulations. Banks may wish to start considering how they will incorporate the AML/CFT Priorities into their risk-based BSA compliance programs by assessing the potential related risks associated with the products and services they offer, the customers they serve, and the geographic areas in which they operate. The term 'bank' includes each agent, agency, branch, or office within the United States of banks, credit unions, savings associations, and foreign banks.", "output": "Banks are not required to incorporate the AML/CFT Priorities into their risk-based BSA compliance programs until the effective date of the final revised regulations [1].", "intermediate": ["[Retrieval]", "Banks are not required to incorporate the AML/CFT Priorities into their risk-based BSA compliance programs until the effective date of the final revised regulations."]}, {"qa_pairs": [{"context": "No context provided", "question": "What is the purpose of the Interagency Statement on Model Risk Management for Bank Systems?", "short_answers": ["Address industry questions on model risk management", "Relate risk management principles to systems/models for BSA compliance"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "Which organizations were involved in issuing the statement on model risk management?", "short_answers": ["Federal Reserve", "FDIC", "OCC", "Financial Crimes Enforcement Network", "National Credit Union Administration"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "Does the Model Risk Management Guidance have the force of law?", "short_answers": ["Does not have the force of law"], "wikipage": null}], "wikipages": [{"title": "AML Topic 201", "url": "https://www.fdic.gov/news/press-releases/2021/pr21032a.pdf"}], "annotations": [{"knowledge": [], "long_answer": "The purpose of the Interagency Statement on Model Risk Management for Bank Systems is to address industry questions on model risk management and how the risk management principles described in the agencies\u2019 'Supervisory Guidance on Model Risk Management' relate to systems or models used by banks to assist in complying with the requirements of Bank Secrecy Act laws and regulations. The organizations involved in issuing the statement on model risk management are the Board of Governors of the Federal Reserve System, the Federal Deposit Insurance Corporation (FDIC), and the Office of the Comptroller of the Currency (OCC), in consultation with the Financial Crimes Enforcement Network and the National Credit Union Administration. No, the Model Risk Management Guidance does not have the force and effect of law."}, {"knowledge": [{"content": "This statement addresses how the risk management principles described in the agencies\u2019 'Supervisory Guidance on Model Risk Management' relate to systems or models used by banks to assist in complying with the requirements of Bank Secrecy Act laws and regulations.", "wikipage": "AML Topic 201"}, {"content": "The Board of Governors of the Federal Reserve System (Federal Reserve), the Federal Deposit Insurance Corporation (FDIC), and the Office of the Comptroller of the Currency (OCC) (collectively, the agencies), in consultation with the Financial Crimes Enforcement Network and the National Credit Union Administration, are issuing this statement...", "wikipage": "AML Topic 201"}, {"content": "The MRMG (like all supervisory guidance) does not have the force and effect of law.", "wikipage": "AML Topic 201"}], "long_answer": "The purpose of the Interagency Statement on Model Risk Management for Bank Systems is to address industry questions on model risk management and how the risk management principles described in the agencies\u2019 'Supervisory Guidance on Model Risk Management' relate to systems or models used by banks to assist in complying with the requirements of Bank Secrecy Act laws and regulations.\n\nThe organizations involved in issuing the statement on model risk management are the Board of Governors of the Federal Reserve System, the Federal Deposit Insurance Corporation (FDIC), and the Office of the Comptroller of the Currency (OCC), in consultation with the Financial Crimes Enforcement Network and the National Credit Union Administration.\n\nNo, the Model Risk Management Guidance does not have the force and effect of law."}], "sample_id": "aml-topic-201-1", "question": "What is the purpose of the Interagency Statement on Model Risk Management for Bank Systems?", "docs": [{"id": "doc-aml-topic-201-1-1", "title": "AML Topic 201 Part 1", "text": "Board of Governors of the Federal Reserve System\nFederal Deposit Insurance Corporation\nOffice of the Comptroller of the Currency\nInteragency Statement on Model Risk Management for Bank Systems Supporting Bank\nSecrecy Act/Anti-Money Laundering Compliance\nApril 9, 2021\nIntroduction\nThe Board of Governors of the Federal Reserve System (Federal Reserve), the Federal Deposit\nInsurance Corporation (FDIC), and the Office of the Comptroller of the Currency (OCC)\n(collectively, the agencies), in consulta", "score": 0.9, "summary": "Board of Governors of the Federal Reserve System Federal Deposit Insurance Corporation Office of the Comptroller of the Currency Interagency Statement...", "extraction": "This statement addresses how the risk management principles described in the agencies\u2019 'Supervisory Guidance on Model Risk Management' relate to systems or models used by banks to assist in complying with the requirements of Bank Secrecy Act laws and regulations."}], "answer": "The purpose of the Interagency Statement on Model Risk Management for Bank Systems is to address industry questions on model risk management and how the risk management principles described in the agencies\u2019 'Supervisory Guidance on Model Risk Management' relate to systems or models used by banks to assist in complying with the requirements of Bank Secrecy Act laws and regulations. The organizations involved in issuing the statement on model risk management are the Board of Governors of the Federal Reserve System, the Federal Deposit Insurance Corporation (FDIC), and the Office of the Comptroller of the Currency (OCC), in consultation with the Financial Crimes Enforcement Network and the National Credit Union Administration. No, the Model Risk Management Guidance does not have the force and effect of law.", "output": "Answer: The purpose of the Interagency Statement on Model Risk Management for Bank Systems is to provide guidance to banking organizations on managing model risk [1].", "intermediate": ["[Retrieval]", "Answer: The purpose of the Interagency Statement on Model Risk Management for Bank Systems is to provide guidance to banking organizations on managing model risk."]}, {"qa_pairs": [{"context": "No context provided", "question": "How can the MRMG be utilized by banks for BSA/AML compliance?", "short_answers": ["MRMG guides MRM framework.", "Assists with BSA/AML compliance."], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "Does the statement regarding MRMG require banks to change their existing risk management practices?", "short_answers": ["No change required.", "Use effective existing practices."], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "What is the purpose of the MRMG according to the agencies?", "short_answers": ["Articulates agencies' views.", "Assists banks in safe model use."], "wikipage": null}], "wikipages": [{"title": "AML Topic 201", "url": "https://www.fdic.gov/news/press-releases/2021/pr21032a.pdf"}], "annotations": [{"knowledge": [], "long_answer": "The MRMG can be a useful resource for guiding a bank\u2019s model risk management (MRM) framework, whether formal or informal, to assist with BSA/AML compliance. No, the statement does not suggest that a bank change existing risk management practices if the bank uses them to effectively manage its risk. The purpose of the MRMG is to articulate the agencies\u2019 general views regarding appropriate practices for model risk management (MRM) and to assist banks that rely on models to do so in a safe and sound manner."}, {"knowledge": [{"content": "This statement is intended to clarify how the MRMG may be a useful resource to guide a bank\u2019s MRM framework, whether formal or informal, and assist with BSA/AML compliance.", "wikipage": "AML Topic 201"}, {"content": "In addition, this statement does not suggest that a bank change existing risk management practices if the bank uses them to effectively manage its risk.", "wikipage": "AML Topic 201"}, {"content": "The MRMG is principles-based and articulates the agencies\u2019 general views regarding appropriate practices for MRM. It is intended to assist banks that rely on models to do so in a safe and sound manner.", "wikipage": "AML Topic 201"}], "long_answer": "The MRMG can be a useful resource for guiding a bank\u2019s model risk management (MRM) framework, whether formal or informal, to assist with BSA/AML compliance. It provides principles-based guidance to help banks manage their systems in a way that is consistent with safety and soundness principles.\n\nNo, the statement does not suggest that a bank change existing risk management practices if the bank uses them to effectively manage its risk.\n\nThe purpose of the MRMG is to articulate the agencies\u2019 general views regarding appropriate practices for model risk management (MRM) and to assist banks that rely on models to do so in a safe and sound manner."}], "sample_id": "aml-topic-201-2", "question": "How can the MRMG be utilized by banks for BSA/AML compliance?", "docs": [{"id": "doc-aml-topic-201-2-1", "title": "AML Topic 201 Part 1", "text": "A/AML) systems and models to quickly adapt to an evolving threat environment.\nThe agencies recognize that not all banks use models such as those described in the MRMG for\nBSA/AML compliance or have formalized model risk management (MRM) frameworks. This\nstatement is intended to clarify how the MRMG may be a useful resource to guide a bank\u2019s\nMRM framework, whether formal or informal, and assist with BSA/AML compliance. Whether\na bank characterizes a BSA/AML system (or portions of that system) as", "score": 0.9, "summary": "A/AML) systems and models to quickly adapt to an evolving threat environment. The agencies recognize that not all banks use models such as those descr...", "extraction": "This statement is intended to clarify how the MRMG may be a useful resource to guide a bank\u2019s MRM framework, whether formal or informal, and assist with BSA/AML compliance."}], "answer": "The MRMG can be a useful resource for guiding a bank\u2019s model risk management (MRM) framework, whether formal or informal, to assist with BSA/AML compliance. No, the statement does not suggest that a bank change existing risk management practices if the bank uses them to effectively manage its risk. The purpose of the MRMG is to articulate the agencies\u2019 general views regarding appropriate practices for model risk management (MRM) and to assist banks that rely on models to do so in a safe and sound manner.", "output": "Answer: The MRMG can be utilized by banks for BSA/AML compliance by using it as a resource to guide a bank's model risk management framework, whether formal or informal, and assist with BSA/AML compliance [1].", "intermediate": ["[Retrieval]", "Answer: The MRMG can be utilized by banks for BSA/AML compliance by using it as a resource to guide a bank's model risk management framework, whether formal or informal, and assist with BSA/AML compliance."]}, {"qa_pairs": [{"context": "No context provided", "question": "What is the scope of the term 'bank' as used in the interagency statement?", "short_answers": ["Includes agents, agencies, branches, or offices of banks", "Does not apply to credit unions"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "What flexibility do the MRMG principles provide for banks regarding model risk management?", "short_answers": ["Flexibility in developing and updating models", "Depends on bank's reliance and nature of models"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "Which regulatory guidelines establish standards for safety and soundness for banks?", "short_answers": ["12 CFR 208, Appendix D-1", "12 CFR 364, Appendix A", "12 CFR 30, Appendix A"], "wikipage": null}], "wikipages": [{"title": "AML Topic 201", "url": "https://www.fdic.gov/news/press-releases/2021/pr21032a.pdf"}], "annotations": [{"knowledge": [], "long_answer": "The term 'bank' as used in the interagency statement includes each agent, agency, branch, or office within the United States of banks, savings associations, and foreign banks. The MRMG principles provide flexibility for banks in developing, implementing, and updating models, including those used for BSA/AML activities. The regulatory guidelines establishing standards for safety and soundness for banks are the 'Interagency Guidelines Establishing Standards for Safety and Soundness,' which can be found at 12 CFR 208, Appendix D-1 (Federal Reserve); 12 CFR 364, Appendix A (FDIC); and 12 CFR 30, Appendix A (OCC)."}, {"knowledge": [{"content": "The term 'bank' is used here as in Bank Secrecy Act regulations at 31 CFR 1010.100(d) other than subsection (d)(6). This interagency statement does not apply to credit unions. The term 'bank' as used in this interagency statement does include each agent, agency, branch, or office within the United States of banks, savings associations, and foreign banks.", "wikipage": "AML Topic 201"}, {"content": "The MRMG principles provide flexibility for banks in developing, implementing and updating models, including those used for BSA/AML activities. While the MRMG provides a comprehensive discussion of all aspects of model risk management, the practical application of any principle discussed in the MRMG by a bank depends, in part, on the bank\u2019s reliance on, and the nature of, its models.", "wikipage": "AML Topic 201"}, {"content": "Refer to the 'Interagency Guidelines Establishing Standards for Safety and Soundness,' 12 CFR 208, Appendix D-1 (Federal Reserve); 12 CFR 364, Appendix A (FDIC); and 12 CFR 30, Appendix A (OCC).", "wikipage": "AML Topic 201"}], "long_answer": "The term 'bank' as used in the interagency statement includes each agent, agency, branch, or office within the United States of banks, savings associations, and foreign banks. However, it does not apply to credit unions.\n\nThe MRMG principles provide flexibility for banks in developing, implementing, and updating models, including those used for BSA/AML activities. The practical application of any principle discussed in the MRMG by a bank depends, in part, on the bank\u2019s reliance on, and the nature of, its models.\n\nThe regulatory guidelines establishing standards for safety and soundness for banks are the 'Interagency Guidelines Establishing Standards for Safety and Soundness,' which can be found at 12 CFR 208, Appendix D-1 (Federal Reserve); 12 CFR 364, Appendix A (FDIC); and 12 CFR 30, Appendix A (OCC)."}], "sample_id": "aml-topic-201-3", "question": "What is the scope of the term 'bank' as used in the interagency statement?", "docs": [{"id": "doc-aml-topic-201-3-1", "title": "AML Topic 201 Part 1", "text": "e \u201cSupervisory Guidance on Model Risk Management,\u201d Federal Reserve SR Letter 11-7; OCC Bulletin\n2011-12; and FDIC FIL 22-2017.\n2 The term \u201cbank\u201d is used here as in Bank Secrecy Act regulations at 31 CFR 1010.100(d) other than subsection\n(d)(6). This interagency statement does not apply to credit unions. The term \u201cbank\u201d as used in this interagency\nstatement does include each agent, agency, branch, or office within the United States of banks, savings associations,\nand foreign banks.\n3 Refer to the", "score": 0.9, "summary": "e \u201cSupervisory Guidance on Model Risk Management,\u201d Federal Reserve SR Letter 11-7; OCC Bulletin 2011-12; and FDIC FIL 22-2017. 2 The term \u201cbank\u201d is us...", "extraction": "The term 'bank' is used here as in Bank Secrecy Act regulations at 31 CFR 1010.100(d) other than subsection (d)(6). This interagency statement does not apply to credit unions. The term 'bank' as used in this interagency statement does include each agent, agency, branch, or office within the United States of banks, savings associations, and foreign banks."}], "answer": "The term 'bank' as used in the interagency statement includes each agent, agency, branch, or office within the United States of banks, savings associations, and foreign banks. The MRMG principles provide flexibility for banks in developing, implementing, and updating models, including those used for BSA/AML activities. The regulatory guidelines establishing standards for safety and soundness for banks are the 'Interagency Guidelines Establishing Standards for Safety and Soundness,' which can be found at 12 CFR 208, Appendix D-1 (Federal Reserve); 12 CFR 364, Appendix A (FDIC); and 12 CFR 30, Appendix A (OCC).", "output": "Answer: The term \"bank\" as used in the interagency statement does not include each agent, agency, branch, or office within the United States of banks, savings associations, and foreign banks [1].", "intermediate": ["[Retrieval]", "Answer: The term \"bank\" as used in the interagency statement does not include each agent, agency, branch, or office within the United States of banks, savings associations, and foreign banks."]}, {"qa_pairs": [{"context": "No context provided", "question": "What is the purpose of the FAQs issued by FinCEN and other federal banking agencies regarding suspicious activity reports?", "short_answers": ["Clarify SAR regulatory requirements", "Assist with compliance obligations"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "Which organizations collaborated to issue the FAQs on suspicious activity reporting?", "short_answers": ["FinCEN", "Federal Reserve", "FDIC", "NCUA", "OCC"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "How do the FAQs help financial institutions in relation to their resources?", "short_answers": ["Focus resources on compliance", "Clarify SAR requirements"], "wikipage": null}], "wikipages": [{"title": "AML Topic 202", "url": "https://www.fdic.gov/news/financial-institution-letters/2021/fil21005a.pdf"}], "annotations": [{"knowledge": [], "long_answer": "The FAQs issued by FinCEN and other federal banking agencies clarify the regulatory requirements related to suspicious activity reports (SARs) to assist financial institutions with their compliance obligations. The FAQs on suspicious activity reporting were issued collaboratively by the Financial Crimes Enforcement Network (FinCEN), the Board of Governors of the Federal Reserve System, the Federal Deposit Insurance Corporation, the National Credit Union Administration, and the Office of the Comptroller of the Currency. The FAQs help financial institutions by enabling them to focus resources on activities that are most relevant to compliance with suspicious activity reporting requirements."}, {"knowledge": [{"content": "The Financial Crimes Enforcement Network (FinCEN), jointly with the Board of Governors of the Federal Reserve System (Federal Reserve), the Federal Deposit Insurance Corporation (FDIC), the National Credit Union Administration (NCUA), and the Office of the Comptroller of the Currency (OCC) (collectively, the Federal banking agencies), and in consultation with the staff of certain other federal functional regulators, is issuing answers to frequently asked questions (FAQs) regarding suspicious activity reports (SARs) and other anti-money laundering (AML) considerations for financial institutions covered by SAR rules.1 The answers to these FAQs clarify the regulatory requirements related to SARs to assist such financial institutions with their compliance obligations...", "wikipage": "AML Topic 202"}, {"content": "The Financial Crimes Enforcement Network (FinCEN), jointly with the Board of Governors of the Federal Reserve System (Federal Reserve), the Federal Deposit Insurance Corporation (FDIC), the National Credit Union Administration (NCUA), and the Office of the Comptroller of the Currency (OCC) (collectively, the Federal banking agencies), and in consultation with the staff of certain other federal functional regulators, is issuing answers to frequently asked questions...", "wikipage": "AML Topic 202"}, {"content": "...The answers to these FAQs clarify the regulatory requirements related to SARs to assist such financial institutions with their compliance obligations, while enabling financial institutions to focus resources on activities that prod...", "wikipage": "AML Topic 202"}], "long_answer": "The FAQs issued by FinCEN and other federal banking agencies clarify the regulatory requirements related to suspicious activity reports (SARs) to assist financial institutions with their compliance obligations.\n\nThe FAQs on suspicious activity reporting were issued collaboratively by the Financial Crimes Enforcement Network (FinCEN), the Board of Governors of the Federal Reserve System, the Federal Deposit Insurance Corporation, the National Credit Union Administration, and the Office of the Comptroller of the Currency.\n\nThe FAQs help financial institutions by enabling them to focus resources on activities that are most relevant to compliance with suspicious activity reporting requirements."}], "sample_id": "aml-topic-202-1", "question": "What is the purpose of the FAQs issued by FinCEN and other federal banking agencies regarding suspicious activity reports?", "docs": [{"id": "doc-aml-topic-202-1-1", "title": "AML Topic 202 Part 1", "text": "Board of Governors of the Federal Reserve System\nFederal Deposit Insurance Corporation\nFinancial Crimes Enforcement Network\nNational Credit Union Administration\nOffice of the Comptroller of the Currency\nAnswers to Frequently Asked Questions Regarding Suspicious Activity Reporting\nand Other Anti-Money Laundering Considerations\nJanuary 19, 2021\nThe Financial Crimes Enforcement Network (FinCEN), jointly with the Board of Governors of the\nFederal Reserve System (Federal Reserve), the Federal Deposit", "score": 0.9, "summary": "Board of Governors of the Federal Reserve System Federal Deposit Insurance Corporation Financial Crimes Enforcement Network National Credit Union Admi...", "extraction": "The Financial Crimes Enforcement Network (FinCEN), jointly with the Board of Governors of the Federal Reserve System (Federal Reserve), the Federal Deposit Insurance Corporation (FDIC), the National Credit Union Administration (NCUA), and the Office of the Comptroller of the Currency (OCC) (collectively, the Federal banking agencies), and in consultation with the staff of certain other federal functional regulators, is issuing answers to frequently asked questions (FAQs) regarding suspicious activity reports (SARs) and other anti-money laundering (AML) considerations for financial institutions covered by SAR rules.1 The answers to these FAQs clarify the regulatory requirements related to SARs to assist such financial institutions with their compliance obligations..."}], "answer": "The FAQs issued by FinCEN and other federal banking agencies clarify the regulatory requirements related to suspicious activity reports (SARs) to assist financial institutions with their compliance obligations. The FAQs on suspicious activity reporting were issued collaboratively by the Financial Crimes Enforcement Network (FinCEN), the Board of Governors of the Federal Reserve System, the Federal Deposit Insurance Corporation, the National Credit Union Administration, and the Office of the Comptroller of the Currency. The FAQs help financial institutions by enabling them to focus resources on activities that are most relevant to compliance with suspicious activity reporting requirements.", "output": "Answer: The purpose of the FAQs issued by FinCEN and other federal banking agencies regarding suspicious activity reports is to provide guidance to financial institutions on their obligations under the Bank Secrecy Act (BSA) and its implementing regulations, including the filing of suspicious activity reports (SARs) [1].", "intermediate": ["[Retrieval]", "Answer: The purpose of the FAQs issued by FinCEN and other federal banking agencies regarding suspicious activity reports is to provide guidance to financial institutions on their obligations under the Bank Secrecy Act (BSA) and its implementing regulations, including the filing of suspicious activity reports (SARs)."]}, {"qa_pairs": [{"context": "No context provided", "question": "Can a financial institution maintain an account if law enforcement requests it, even if suspicious activity is detected?", "short_answers": ["Yes, if there's a 'keep open' request.", "Law enforcement request allows maintaining the account."], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "What is the purpose of the FAQs related to the Bank Secrecy Act (BSA)?", "short_answers": ["FAQs provide clarification.", "Developed from BSAAG recommendations."], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "Which entities are subject to Suspicious Activity Report (SAR) requirements under the Bank Secrecy Act?", "short_answers": ["Banks, casinos, and card clubs.", "Money services, brokers, and mutual funds."], "wikipage": null}], "wikipages": [{"title": "AML Topic 202", "url": "https://www.fdic.gov/news/financial-institution-letters/2021/fil21005a.pdf"}], "annotations": [{"knowledge": [], "long_answer": "Yes, a financial institution can maintain an account or customer relationship if it receives a written 'keep open' request from law enforcement, even if suspicious or potentially illicit activity has been identified. The FAQs are intended to provide clarification and do not alter existing BSA/AML legal or regulatory requirements. Entities subject to SAR requirements include banks, casinos and card clubs, money services businesses, brokers or dealers in securities, mutual funds, insurance companies, and futures commission merchants."}, {"knowledge": [{"content": "Can a financial institution maintain an account or customer relationship for which it has received a written 'keep open' request from law enforcement, even though the financial institution has identified suspicious or potentially illicit activity?", "wikipage": "AML Topic 202"}, {"content": "The answers to these FAQs neither alter existing BSA/AML legal or regulatory requirements, nor establish new supervisory expectations; they were developed in response to recent Bank Secrecy Act Advisory Group (BSAAG) recommendations, as described in more detail in FinCEN\u2019s Advance Notice of Proposed Rulemaking (ANPRM) on Anti-Money Laundering Program Effectiveness, published in September 2020.", "wikipage": "AML Topic 202"}, {"content": "Financial institutions subject to SAR requirements include: Banks (31 CFR \u00a7 1020.320), Casinos and Card Clubs (31 CFR \u00a7 1021.320), Money Services Businesses (31 CFR \u00a7 1022.320), Brokers or Dealers in Securities (31 CFR \u00a7 1023.320), Mutual Funds (31 CFR \u00a7 1024.320), Insurance Companies (31 CFR \u00a7 1025.320), Futures Commission Merchants.", "wikipage": "AML Topic 202"}], "long_answer": "Yes, a financial institution can maintain an account or customer relationship if it receives a written 'keep open' request from law enforcement, even if suspicious or potentially illicit activity has been identified.\n\nThe FAQs are intended to provide clarification and do not alter existing BSA/AML legal or regulatory requirements. They were developed in response to recommendations from the Bank Secrecy Act Advisory Group (BSAAG) and are described in FinCEN's Advance Notice of Proposed Rulemaking on Anti-Money Laundering Program Effectiveness.\n\nEntities subject to SAR requirements include banks, casinos and card clubs, money services businesses, brokers or dealers in securities, mutual funds, insurance companies, and futures commission merchants."}], "sample_id": "aml-topic-202-2", "question": "Can a financial institution maintain an account if law enforcement requests it, even if suspicious activity is detected?", "docs": [{"id": "doc-aml-topic-202-2-2", "title": "AML Topic 202 Part 2", "text": "ember 2020.2\nQuestion 1: Requests by Law Enforcement for Financial Institutions to Maintain Accounts\nCan a financial institution maintain an account or customer relationship for which it has\nreceived a written \u201ckeep open\u201d request from law enforcement, even though the financial\ninstitution has identified suspicious or potentially illicit activity?3\n1. Financial institutions subject to SAR requirements include: Banks (31 CFR \u00a7 1020.320), Casinos and Card Clubs (31\nCFR \u00a7 1021.320), Money Services B", "score": 0.85, "summary": "ember 2020.2 Question 1: Requests by Law Enforcement for Financial Institutions to Maintain Accounts Can a financial institution maintain an account o...", "extraction": "ember 2020.2\nQuestion 1: Requests by Law Enforcement for Financial Institutions to Maintain Accounts\nCan a financial institution maintain an account or customer relationship for which it has\nreceived "}], "answer": "Yes, a financial institution can maintain an account or customer relationship if it receives a written 'keep open' request from law enforcement, even if suspicious or potentially illicit activity has been identified. The FAQs are intended to provide clarification and do not alter existing BSA/AML legal or regulatory requirements. Entities subject to SAR requirements include banks, casinos and card clubs, money services businesses, brokers or dealers in securities, mutual funds, insurance companies, and futures commission merchants.", "output": "Answer: A financial institution may maintain an account if law enforcement requests it, even if suspicious activity is detected [1].", "intermediate": ["[Retrieval]", "Answer: A financial institution may maintain an account if law enforcement requests it, even if suspicious activity is detected."]}, {"qa_pairs": [{"context": "No context provided", "question": "What is a 'keep open' request in the context of anti-money laundering regulations?", "short_answers": ["Written request from law enforcement", "Maintain accounts despite suspicious activity"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "Is a financial institution obligated to comply with a 'keep open' request from law enforcement?", "short_answers": ["Not obligated", "May decide to maintain"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "What guidance is forthcoming under the Anti-Money Laundering Act of 2020 regarding 'keep open' requests?", "short_answers": ["Guidance required by Secretary of the Treasury", "Forthcoming guidance on 'keep open' requests"], "wikipage": null}], "wikipages": [{"title": "AML Topic 202", "url": "https://www.fdic.gov/news/financial-institution-letters/2021/fil21005a.pdf"}], "annotations": [{"knowledge": [], "long_answer": "A 'keep open' request is a written request from a law enforcement agency asking a financial institution to maintain certain accounts and customer relationships open, despite suspicious or potential criminal activity associated with the account. No, a financial institution is not obligated to comply with a 'keep open' request from law enforcement. Under the Anti-Money Laundering Act of 2020, the Secretary of the Treasury is required to issue guidance on the required elements of a 'keep open' request."}, {"knowledge": [{"content": "A financial institution may decide to maintain an account based on a written 'keep open' request from a law enforcement agency, however, it is not obligated to do so. The written request should be specific and indicate both that the law enforcement agency ha", "wikipage": "AML Topic 202"}, {"content": "A financial institution may decide to maintain an account based on a written 'keep open' request from a law enforcement agency, however, it is not obligated to do so.", "wikipage": "AML Topic 202"}, {"content": "Under the recently-enacted Anti-Money Laundering Act of 2020, the Secretary of the Treasury is required to issue guidance on the required elements of a keep open request, which is forthcoming.", "wikipage": "AML Topic 202"}], "long_answer": "A 'keep open' request is a written request from a law enforcement agency asking a financial institution to maintain certain accounts and customer relationships open, despite suspicious or potential criminal activity associated with the account. The request should be specific and indicate the law enforcement agency's interest.\n\nNo, a financial institution is not obligated to comply with a 'keep open' request from law enforcement. It may decide to maintain an account based on such a request, but it is not required to do so.\n\nUnder the Anti-Money Laundering Act of 2020, the Secretary of the Treasury is required to issue guidance on the required elements of a 'keep open' request. This guidance is forthcoming."}], "sample_id": "aml-topic-202-3", "question": "What is a 'keep open' request in the context of anti-money laundering regulations?", "docs": [{"id": "doc-aml-topic-202-3-2", "title": "AML Topic 202 Part 2", "text": "d elements\nof a keep open request, which is forthcoming. See \u00a7 6306 of the Anti-Money Laundering Act of 2020, Pub. L. No. 116-\n283, \u00a7\u00a7 6001 \u2013 6511 (enacted as Division F of the National Defense Authorization Act for Fiscal Year 2021).\n1\nYes. Law enforcement may have an interest in ensuring that certain accounts and customer\nrelationships remain open notwithstanding suspicious or potential criminal activity in\nconnection with the account. A financial institution may decide to maintain an account", "score": 0.85, "summary": "d elements of a keep open request, which is forthcoming. See \u00a7 6306 of the Anti-Money Laundering Act of 2020, Pub. L. No. 116- 283, \u00a7\u00a7 6001 \u2013 6511 (en...", "extraction": "d elements\nof a keep open request, which is forthcoming. See \u00a7 6306 of the Anti-Money Laundering Act of 2020, Pub. L. No. 116-\n283, \u00a7\u00a7 6001 \u2013 6511 (enacted as Division F of the National Defense Author"}], "answer": "A 'keep open' request is a written request from a law enforcement agency asking a financial institution to maintain certain accounts and customer relationships open, despite suspicious or potential criminal activity associated with the account. No, a financial institution is not obligated to comply with a 'keep open' request from law enforcement. Under the Anti-Money Laundering Act of 2020, the Secretary of the Treasury is required to issue guidance on the required elements of a 'keep open' request.", "output": "Answer: A keep open request is a request from a law enforcement agency to a financial institution to maintain an account or customer relationship that would otherwise be suspended or terminated due to suspicious or potential criminal activity [1].", "intermediate": ["[Retrieval]", "Answer: A keep open request is a request from a law enforcement agency to a financial institution to maintain an account or customer relationship that would otherwise be suspended or terminated due to suspicious or potential criminal activity."]}, {"qa_pairs": [{"context": "No context provided", "question": "What is the purpose of the joint fact sheet issued by the Federal Reserve, FDIC, FinCEN, NCUA, and OCC?", "short_answers": ["Provide clarity on risk-based approach", "Consistent with CDD requirements"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "How does the U.S. government view the charitable sector in terms of risk?", "short_answers": ["Charitable sector not seen as uniform risk"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "Why might some charities face difficulty in accessing financial services according to the joint fact sheet?", "short_answers": ["Difficulty accessing financial services", "Jeopardizes contributions to vulnerable"], "wikipage": null}], "wikipages": [{"title": "AML Topic 203", "url": "https://www.fdic.gov/news/financial-institution-letters/2020/fil20106a.pdf"}], "annotations": [{"knowledge": [], "long_answer": "The purpose of the joint fact sheet is to provide clarity to banks on how to apply a risk-based approach to charities and other non-profit organizations, consistent with the customer due diligence (CDD) requirements contained in FinCEN\u2019s 2016 CDD Final Rule. The U. Some charities have reported difficulty in obtaining and maintaining access to financial services, which jeopardizes the important contributions charities make to the most vulnerable."}, {"knowledge": [{"content": "The Board of Governors of the Federal Reserve System (Federal Reserve), the Federal Deposit Insurance Corporation (FDIC), the Financial Crimes Enforcement Network (FinCEN), the National Credit Union Administration (NCUA), and the Office of the Comptroller of the Currency (OCC) (collectively, the Agencies) are issuing this joint fact sheet to provide clarity to banks on how to apply a risk-based approach to charities and other non-profit organizations, consistent with the customer due diligence (CDD) requirements contained in FinCEN\u2019s 2016 CDD Final Rule.", "wikipage": "AML Topic 203"}, {"content": "The Agencies remind banks that the U.S. government does not view the charitable sector as a whole as presenting a uniform risk.", "wikipage": "AML Topic 203"}, {"content": "Some charities have reported difficulty in obtaining and maintaining access to financial services, jeopardizing the important contributions charities make to the most vulnerable.", "wikipage": "AML Topic 203"}], "long_answer": "The purpose of the joint fact sheet is to provide clarity to banks on how to apply a risk-based approach to charities and other non-profit organizations, consistent with the customer due diligence (CDD) requirements contained in FinCEN\u2019s 2016 CDD Final Rule.\n\nThe U.S. government does not view the charitable sector as a whole as presenting a uniform risk.\n\nSome charities have reported difficulty in obtaining and maintaining access to financial services, which jeopardizes the important contributions charities make to the most vulnerable."}], "sample_id": "aml-topic-203-1", "question": "What is the purpose of the joint fact sheet issued by the Federal Reserve, FDIC, FinCEN, NCUA, and OCC?", "docs": [{"id": "doc-aml-topic-203-1-2", "title": "AML Topic 203 Part 2", "text": "the National Credit Union Administration (NCUA), and the\nOffice of the Comptroller of the Currency (OCC) (collectively, the Agencies) are\nissuing this joint fact sheet to provide clarity to banks1 on how to apply a risk-\nbased approach to charities and other non-profit organizations (NPOs), consistent\nwith the customer due diligence (CDD) requirements contained in FinCEN\u2019s\n2016 CDD Final Rule.2 Some charities have reported difficulty in obtaining and\nmaintaining access to financial services, je", "score": 0.85, "summary": "the National Credit Union Administration (NCUA), and the Office of the Comptroller of the Currency (OCC) (collectively, the Agencies) are issuing this...", "extraction": "the National Credit Union Administration (NCUA), and the\nOffice of the Comptroller of the Currency (OCC) (collectively, the Agencies) are\nissuing this joint fact sheet to provide clarity to banks1 on "}], "answer": "The purpose of the joint fact sheet is to provide clarity to banks on how to apply a risk-based approach to charities and other non-profit organizations, consistent with the customer due diligence (CDD) requirements contained in FinCEN\u2019s 2016 CDD Final Rule. The U. Some charities have reported difficulty in obtaining and maintaining access to financial services, which jeopardizes the important contributions charities make to the most vulnerable.", "output": "Answer: The purpose of the joint fact sheet issued by the Federal Reserve, FDIC, FinCEN, NCUA, and OCC is to provide clarity to banks on how to apply a risk-based approach to charities and other non-profit organizations (NPOs), consistent with the customer due diligence (CDD) requirements contained in FinCEN's 2016 CDD Final Rule [1].", "intermediate": ["[Retrieval]", "Answer: The purpose of the joint fact sheet issued by the Federal Reserve, FDIC, FinCEN, NCUA, and OCC is to provide clarity to banks on how to apply a risk-based approach to charities and other non-profit organizations (NPOs), consistent with the customer due diligence (CDD) requirements contained in FinCEN's 2016 CDD Final Rule."]}, {"qa_pairs": [{"context": "No context provided", "question": "How should banks evaluate charities to mitigate money laundering risks?", "short_answers": ["Risk-based approach", "Evaluate particular characteristics"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "Does the joint fact sheet alter existing BSA/AML legal or regulatory requirements?", "short_answers": ["Does not alter BSA/AML requirements", "No new supervisory expectations"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "What is the United States' commitment regarding humanitarian assistance during the COVID-19 pandemic?", "short_answers": ["Ensure humanitarian assistance", "Reach at-risk populations"], "wikipage": null}], "wikipages": [{"title": "AML Topic 203", "url": "https://www.fdic.gov/news/financial-institution-letters/2020/fil20106a.pdf"}], "annotations": [{"knowledge": [], "long_answer": "Banks should apply a risk-based approach and evaluate charities according to their particular characteristics to determine whether they can effectively mitigate the potential risk some charities may pose. No, the joint fact sheet does not alter existing Bank Secrecy Act/Anti-Money Laundering (BSA/AML) legal or regulatory requirements, nor does it establish new supervisory expectations. The United States is committed to ensuring that humanitarian assistance continues to reach at-risk populations through legitimate and transparent channels, including during the COVID-19 pandemic."}, {"knowledge": [{"content": "Banks should apply the risk-based approach and evaluate charities according to their particular characteristics to determine whether they can effectively mitigate the potential risk some charities may pose.", "wikipage": "AML Topic 203"}, {"content": "This joint fact sheet does not alter existing Bank Secrecy Act/Anti-Money Laundering (BSA/AML) legal or regulatory requirements, nor does it establish new supervisory expectations.", "wikipage": "AML Topic 203"}, {"content": "The United States is committed to ensuring that humanitarian assistance continues to reach at-risk populations through legitimate and transparent channels, including during the COVID-19 pandemic.", "wikipage": "AML Topic 203"}], "long_answer": "Banks should apply a risk-based approach and evaluate charities according to their particular characteristics to determine whether they can effectively mitigate the potential risk some charities may pose.\n\nNo, the joint fact sheet does not alter existing Bank Secrecy Act/Anti-Money Laundering (BSA/AML) legal or regulatory requirements, nor does it establish new supervisory expectations.\n\nThe United States is committed to ensuring that humanitarian assistance continues to reach at-risk populations through legitimate and transparent channels, including during the COVID-19 pandemic."}], "sample_id": "aml-topic-203-2", "question": "How should banks evaluate charities to mitigate money laundering risks?", "docs": [{"id": "doc-aml-topic-203-2-1", "title": "AML Topic 203 Part 1", "text": "or unacceptably high risk of being used or exploited for money laundering,\nterrorist financing (ML/TF), or sanctions violations.3 The Agencies remind banks\nthat charities vary in their risk profiles and should be treated according to such\nprofiles. Banks should apply the risk-based approach and evaluate charities\naccording to their particular characteristics to determine whether they can\neffectively mitigate the potential risk some charities may pose. This approach\nhelps to minimize illicit fina", "score": 0.9, "summary": "or unacceptably high risk of being used or exploited for money laundering, terrorist financing (ML/TF), or sanctions violations.3 The Agencies remind ...", "extraction": "Banks should apply the risk-based approach and evaluate charities according to their particular characteristics to determine whether they can effectively mitigate the potential risk some charities may pose."}], "answer": "Banks should apply a risk-based approach and evaluate charities according to their particular characteristics to determine whether they can effectively mitigate the potential risk some charities may pose. No, the joint fact sheet does not alter existing Bank Secrecy Act/Anti-Money Laundering (BSA/AML) legal or regulatory requirements, nor does it establish new supervisory expectations. The United States is committed to ensuring that humanitarian assistance continues to reach at-risk populations through legitimate and transparent channels, including during the COVID-19 pandemic.", "output": "Banks should evaluate charities by assessing their risk profile, understanding their activities and operations, and reviewing their governance and management [1].", "intermediate": ["[Retrieval]", "Banks should evaluate charities by assessing their risk profile, understanding their activities and operations, and reviewing their governance and management."]}, {"qa_pairs": [{"context": "No context provided", "question": "What are the Customer Due Diligence requirements for financial institutions?", "short_answers": ["Customer Due Diligence requirements", "May 2016 document", "31 CFR Parts 1010, 1020, 1023, 1024, 1026"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "How does the U.S. Department of the Treasury support humanitarian assistance during the COVID-19 pandemic?", "short_answers": ["OFAC Fact Sheet", "OFAC compliance concerns", "Commitment to global humanitarian aid"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "What is the stance of agencies on charities and NPOs in relation to financial services?", "short_answers": ["Agencies recognize compliance", "Access to financial services", "Importance of fund transmission"], "wikipage": null}], "wikipages": [{"title": "AML Topic 203", "url": "https://www.fdic.gov/news/financial-institution-letters/2020/fil20106a.pdf"}], "annotations": [{"knowledge": [], "long_answer": "The Customer Due Diligence requirements for financial institutions are outlined in the document published in May 2016, referenced as 81 FR 29398, and can be found in 31 CFR Parts 1010, 1020, 1023, 1024, and 1026. The U. Agencies recognize that while some charities and NPOs have been misused to facilitate money laundering or terrorist financing, the vast majority comply with the law and properly support their humanitarian missions."}, {"knowledge": [{"content": "2. Customer Due Diligence Requirements for Financial Institutions, 81 FR 29398 (May 2016); see also 31 CFR Parts 1010, 1020, 1023, 1024, and 1026.", "wikipage": "AML Topic 203"}, {"content": "4. See U.S. Department of the Treasury\u2019s Office of Foreign Assets Control (OFAC) Fact Sheet: Provision of Humanitarian Assistance and Trade to Combat COVID-19 (April 16, 2020). See also OFAC Encourages Persons to Communicate OFAC Compliance Concerns Related to the Coronavirus Disease 2019 (COVID-19) (April 20, 2020) and U.S. Department of the Treasury\u2019s Press Release: Treasury Underscores Commitment to Global Flow of Humanitarian Aid in Face of Covid-19 Pandemic (April 9, 2020).", "wikipage": "AML Topic 203"}, {"content": "NPOs to have access to financial services, including the ability to transmit funds. Charities and other NPOs rely on banks to facilitate the flow of funds transfers in a timely fashion. Although some charities and other NPOs have been misused to facilitate ML/TF5 or evade sanctions, the Agencies recognize that the vast majority of charities and other NPOs comply with the law and properly suppo", "wikipage": "AML Topic 203"}], "long_answer": "The Customer Due Diligence requirements for financial institutions are outlined in the document published in May 2016, referenced as 81 FR 29398, and can be found in 31 CFR Parts 1010, 1020, 1023, 1024, and 1026.\n\nThe U.S. Department of the Treasury supports humanitarian assistance during the COVID-19 pandemic by providing guidance through the Office of Foreign Assets Control (OFAC) Fact Sheet and encouraging communication of OFAC compliance concerns related to COVID-19. They also underscore their commitment to the global flow of humanitarian aid.\n\nAgencies recognize that while some charities and NPOs have been misused to facilitate money laundering or terrorist financing, the vast majority comply with the law and properly support their humanitarian missions. They acknowledge the importance of NPOs having access to financial services, including the ability to transmit funds."}], "sample_id": "aml-topic-203-3", "question": "What are the Customer Due Diligence requirements for financial institutions?", "docs": [{"id": "doc-aml-topic-203-3-1", "title": "AML Topic 203 Part 1", "text": "ranch, or office within the United States of banks, savings associations,\ncredit unions, and foreign banks.\n2. Customer Due Diligence Requirements for Financial Institutions, 81 FR 29398 (May 2016); see also\n31 CFR Parts 1010, 1020, 1023, 1024, and 1026.\n3. National Terrorist Financing Risk Assessment (2018), p. 23.\n4. See U.S. Department of the Treasury\u2019s Office of Foreign Assets Control (OFAC) Fact Sheet:\nProvision of Humanitarian Assistance and Trade to Combat COVID-19 (April 16, 2020). See\na", "score": 0.9, "summary": "ranch, or office within the United States of banks, savings associations, credit unions, and foreign banks. 2. Customer Due Diligence Requirements for...", "extraction": "2. Customer Due Diligence Requirements for Financial Institutions, 81 FR 29398 (May 2016); see also 31 CFR Parts 1010, 1020, 1023, 1024, and 1026."}], "answer": "The Customer Due Diligence requirements for financial institutions are outlined in the document published in May 2016, referenced as 81 FR 29398, and can be found in 31 CFR Parts 1010, 1020, 1023, 1024, and 1026. The U. Agencies recognize that while some charities and NPOs have been misused to facilitate money laundering or terrorist financing, the vast majority comply with the law and properly support their humanitarian missions.", "output": "Answer: The Customer Due Diligence requirements for financial institutions are to identify and verify the identity of customers, understand their risk profile, and monitor transactions for suspicious activity [1].", "intermediate": ["[Retrieval]", "Answer: The Customer Due Diligence requirements for financial institutions are to identify and verify the identity of customers, understand their risk profile, and monitor transactions for suspicious activity."]}, {"qa_pairs": [{"context": "No context provided", "question": "What is the purpose of the joint statement issued by the Federal Reserve, FDIC, FinCEN, NCUA, and OCC regarding politically exposed persons?", "short_answers": ["Address due diligence questions", "Clarify BSA/AML requirements for PEPs"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "Which organizations collaborated to issue the joint statement on BSA due diligence requirements for PEPs?", "short_answers": ["Federal Reserve", "FDIC", "FinCEN", "NCUA", "OCC"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "How do the Agencies interpret the term 'politically exposed persons' in the context of U.S. public officials?", "short_answers": ["PEPs do not include U.S. public officials"], "wikipage": null}], "wikipages": [{"title": "AML Topic 204", "url": "https://www.fdic.gov/news/press-releases/2020/pr20092a.pdf"}], "annotations": [{"knowledge": [], "long_answer": "The purpose of the joint statement is to address due diligence questions raised by banks related to Bank Secrecy Act/Anti-Money Laundering (BSA/AML) regulatory requirements for customers whom banks may consider to be politically exposed persons (PEPs). The organizations that collaborated to issue the joint statement are the Board of Governors of the Federal Reserve System, the Federal Deposit Insurance Corporation, the Financial Crimes Enforcement Network, the National Credit Union Administration, and the Office of the Comptroller of the Currency. The Agencies do not interpret the term 'politically exposed persons' to include U."}, {"knowledge": [{"content": "The Board of Governors of the Federal Reserve System (Federal Reserve), the Federal Deposit Insurance Corporation (FDIC), the Financial Crimes Enforcement Network (FinCEN), the National Credit Union Administration (NCUA), and the Office of the Comptroller of the Currency (OCC) (collectively, the Agencies) are issuing this joint statement to address due diligence questions raised by banks related to Bank Secrecy Act/Anti-Money Laundering (BSA/AML) regulatory requirements for customers whom banks may consider to be politically exposed persons (PEPs).", "wikipage": "AML Topic 204"}, {"content": "The Board of Governors of the Federal Reserve System (Federal Reserve), the Federal Deposit Insurance Corporation (FDIC), the Financial Crimes Enforcement Network (FinCEN), the National Credit Union Administration (NCUA), and the Office of the Comptroller of the Currency (OCC) (collectively, the Agencies) are issuing this joint statement.", "wikipage": "AML Topic 204"}, {"content": "The Agencies do not interpret the term \u201cpolitically exposed persons\u201d to include U.S. public offic", "wikipage": "AML Topic 204"}], "long_answer": "The purpose of the joint statement is to address due diligence questions raised by banks related to Bank Secrecy Act/Anti-Money Laundering (BSA/AML) regulatory requirements for customers whom banks may consider to be politically exposed persons (PEPs).\n\nThe organizations that collaborated to issue the joint statement are the Board of Governors of the Federal Reserve System, the Federal Deposit Insurance Corporation, the Financial Crimes Enforcement Network, the National Credit Union Administration, and the Office of the Comptroller of the Currency.\n\nThe Agencies do not interpret the term 'politically exposed persons' to include U.S. public officials."}], "sample_id": "aml-topic-204-1", "question": "What is the purpose of the joint statement issued by the Federal Reserve, FDIC, FinCEN, NCUA, and OCC regarding politically exposed persons?", "docs": [{"id": "doc-aml-topic-204-1-2", "title": "AML Topic 204 Part 2", "text": "s Enforcement Network (FinCEN), the National\nCredit Union Administration (NCUA), and the Office of the Comptroller of the Currency (OCC)\n(collectively, the Agencies) are issuing this joint statement to address due diligence questions\nraised by banks1 related to Bank Secrecy Act/Anti-Money Laundering (BSA/AML) regulatory\nrequirements for customers whom banks may consider to be politically exposed persons (PEPs).2\nBanks have requested clarification on how to apply a risk-based approach to PEPs con", "score": 0.85, "summary": "s Enforcement Network (FinCEN), the National Credit Union Administration (NCUA), and the Office of the Comptroller of the Currency (OCC) (collectively...", "extraction": "s Enforcement Network (FinCEN), the National\nCredit Union Administration (NCUA), and the Office of the Comptroller of the Currency (OCC)\n(collectively, the Agencies) are issuing this joint statement t"}], "answer": "The purpose of the joint statement is to address due diligence questions raised by banks related to Bank Secrecy Act/Anti-Money Laundering (BSA/AML) regulatory requirements for customers whom banks may consider to be politically exposed persons (PEPs). The organizations that collaborated to issue the joint statement are the Board of Governors of the Federal Reserve System, the Federal Deposit Insurance Corporation, the Financial Crimes Enforcement Network, the National Credit Union Administration, and the Office of the Comptroller of the Currency. The Agencies do not interpret the term 'politically exposed persons' to include U.", "output": "Answer: The purpose of the joint statement issued by the Federal Reserve, FDIC, FinCEN, NCUA, and OCC regarding politically exposed persons is to provide clarification on how to apply a risk-based approach to politically exposed persons (PEPs) under the Bank Secrecy Act/Anti-Money Laundering (BSA/AML) regulations [1].", "intermediate": ["[Retrieval]", "Answer: The purpose of the joint statement issued by the Federal Reserve, FDIC, FinCEN, NCUA, and OCC regarding politically exposed persons is to provide clarification on how to apply a risk-based approach to politically exposed persons (PEPs) under the Bank Secrecy Act/Anti-Money Laundering (BSA/AML) regulations."]}, {"qa_pairs": [{"context": "No context provided", "question": "What does the term 'PEPs' refer to in the financial industry?", "short_answers": ["Politically exposed persons", "Foreign individuals with prominent public function"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "Why might politically exposed persons (PEPs) present a higher risk in financial transactions?", "short_answers": ["Higher risk of corruption", "Funds may be illicit"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "Are all politically exposed persons (PEPs) considered high risk by default?", "short_answers": ["Risk varies", "Not all PEPs are high risk"], "wikipage": null}], "wikipages": [{"title": "AML Topic 204", "url": "https://www.fdic.gov/news/press-releases/2020/pr20092a.pdf"}], "annotations": [{"knowledge": [], "long_answer": "In the financial industry, 'PEPs' refers to politically exposed persons, who are foreign individuals that are or have been entrusted with a prominent public function, as well as their immediate family members and close associates. PEPs may present a higher risk because, by virtue of their public position or relationship, their funds may be the proceeds of corruption or other illicit activity. No, not all PEPs are automatically considered higher risk."}, {"knowledge": [{"content": "BSA/AML regulations do not define PEPs, but the term is commonly used in the financial industry to refer to foreign individuals who are or have been entrusted with a prominent public function, as well as their immediate family members and close associates.", "wikipage": "AML Topic 204"}, {"content": "By virtue of this public position or relationship, these individuals may present a higher risk that their funds may be the proceeds of corruption or other illicit activity.", "wikipage": "AML Topic 204"}, {"content": "The level of risk associated with PEPs, however, varies and not all PEPs are automatically higher risk.", "wikipage": "AML Topic 204"}], "long_answer": "In the financial industry, 'PEPs' refers to politically exposed persons, who are foreign individuals that are or have been entrusted with a prominent public function, as well as their immediate family members and close associates.\n\nPEPs may present a higher risk because, by virtue of their public position or relationship, their funds may be the proceeds of corruption or other illicit activity.\n\nNo, not all PEPs are automatically considered higher risk. The level of risk associated with PEPs varies."}], "sample_id": "aml-topic-204-2", "question": "What does the term 'PEPs' refer to in the financial industry?", "docs": [{"id": "doc-aml-topic-204-2-1", "title": "AML Topic 204 Part 1", "text": "ials. BSA/AML regulations do not define PEPs, but the term is commonly used in the financial\nindustry to refer to foreign individuals who are or have been entrusted with a prominent public\nfunction, as well as their immediate family members and close associates. By virtue of this public\nposition or relationship, these individuals may present a higher risk that their funds may be the\nproceeds of corruption or other illicit activity. The level of risk associated with PEPs, however,\nvaries and not", "score": 0.9, "summary": "ials. BSA/AML regulations do not define PEPs, but the term is commonly used in the financial industry to refer to foreign individuals who are or have ...", "extraction": "BSA/AML regulations do not define PEPs, but the term is commonly used in the financial industry to refer to foreign individuals who are or have been entrusted with a prominent public function, as well as their immediate family members and close associates."}], "answer": "In the financial industry, 'PEPs' refers to politically exposed persons, who are foreign individuals that are or have been entrusted with a prominent public function, as well as their immediate family members and close associates. PEPs may present a higher risk because, by virtue of their public position or relationship, their funds may be the proceeds of corruption or other illicit activity. No, not all PEPs are automatically considered higher risk.", "output": "Answer: PEPs refers to Politically Exposed Persons in the financial industry [1].", "intermediate": ["[Retrieval]", "Answer: PEPs refers to Politically Exposed Persons in the financial industry."]}, {"qa_pairs": [{"context": "No context provided", "question": "What is the expectation for banks regarding customer due diligence (CDD) for politically exposed persons (PEPs)?", "short_answers": ["CDD should match PEP risk", "No unique CDD steps required for PEPs"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "Does the joint statement require banks to change their current risk management practices regarding PEPs?", "short_answers": ["No change required to current practices", "Banks can maintain existing risk management"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "Does the joint statement establish new regulatory requirements or supervisory expectations for handling PEPs?", "short_answers": ["No new regulatory requirements", "No new supervisory expectations"], "wikipage": null}], "wikipages": [{"title": "AML Topic 204", "url": "https://www.fdic.gov/news/press-releases/2020/pr20092a.pdf"}], "annotations": [{"knowledge": [], "long_answer": "The expectation for banks is that the level and type of customer due diligence (CDD) for politically exposed persons (PEPs) should be commensurate with the risks presented by the PEP relationship. No, the joint statement does not require banks to change their current risk management practices regarding politically exposed persons (PEPs). The joint statement does not establish new regulatory requirements or supervisory expectations for handling politically exposed persons (PEPs)."}, {"knowledge": [{"content": "The Agencies recognize that, consistent with a risk-based approach, the level and type of CDD should be commensurate with the risks presented by the PEP relationship. The CDD rule does not create a regulatory requirement, and there is no supervisory expectation, for banks to have unique, additional due diligence steps for customers who are considered PEPs.", "wikipage": "AML Topic 204"}, {"content": "In addition, it does not require banks to cease existing risk management practices if the bank considers them necessary to effectively manage risk.", "wikipage": "AML Topic 204"}, {"content": "This joint statement does not alter existing BSA/AML legal or regulatory requirements, nor does it establish new supervisory expectations.", "wikipage": "AML Topic 204"}], "long_answer": "The expectation for banks is that the level and type of customer due diligence (CDD) for politically exposed persons (PEPs) should be commensurate with the risks presented by the PEP relationship. The CDD rule does not create a regulatory requirement for unique, additional due diligence steps for PEPs, and there is no supervisory expectation for such measures. Instead, the CDD should be appropriate for the customer risk.\n\nNo, the joint statement does not require banks to change their current risk management practices regarding politically exposed persons (PEPs). Banks can continue their existing risk management practices if they consider them necessary to effectively manage risk.\n\nThe joint statement does not establish new regulatory requirements or supervisory expectations for handling politically exposed persons (PEPs). It does not alter existing BSA/AML legal or regulatory requirements."}], "sample_id": "aml-topic-204-3", "question": "What is the expectation for banks regarding customer due diligence (CDD) for politically exposed persons (PEPs)?", "docs": [{"id": "doc-aml-topic-204-3-2", "title": "AML Topic 204 Part 2", "text": "or banks to have unique,\nadditional due diligence steps for customers who are considered PEPs.5 Instead, the level and type\nof CDD should be appropriate for the customer risk.\nThis joint statement does not alter existing BSA/AML legal or regulatory requirements, nor does it\nestablish new supervisory expectations. In addition, it does not require banks to cease existing risk\nmanagement practices if the bank considers them necessary to effectively manage risk. Further,\nthis statement does not, and", "score": 0.85, "summary": "or banks to have unique, additional due diligence steps for customers who are considered PEPs.5 Instead, the level and type of CDD should be appropria...", "extraction": "or banks to have unique,\nadditional due diligence steps for customers who are considered PEPs.5 Instead, the level and type\nof CDD should be appropriate for the customer risk.\nThis joint statement doe"}], "answer": "The expectation for banks is that the level and type of customer due diligence (CDD) for politically exposed persons (PEPs) should be commensurate with the risks presented by the PEP relationship. No, the joint statement does not require banks to change their current risk management practices regarding politically exposed persons (PEPs). The joint statement does not establish new regulatory requirements or supervisory expectations for handling politically exposed persons (PEPs).", "output": "Banks are expected to have unique, additional due diligence steps for customers who are considered PEPs [1].", "intermediate": ["[Retrieval]", "Banks are expected to have unique, additional due diligence steps for customers who are considered PEPs."]}, {"qa_pairs": [{"context": "No context provided", "question": "What is the purpose of the joint statement issued by the Federal Reserve, FDIC, NCUA, and OCC?", "short_answers": ["Set policy for issuing cease and desist orders", "Address BSA/AML noncompliance"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "Which agencies are involved in issuing the joint statement on BSA/AML enforcement?", "short_answers": ["Federal Reserve", "FDIC", "NCUA", "OCC"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "Under which sections of law does the joint statement describe BSA/AML compliance provisions?", "short_answers": ["Section 8(s) of FDIA", "Section 206(q) of FCUA"], "wikipage": null}], "wikipages": [{"title": "AML Topic 205", "url": "https://www.fdic.gov/news/press-releases/2020/pr20091a.pdf"}], "annotations": [{"knowledge": [], "long_answer": "The joint statement sets forth the Agencies' policy on when a mandatory cease and desist order will be issued for noncompliance with certain Bank Secrecy Act/anti-money laundering requirements, particularly in relation to specific compliance provisions in section 8(s) of the Federal Deposit Insurance Act and section 206(q) of the Federal Credit Union Act. The agencies involved in issuing the joint statement are the Board of Governors of the Federal Reserve System, the Federal Deposit Insurance Corporation, the National Credit Union Administration, and the Office of the Comptroller of the Currency. The joint statement describes BSA/AML compliance provisions under section 8(s) of the Federal Deposit Insurance Act and section 206(q) of the Federal Credit Union Act."}, {"knowledge": [{"content": "The Board of Governors of the Federal Reserve System (\u201cFederal Reserve\u201d), the Federal Deposit Insurance Corporation (\u201cFDIC\u201d), the National Credit Union Administration (\u201cNCUA\u201d), and the Office of the Comptroller of the Currency (\u201cOCC\u201d), (an \u201cAgency\u201d or collectively the \u201cAgencies\u201d), are issuing this statement to set forth the Agencies\u2019 policy on the circumstances in which an Agency will issue a mandatory cease and desist order to address noncompliance with certain Bank Secrecy Act/anti-money laundering (\u201cBSA/AML\u201d) requirements,2 particularly in light of the specific BSA/AML compliance provisions in section 8(s) of the Federal Deposit Insurance Act (\u201cFDIA\u201d) and section 206(q) of the Federal Credit Union Act (\u201cFCUA\u201d).", "wikipage": "AML Topic 205"}, {"content": "The Board of Governors of the Federal Reserve System (\u201cFederal Reserve\u201d), the Federal Deposit Insurance Corporation (\u201cFDIC\u201d), the National Credit Union Administration (\u201cNCUA\u201d), and the Office of the Comptroller of the Currency (\u201cOCC\u201d), (an \u201cAgency\u201d or collectively the \u201cAgencies\u201d), are issuing this statement...", "wikipage": "AML Topic 205"}, {"content": "The Agencies\u2019 policy on the circumstances in which an Agency will issue a mandatory cease and desist order to address noncompliance with certain Bank Secrecy Act/anti-money laundering (\u201cBSA/AML\u201d) requirements,2 particularly in light of the specific BSA/AML compliance provisions in section 8(s) of the Federal Deposit Insurance Act (\u201cFDIA\u201d) and section 206(q) of the Federal Credit Union Act (\u201cFCUA\u201d).", "wikipage": "AML Topic 205"}], "long_answer": "The joint statement sets forth the Agencies' policy on when a mandatory cease and desist order will be issued for noncompliance with certain Bank Secrecy Act/anti-money laundering requirements, particularly in relation to specific compliance provisions in section 8(s) of the Federal Deposit Insurance Act and section 206(q) of the Federal Credit Union Act.\n\nThe agencies involved in issuing the joint statement are the Board of Governors of the Federal Reserve System, the Federal Deposit Insurance Corporation, the National Credit Union Administration, and the Office of the Comptroller of the Currency.\n\nThe joint statement describes BSA/AML compliance provisions under section 8(s) of the Federal Deposit Insurance Act and section 206(q) of the Federal Credit Union Act."}], "sample_id": "aml-topic-205-1", "question": "What is the purpose of the joint statement issued by the Federal Reserve, FDIC, NCUA, and OCC?", "docs": [{"id": "doc-aml-topic-205-1-1", "title": "AML Topic 205 Part 1", "text": "Board of Governors of the Federal Reserve System\nFederal Deposit Insurance Corporation\nNational Credit Union Administration\nOffice of the Comptroller of the Currency\nAugust 13, 2020\nJOINT STATEMENT ON ENFORCEMENT OF BANK SECRECY ACT/ ANTI-\nMONEY LAUNDERING REQUIREMENTS1\nThe Board of Governors of the Federal Reserve System (\u201cFederal Reserve\u201d), the\nFederal Deposit Insurance Corporation (\u201cFDIC\u201d), the National Credit Union\nAdministration (\u201cNCUA\u201d), and the Office of the Comptroller of the Currency (\u201c", "score": 0.9, "summary": "Board of Governors of the Federal Reserve System Federal Deposit Insurance Corporation National Credit Union Administration Office of the Comptroller ...", "extraction": "The Board of Governors of the Federal Reserve System (\u201cFederal Reserve\u201d), the Federal Deposit Insurance Corporation (\u201cFDIC\u201d), the National Credit Union Administration (\u201cNCUA\u201d), and the Office of the Comptroller of the Currency (\u201cOCC\u201d), (an \u201cAgency\u201d or collectively the \u201cAgencies\u201d), are issuing this statement to set forth the Agencies\u2019 policy on the circumstances in which an Agency will issue a mandatory cease and desist order to address noncompliance with certain Bank Secrecy Act/anti-money laundering (\u201cBSA/AML\u201d) requirements,2 particularly in light of the specific BSA/AML compliance provisions in section 8(s) of the Federal Deposit Insurance Act (\u201cFDIA\u201d) and section 206(q) of the Federal Credit Union Act (\u201cFCUA\u201d)."}], "answer": "The joint statement sets forth the Agencies' policy on when a mandatory cease and desist order will be issued for noncompliance with certain Bank Secrecy Act/anti-money laundering requirements, particularly in relation to specific compliance provisions in section 8(s) of the Federal Deposit Insurance Act and section 206(q) of the Federal Credit Union Act. The agencies involved in issuing the joint statement are the Board of Governors of the Federal Reserve System, the Federal Deposit Insurance Corporation, the National Credit Union Administration, and the Office of the Comptroller of the Currency. The joint statement describes BSA/AML compliance provisions under section 8(s) of the Federal Deposit Insurance Act and section 206(q) of the Federal Credit Union Act.", "output": "Answer: The purpose of the joint statement issued by the Federal Reserve, FDIC, NCUA, and OCC is to provide guidance to financial institutions on complying with the Bank Secrecy Act/Anti-Money Laundering (BSA/AML) requirements [1].", "intermediate": ["[Retrieval]", "Answer: The purpose of the joint statement issued by the Federal Reserve, FDIC, NCUA, and OCC is to provide guidance to financial institutions on complying with the Bank Secrecy Act/Anti-Money Laundering (BSA/AML) requirements."]}, {"qa_pairs": [{"context": "No context provided", "question": "What does the statement clarify regarding the enforcement of the BSA?", "short_answers": ["Clarifies enforcement of BSA", "Conditions for mandatory cease and desist orders"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "Does the statement create new expectations or standards for BSA compliance?", "short_answers": ["Does not create new expectations", "Provides general policy guidance"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "What authority does FinCEN have regarding civil money penalties under the BSA?", "short_answers": ["FinCEN can assess civil money penalties", "Independent authority under BSA"], "wikipage": null}], "wikipages": [{"title": "AML Topic 205", "url": "https://www.fdic.gov/news/press-releases/2020/pr20091a.pdf"}], "annotations": [{"knowledge": [], "long_answer": "The statement is intended to further clarify the Agencies\u2019 enforcement of the BSA and the conditions that require the issuance of a mandatory cease and desist order under sections 8(s) and 206(q). No, the statement does not create new expectations or standards. FinCEN has independent authority to assess civil money penalties under the BSA."}, {"knowledge": [{"content": "expectations or standards. Rather, it is intended to further clarify the Agencies\u2019 enforcement of the BSA and the conditions that require the issuance of a mandatory cease and desist order under sections 8(s) and 206(q).", "wikipage": "AML Topic 205"}, {"content": "expectations or standards. Rather, it is intended to further clarify the Agencies\u2019 enforcement of the BSA and the conditions that require the issuance of a mandatory cease and desist order under sections 8(s) and 206(q).", "wikipage": "AML Topic 205"}, {"content": "Likewise, the Financial Crimes Enforcement Network (\u201cFinCEN\u201d) has independent authority to assess civil money penalties under the BSA.", "wikipage": "AML Topic 205"}], "long_answer": "The statement is intended to further clarify the Agencies\u2019 enforcement of the BSA and the conditions that require the issuance of a mandatory cease and desist order under sections 8(s) and 206(q).\n\nNo, the statement does not create new expectations or standards. It is meant to provide general policy guidance.\n\nFinCEN has independent authority to assess civil money penalties under the BSA."}], "sample_id": "aml-topic-205-2", "question": "What does the statement clarify regarding the enforcement of the BSA?", "docs": [{"id": "doc-aml-topic-205-2-2", "title": "AML Topic 205 Part 2", "text": "ss the assessment of civil money penalties for violations of the BSA or its\nimplementing regulations. The Agencies have such authority under their general enforcement statutes. 12\nU.S.C. \u00a7\u00a7 1786(k)(2) and 1818(i)(2). Likewise, the Financial Crimes Enforcement Network (\u201cFinCEN\u201d)\nhas independent authority to assess civil money penalties under the BSA.\n3 12 U.S.C. \u00a7\u00a7 1786(q), 1818(s).\nexpectations or standards. Rather, it is intended to further clarify the Agencies\u2019\nenforcement of the BSA and the c", "score": 0.85, "summary": "ss the assessment of civil money penalties for violations of the BSA or its implementing regulations. The Agencies have such authority under their gen...", "extraction": "ss the assessment of civil money penalties for violations of the BSA or its\nimplementing regulations. The Agencies have such authority under their general enforcement statutes. 12\nU.S.C. \u00a7\u00a7 1786(k)(2)"}], "answer": "The statement is intended to further clarify the Agencies\u2019 enforcement of the BSA and the conditions that require the issuance of a mandatory cease and desist order under sections 8(s) and 206(q). No, the statement does not create new expectations or standards. FinCEN has independent authority to assess civil money penalties under the BSA.", "output": "Answer: The statement clarifies that the enforcement of the BSA is intended to further clarify the agencies' enforcement of the BSA and the consequences for violations [1].", "intermediate": ["[Retrieval]", "Answer: The statement clarifies that the enforcement of the BSA is intended to further clarify the agencies' enforcement of the BSA and the consequences for violations."]}, {"qa_pairs": [{"context": "No context provided", "question": "What is required under section 8(s) of the FDIA for BSA/AML compliance?", "short_answers": ["Establish and maintain BSA/AML compliance procedures.", "Assure and monitor compliance with BSA requirements."], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "What must be included in an agency's examination of an institution regarding BSA/AML compliance?", "short_answers": ["Review of BSA/AML compliance program.", "Describe any problems in examination reports."], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "What happens if an institution fails to establish or maintain a BSA/AML compliance program?", "short_answers": ["Agencies will tailor actions to address deficiencies.", "Failure to establish or correct compliance issues."], "wikipage": null}], "wikipages": [{"title": "AML Topic 205", "url": "https://www.fdic.gov/news/press-releases/2020/pr20091a.pdf"}], "annotations": [{"knowledge": [], "long_answer": "Under section 8(s) of the FDIA, each insured depository institution is required to establish and maintain procedures reasonably designed to assure and monitor the institution\u2019s compliance with the requirements of the BSA. An agency's examination of an institution must include a review of the institution\u2019s BSA/AML compliance program, and the reports of examination must describe any problem with the BSA/AML compliance program. If an institution fails to establish and maintain a BSA/AML compliance program, or fails to correct any problem with the BSA/AML compliance program, the Agencies will tailor actions to address the deficiencies specific to the institution."}, {"knowledge": [{"content": "Under section 8(s) of the FDIA and section 206(q) of the FCUA, each of the Agencies is directed to prescribe regulations requiring each insured depository institution to establish and maintain procedures reasonably designed to assure and monitor the institution\u2019s compliance with the requirements of the BSA (collectively, these procedures form the basis of each institution\u2019s \u201cBSA/AML compliance program\u201d).", "wikipage": "AML Topic 205"}, {"content": "Sections 8(s) and 206(q) require that each Agency\u2019s examination of an institution include a review of the institution\u2019s BSA/AML compliance program and that reports of examination describe any problem with the BSA/AML compliance program.", "wikipage": "AML Topic 205"}, {"content": "Finally, sections 8(s) and 206(q) state that if an institution has failed to establish and maintain a BSA/AML compliance program or has failed to correct any problem with the BSA/AML compliance program...", "wikipage": "AML Topic 205"}], "long_answer": "Under section 8(s) of the FDIA, each insured depository institution is required to establish and maintain procedures reasonably designed to assure and monitor the institution\u2019s compliance with the requirements of the BSA. These procedures form the basis of each institution\u2019s BSA/AML compliance program.\n\nAn agency's examination of an institution must include a review of the institution\u2019s BSA/AML compliance program, and the reports of examination must describe any problem with the BSA/AML compliance program.\n\nIf an institution fails to establish and maintain a BSA/AML compliance program, or fails to correct any problem with the BSA/AML compliance program, the Agencies will tailor actions to address the deficiencies specific to the institution."}], "sample_id": "aml-topic-205-3", "question": "What is required under section 8(s) of the FDIA for BSA/AML compliance?", "docs": [{"id": "doc-aml-topic-205-3-2", "title": "AML Topic 205 Part 2", "text": "ce with the requirements of the BSA (collectively, these procedures\nform the basis of each institution\u2019s \u201cBSA/AML compliance program\u201d). Sections 8(s) and\n206(q) require that each Agency\u2019s examination of an institution include a review of the\ninstitution\u2019s BSA/AML compliance program and that reports of examination describe any\nproblem with the BSA/AML compliance program. Finally, sections 8(s) and 206(q) state\nthat if an institution has failed to establish and maintain a BSA/AML compliance\nprogra", "score": 0.85, "summary": "ce with the requirements of the BSA (collectively, these procedures form the basis of each institution\u2019s \u201cBSA/AML compliance program\u201d). Sections 8(s) ...", "extraction": "ce with the requirements of the BSA (collectively, these procedures\nform the basis of each institution\u2019s \u201cBSA/AML compliance program\u201d). Sections 8(s) and\n206(q) require that each Agency\u2019s examination "}], "answer": "Under section 8(s) of the FDIA, each insured depository institution is required to establish and maintain procedures reasonably designed to assure and monitor the institution\u2019s compliance with the requirements of the BSA. An agency's examination of an institution must include a review of the institution\u2019s BSA/AML compliance program, and the reports of examination must describe any problem with the BSA/AML compliance program. If an institution fails to establish and maintain a BSA/AML compliance program, or fails to correct any problem with the BSA/AML compliance program, the Agencies will tailor actions to address the deficiencies specific to the institution.", "output": "Section 8(s) of the FDIA requires that each agency's examination of an institution include a review of the institution's BSA/AML compliance program and that reports of examination describe any problems with the BSA/AML compliance program [1].", "intermediate": ["[Retrieval]", "Section 8(s) of the FDIA requires that each agency's examination of an institution include a review of the institution's BSA/AML compliance program and that reports of examination describe any problems with the BSA/AML compliance program."]}, {"qa_pairs": [{"context": "No context provided", "question": "What agencies are involved in providing guidance on financial services for hemp-related businesses?", "short_answers": ["Federal Reserve System", "FDIC", "FinCEN", "Office of the Comptroller of the Currency", "Conference of State Bank Supervisors"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "What prompted the issuance of guidance regarding hemp-related financial services?", "short_answers": ["Agriculture Improvement Act of 2018", "removal of hemp as a Schedule I controlled substance"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "What future guidance is expected from FinCEN regarding hemp-related businesses?", "short_answers": ["FinCEN additional guidance", "review of USDA interim final rule"], "wikipage": null}], "wikipages": [{"title": "AML Topic 206", "url": "https://www.fdic.gov/sites/default/files/2024-03/pr19115a.pdf"}], "annotations": [{"knowledge": [], "long_answer": "The agencies involved are the Board of Governors of the Federal Reserve System, the Federal Deposit Insurance Corporation (FDIC), the Financial Crimes Enforcement Network (FinCEN), the Office of the Comptroller of the Currency, and the Conference of State Bank Supervisors. The issuance of guidance was prompted by the Agriculture Improvement Act of 2018 (2018 Farm Bill), which removed hemp as a Schedule I controlled substance under the Controlled Substances Act. FinCEN is expected to issue additional guidance after further reviewing and evaluating the U."}, {"knowledge": [{"content": "The Board of Governors of the Federal Reserve System, the Federal Deposit Insurance Corporation, the Financial Crimes Enforcement Network (FinCEN), and the Office of the Comptroller of the Currency in consultation with the Conference of State Bank Supervisors, are issuing this statement to provide clarity regarding the legal status of commercial growth and production of hemp.", "wikipage": "AML Topic 206"}, {"content": "The Agriculture Improvement Act of 2018 (2018 Farm Bill), which removed hemp as a Schedule I controlled substance under the Controlled Substances Act was signed into law on December 20, 2018.", "wikipage": "AML Topic 206"}, {"content": "FinCEN will issue additional guidance after further reviewing and evaluating the U.S. Department of Agriculture (USDA) interim final rule.", "wikipage": "AML Topic 206"}], "long_answer": "The agencies involved are the Board of Governors of the Federal Reserve System, the Federal Deposit Insurance Corporation (FDIC), the Financial Crimes Enforcement Network (FinCEN), the Office of the Comptroller of the Currency, and the Conference of State Bank Supervisors.\n\nThe issuance of guidance was prompted by the Agriculture Improvement Act of 2018 (2018 Farm Bill), which removed hemp as a Schedule I controlled substance under the Controlled Substances Act.\n\nFinCEN is expected to issue additional guidance after further reviewing and evaluating the U.S. Department of Agriculture (USDA) interim final rule."}], "sample_id": "aml-topic-206-1", "question": "What agencies are involved in providing guidance on financial services for hemp-related businesses?", "docs": [{"id": "doc-aml-topic-206-1-1", "title": "AML Topic 206 Part 1", "text": "Board of Governors of the Federal Reserve System\nFederal Deposit Insurance Corporation\nFinancial Crimes Enforcement Network\nOffice of the Comptroller of the Currency\nConference of State Bank Supervisors\nProviding Financial Services to Customers Engaged in Hemp-Related Businesses\nThe Board of Governors of the Federal Reserve System, the Federal Deposit Insurance\nCorporation, the Financial Crimes Enforcement Network (FinCEN), and the Office of the\nComptroller of the Currency in consultation with t", "score": 0.9, "summary": "Board of Governors of the Federal Reserve System Federal Deposit Insurance Corporation Financial Crimes Enforcement Network Office of the Comptroller ...", "extraction": "The Board of Governors of the Federal Reserve System, the Federal Deposit Insurance Corporation, the Financial Crimes Enforcement Network (FinCEN), and the Office of the Comptroller of the Currency in consultation with the Conference of State Bank Supervisors, are issuing this statement to provide clarity regarding the legal status of commercial growth and production of hemp."}], "answer": "The agencies involved are the Board of Governors of the Federal Reserve System, the Federal Deposit Insurance Corporation (FDIC), the Financial Crimes Enforcement Network (FinCEN), the Office of the Comptroller of the Currency, and the Conference of State Bank Supervisors. The issuance of guidance was prompted by the Agriculture Improvement Act of 2018 (2018 Farm Bill), which removed hemp as a Schedule I controlled substance under the Controlled Substances Act. FinCEN is expected to issue additional guidance after further reviewing and evaluating the U.", "output": "Answer: The Board of Governors of the Federal Reserve System, the Federal Deposit Insurance Corporation, the Financial Crimes Enforcement Network (FinCEN), and the Office of the Comptroller of the Currency in consultation with the Conference of State Bank Supervisors provide guidance on financial services for hemp-related businesses [1].", "intermediate": ["[Retrieval]", "Answer: The Board of Governors of the Federal Reserve System, the Federal Deposit Insurance Corporation, the Financial Crimes Enforcement Network (FinCEN), and the Office of the Comptroller of the Currency in consultation with the Conference of State Bank Supervisors provide guidance on financial services for hemp-related businesses."]}, {"qa_pairs": [{"context": "No context provided", "question": "What is the role of the USDA in regulating hemp production according to the 2018 Farm Bill?", "short_answers": ["USDA establishes hemp regulatory plan", "USDA issued interim final rule"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "How can state departments of agriculture participate in hemp regulation under the USDA's interim final rule?", "short_answers": ["Submit plans to USDA", "Plans must be USDA-approved"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "What happens if a state or tribal territory does not have a USDA-approved hemp production plan?", "short_answers": ["Federal licensing plan established", "Regulates hemp producers without state plans"], "wikipage": null}], "wikipages": [{"title": "AML Topic 206", "url": "https://www.fdic.gov/sites/default/files/2024-03/pr19115a.pdf"}], "annotations": [{"knowledge": [], "long_answer": "The USDA is responsible for establishing a regulatory plan for hemp production as stated in the 2018 Farm Bill. State departments of agriculture can participate in hemp regulation by submitting plans to the USDA for monitoring and regulating the domestic production of hemp. If a state or tribal territory does not have a USDA-approved hemp production plan, the USDA's interim final rule establishes a federal licensing plan to regulate hemp producers in those areas."}, {"knowledge": [{"content": "The 2018 Farm Bill states that hemp production shall be subject to a hemp production regulatory plan established by the USDA, the states, or tribal governments. On October 31, 2019, the USDA issued an interim final rule establishing the domestic hemp production regulatory program to facilitate the legal production of hemp, as set forth in the 2018 Farm Bill. Under the interim final rule, state departments of agriculture and tribal governments may submit plans for monitoring and regulating the domestic production of hemp to the USDA for approval. The interim final rule establishes a federal licensing plan for regulating hemp producers in states and tribal territories that do not have their own USDA-approved plans.", "wikipage": "AML Topic 206"}, {"content": "Under the interim final rule, state departments of agriculture and tribal governments may submit plans for monitoring and regulating the domestic production of hemp to the USDA for approval.", "wikipage": "AML Topic 206"}, {"content": "The interim final rule establishes a federal licensing plan for regulating hemp producers in states and tribal territories that do not have their own USDA-approved plans.", "wikipage": "AML Topic 206"}], "long_answer": "The USDA is responsible for establishing a regulatory plan for hemp production as stated in the 2018 Farm Bill. The USDA issued an interim final rule to facilitate legal hemp production, allowing state departments of agriculture and tribal governments to submit plans for monitoring and regulating hemp production. The USDA also establishes a federal licensing plan for states and tribal territories without USDA-approved plans.\n\nState departments of agriculture can participate in hemp regulation by submitting plans to the USDA for monitoring and regulating the domestic production of hemp. These plans must be approved by the USDA to be implemented.\n\nIf a state or tribal territory does not have a USDA-approved hemp production plan, the USDA's interim final rule establishes a federal licensing plan to regulate hemp producers in those areas."}], "sample_id": "aml-topic-206-2", "question": "What is the role of the USDA in regulating hemp production according to the 2018 Farm Bill?", "docs": [{"id": "doc-aml-topic-206-2-1", "title": "AML Topic 206 Part 1", "text": "te hemp production.4 The 2018 Farm Bill states that hemp\nproduction shall be subject to a hemp production regulatory plan established by the USDA, the\nstates, 5 or tribal governments.\nOn October 31, 2019, the USDA issued an interim final rule establishing the domestic hemp\nproduction regulatory program to facilitate the legal production of hemp, as set forth in the 2018\nFarm Bill.6 Under the interim final rule, state departments of agriculture and tribal governments\nmay submit plans for monitori", "score": 0.9, "summary": "te hemp production.4 The 2018 Farm Bill states that hemp production shall be subject to a hemp production regulatory plan established by the USDA, the...", "extraction": "The 2018 Farm Bill states that hemp production shall be subject to a hemp production regulatory plan established by the USDA, the states, or tribal governments. On October 31, 2019, the USDA issued an interim final rule establishing the domestic hemp production regulatory program to facilitate the legal production of hemp, as set forth in the 2018 Farm Bill. Under the interim final rule, state departments of agriculture and tribal governments may submit plans for monitoring and regulating the domestic production of hemp to the USDA for approval. The interim final rule establishes a federal licensing plan for regulating hemp producers in states and tribal territories that do not have their own USDA-approved plans."}], "answer": "The USDA is responsible for establishing a regulatory plan for hemp production as stated in the 2018 Farm Bill. State departments of agriculture can participate in hemp regulation by submitting plans to the USDA for monitoring and regulating the domestic production of hemp. If a state or tribal territory does not have a USDA-approved hemp production plan, the USDA's interim final rule establishes a federal licensing plan to regulate hemp producers in those areas.", "output": "The USDA is responsible for regulating hemp production according to the 2018 Farm Bill [1].", "intermediate": ["[Retrieval]", "The USDA is responsible for regulating hemp production according to the 2018 Farm Bill."]}, {"qa_pairs": [{"context": "No context provided", "question": "What happens if a state or tribal government does not have a regulatory plan for hemp production?", "short_answers": ["USDA regulates directly", "State or tribal prohibition"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "What are the requirements for hemp producers according to the USDA interim final rule?", "short_answers": ["Land information maintenance", "THC level testing", "Disposal of high THC plants", "Licensing"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "Is it possible to grow hemp without a USDA-issued license?", "short_answers": ["USDA-issued license required", "USDA-approved state"], "wikipage": null}], "wikipages": [{"title": "AML Topic 206", "url": "https://www.fdic.gov/sites/default/files/2024-03/pr19115a.pdf"}], "annotations": [{"knowledge": [], "long_answer": "In the absence of a state or tribal regulatory plan, hemp producers will be subject to regulation directly by the USDA unless the state or tribal government prohibits hemp production. The interim final rule includes requirements for maintaining information on the land where hemp is produced, testing hemp for tetrahydrocannabinol (THC) levels, disposing of plants with more than 0. Hemp may be grown only with a valid USDA-issued license or under a USDA-approved state."}, {"knowledge": [{"content": "absence of a state or tribal regulatory plan, hemp producers will be subject to regulation directly by the USDA unless the state or tribal government prohibits hemp production.", "wikipage": "AML Topic 206"}, {"content": "The interim final rule includes requirements for maintaining information on the land where hemp is produced, testing hemp for tetrahydrocannabinol (THC) levels, disposing of plants with more than 0.3 percent THC, and licensing for hemp producers.", "wikipage": "AML Topic 206"}, {"content": "Key Points \u2022 Consistent with the USDA interim final rule, hemp may be grown only with a valid USDA-issued license or under a USDA-approved state", "wikipage": "AML Topic 206"}], "long_answer": "In the absence of a state or tribal regulatory plan, hemp producers will be subject to regulation directly by the USDA unless the state or tribal government prohibits hemp production.\n\nThe interim final rule includes requirements for maintaining information on the land where hemp is produced, testing hemp for tetrahydrocannabinol (THC) levels, disposing of plants with more than 0.3 percent THC, and licensing for hemp producers.\n\nHemp may be grown only with a valid USDA-issued license or under a USDA-approved state."}], "sample_id": "aml-topic-206-3", "question": "What happens if a state or tribal government does not have a regulatory plan for hemp production?", "docs": [{"id": "doc-aml-topic-206-3-2", "title": "AML Topic 206 Part 2", "text": ". 31, 2019) (to be codified at\n7 CFR 990).\nPage 1 of 3\nabsence of a state or tribal regulatory plan, hemp producers will be subject to regulation directly\nby the USDA unless the state or tribal government prohibits hemp production.\nThe interim final rule includes requirements for maintaining information on the land where hemp\nis produced, testing hemp for tetrahydrocannabinol (THC) levels, disposing of plants with more\nthan 0.3 percent THC, and licensing for hemp producers. The USDA regulations", "score": 0.85, "summary": ". 31, 2019) (to be codified at 7 CFR 990). Page 1 of 3 absence of a state or tribal regulatory plan, hemp producers will be subject to regulation dire...", "extraction": ". 31, 2019) (to be codified at\n7 CFR 990).\nPage 1 of 3\nabsence of a state or tribal regulatory plan, hemp producers will be subject to regulation directly\nby the USDA unless the state or tribal govern"}], "answer": "In the absence of a state or tribal regulatory plan, hemp producers will be subject to regulation directly by the USDA unless the state or tribal government prohibits hemp production. The interim final rule includes requirements for maintaining information on the land where hemp is produced, testing hemp for tetrahydrocannabinol (THC) levels, disposing of plants with more than 0. Hemp may be grown only with a valid USDA-issued license or under a USDA-approved state.", "output": "If a state or tribal government does not have a regulatory plan for hemp production, hemp producers will be subject to regulation directly by the USDA [1].", "intermediate": ["[Retrieval]", "If a state or tribal government does not have a regulatory plan for hemp production, hemp producers will be subject to regulation directly by the USDA."]}, {"qa_pairs": [{"context": "No context provided", "question": "What is the purpose of the joint statement issued by the Federal Reserve and other agencies on July 22, 2019?", "short_answers": ["Emphasize risk-focused BSA/AML examinations", "Improve transparency in examination approach"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "Which agencies are involved in the issuance of the joint statement regarding the BSA/AML compliance programs?", "short_answers": ["Federal Reserve", "FDIC", "NCUA", "OCC", "FinCEN"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "Does the joint statement introduce any new requirements for BSA/AML compliance programs?", "short_answers": ["No new requirements", "Improve transparency"], "wikipage": null}], "wikipages": [{"title": "AML Topic 207", "url": "https://www.fdic.gov/news/press-releases/2019/pr19065a.pdf"}], "annotations": [{"knowledge": [], "long_answer": "The joint statement issued by the Federal Reserve and other agencies on July 22, 2019, aims to emphasize a risk-focused approach to examinations of banks' Bank Secrecy Act/anti-money laundering (BSA/AML) compliance programs. The agencies involved in the issuance of the joint statement regarding the BSA/AML compliance programs include the Board of Governors of the Federal Reserve System, the Federal Deposit Insurance Corporation, the National Credit Union Administration, the Office of the Comptroller of the Currency, and the U. No, the joint statement does not establish new requirements for BSA/AML compliance programs."}, {"knowledge": [{"content": "This statement is being issued as part of a broader effort to reinforce and enhance the effectiveness and efficiency of the BSA/AML regime. This statement is intended to improve transparency into the risk-focused approach used for planning and performing BSA/AML examinations and does not establish new requirements.", "wikipage": "AML Topic 207"}, {"content": "The Board of Governors of the Federal Reserve System, the Federal Deposit Insurance Corporation, the National Credit Union Administration, the Office of the Comptroller of the Currency (collectively, the federal banking agencies), and the U.S. Department of the Treasury\u2019s Financial Crimes Enforcement Network (FinCEN) are issuing this joint statement.", "wikipage": "AML Topic 207"}, {"content": "This statement is intended to improve transparency into the risk-focused approach used for planning and performing BSA/AML examinations and does not establish new requirements.", "wikipage": "AML Topic 207"}], "long_answer": "The joint statement issued by the Federal Reserve and other agencies on July 22, 2019, aims to emphasize a risk-focused approach to examinations of banks' Bank Secrecy Act/anti-money laundering (BSA/AML) compliance programs. It seeks to improve transparency into the risk-focused approach used for planning and performing BSA/AML examinations and does not establish new requirements.\n\nThe agencies involved in the issuance of the joint statement regarding the BSA/AML compliance programs include the Board of Governors of the Federal Reserve System, the Federal Deposit Insurance Corporation, the National Credit Union Administration, the Office of the Comptroller of the Currency, and the U.S. Department of the Treasury\u2019s Financial Crimes Enforcement Network (FinCEN).\n\nNo, the joint statement does not establish new requirements for BSA/AML compliance programs. It is intended to improve transparency into the risk-focused approach used for planning and performing BSA/AML examinations."}], "sample_id": "aml-topic-207-1", "question": "What is the purpose of the joint statement issued by the Federal Reserve and other agencies on July 22, 2019?", "docs": [{"id": "doc-aml-topic-207-1-2", "title": "AML Topic 207 Part 2", "text": "ollectively, the\nfederal banking agencies), and the U.S. Department of the Treasury\u2019s Financial Crimes Enforcement\nNetwork (FinCEN) are issuing this joint statement to emphasize their risk-focused approach to\nexaminations of banks\u2019 Bank Secrecy Act1/anti-money laundering (BSA/AML) compliance programs.\nThis statement is being issued as part of a broader effort to reinforce and enhance the effectiveness and\nefficiency of the BSA/AML regime.2 This statement is intended to improve transparency into", "score": 0.85, "summary": "ollectively, the federal banking agencies), and the U.S. Department of the Treasury\u2019s Financial Crimes Enforcement Network (FinCEN) are issuing this j...", "extraction": "ollectively, the\nfederal banking agencies), and the U.S. Department of the Treasury\u2019s Financial Crimes Enforcement\nNetwork (FinCEN) are issuing this joint statement to emphasize their risk-focused app"}], "answer": "The joint statement issued by the Federal Reserve and other agencies on July 22, 2019, aims to emphasize a risk-focused approach to examinations of banks' Bank Secrecy Act/anti-money laundering (BSA/AML) compliance programs. The agencies involved in the issuance of the joint statement regarding the BSA/AML compliance programs include the Board of Governors of the Federal Reserve System, the Federal Deposit Insurance Corporation, the National Credit Union Administration, the Office of the Comptroller of the Currency, and the U. No, the joint statement does not establish new requirements for BSA/AML compliance programs.", "output": "Answer: The purpose of the joint statement issued by the Federal Reserve and other agencies on July 22, 2019 is to emphasize their risk-focused approach to examinations of banks' Bank Secrecy Act/anti-money laundering (BSA/AML) compliance programs [1].", "intermediate": ["[Retrieval]", "Answer: The purpose of the joint statement issued by the Federal Reserve and other agencies on July 22, 2019 is to emphasize their risk-focused approach to examinations of banks' Bank Secrecy Act/anti-money laundering (BSA/AML) compliance programs."]}, {"qa_pairs": [{"context": "No context provided", "question": "What are the statutory requirements for banks to establish a BSA/AML compliance program?", "short_answers": ["Federal Deposit Insurance Act", "Federal Credit Union Act", "BSA/AML compliance program"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "How do federal banking agencies ensure banks comply with BSA/AML requirements?", "short_answers": ["Review during examination cycle", "Ensure BSA compliance"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "Why is a risk-based compliance program important for banks?", "short_answers": ["Allocate resources", "Identify illicit activity", "Sound risk management"], "wikipage": null}], "wikipages": [{"title": "AML Topic 207", "url": "https://www.fdic.gov/news/press-releases/2019/pr19065a.pdf"}], "annotations": [{"knowledge": [], "long_answer": "Under section 8(s) of the Federal Deposit Insurance Act and section 206 of the Federal Credit Union Act, federal banking agencies have prescribed regulations requiring each bank to establish and maintain procedures reasonably designed to assure and monitor compliance with the requirements of the BSA. Federal banking agencies review banks' BSA/AML compliance programs during each examination cycle to ensure compliance with the requirements of the BSA. A risk-based compliance program is crucial because it enables a bank to allocate compliance resources commensurate with its risk, helping to identify and report potential money laundering, terrorist financing, and other illicit financial activity."}, {"knowledge": [{"content": "Under existing statutory requirements, specifically section 8(s) of the Federal Deposit Insurance Act and section 206 of the Federal Credit Union Act, the federal banking agencies have prescribed regulations requiring each bank to establish and maintain procedures reasonably designed to assure and monitor compliance with the requirements of the BSA (collectively, these procedures form the basis of each bank\u2019s \u201cBSA/AML compliance program\u201d).", "wikipage": "AML Topic 207"}, {"content": "In addition, pursuant to these statutes, the federal banking agencies review banks\u2019 BSA/AML compliance programs during each examination cycle.", "wikipage": "AML Topic 207"}, {"content": "To assure that BSA/AML compliance programs are reasonably designed to meet the requirements of the BSA, banks structure their compliance programs to be risk-based and to identify and report potential money laundering, terrorist financing, and other illicit financial activity. A risk-based compliance program enables a bank to allocate compliance resources commensurate with its risk. A bank\u2019s well-developed risk assessment is a critical part of sound risk management and assists examiners.", "wikipage": "AML Topic 207"}], "long_answer": "Under section 8(s) of the Federal Deposit Insurance Act and section 206 of the Federal Credit Union Act, federal banking agencies have prescribed regulations requiring each bank to establish and maintain procedures reasonably designed to assure and monitor compliance with the requirements of the BSA. These procedures form the basis of each bank\u2019s BSA/AML compliance program.\n\nFederal banking agencies review banks' BSA/AML compliance programs during each examination cycle to ensure compliance with the requirements of the BSA.\n\nA risk-based compliance program is crucial because it enables a bank to allocate compliance resources commensurate with its risk, helping to identify and report potential money laundering, terrorist financing, and other illicit financial activity. A well-developed risk assessment is also a critical part of sound risk management and assists examiners."}], "sample_id": "aml-topic-207-2", "question": "What are the statutory requirements for banks to establish a BSA/AML compliance program?", "docs": [{"id": "doc-aml-topic-207-2-1", "title": "AML Topic 207 Part 1", "text": "g practices for\nrisk-focused safety and soundness examinations.3\nUnder existing statutory requirements, specifically section 8(s) of the Federal Deposit Insurance Act and\nsection 206 of the Federal Credit Union Act, the federal banking agencies have prescribed regulations\nrequiring each bank4 to establish and maintain procedures reasonably designed to assure and monitor\ncompliance with the requirements of the BSA (collectively, these procedures form the basis of each\nbank\u2019s \u201cBSA/AML compliance p", "score": 0.9, "summary": "g practices for risk-focused safety and soundness examinations.3 Under existing statutory requirements, specifically section 8(s) of the Federal Depos...", "extraction": "Under existing statutory requirements, specifically section 8(s) of the Federal Deposit Insurance Act and section 206 of the Federal Credit Union Act, the federal banking agencies have prescribed regulations requiring each bank to establish and maintain procedures reasonably designed to assure and monitor compliance with the requirements of the BSA (collectively, these procedures form the basis of each bank\u2019s \u201cBSA/AML compliance program\u201d)."}], "answer": "Under section 8(s) of the Federal Deposit Insurance Act and section 206 of the Federal Credit Union Act, federal banking agencies have prescribed regulations requiring each bank to establish and maintain procedures reasonably designed to assure and monitor compliance with the requirements of the BSA. Federal banking agencies review banks' BSA/AML compliance programs during each examination cycle to ensure compliance with the requirements of the BSA. A risk-based compliance program is crucial because it enables a bank to allocate compliance resources commensurate with its risk, helping to identify and report potential money laundering, terrorist financing, and other illicit financial activity.", "output": "Answer: Banks must establish a BSA/AML compliance program that includes a system of internal controls, policies, and procedures reasonably designed to assure and monitor compliance with the BSA and its implementing regulations [1].", "intermediate": ["[Retrieval]", "Answer: Banks must establish a BSA/AML compliance program that includes a system of internal controls, policies, and procedures reasonably designed to assure and monitor compliance with the BSA and its implementing regulations."]}, {"qa_pairs": [{"context": "No context provided", "question": "What is the primary purpose of the Bank Secrecy Act (BSA) as outlined in 31 USC \u00a7 5311?", "short_answers": ["Require useful reports", "Protect against terrorism"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "How does the Bank Secrecy Act define a 'bank' under its regulations?", "short_answers": ["Includes agents, branches", "Covers foreign banks"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "What are some examples of interagency statements related to the BSA mentioned in the content?", "short_answers": ["Interagency Statement on BSA Resources", "Joint Statement on Money Laundering"], "wikipage": null}], "wikipages": [{"title": "AML Topic 207", "url": "https://www.fdic.gov/news/press-releases/2019/pr19065a.pdf"}], "annotations": [{"knowledge": [], "long_answer": "The primary purpose of the Bank Secrecy Act (BSA), as outlined in 31 USC \u00a7 5311, is to require certain reports or records that have a high degree of usefulness in criminal, tax, or regulatory investigations or proceedings, or in the conduct of intelligence or counterintelligence activities, including analysis, to protect against international terrorism. Under the BSA, the term 'bank' is defined in 31 CFR 1010. Examples of interagency statements related to the BSA mentioned in the content include the October 3, 2018, Interagency Statement on Sharing Bank Secrecy Act Resources and the December 3, 2018, Joint Statement on Innovative Efforts to Combat Money Laundering and Terrorist Financing."}, {"knowledge": [{"content": "stated in 31 USC \u00a7 5311, the purpose of the BSA is to require certain reports or records where they have a high degree of usefulness in criminal, tax, or regulatory investigations or proceedings, or in the conduct of intelligence or counterintelligence activities, including analysis, to protect against international terrorism.", "wikipage": "AML Topic 207"}, {"content": "Under the BSA the term \u201cbank\u201d is defined in 31 CFR 1010.100(d) and includes each agent, agency, branch or office within the United States of banks, savings associations, credit unions, and foreign banks.", "wikipage": "AML Topic 207"}, {"content": "See, for example, the October 3, 2018, Interagency Statement on Sharing Bank Secrecy Act Resources and the December 3, 2018, Joint Statement on Innovative Efforts to Combat Money Laundering and Terrorist Financing, issued by the federal banking agencies and FinCEN.", "wikipage": "AML Topic 207"}], "long_answer": "The primary purpose of the Bank Secrecy Act (BSA), as outlined in 31 USC \u00a7 5311, is to require certain reports or records that have a high degree of usefulness in criminal, tax, or regulatory investigations or proceedings, or in the conduct of intelligence or counterintelligence activities, including analysis, to protect against international terrorism.\n\nUnder the BSA, the term 'bank' is defined in 31 CFR 1010.100(d) and includes each agent, agency, branch, or office within the United States of banks, savings associations, credit unions, and foreign banks.\n\nExamples of interagency statements related to the BSA mentioned in the content include the October 3, 2018, Interagency Statement on Sharing Bank Secrecy Act Resources and the December 3, 2018, Joint Statement on Innovative Efforts to Combat Money Laundering and Terrorist Financing."}], "sample_id": "aml-topic-207-3", "question": "What is the primary purpose of the Bank Secrecy Act (BSA) as outlined in 31 USC \u00a7 5311?", "docs": [{"id": "doc-aml-topic-207-3-1", "title": "AML Topic 207 Part 1", "text": "stated in 31 USC \u00a7 5311, the purpose of the BSA is to require certain reports or records where they have a high degree of\nusefulness in criminal, tax, or regulatory investigations or proceedings, or in the conduct of intelligence or counterintelligence\nactivities, including analysis, to protect against international terrorism.\n2 See, for example, the October 3, 2018, Interagency Statement on Sharing Bank Secrecy Act Resources and the December 3,\n2018, Joint Statement on Innovative Efforts to Com", "score": 0.9, "summary": "stated in 31 USC \u00a7 5311, the purpose of the BSA is to require certain reports or records where they have a high degree of usefulness in criminal, tax,...", "extraction": "stated in 31 USC \u00a7 5311, the purpose of the BSA is to require certain reports or records where they have a high degree of usefulness in criminal, tax, or regulatory investigations or proceedings, or in the conduct of intelligence or counterintelligence activities, including analysis, to protect against international terrorism."}], "answer": "The primary purpose of the Bank Secrecy Act (BSA), as outlined in 31 USC \u00a7 5311, is to require certain reports or records that have a high degree of usefulness in criminal, tax, or regulatory investigations or proceedings, or in the conduct of intelligence or counterintelligence activities, including analysis, to protect against international terrorism. Under the BSA, the term 'bank' is defined in 31 CFR 1010. Examples of interagency statements related to the BSA mentioned in the content include the October 3, 2018, Interagency Statement on Sharing Bank Secrecy Act Resources and the December 3, 2018, Joint Statement on Innovative Efforts to Combat Money Laundering and Terrorist Financing.", "output": "Answer: The primary purpose of the Bank Secrecy Act (BSA) as outlined in 31 USC \u00a7 5311 is to require certain reports or records where they have a high degree of usefulness in criminal, tax, or regulatory investigations or proceedings, or in the conduct of intelligence or counterintelligence activities, including analysis, to protect against international terrorism [1].", "intermediate": ["[Retrieval]", "Answer: The primary purpose of the Bank Secrecy Act (BSA) as outlined in 31 USC \u00a7 5311 is to require certain reports or records where they have a high degree of usefulness in criminal, tax, or regulatory investigations or proceedings, or in the conduct of intelligence or counterintelligence activities, including analysis, to protect against international terrorism."]}, {"qa_pairs": [{"context": "No context provided", "question": "What change did the Economic Growth Act introduce regarding the on-site examination cycle for qualifying IDIs?", "short_answers": ["Economic Growth Act broadened eligibility for 18-month cycle", "Previously limited to IDIs under $1 billion"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "When did the final rules related to the Economic Growth Act amendments become effective?", "short_answers": ["Effective January 28, 2019"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "Which act does the on-site examination cycle for U.S. branches and agencies of foreign banks align with?", "short_answers": ["Consistent with International Banking Act of 1978"], "wikipage": null}], "wikipages": [{"title": "AML Topic 208", "url": "https://www.fdic.gov/news/board/2018/2018-12-18-notice-sum-g-fr.pdf"}], "annotations": [{"knowledge": [], "long_answer": "Prior to the enactment of the Economic Growth Act, only qualifying IDIs with under $1 billion in total assets were eligible for an 18-month on-site examination cycle. The final rules related to the amendments of the Economic Growth Act became effective on January 28, 2019. The on-site examination cycle for U."}, {"knowledge": [{"content": "Prior to the enactment of the Economic Growth Act, only qualifying IDIs with under $1 billion in total assets were eligible for an 18-month on-site examination cycle.", "wikipage": "AML Topic 208"}, {"content": "DATES: These final rules are effective on January 28, 2019.", "wikipage": "AML Topic 208"}, {"content": "examination cycle for U.S. branches and examination cycle.3 The Code of Federal Regulations is sold by agencies of foreign banks, consistent with the International Banking Act of 1978 (IBA).", "wikipage": "AML Topic 208"}], "long_answer": "Prior to the enactment of the Economic Growth Act, only qualifying IDIs with under $1 billion in total assets were eligible for an 18-month on-site examination cycle. The Economic Growth Act amended this to allow for a broader application of the 18-month examination cycle.\n\nThe final rules related to the amendments of the Economic Growth Act became effective on January 28, 2019.\n\nThe on-site examination cycle for U.S. branches and agencies of foreign banks is consistent with the International Banking Act of 1978."}], "sample_id": "aml-topic-208-1", "question": "What change did the Economic Growth Act introduce regarding the on-site examination cycle for qualifying IDIs?", "docs": [{"id": "doc-aml-topic-208-1-1", "title": "AML Topic 208 Part 1", "text": "67033\nRules and Regulations Federal Register\nVol. 83, No. 248\nFriday, December 28, 2018\nThis section of the FEDERAL REGISTER in total assets not less than once during once during each 12-month period.\ncontains regulatory documents having general each 18-month period. In addition, Prior to the enactment of the Economic\napplicability and legal effect, most of which these final rules adopt as final the Growth Act, only qualifying IDIs with\nare keyed to and codified in the Code of parallel changes t", "score": 0.9, "summary": "67033 Rules and Regulations Federal Register Vol. 83, No. 248 Friday, December 28, 2018 This section of the FEDERAL REGISTER in total assets not less ...", "extraction": "Prior to the enactment of the Economic Growth Act, only qualifying IDIs with under $1 billion in total assets were eligible for an 18-month on-site examination cycle."}], "answer": "Prior to the enactment of the Economic Growth Act, only qualifying IDIs with under $1 billion in total assets were eligible for an 18-month on-site examination cycle. The final rules related to the amendments of the Economic Growth Act became effective on January 28, 2019. The on-site examination cycle for U.", "output": "Answer: The Economic Growth Act introduced a change regarding the on-site examination cycle for qualifying IDIs, shortening the cycle from once every 12 months to once every 18 months [1].", "intermediate": ["[Retrieval]", "Answer: The Economic Growth Act introduced a change regarding the on-site examination cycle for qualifying IDIs, shortening the cycle from once every 12 months to once every 18 months."]}, {"qa_pairs": [{"context": "No context provided", "question": "What is the qualifying asset threshold for IDIs to benefit from the extended examination cycle?", "short_answers": ["Under $3 billion in total assets", "Extended 18-month examination cycle"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "Who can I contact at the OCC for more information about the examination cycle for midsize and community banks?", "short_answers": ["Enice Thomas", "Senior Advisor for Midsize and Community Bank Supervision"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "What does Section 10(d)(1) of the FDI Act require regarding IDI examinations?", "short_answers": ["Full-scope, on-site examination", "At least once during each 12-month period"], "wikipage": null}], "wikipages": [{"title": "AML Topic 208", "url": "https://www.fdic.gov/news/board/2018/2018-12-18-notice-sum-g-fr.pdf"}], "annotations": [{"knowledge": [], "long_answer": "Qualifying IDIs with under $3 billion in total assets can benefit from the extended 18-month examination cycle. You can contact Enice Thomas, the Senior Advisor to the Senior Deputy Comptroller, Midsize and Community Bank Supervision, at (202) 649\u20135420. Section 10(d)(1) of the FDI Act generally requires the appropriate Federal banking agency to conduct a full-scope, on-site examination of an IDI at least once during each 12-month period."}, {"knowledge": [{"content": "INFORMATION CONTACT: qualifying IDIs with under $3 billion in Office of the Comptroller of the OCC: Enice Thomas, Senior Advisor total assets to benefit from the extended 18-month examination cycle.", "wikipage": "AML Topic 208"}, {"content": "OCC: Enice Thomas, Senior Advisor to Senior Deputy Comptroller, Midsize and Community Bank Supervision, (202) 649\u20135420;", "wikipage": "AML Topic 208"}, {"content": "Section 10(d)(1) of the FDI Act generally requires the appropriate Federal banking agency for an IDI to conduct a full-scope, on-site examination of an IDI at least once during each 12-month period.", "wikipage": "AML Topic 208"}], "long_answer": "Qualifying IDIs with under $3 billion in total assets can benefit from the extended 18-month examination cycle.\n\nYou can contact Enice Thomas, the Senior Advisor to the Senior Deputy Comptroller, Midsize and Community Bank Supervision, at (202) 649\u20135420.\n\nSection 10(d)(1) of the FDI Act generally requires the appropriate Federal banking agency to conduct a full-scope, on-site examination of an IDI at least once during each 12-month period."}], "sample_id": "aml-topic-208-2", "question": "What is the qualifying asset threshold for IDIs to benefit from the extended examination cycle?", "docs": [{"id": "doc-aml-topic-208-2-1", "title": "AML Topic 208 Part 1", "text": "INFORMATION CONTACT: qualifying IDIs with under $3 billion in\nOffice of the Comptroller of the\nOCC: Enice Thomas, Senior Advisor total assets to benefit from the extended\nCurrency\nto Senior Deputy Comptroller, Midsize 18-month examination cycle. In\nand Community Bank Supervision, addition, the interim final rules made\n12 CFR Part 4\n(202) 649\u20135420; and Deborah Katz, parallel changes to the agencies\u2019\n[Docket ID OCC\u20132018\u20130014] Assistant Director, Melissa J. Lisenbee, regulations governing the on-si", "score": 0.9, "summary": "INFORMATION CONTACT: qualifying IDIs with under $3 billion in Office of the Comptroller of the OCC: Enice Thomas, Senior Advisor total assets to benef...", "extraction": "INFORMATION CONTACT: qualifying IDIs with under $3 billion in Office of the Comptroller of the OCC: Enice Thomas, Senior Advisor total assets to benefit from the extended 18-month examination cycle."}], "answer": "Qualifying IDIs with under $3 billion in total assets can benefit from the extended 18-month examination cycle. You can contact Enice Thomas, the Senior Advisor to the Senior Deputy Comptroller, Midsize and Community Bank Supervision, at (202) 649\u20135420. Section 10(d)(1) of the FDI Act generally requires the appropriate Federal banking agency to conduct a full-scope, on-site examination of an IDI at least once during each 12-month period.", "output": "Answer: Qualifying IDIs with under $3 billion in total assets are eligible to benefit from the extended examination cycle [1].", "intermediate": ["[Retrieval]", "Answer: Qualifying IDIs with under $3 billion in total assets are eligible to benefit from the extended examination cycle."]}, {"qa_pairs": [{"context": "No context provided", "question": "What criteria must a small insured depository institution meet to qualify for an extended on-site examination cycle?", "short_answers": ["Assets < $3 billion", "Well capitalized", "Well managed"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "Who are the key contacts for inquiries about the expanded examination cycle for small insured depository institutions?", "short_answers": ["Assetou Traore", "Virginia Gibbs", "Alexander Kobulsky"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "Which division within the FDIC handles policy and program management related to the expanded examination cycle?", "short_answers": ["Policy Branch Division", "Risk Management and Supervision"], "wikipage": null}], "wikipages": [{"title": "AML Topic 208", "url": "https://www.fdic.gov/news/board/2018/2018-12-18-notice-sum-g-fr.pdf"}], "annotations": [{"knowledge": [], "long_answer": "To qualify for an extended on-site examination cycle, a small insured depository institution (IDI) must have total assets of less than $3 billion, be well capitalized as defined in 12 U. Key contacts for inquiries about the expanded examination cycle include Assetou Traore, Supervisory Financial Analyst; Virginia Gibbs, Manager; and Alexander Kobulsky, Supervisory Financial Analyst. The Policy Branch Division of Risk Management and Supervision within the FDIC handles policy and program management related to the expanded examination cycle."}, {"knowledge": [{"content": "section 10(d)(4) of the FDI Act authorizes the appropriate Federal banking agency to extend the on-site examination cycle for an IDI to at least once during an 18-month period if the IDI (1) has total assets of less than $3 billion; (2) is well capitalized (as defined in 12 U.S.C. 1831o (prompt corrective action)); (3) was found, at its most recent examination, to be well managed", "wikipage": "AML Topic 208"}, {"content": "Assetou Traore, Supervisory Financial Analyst, (202) 974\u20137066; Virginia Gibbs, Manager, (202) 452\u20132521; or Alexander Kobulsky, Supervisory Financial Analyst, (202) 452\u20132031", "wikipage": "AML Topic 208"}, {"content": "FDIC: Policy Branch Division of Risk Management and Supervision\u2014Thomas F. Lyons, Chief, Policy and Program", "wikipage": "AML Topic 208"}], "long_answer": "To qualify for an extended on-site examination cycle, a small insured depository institution (IDI) must have total assets of less than $3 billion, be well capitalized as defined in 12 U.S.C. 1831o, and have been found to be well managed at its most recent examination.\n\nKey contacts for inquiries about the expanded examination cycle include Assetou Traore, Supervisory Financial Analyst; Virginia Gibbs, Manager; and Alexander Kobulsky, Supervisory Financial Analyst.\n\nThe Policy Branch Division of Risk Management and Supervision within the FDIC handles policy and program management related to the expanded examination cycle."}], "sample_id": "aml-topic-208-3", "question": "What criteria must a small insured depository institution meet to qualify for an extended on-site examination cycle?", "docs": [{"id": "doc-aml-topic-208-3-1", "title": "AML Topic 208 Part 1", "text": "e\nFEDERAL DEPOSIT INSURANCE Assetou Traore, Supervisory Financial enactment of section 210 of the\nCORPORATION Analyst, (202) 974\u20137066; Virginia Gibbs, Economic Growth Act, section 10(d)(4)\nManager, (202) 452\u20132521; or Alexander of the FDI Act authorizes the\n12 CFR Parts 337 and 347 Kobulsky, Supervisory Financial appropriate Federal banking agency to\nAnalyst, (202) 452\u20132031; and Legal extend the on-site examination cycle for\nRIN 3064\u2013AE76 Division\u2014Laurie Schaffer, Associate an IDI to at least onc", "score": 0.9, "summary": "e FEDERAL DEPOSIT INSURANCE Assetou Traore, Supervisory Financial enactment of section 210 of the CORPORATION Analyst, (202) 974\u20137066; Virginia Gibbs,...", "extraction": "section 10(d)(4) of the FDI Act authorizes the appropriate Federal banking agency to extend the on-site examination cycle for an IDI to at least once during an 18-month period if the IDI (1) has total assets of less than $3 billion; (2) is well capitalized (as defined in 12 U.S.C. 1831o (prompt corrective action)); (3) was found, at its most recent examination, to be well managed"}], "answer": "To qualify for an extended on-site examination cycle, a small insured depository institution (IDI) must have total assets of less than $3 billion, be well capitalized as defined in 12 U. Key contacts for inquiries about the expanded examination cycle include Assetou Traore, Supervisory Financial Analyst; Virginia Gibbs, Manager; and Alexander Kobulsky, Supervisory Financial Analyst. The Policy Branch Division of Risk Management and Supervision within the FDIC handles policy and program management related to the expanded examination cycle.", "output": "Answer: The FDIC's mission is to maintain stability and public confidence in the U. S. financial system [1].", "intermediate": ["[Retrieval]", "Answer: The FDIC's mission is to maintain stability and public confidence in the U.S. financial system."]}, {"qa_pairs": [{"context": "No context provided", "question": "What is the purpose of the joint statement issued by the Board of Governors and other agencies?", "short_answers": ["Encourage innovative BSA/AML approaches", "Strengthen financial system against illicit activity"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "Which agencies are involved in issuing the joint statement on combating money laundering?", "short_answers": ["Federal Reserve System", "FDIC", "FinCEN", "NCUA", "OCC"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "How can private sector innovation help banks with their BSA/AML compliance obligations?", "short_answers": ["Enhance BSA/AML effectiveness", "Use new technologies"], "wikipage": null}], "wikipages": [{"title": "AML Topic 209", "url": "https://www.fdic.gov/sites/default/files/2024-03/pr18091a.pdf"}], "annotations": [{"knowledge": [], "long_answer": "The joint statement is issued to encourage banks to consider, evaluate, and responsibly implement innovative approaches to meet their Bank Secrecy Act/anti-money laundering (BSA/AML) compliance obligations. The agencies involved are the Board of Governors of the Federal Reserve System, the Federal Deposit Insurance Corporation, the Financial Crimes Enforcement Network (FinCEN), the National Credit Union Administration, and the Office of the Comptroller of the Currency. Private sector innovation can help banks by providing new ways of using existing tools or adopting new technologies, which enhance the effectiveness and efficiency of banks' BSA/AML efforts."}, {"knowledge": [{"content": "The Board of Governors of the Federal Reserve System, the Federal Deposit Insurance Corporation, the Financial Crimes Enforcement Network (FinCEN), the National Credit Union Administration, and the Office of the Comptroller of the Currency (collectively, the Agencies) are issuing this joint statement to encourage banks to consider, evaluate, and, where appropriate, responsibly implement innovative approaches to meet their Bank Secrecy Act/anti-money laundering (BSA/AML) compliance obligations, in order to further strengthen the financial system against illicit financial activity.", "wikipage": "AML Topic 209"}, {"content": "The Board of Governors of the Federal Reserve System, the Federal Deposit Insurance Corporation, the Financial Crimes Enforcement Network (FinCEN), the National Credit Union Administration, and the Office of the Comptroller of the Currency (collectively, the Agencies) are issuing this joint statement...", "wikipage": "AML Topic 209"}, {"content": "The Agencies recognize that private sector innovation, including new ways of using existing tools or adopting new technologies, can help banks identify and report money laundering, terrorist financing, and other illicit financial activity by enhancing the effectiveness and efficiency of banks\u2019 BSA/AML...", "wikipage": "AML Topic 209"}], "long_answer": "The joint statement is issued to encourage banks to consider, evaluate, and responsibly implement innovative approaches to meet their Bank Secrecy Act/anti-money laundering (BSA/AML) compliance obligations. This is aimed at strengthening the financial system against illicit financial activity.\n\nThe agencies involved are the Board of Governors of the Federal Reserve System, the Federal Deposit Insurance Corporation, the Financial Crimes Enforcement Network (FinCEN), the National Credit Union Administration, and the Office of the Comptroller of the Currency.\n\nPrivate sector innovation can help banks by providing new ways of using existing tools or adopting new technologies, which enhance the effectiveness and efficiency of banks' BSA/AML efforts. This helps in identifying and reporting money laundering, terrorist financing, and other illicit financial activities."}], "sample_id": "aml-topic-209-1", "question": "What is the purpose of the joint statement issued by the Board of Governors and other agencies?", "docs": [{"id": "doc-aml-topic-209-1-2", "title": "AML Topic 209 Part 2", "text": "nd the Office of the Comptroller of the Currency (collectively, the Agencies)\nare issuing this joint statement to encourage banks1 to consider, evaluate, and, where appropriate,\nresponsibly implement innovative approaches to meet their Bank Secrecy Act/anti-money\nlaundering (BSA/AML) compliance obligations, in order to further strengthen the financial\nsystem against illicit financial activity.\nThe Agencies recognize that private sector innovation, including new ways of using existing\ntools or ad", "score": 0.85, "summary": "nd the Office of the Comptroller of the Currency (collectively, the Agencies) are issuing this joint statement to encourage banks1 to consider, evalua...", "extraction": "nd the Office of the Comptroller of the Currency (collectively, the Agencies)\nare issuing this joint statement to encourage banks1 to consider, evaluate, and, where appropriate,\nresponsibly implement "}], "answer": "The joint statement is issued to encourage banks to consider, evaluate, and responsibly implement innovative approaches to meet their Bank Secrecy Act/anti-money laundering (BSA/AML) compliance obligations. The agencies involved are the Board of Governors of the Federal Reserve System, the Federal Deposit Insurance Corporation, the Financial Crimes Enforcement Network (FinCEN), the National Credit Union Administration, and the Office of the Comptroller of the Currency. Private sector innovation can help banks by providing new ways of using existing tools or adopting new technologies, which enhance the effectiveness and efficiency of banks' BSA/AML efforts.", "output": "Answer: The purpose of the joint statement issued by the Board of Governors and other agencies is to encourage banks to consider, evaluate, and, where appropriate, responsibly implement innovative approaches to meet their Bank Secrecy Act/anti-money laundering (BSA/AML) compliance obligations [1].", "intermediate": ["[Retrieval]", "Answer: The purpose of the joint statement issued by the Board of Governors and other agencies is to encourage banks to consider, evaluate, and, where appropriate, responsibly implement innovative approaches to meet their Bank Secrecy Act/anti-money laundering (BSA/AML) compliance obligations."]}, {"qa_pairs": [{"context": "No context provided", "question": "How do the Agencies view banks that choose not to use innovative approaches in their BSA/AML compliance programs?", "short_answers": ["No penalties for not using innovation", "Effective programs are sufficient"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "Are banks required to use specific methods or technologies for BSA/AML compliance?", "short_answers": ["No specific methods required", "Agencies do not advocate particular technologies"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "What potential benefits can innovation bring to BSA/AML compliance programs?", "short_answers": ["Enhances risk identification", "Improves transaction monitoring"], "wikipage": null}], "wikipages": [{"title": "AML Topic 209", "url": "https://www.fdic.gov/sites/default/files/2024-03/pr18091a.pdf"}], "annotations": [{"knowledge": [], "long_answer": "The Agencies will not penalize or criticize banks that maintain effective BSA/AML compliance programs commensurate with their risk profiles but choose not to pursue innovative approaches. The Agencies will not advocate a particular method or technology for banks to comply with BSA/AML requirements. Innovation has the potential to augment aspects of banks\u2019 BSA/AML compliance programs, such as risk identification, transaction monitoring, and suspicious activity reporting."}, {"knowledge": [{"content": "The Agencies will not penalize or criticize banks that maintain effective BSA/AML compliance programs commensurate with their risk profiles but choose not to pursue innovative approaches.", "wikipage": "AML Topic 209"}, {"content": "While banks are expected to maintain effective BSA/AML compliance programs, the Agencies will not advocate a particular method or technology for banks to comply with BSA/AML requirements.", "wikipage": "AML Topic 209"}, {"content": "Innovation has the potential to augment aspects of banks\u2019 BSA/AML compliance programs, such as risk identification, transaction monitoring, and suspicious activity reporting.", "wikipage": "AML Topic 209"}], "long_answer": "The Agencies will not penalize or criticize banks that maintain effective BSA/AML compliance programs commensurate with their risk profiles but choose not to pursue innovative approaches.\n\nThe Agencies will not advocate a particular method or technology for banks to comply with BSA/AML requirements.\n\nInnovation has the potential to augment aspects of banks\u2019 BSA/AML compliance programs, such as risk identification, transaction monitoring, and suspicious activity reporting."}], "sample_id": "aml-topic-209-2", "question": "How do the Agencies view banks that choose not to use innovative approaches in their BSA/AML compliance programs?", "docs": [{"id": "doc-aml-topic-209-2-1", "title": "AML Topic 209 Part 1", "text": "compliance programs. To assist banks in this effort, the\nAgencies are committed to continued engagement with the private sector and other interested\nparties.\nThe Agencies will not penalize or criticize banks that maintain effective BSA/AML compliance\nprograms commensurate with their risk profiles but choose not to pursue innovative approaches.\nWhile banks are expected to maintain effective BSA/AML compliance programs, the Agencies\nwill not advocate a particular method or technology for banks to", "score": 0.9, "summary": "compliance programs. To assist banks in this effort, the Agencies are committed to continued engagement with the private sector and other interested p...", "extraction": "The Agencies will not penalize or criticize banks that maintain effective BSA/AML compliance programs commensurate with their risk profiles but choose not to pursue innovative approaches."}], "answer": "The Agencies will not penalize or criticize banks that maintain effective BSA/AML compliance programs commensurate with their risk profiles but choose not to pursue innovative approaches. The Agencies will not advocate a particular method or technology for banks to comply with BSA/AML requirements. Innovation has the potential to augment aspects of banks\u2019 BSA/AML compliance programs, such as risk identification, transaction monitoring, and suspicious activity reporting.", "output": "Answer: The Agencies view banks that choose not to use innovative approaches in their BSA/AML compliance programs as maintaining effective BSA/AML compliance programs commensurate with their risk profiles [1].", "intermediate": ["[Retrieval]", "Answer: The Agencies view banks that choose not to use innovative approaches in their BSA/AML compliance programs as maintaining effective BSA/AML compliance programs commensurate with their risk profiles."]}, {"qa_pairs": [{"context": "No context provided", "question": "How can artificial intelligence enhance BSA/AML compliance programs in banks?", "short_answers": ["Strengthen compliance approaches", "Enhance transaction monitoring"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "What is the purpose of pilot programs in the context of BSA/AML compliance?", "short_answers": ["Test and validate effectiveness", "No supervisory criticism if unsuccessful"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "Will a bank face supervisory action if a pilot program exposes gaps in their BSA/AML compliance program?", "short_answers": ["No supervisory action for gaps", "Agencies provide feedback"], "wikipage": null}], "wikipages": [{"title": "AML Topic 209", "url": "https://www.fdic.gov/sites/default/files/2024-03/pr18091a.pdf"}], "annotations": [{"knowledge": [], "long_answer": "Artificial intelligence can enhance BSA/AML compliance programs by strengthening compliance approaches and enhancing transaction monitoring systems. Pilot programs are used to test and validate the effectiveness of innovative approaches in conjunction with existing BSA/AML processes. No, a bank will not necessarily face supervisory action if a pilot program exposes gaps in their BSA/AML compliance program."}, {"knowledge": [{"content": "Some banks are also experimenting with artificial intelligence and digital identity technologies applicable to their BSA/AML compliance programs. These innovations and technologies can strengthen BSA/AML compliance approaches, as well as enhance transaction monitoring systems.", "wikipage": "AML Topic 209"}, {"content": "Pilot programs undertaken by banks, in conjunction with existing BSA/AML processes, are an important means of testing and validating the effectiveness of innovative approaches.", "wikipage": "AML Topic 209"}, {"content": "Pilot programs that expose gaps in a BSA/AML compliance program will not necessarily result in supervisory action.", "wikipage": "AML Topic 209"}], "long_answer": "Artificial intelligence can enhance BSA/AML compliance programs by strengthening compliance approaches and enhancing transaction monitoring systems. These technologies help protect the financial system against illicit financial activity and maximize the utilization of banks' compliance resources.\n\nPilot programs are used to test and validate the effectiveness of innovative approaches in conjunction with existing BSA/AML processes. They serve as an important means for banks to experiment and assess new technologies or methods without fear of supervisory criticism if they prove unsuccessful.\n\nNo, a bank will not necessarily face supervisory action if a pilot program exposes gaps in their BSA/AML compliance program. The Agencies do not intend to criticize banks for pilot programs that reveal such gaps."}], "sample_id": "aml-topic-209-3", "question": "How can artificial intelligence enhance BSA/AML compliance programs in banks?", "docs": [{"id": "doc-aml-topic-209-3-1", "title": "AML Topic 209 Part 1", "text": "es each agent, agency,\nbranch, or office within the United States of banks, savings associations, credit unions, and foreign banks.\n1 | P age\nvulnerabilities and threats. Some banks are also experimenting with artificial intelligence and\ndigital identity technologies applicable to their BSA/AML compliance programs. These\ninnovations and technologies can strengthen BSA/AML compliance approaches, as well as\nenhance transaction monitoring systems. The Agencies welcome these types of innovative\nappr", "score": 0.9, "summary": "es each agent, agency, branch, or office within the United States of banks, savings associations, credit unions, and foreign banks. 1 | P age vulnerab...", "extraction": "Some banks are also experimenting with artificial intelligence and digital identity technologies applicable to their BSA/AML compliance programs. These innovations and technologies can strengthen BSA/AML compliance approaches, as well as enhance transaction monitoring systems."}], "answer": "Artificial intelligence can enhance BSA/AML compliance programs by strengthening compliance approaches and enhancing transaction monitoring systems. Pilot programs are used to test and validate the effectiveness of innovative approaches in conjunction with existing BSA/AML processes. No, a bank will not necessarily face supervisory action if a pilot program exposes gaps in their BSA/AML compliance program.", "output": "Answer: AI can enhance BSA/AML compliance programs in banks by automating processes, detecting and monitoring suspicious activity, and providing real-time alerts [1].", "intermediate": ["[Retrieval]", "Answer: AI can enhance BSA/AML compliance programs in banks by automating processes, detecting and monitoring suspicious activity, and providing real-time alerts."]}, {"qa_pairs": [{"context": "No context provided", "question": "What is the purpose of the Interagency Statement on Sharing Bank Secrecy Act Resources issued on October 3, 2018?", "short_answers": ["Collaborative arrangements", "Efficient BSA/AML management"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "Which types of banks are collaborative arrangements for BSA/AML obligations most suitable for, according to the 2018 interagency statement?", "short_answers": ["Community-focused banks", "Less complex operations", "Lower-risk profiles"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "What factors should a bank consider when determining its suitability for entering collaborative arrangements for BSA/AML obligations?", "short_answers": ["Bank-specific risk profile", "Risk assessment"], "wikipage": null}], "wikipages": [{"title": "AML Topic 210", "url": "https://www.fdic.gov/sites/default/files/2024-03/pr18068a.pdf"}], "annotations": [{"knowledge": [], "long_answer": "The purpose of the Interagency Statement on Sharing Bank Secrecy Act Resources is to address instances where banks may decide to enter into collaborative arrangements to share resources in order to manage their Bank Secrecy Act (BSA) and anti-money laundering (AML) obligations more efficiently and effectively. Collaborative arrangements for BSA/AML obligations are most suitable for banks with a community focus, less complex operations, and lower-risk profiles for money laundering or terrorist financing. A bank should consider its specific risk profile, which is determined by a risk assessment that properly considers all risk areas, when determining its suitability for entering collaborative arrangements for BSA/AML obligations."}, {"knowledge": [{"content": "The Board of Governors of the Federal Reserve System (FRB), the Federal Deposit Insurance Corporation (FDIC), the National Credit Union Administration (NCUA), the Office of the Comptroller of the Currency (OCC), and the U.S. Department of Treasury\u2019s Financial Crimes Enforcement Network (FinCEN) (collectively, the Agencies), are publishing this statement to address instances in which banks may decide to enter into collaborative arrangements to share resources to manage their Bank Secrecy Act (BSA) and anti-money laundering (AML) obligations more efficiently and effectively.", "wikipage": "AML Topic 210"}, {"content": "Collaborative arrangements as described in this statement generally are most suitable for banks with a community focus, less complex operations, and lower-risk profiles for money laundering or terrorist financing.", "wikipage": "AML Topic 210"}, {"content": "The risk profile is bank-specific, and should be based on a risk assessment that properly considers all risk area", "wikipage": "AML Topic 210"}], "long_answer": "The purpose of the Interagency Statement on Sharing Bank Secrecy Act Resources is to address instances where banks may decide to enter into collaborative arrangements to share resources in order to manage their Bank Secrecy Act (BSA) and anti-money laundering (AML) obligations more efficiently and effectively.\n\nCollaborative arrangements for BSA/AML obligations are most suitable for banks with a community focus, less complex operations, and lower-risk profiles for money laundering or terrorist financing.\n\nA bank should consider its specific risk profile, which is determined by a risk assessment that properly considers all risk areas, when determining its suitability for entering collaborative arrangements for BSA/AML obligations."}], "sample_id": "aml-topic-210-1", "question": "What is the purpose of the Interagency Statement on Sharing Bank Secrecy Act Resources issued on October 3, 2018?", "docs": [{"id": "doc-aml-topic-210-1-1", "title": "AML Topic 210 Part 1", "text": "Board of Governors of the Federal Reserve System\nFederal Deposit Insurance Corporation\nFinancial Crimes Enforcement Network\nNational Credit Union Administration\nOffice of the Comptroller of the Currency\nInteragency Statement on Sharing Bank Secrecy Act Resources\nOctober 3, 2018\nIntroduction\nThe Board of Governors of the Federal Reserve System (FRB), the Federal Deposit Insurance\nCorporation (FDIC), the National Credit Union Administration (NCUA), the Office of the\nComptroller of the Currency (OC", "score": 0.9, "summary": "Board of Governors of the Federal Reserve System Federal Deposit Insurance Corporation Financial Crimes Enforcement Network National Credit Union Admi...", "extraction": "The Board of Governors of the Federal Reserve System (FRB), the Federal Deposit Insurance Corporation (FDIC), the National Credit Union Administration (NCUA), the Office of the Comptroller of the Currency (OCC), and the U.S. Department of Treasury\u2019s Financial Crimes Enforcement Network (FinCEN) (collectively, the Agencies), are publishing this statement to address instances in which banks may decide to enter into collaborative arrangements to share resources to manage their Bank Secrecy Act (BSA) and anti-money laundering (AML) obligations more efficiently and effectively."}], "answer": "The purpose of the Interagency Statement on Sharing Bank Secrecy Act Resources is to address instances where banks may decide to enter into collaborative arrangements to share resources in order to manage their Bank Secrecy Act (BSA) and anti-money laundering (AML) obligations more efficiently and effectively. Collaborative arrangements for BSA/AML obligations are most suitable for banks with a community focus, less complex operations, and lower-risk profiles for money laundering or terrorist financing. A bank should consider its specific risk profile, which is determined by a risk assessment that properly considers all risk areas, when determining its suitability for entering collaborative arrangements for BSA/AML obligations.", "output": "The purpose of the Interagency Statement on Sharing Bank Secrecy Act Resources issued on October 3, 2018, is to provide guidance to financial institutions on sharing Bank Secrecy Act (BSA) resources, including suspicious activity reports (SARs) and currency transaction reports (CTRs) [1].", "intermediate": ["[Retrieval]", "The purpose of the Interagency Statement on Sharing Bank Secrecy Act Resources issued on October 3, 2018, is to provide guidance to financial institutions on sharing Bank Secrecy Act (BSA) resources, including suspicious activity reports (SARs) and currency transaction reports (CTRs)."]}, {"qa_pairs": [{"context": "No context provided", "question": "What should banks include in their BSA/AML compliance programs?", "short_answers": ["System of internal controls", "Independent testing of compliance"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "Are collaborative arrangements for sharing information under Section 314(b) of the USA PATRIOT Act covered by the interagency statement?", "short_answers": ["Not covered by the statement", "Section 314(b) not included"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "What should banks do if they need more information about the 314(b) program and requirements?", "short_answers": ["Contact FinCEN", "For 314(b) information"], "wikipage": null}], "wikipages": [{"title": "AML Topic 210", "url": "https://www.fdic.gov/sites/default/files/2024-03/pr18068a.pdf"}], "annotations": [{"knowledge": [], "long_answer": "Banks are required to develop and implement BSA/AML programs that include a system of internal controls to ensure ongoing compliance and independent testing of BSA/AML compliance. No, the interagency statement does not apply to collaborative arrangements or consortia formed for the purpose of sharing information under Section 314(b) of the USA PATRIOT Act. Banks should contact FinCEN for additional information concerning the 314(b) program and requirements."}, {"knowledge": [{"content": "All banks are required to establish and maintain procedures reasonably designed to ensure compliance with the BSA and to develop and implement BSA/AML programs. The BSA/AML compliance program must include the following: 1) a system of internal controls to ensure ongoing compliance; 2) independent testing of BSA/AML compliance;", "wikipage": "AML Topic 210"}, {"content": "Notably, this interagency statement does not apply to collaborative arrangements or consortia formed for the purpose of sharing information under Section 314(b) of the USA PATRIOT Act.", "wikipage": "AML Topic 210"}, {"content": "Banks should contact FinCEN for additional information concerning the 314(b) program and requirements.", "wikipage": "AML Topic 210"}], "long_answer": "Banks are required to develop and implement BSA/AML programs that include a system of internal controls to ensure ongoing compliance and independent testing of BSA/AML compliance.\n\nNo, the interagency statement does not apply to collaborative arrangements or consortia formed for the purpose of sharing information under Section 314(b) of the USA PATRIOT Act.\n\nBanks should contact FinCEN for additional information concerning the 314(b) program and requirements."}], "sample_id": "aml-topic-210-2", "question": "What should banks include in their BSA/AML compliance programs?", "docs": [{"id": "doc-aml-topic-210-2-2", "title": "AML Topic 210 Part 2", "text": "ia\nformed for the purpose of sharing information under Section 314(b) of the USA PATRIOT Act.\nFurther, banks that form collaborative arrangements as described in this interagency statement\nare not an association for purposes of Section 314(b) of the USA PATRIOT Act.3 Banks should\ncontact FinCEN for additional information concerning the 314(b) program and requirements.\nAll banks are required to establish and maintain procedures reasonably designed to ensure\ncompliance with the BSA and to develop", "score": 0.85, "summary": "ia formed for the purpose of sharing information under Section 314(b) of the USA PATRIOT Act. Further, banks that form collaborative arrangements as d...", "extraction": "ia\nformed for the purpose of sharing information under Section 314(b) of the USA PATRIOT Act.\nFurther, banks that form collaborative arrangements as described in this interagency statement\nare not an "}], "answer": "Banks are required to develop and implement BSA/AML programs that include a system of internal controls to ensure ongoing compliance and independent testing of BSA/AML compliance. No, the interagency statement does not apply to collaborative arrangements or consortia formed for the purpose of sharing information under Section 314(b) of the USA PATRIOT Act. Banks should contact FinCEN for additional information concerning the 314(b) program and requirements.", "output": "Banks should include in their BSA/AML compliance programs: 1) the development of internal policies, procedures, and controls; 2) the designation of a compliance officer; 3) the development of a training program; 4) the implementation of an ongoing monitoring system; 5) the development of a system for the timely detection and reporting of suspicious activities; and 6) the development of a system for the timely filing of CTRs and SARs [1].", "intermediate": ["[Retrieval]", "Banks should include in their BSA/AML compliance programs: 1) the development of internal policies, procedures, and controls; 2) the designation of a compliance officer; 3) the development of a training program; 4) the implementation of an ongoing monitoring system; 5) the development of a system for the timely detection and reporting of suspicious activities; and 6) the development of a system for the timely filing of CTRs and SARs."]}, {"qa_pairs": [{"context": "No context provided", "question": "Who is responsible for managing BSA compliance within a financial institution?", "short_answers": ["BSA compliance officer", "designated individual"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "What entities are included under the definition of 'bank' according to the BSA?", "short_answers": ["banks", "savings associations", "credit unions", "foreign banks"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "What is a bank expected to have in relation to its risk profile for BSA/AML compliance?", "short_answers": ["BSA/AML compliance program", "commensurate with risk profile"], "wikipage": null}], "wikipages": [{"title": "AML Topic 210", "url": "https://www.fdic.gov/sites/default/files/2024-03/pr18068a.pdf"}], "annotations": [{"knowledge": [], "long_answer": "An individual or individuals designated as the BSA compliance officer are responsible for managing BSA compliance. Under the BSA, the term 'bank' includes each agent, agency, branch, or office within the United States of banks, savings associations, credit unions, and foreign banks. A bank is expected to have a BSA/AML compliance program commensurate with its respective risk profile."}, {"knowledge": [{"content": "3) designating an individual or individuals responsible for managing BSA compliance (BSA compliance officer);", "wikipage": "AML Topic 210"}, {"content": "1 Under the BSA the term \u201cbank\u201d is defined in 31 CFR 1010.100(d) and includes each agent, agency, branch or office within the United States of banks, savings associations, credit unions, and foreign banks.", "wikipage": "AML Topic 210"}, {"content": "A bank is expected to have a BSA/AML compliance program commensurate with its respective risk profile.", "wikipage": "AML Topic 210"}], "long_answer": "An individual or individuals designated as the BSA compliance officer are responsible for managing BSA compliance.\n\nUnder the BSA, the term 'bank' includes each agent, agency, branch, or office within the United States of banks, savings associations, credit unions, and foreign banks.\n\nA bank is expected to have a BSA/AML compliance program commensurate with its respective risk profile."}], "sample_id": "aml-topic-210-3", "question": "Who is responsible for managing BSA compliance within a financial institution?", "docs": [{"id": "doc-aml-topic-210-3-1", "title": "AML Topic 210 Part 1", "text": "3) designating an\nindividual or individuals responsible for managing BSA compliance (BSA compliance officer);\n1 Under the BSA the term \u201cbank\u201d is defined in 31 CFR 1010.100(d) and includes each agent, agency, branch or\noffice within the United States of banks, savings associations, credit unions, and foreign banks.\n2 See Federal Financial Institutions Examination Council (FFIEC) Bank Secrecy Act/Anti-Money Laundering\nExamination Manual (2014), at https://bsaaml.ffiec.gov/pages_manual/manual_onlin", "score": 0.9, "summary": "3) designating an individual or individuals responsible for managing BSA compliance (BSA compliance officer); 1 Under the BSA the term \u201cbank\u201d is defin...", "extraction": "3) designating an individual or individuals responsible for managing BSA compliance (BSA compliance officer);"}], "answer": "An individual or individuals designated as the BSA compliance officer are responsible for managing BSA compliance. Under the BSA, the term 'bank' includes each agent, agency, branch, or office within the United States of banks, savings associations, credit unions, and foreign banks. A bank is expected to have a BSA/AML compliance program commensurate with its respective risk profile.", "output": "Answer: The BSA compliance officer is responsible for managing BSA compliance within a financial institution [1].", "intermediate": ["[Retrieval]", "Answer: The BSA compliance officer is responsible for managing BSA compliance within a financial institution."]}, {"qa_pairs": [{"context": "No context provided", "question": "What resources does the FDIC provide for stakeholders?", "short_answers": ["Financial education materials", "Data tools", "Documentation of laws and regulations"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "Where can bankers find resources related to deposit insurance?", "short_answers": ["Banker Resource Center", "Deposit Insurance"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "What type of research does the FDIC conduct regarding U.S. banking?", "short_answers": ["U.S. banking industry research", "Pre-eminent source"], "wikipage": null}], "wikipages": [{"title": "AML Topic 214", "url": "https://www.fdic.gov/banker-resource-center/anti-money-laundering-countering-financing-terrorism-amlcft#footnoteref1_aMqry2mPlEuNlmxGCcdvBz3c5PX1QchrN9HD-yed9M_vMCrQMhFcbdc"}], "annotations": [{"knowledge": [], "long_answer": "The FDIC provides a wealth of resources for consumers, bankers, analysts, and other stakeholders. Bankers can find resources related to deposit insurance in the Banker Resource Center provided by the FDIC. The FDIC conducts research as a pre-eminent source of U."}, {"knowledge": [{"content": "The FDIC provides a wealth of resources for consumers, bankers, analysts, and other stakeholders. Browse our collection of financial education materials, data tools, documentation of laws and regulations, information on important initiatives, and more.", "wikipage": "AML Topic 214"}, {"content": "Additional FDIC Resources Consumer Resource Center Banker Resource Center Deposit Insurance", "wikipage": "AML Topic 214"}, {"content": "The FDIC is proud to be a pre-eminent source of U.S. banking industry research, including", "wikipage": "AML Topic 214"}], "long_answer": "The FDIC provides a wealth of resources for consumers, bankers, analysts, and other stakeholders. These include financial education materials, data tools, documentation of laws and regulations, information on important initiatives, and more.\n\nBankers can find resources related to deposit insurance in the Banker Resource Center provided by the FDIC.\n\nThe FDIC conducts research as a pre-eminent source of U.S. banking industry research."}], "sample_id": "aml-topic-214-2", "question": "What resources does the FDIC provide for stakeholders?", "docs": [{"id": "doc-aml-topic-214-2-1", "title": "AML Topic 214 Part 1", "text": "RESS RELEASE / January 17, 2025\nMillennium Bank, Des Plaines, Ill. Assumes All Deposits of Pulaski Savings\u2026\nPODCASTS / January 06, 2025\nSmall Business Lending\nResources\nToggle submenu\nResources\nThe FDIC provides a wealth of resources for consumers, bankers, analysts, and other stakeholders. Browse our collection of financial education materials, data tools, documentation of laws and regulations, information on important initiatives, and more.\nAdditional FDIC Resources\nConsumer Resource Center\nBa", "score": 0.9, "summary": "RESS RELEASE / January 17, 2025 Millennium Bank, Des Plaines, Ill. Assumes All Deposits of Pulaski Savings\u2026 PODCASTS / January 06, 2025 Small Business...", "extraction": "The FDIC provides a wealth of resources for consumers, bankers, analysts, and other stakeholders. Browse our collection of financial education materials, data tools, documentation of laws and regulations, information on important initiatives, and more."}], "answer": "The FDIC provides a wealth of resources for consumers, bankers, analysts, and other stakeholders. Bankers can find resources related to deposit insurance in the Banker Resource Center provided by the FDIC. The FDIC conducts research as a pre-eminent source of U.", "output": "Answer: The FDIC provides a wealth of resources for consumers, bankers, analysts, and other stakeholders [1].", "intermediate": ["[Retrieval]", "Answer: The FDIC provides a wealth of resources for consumers, bankers, analysts, and other stakeholders."]}, {"qa_pairs": [{"context": "No context provided", "question": "What role do financial institutions play in combating money laundering and terrorism financing?", "short_answers": ["Monitor and report suspicious activities", "Comply with the Bank Secrecy Act"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "How does the Bank Secrecy Act support financial transparency?", "short_answers": ["Promotes financial transparency", "Requires reporting of suspicious activities"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "What is the responsibility of financial institutions under the Bank Secrecy Act regarding suspicious activities?", "short_answers": ["Monitor suspicious activities", "Report to law enforcement"], "wikipage": null}], "wikipages": [{"title": "AML Topic 226", "url": "https://www.fdic.gov/regulations/examinations/supervisory/insights/sisum17/sisum17-article2.pdf"}], "annotations": [{"knowledge": [], "long_answer": "Financial institutions play a crucial role in combating money laundering and the financing of terrorism by complying with the Bank Secrecy Act (BSA). The Bank Secrecy Act provides a foundation to promote financial transparency by requiring financial institutions to monitor and report suspicious activities. Under the Bank Secrecy Act, financial institutions have the responsibility to monitor for suspicious activities and to identify and report those activities to law enforcement."}, {"knowledge": [{"content": "Financial institutions play a crucial role in our nation\u2019s efforts to combat financial fraud, money laundering, and the financing of terrorism through their compliance with the Bank Secrecy Act (BSA).", "wikipage": "AML Topic 226"}, {"content": "The BSA provides a foundation to promote financial transparency and deter and detect those who seek to misuse the U.S. financial system.", "wikipage": "AML Topic 226"}, {"content": "Under the law, financial institutions have a responsibility to monitor for suspicious activities and to identify and report those suspicious activities to law enforcement.", "wikipage": "AML Topic 226"}], "long_answer": "Financial institutions play a crucial role in combating money laundering and the financing of terrorism by complying with the Bank Secrecy Act (BSA). They are responsible for monitoring suspicious activities and reporting them to law enforcement, which helps deter and detect the misuse of the financial system.\n\nThe Bank Secrecy Act provides a foundation to promote financial transparency by requiring financial institutions to monitor and report suspicious activities. This helps to deter and detect those who seek to misuse the financial system for illicit purposes.\n\nUnder the Bank Secrecy Act, financial institutions have the responsibility to monitor for suspicious activities and to identify and report those activities to law enforcement. This is essential for combating drug trafficking, organized crime, and other illicit activities."}], "sample_id": "aml-topic-226-1", "question": "What role do financial institutions play in combating money laundering and terrorism financing?", "docs": [{"id": "doc-aml-topic-226-1-1", "title": "AML Topic 226 Part 1", "text": "The Bank Secrecy Act: A Supervisory Update\nand the financing of terrorism.\nIntroduction\nThe BSA provides a foundation to\npromote financial transparency and\nFinancial institutions play a crucial\ndeter and detect those who seek to\nrole in our nation\u2019s efforts to combat\nmisuse the U.S. financial system to\nfinancial fraud, money laundering, and\nlaunder criminal proceeds, finance\nthe financing of terrorism through their\nterrorist acts, or move funds for other\ncompliance with the Bank Secrecy Act\nilli", "score": 0.9, "summary": "The Bank Secrecy Act: A Supervisory Update and the financing of terrorism. Introduction The BSA provides a foundation to promote financial transparenc...", "extraction": "Financial institutions play a crucial role in our nation\u2019s efforts to combat financial fraud, money laundering, and the financing of terrorism through their compliance with the Bank Secrecy Act (BSA)."}], "answer": "Financial institutions play a crucial role in combating money laundering and the financing of terrorism by complying with the Bank Secrecy Act (BSA). The Bank Secrecy Act provides a foundation to promote financial transparency by requiring financial institutions to monitor and report suspicious activities. Under the Bank Secrecy Act, financial institutions have the responsibility to monitor for suspicious activities and to identify and report those activities to law enforcement.", "output": "Answer: Financial institutions play a crucial role in our nation\u2019s efforts to combat financial fraud, money laundering, and terrorist financing through their compliance with the Bank Secrecy Act and other anti-money laundering laws and regulations [1].", "intermediate": ["[Retrieval]", "Answer: Financial institutions play a crucial role in our nation\u2019s efforts to combat financial fraud, money laundering, and terrorist financing through their compliance with the Bank Secrecy Act and other anti-money laundering laws and regulations."]}, {"qa_pairs": [{"context": "No context provided", "question": "How have financial institution reporting requirements contributed to the success of fraud and money laundering investigations?", "short_answers": ["Instrumental in fraud investigations", "Supports money laundering probes"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "What happens if deficiencies are identified in FDIC-supervised institutions during examinations?", "short_answers": ["Most deficiencies addressed without enforcement", "Handled in normal course"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "What does the BSA compliance program include beyond currency transaction reporting?", "short_answers": ["Includes compliance programs", "Involves suspicious activity monitoring"], "wikipage": null}], "wikipages": [{"title": "AML Topic 226", "url": "https://www.fdic.gov/regulations/examinations/supervisory/insights/sisum17/sisum17-article2.pdf"}], "annotations": [{"knowledge": [], "long_answer": "Financial institution reporting has been instrumental in the successful investigations of fraud schemes, drug trafficking, money laundering, foreign terrorist fighters, and the proliferation of weapons of mass destruction. Although deficiencies may be identified during examinations, the vast majority of FDIC-supervised institutions are able to address any BSA compliance deficiencies identified through the supervisory process in the normal course, without the need for a formal enforcement action. The BSA compliance program has evolved to include required BSA compliance programs, suspicious activity monitoring, and other reporting requirements aiming to better identify money laundering, terrorist financing, and other illicit financial activities."}, {"knowledge": [{"content": "Financial institution reporting has been instrumental in the successful investigations of fraud schemes, drug trafficking, money laundering, foreign terrorist fighters, and the proliferation of weapons of mass destruction.", "wikipage": "AML Topic 226"}, {"content": "Although deficiencies may be identified during examinations, the vast majority of FDIC-supervised institutions are able to address any BSA compliance deficiencies identified through the supervisory process in the normal course, without the need for a formal enforcement action.", "wikipage": "AML Topic 226"}, {"content": "The BSA has evolved from currency transaction reporting requirements to include required BSA compliance programs, suspicious activity monitoring, and other reporting requirements aiming to better identify money laundering, terrorist financing, and other illicit financial activities.", "wikipage": "AML Topic 226"}], "long_answer": "Financial institution reporting has been instrumental in the successful investigations of fraud schemes, drug trafficking, money laundering, foreign terrorist fighters, and the proliferation of weapons of mass destruction.\n\nAlthough deficiencies may be identified during examinations, the vast majority of FDIC-supervised institutions are able to address any BSA compliance deficiencies identified through the supervisory process in the normal course, without the need for a formal enforcement action.\n\nThe BSA compliance program has evolved to include required BSA compliance programs, suspicious activity monitoring, and other reporting requirements aiming to better identify money laundering, terrorist financing, and other illicit financial activities."}], "sample_id": "aml-topic-226-2", "question": "How have financial institution reporting requirements contributed to the success of fraud and money laundering investigations?", "docs": [{"id": "doc-aml-topic-226-2-1", "title": "AML Topic 226 Part 1", "text": "al activity, and terrorism.\nmoney laundering and terrorist\nFinancial institution reporting has\nfinancing risk. Although deficiencies\nbeen instrumental in the successful\nmay be identified during examinations,\ninvestigations of fraud schemes, drug\nthe vast majority of FDIC-supervised\ntrafficking, money laundering, foreign\ninstitutions are able to address any\nterrorist fighters, and the proliferation\nBSA compliance deficiencies identified\nof weapons of mass destruction.1\nthrough the supervisory pro", "score": 0.9, "summary": "al activity, and terrorism. money laundering and terrorist Financial institution reporting has financing risk. Although deficiencies been instrumental...", "extraction": "Financial institution reporting has been instrumental in the successful investigations of fraud schemes, drug trafficking, money laundering, foreign terrorist fighters, and the proliferation of weapons of mass destruction."}], "answer": "Financial institution reporting has been instrumental in the successful investigations of fraud schemes, drug trafficking, money laundering, foreign terrorist fighters, and the proliferation of weapons of mass destruction. Although deficiencies may be identified during examinations, the vast majority of FDIC-supervised institutions are able to address any BSA compliance deficiencies identified through the supervisory process in the normal course, without the need for a formal enforcement action. The BSA compliance program has evolved to include required BSA compliance programs, suspicious activity monitoring, and other reporting requirements aiming to better identify money laundering, terrorist financing, and other illicit financial activities.", "output": "Financial institution reporting requirements have contributed to the success of fraud and money laundering investigations by providing law enforcement with information that can be used to identify and track suspicious activity [1].", "intermediate": ["[Retrieval]", "Financial institution reporting requirements have contributed to the success of fraud and money laundering investigations by providing law enforcement with information that can be used to identify and track suspicious activity."]}, {"qa_pairs": [{"context": "No context provided", "question": "What is the primary purpose of the Bank Secrecy Act (BSA)?", "short_answers": ["Require financial institutions to maintain records", "Support criminal and tax evasion investigations"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "Why was the Bank Secrecy Act (BSA) necessary for law enforcement agencies?", "short_answers": ["Lack of financial recordkeeping", "Impeded law enforcement investigations"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "How did the Bank Secrecy Act (BSA) influence compliance programs in financial institutions?", "short_answers": ["Required BSA compliance programs", "Framework for supervision and enforcement"], "wikipage": null}], "wikipages": [{"title": "AML Topic 226", "url": "https://www.fdic.gov/regulations/examinations/supervisory/insights/sisum17/sisum17-article2.pdf"}], "annotations": [{"knowledge": [], "long_answer": "The primary purpose of the Bank Secrecy Act (BSA), enacted in 1970, was to require financial institutions to maintain certain records that the government could use to support criminal and tax evasion investigations. The Bank Secrecy Act (BSA) was necessary because it addressed the lack of financial recordkeeping by financial institutions, which was impeding law enforcement agencies' ability to investigate and prosecute criminal activity. The BSA required depository institutions to establish and maintain BSA compliance programs, providing an early framework for supervision and enforcement of compliance with the BSA."}, {"knowledge": [{"content": "When Congress enacted the BSA in 1970, its primary intent was to require institutions to maintain certain records the government could use to support criminal and tax evasion investigations.", "wikipage": "AML Topic 226"}, {"content": "The Bank Records and Foreign Transaction Act, or BSA, addressed two issues that were impeding law enforcement agencies\u2019 ability to investigate and prosecute criminal activity: the lack of financial recordkeeping by financial institutions.", "wikipage": "AML Topic 226"}, {"content": "The Bank Records and Foreign Transaction Act, or BSA, addressed two issues that were impeding law enforcement agencies\u2019 ability to investigate and prosecute criminal activity: the lack of financial recordkeeping by financial institutions to establish and maintain BSA compliance programs. This requirement provided an early framework for supervision and enforcement of compliance with the BSA.", "wikipage": "AML Topic 226"}], "long_answer": "The primary purpose of the Bank Secrecy Act (BSA), enacted in 1970, was to require financial institutions to maintain certain records that the government could use to support criminal and tax evasion investigations.\n\nThe Bank Secrecy Act (BSA) was necessary because it addressed the lack of financial recordkeeping by financial institutions, which was impeding law enforcement agencies' ability to investigate and prosecute criminal activity.\n\nThe BSA required depository institutions to establish and maintain BSA compliance programs, providing an early framework for supervision and enforcement of compliance with the BSA."}], "sample_id": "aml-topic-226-3", "question": "What is the primary purpose of the Bank Secrecy Act (BSA)?", "docs": [{"id": "doc-aml-topic-226-3-1", "title": "AML Topic 226 Part 1", "text": "he regulatory framework\nBSA-related formal enforcement action\nas it exists today, it is important to\nwas necessary.\nprovide the historical context for\ncertain anti-money laundering (AML)\nand combating the financing of\nWhat is the Bank Secrecy Act\nterrorism (CFT) laws.\nand Why is it Important?\nWhen Congress enacted the BSA\nThe BSA is the common name for in 1970, its primary intent was to\na series of laws and regulations that require institutions to maintain certain\nhave been enacted in the United", "score": 0.9, "summary": "he regulatory framework BSA-related formal enforcement action as it exists today, it is important to was necessary. provide the historical context for...", "extraction": "When Congress enacted the BSA in 1970, its primary intent was to require institutions to maintain certain records the government could use to support criminal and tax evasion investigations."}], "answer": "The primary purpose of the Bank Secrecy Act (BSA), enacted in 1970, was to require financial institutions to maintain certain records that the government could use to support criminal and tax evasion investigations. The Bank Secrecy Act (BSA) was necessary because it addressed the lack of financial recordkeeping by financial institutions, which was impeding law enforcement agencies' ability to investigate and prosecute criminal activity. The BSA required depository institutions to establish and maintain BSA compliance programs, providing an early framework for supervision and enforcement of compliance with the BSA.", "output": "The primary purpose of the Bank Secrecy Act (BSA) is to require financial institutions to maintain certain records and report suspicious activity to the government [1].", "intermediate": ["[Retrieval]", "The primary purpose of the Bank Secrecy Act (BSA) is to require financial institutions to maintain certain records and report suspicious activity to the government."]}, {"qa_pairs": [{"context": "No context provided", "question": "What is the purpose of the Cyber Challenge exercise for community banks?", "short_answers": ["Assess readiness", "Strengthen resilience"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "How are the exercises in the Cyber Challenge structured?", "short_answers": ["Short video vignettes", "Standalone scenarios"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "Who should participate in the facilitated discussions of the Cyber Challenge vignettes?", "short_answers": ["Senior management", "Key representatives"], "wikipage": null}], "wikipages": [{"title": "AML Topic 227", "url": "https://www.fdic.gov/regulations/resources/director/technical/cyber/info.pdf"}], "annotations": [{"knowledge": [], "long_answer": "The Cyber Challenge is a tool that banks can use to assess their readiness to confront operational risks and identify opportunities to strengthen their resilience. The exercises in the Cyber Challenge are presented using short video vignettes, each representing a standalone operational scenario with associated challenge questions to prompt discussion. For maximum effectiveness, it is recommended that members of senior management participate, along with key representatives from each of the financial institution\u2019s business lines."}, {"knowledge": [{"content": "Cyber Challenge is a tool banks can use to help assess their readiness to confront operational risks and identify opportunities to strengthen their resilience.", "wikipage": "AML Topic 227"}, {"content": "The Challenge is made up of different exercises presented using short video vignettes. Each vignette represents a standalone operational scenario and has associated challenge questions to help prompt discussion.", "wikipage": "AML Topic 227"}, {"content": "For maximum effectiveness, we recommend that members of senior management participate, along with key representatives from each of the financial institution\u2019s business lines.", "wikipage": "AML Topic 227"}], "long_answer": "The Cyber Challenge is a tool that banks can use to assess their readiness to confront operational risks and identify opportunities to strengthen their resilience.\n\nThe exercises in the Cyber Challenge are presented using short video vignettes, each representing a standalone operational scenario with associated challenge questions to prompt discussion.\n\nFor maximum effectiveness, it is recommended that members of senior management participate, along with key representatives from each of the financial institution\u2019s business lines."}], "sample_id": "aml-topic-227-1", "question": "What is the purpose of the Cyber Challenge exercise for community banks?", "docs": [{"id": "doc-aml-topic-227-1-1", "title": "AML Topic 227 Part 1", "text": "CYBER CHALLENGE\nCYBER CHALLENGE: A COMMUNITY BANK CYBER EXERCISE\nCyber Challenge is a tool banks can use to help assess their readiness to\nconfront operational risks and identify opportunities to strengthen their\nresilience.\nThe Challenge is made up of different exercises presented using short video\nvignettes. Each exercise is designed to be completed in about an hour.\nEach vignette represents a standalone operational scenario and has\nassociated challenge questions to help prompt discussion. Par", "score": 0.9, "summary": "CYBER CHALLENGE CYBER CHALLENGE: A COMMUNITY BANK CYBER EXERCISE Cyber Challenge is a tool banks can use to help assess their readiness to confront op...", "extraction": "Cyber Challenge is a tool banks can use to help assess their readiness to confront operational risks and identify opportunities to strengthen their resilience."}], "answer": "The Cyber Challenge is a tool that banks can use to assess their readiness to confront operational risks and identify opportunities to strengthen their resilience. The exercises in the Cyber Challenge are presented using short video vignettes, each representing a standalone operational scenario with associated challenge questions to prompt discussion. For maximum effectiveness, it is recommended that members of senior management participate, along with key representatives from each of the financial institution\u2019s business lines.", "output": "Answer: The Cyber Challenge exercise for community banks is designed to help assess their readiness to confront operational risks and identify opportunities to strengthen their resilience [1].", "intermediate": ["[Retrieval]", "Answer: The Cyber Challenge exercise for community banks is designed to help assess their readiness to confront operational risks and identify opportunities to strengthen their resilience."]}, {"qa_pairs": [{"context": "No context provided", "question": "What role does the facilitator play in managing a compliance meeting?", "short_answers": ["Neutral party", "Guide information-sharing", "Focus discussions"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "Why is it important to limit the number of active participants in a compliance meeting?", "short_answers": ["Effective contribution", "Efficient meeting"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "How can a facilitator use video vignettes to enhance discussion in a compliance meeting?", "short_answers": ["Use video vignettes", "Focus on character perspectives"], "wikipage": null}], "wikipages": [{"title": "AML Topic 227", "url": "https://www.fdic.gov/regulations/resources/director/technical/cyber/info.pdf"}], "annotations": [{"knowledge": [], "long_answer": "The facilitator acts as a neutral party who provides structure to the meeting, guides information-sharing among participants, ensures everyone has an opportunity to contribute, and helps clarify goals and objectives. Limiting the number of active participants allows everyone to contribute effectively while keeping the discussion time to less than two hours. A facilitator can select a video vignette and accompanying challenge cards for participants to view and discuss."}, {"knowledge": [{"content": "\u2022 be a neutral party who provides structure to the meeting and the discussion to meet the group\u2019s goals and objectives. \u2022 guide information-sharing among the participants, ensure that all participants have an opportunity to contribute, and help participants clarify their goals and objectives for the discussion. \u2022 ensure that discussions move forward and are focused on the issue at hand.", "wikipage": "AML Topic 227"}, {"content": "2. Limit the number of active participants. Limiting participation will allow for all to contribute while keeping the anticipated discussion time to less than two hours.", "wikipage": "AML Topic 227"}, {"content": "3. Select a video vignette and the accompanying challenge cards for participants to view and discuss. The facilitator may use the perspectives of a character in the vignette as the focal point for the discussion (e.g., the Bank IT Manager).", "wikipage": "AML Topic 227"}], "long_answer": "The facilitator acts as a neutral party who provides structure to the meeting, guides information-sharing among participants, ensures everyone has an opportunity to contribute, and helps clarify goals and objectives. The facilitator also ensures that discussions move forward and remain focused on the issue at hand.\n\nLimiting the number of active participants allows everyone to contribute effectively while keeping the discussion time to less than two hours. This ensures that the meeting remains efficient and focused.\n\nA facilitator can select a video vignette and accompanying challenge cards for participants to view and discuss. The perspectives of a character in the vignette, such as the Bank IT Manager, can be used as the focal point for the discussion, aiding in engagement and focus."}], "sample_id": "aml-topic-227-2", "question": "What role does the facilitator play in managing a compliance meeting?", "docs": [{"id": "doc-aml-topic-227-2-1", "title": "AML Topic 227 Part 1", "text": "\u2022 be a neutral party who provides structure to the meeting and the\ndiscussion to meet the group\u2019s goals and objectives.\n\u2022 guide information-sharing among the participants, ensure that all\nparticipants have an opportunity to contribute, and help\nparticipants clarify their goals and objectives for the discussion.\n\u2022 ensure that discussions move forward and are focused on the\nissue at hand.\n2. Limit the number of active participants. Limiting participation will allow for\nall to contribute while keep", "score": 0.9, "summary": "\u2022 be a neutral party who provides structure to the meeting and the discussion to meet the group\u2019s goals and objectives. \u2022 guide information-sharing am...", "extraction": "\u2022 be a neutral party who provides structure to the meeting and the discussion to meet the group\u2019s goals and objectives. \u2022 guide information-sharing among the participants, ensure that all participants have an opportunity to contribute, and help participants clarify their goals and objectives for the discussion. \u2022 ensure that discussions move forward and are focused on the issue at hand."}], "answer": "The facilitator acts as a neutral party who provides structure to the meeting, guides information-sharing among participants, ensures everyone has an opportunity to contribute, and helps clarify goals and objectives. Limiting the number of active participants allows everyone to contribute effectively while keeping the discussion time to less than two hours. A facilitator can select a video vignette and accompanying challenge cards for participants to view and discuss.", "output": "The facilitator plays a role in managing a compliance meeting by setting the agenda, keeping the discussion focused, and ensuring that all participants have an opportunity to contribute [1].", "intermediate": ["[Retrieval]", "The facilitator plays a role in managing a compliance meeting by setting the agenda, keeping the discussion focused, and ensuring that all participants have an opportunity to contribute."]}, {"qa_pairs": [{"context": "No context provided", "question": "What steps should a bank take if it discovers that its ATM systems are infected with malware?", "short_answers": ["Isolate affected systems", "Review service provider contracts", "Implement stronger security measures"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "What should a financial institution do if it experiences a ransomware attack?", "short_answers": ["Disconnect affected systems", "Notify law enforcement", "Engage cybersecurity experts"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "How should a bank respond if a phishing email is sent to its staff, pretending to be from a high-ranking official?", "short_answers": ["Alert employees", "Block sender", "Conduct security training"], "wikipage": null}], "wikipages": [{"title": "AML Topic 227", "url": "https://www.fdic.gov/regulations/resources/director/technical/cyber/info.pdf"}], "annotations": [{"knowledge": [], "long_answer": "If a bank's ATM systems are infected with malware, it should first identify and isolate the affected systems to prevent further spread. In the event of a ransomware attack, a financial institution should immediately disconnect affected systems from the network to prevent further encryption of files. When a phishing email is detected, the bank should immediately alert all employees to prevent them from interacting with the email."}, {"knowledge": [{"content": "Vignette #6 \u2013 Farmers State Bank of Robertsburgh Automated Teller Machine (ATM) Malware \u2013 ATM malware reveals deficiencies in a bank\u2019s service provider contract.", "wikipage": "AML Topic 227"}, {"content": "Vignette #7 \u2013 People\u2019s State Bank of Morello Ransomware \u2013 A cyber-attack has taken place, and important files are being held for ransom.", "wikipage": "AML Topic 227"}, {"content": "Vignette #3 \u2013 The State Bank of Town City Bank Internal Error/Phishing and Malware Problem \u2013 Bank staff receive a phishing email that appears to have been sent by the institution\u2019s president.", "wikipage": "AML Topic 227"}], "long_answer": "If a bank's ATM systems are infected with malware, it should first identify and isolate the affected systems to prevent further spread. Next, the bank should review its service provider contracts to address any deficiencies that allowed the malware to infiltrate. Additionally, the bank should implement stronger security measures and conduct a thorough investigation to understand the breach's scope.\n\nIn the event of a ransomware attack, a financial institution should immediately disconnect affected systems from the network to prevent further encryption of files. It should then notify law enforcement and engage cybersecurity experts to assist in containment and recovery efforts. The institution should also communicate with stakeholders about the attack and work on restoring data from backups if available.\n\nWhen a phishing email is detected, the bank should immediately alert all employees to prevent them from interacting with the email. The IT department should block the sender and investigate how the email bypassed security measures. Additionally, the bank should conduct a security awareness training to reinforce phishing detection skills among staff."}], "sample_id": "aml-topic-227-3", "question": "What steps should a bank take if it discovers that its ATM systems are infected with malware?", "docs": [{"id": "doc-aml-topic-227-3-2", "title": "AML Topic 227 Part 2", "text": "Distributed Denial of Service (DDoS) Attack \u2013 The bank IT manager\ninvestigates a possible DDoS attack and discovers a second attack\nthat steals data from the institution.\n\u2022 Vignette #6 \u2013 Farmers State Bank of Robertsburgh\nAutomated Teller Machine (ATM) Malware \u2013 ATM malware reveals\ndeficiencies in a bank\u2019s service provider contract.\n\u2022 Vignette #7 \u2013 People\u2019s State Bank of Morello\nRansomware \u2013 A cyber-attack has taken place, and important files\nare being held for ransom.\n\u2022 (Added 2018) Vignette #", "score": 0.85, "summary": "Distributed Denial of Service (DDoS) Attack \u2013 The bank IT manager investigates a possible DDoS attack and discovers a second attack that steals data f...", "extraction": "Distributed Denial of Service (DDoS) Attack \u2013 The bank IT manager\ninvestigates a possible DDoS attack and discovers a second attack\nthat steals data from the institution.\n\u2022 Vignette #6 \u2013 Farmers State"}], "answer": "If a bank's ATM systems are infected with malware, it should first identify and isolate the affected systems to prevent further spread. In the event of a ransomware attack, a financial institution should immediately disconnect affected systems from the network to prevent further encryption of files. When a phishing email is detected, the bank should immediately alert all employees to prevent them from interacting with the email.", "output": "Answer: If a bank discovers that its ATM systems are infected with malware, it should immediately notify law enforcement and the appropriate regulatory agency, such as the Federal Bureau of Investigation (FBI) and the Federal Deposit Insurance Corporation (FDIC) [1].", "intermediate": ["[Retrieval]", "Answer: If a bank discovers that its ATM systems are infected with malware, it should immediately notify law enforcement and the appropriate regulatory agency, such as the Federal Bureau of Investigation (FBI) and the Federal Deposit Insurance Corporation (FDIC)."]}, {"qa_pairs": [{"context": "No context provided", "question": "How can FDICconnect Coordinators manage access to the Banker Engagement Site for other users?", "short_answers": ["Manage Transactions function", "Coordinator Functions Menu"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "What steps should be taken if a user encounters issues signing into FDICconnect?", "short_answers": ["Contact Us link", "Issues signing in"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "What is the role of BES Administrators in managing access permissions?", "short_answers": ["Manage permissions", "BES Contributor Access"], "wikipage": null}], "wikipages": [{"title": "AML Topic 228", "url": "https://www.fdic.gov/resources/bankers/information-technology/r3-banker-engagement-site-access-management-job-aid-external.pdf"}], "annotations": [{"knowledge": [], "long_answer": "FDICconnect Coordinators can manage access to the Banker Engagement Site (BES) for other users by accessing the Business Center Menu, expanding the Coordinator Functions Menu, and selecting 'Manage Transactions'. If a user encounters issues signing into FDICconnect, they should contact the FDIC through the Contact Us link provided on the site. BES Administrators are responsible for managing permissions for BES Contributor Access."}, {"knowledge": [{"content": "FDICconnect Coordinators will access the Banker Engagement Site (BES) through the FDICconnect Business Center (FDICconnect), and will grant other users\u2019 access to BES through the \u201cManage Transactions\u201d function.", "wikipage": "AML Topic 228"}, {"content": "All users should contact the FDIC through the Contact Us link if they encounter any issues signing in to FDICconnect.", "wikipage": "AML Topic 228"}, {"content": "Designated BES Administrators manage permissions for BES Contributor Access. Bank Contributors are individuals who typically respond to FDIC requests for information.", "wikipage": "AML Topic 228"}], "long_answer": "FDICconnect Coordinators can manage access to the Banker Engagement Site (BES) for other users by accessing the Business Center Menu, expanding the Coordinator Functions Menu, and selecting 'Manage Transactions'. They can then set up BES access for each user through this function.\n\nIf a user encounters issues signing into FDICconnect, they should contact the FDIC through the Contact Us link provided on the site.\n\nBES Administrators are responsible for managing permissions for BES Contributor Access. They ensure that users are assigned the appropriate roles and manage permissions accordingly."}], "sample_id": "aml-topic-228-1", "question": "How can FDICconnect Coordinators manage access to the Banker Engagement Site for other users?", "docs": [{"id": "doc-aml-topic-228-1-1", "title": "AML Topic 228 Part 1", "text": "CONTROLLED//FDIC BUSINESS\nBanker Engagement Site Access Management\nAssign Authenticated Users to the Banker Engagement Site\nFDICconnect Coordinators will access the Banker Engagement Site (BES) through the FDICconnect Business Center\n(FDICconnect), and will grant other users\u2019 access to BES through the \u201cManage Transactions\u201d function. All users should\ncontact the FDIC through the Contact Us link if they encounter any issues signing in to FDICconnect.\n1. At the Business Center Menu, EXPAND the Coor", "score": 0.9, "summary": "CONTROLLED//FDIC BUSINESS Banker Engagement Site Access Management Assign Authenticated Users to the Banker Engagement Site FDICconnect Coordinators w...", "extraction": "FDICconnect Coordinators will access the Banker Engagement Site (BES) through the FDICconnect Business Center (FDICconnect), and will grant other users\u2019 access to BES through the \u201cManage Transactions\u201d function."}], "answer": "FDICconnect Coordinators can manage access to the Banker Engagement Site (BES) for other users by accessing the Business Center Menu, expanding the Coordinator Functions Menu, and selecting 'Manage Transactions'. If a user encounters issues signing into FDICconnect, they should contact the FDIC through the Contact Us link provided on the site. BES Administrators are responsible for managing permissions for BES Contributor Access.", "output": "Answer: FDICconnect Coordinators can manage access to the Banker Engagement Site for other users through the \"Manage Transactions\" function [1].", "intermediate": ["[Retrieval]", "Answer: FDICconnect Coordinators can manage access to the Banker Engagement Site for other users through the \"Manage Transactions\" function."]}, {"qa_pairs": [{"context": "No context provided", "question": "How quickly can a user expect to receive access to the Banker Engagement Site (BES) after privileges are granted?", "short_answers": ["15 minutes", "email notification"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "What should a user do if they do not receive an email notification after being granted access to the BES?", "short_answers": ["Check junk mail folders", "No email received"], "wikipage": null}, {"context": "No context provided", "question": "What steps should BES Administrators take to manage permissions for a BES Contributor?", "short_answers": ["Navigate to People Tab", "Manage permissions"], "wikipage": null}], "wikipages": [{"title": "AML Topic 228", "url": "https://www.fdic.gov/resources/bankers/information-technology/r3-banker-engagement-site-access-management-job-aid-external.pdf"}], "annotations": [{"knowledge": [], "long_answer": "Access to the Banker Engagement Site is provided within approximately 15 minutes after privileges are granted. Users should check their junk mail folders if no email is received after being granted access to the BES. BES Administrators should navigate to the People Tab, select the icon to manage permissions for the desired Contributor, and then select the desired permissions for each relevant area in the Manage Permissions overlay."}, {"knowledge": [{"content": "Access is provided within approximately 15 minutes, and users will receive an email from the FDIC that BES access has been granted.", "wikipage": "AML Topic 228"}, {"content": "Users should check their junk mail folders, if no email is received.", "wikipage": "AML Topic 228"}, {"content": "BES Administrators will manage permissions of each BES Contributor within the BES interface. NAVIGATE to the People Tab and SELECT the icon to Manage Permissions for the desired Contributor. In the Manage Permissions overlay, SELECT desired permissions for each relevant area (Information Package Letter, CIDR, etc.).", "wikipage": "AML Topic 228"}], "long_answer": "Access to the Banker Engagement Site is provided within approximately 15 minutes after privileges are granted.\n\nUsers should check their junk mail folders if no email is received after being granted access to the BES.\n\nBES Administrators should navigate to the People Tab, select the icon to manage permissions for the desired Contributor, and then select the desired permissions for each relevant area in the Manage Permissions overlay."}], "sample_id": "aml-topic-228-2", "question": "How quickly can a user expect to receive access to the Banker Engagement Site (BES) after privileges are granted?", "docs": [{"id": "doc-aml-topic-228-2-2", "title": "AML Topic 228 Part 2", "text": "their\nUser Functions Menu when the user signs in to Connect.\nNotes: Access is provided within approximately 15 minutes, and users will receive an email from the FDIC that\nBES access has been granted. Users should check their junk mail folders, if no email is received.\n5. BES Administrators will manage permissions of each BES Contributor within the BES interface. NAVIGATE to\nthe People Tab and SELECT the icon to Manage Permissions for the desired Contributor.\nNote: User permission updates are imm", "score": 0.85, "summary": "their User Functions Menu when the user signs in to Connect. Notes: Access is provided within approximately 15 minutes, and users will receive an emai...", "extraction": "their\nUser Functions Menu when the user signs in to Connect.\nNotes: Access is provided within approximately 15 minutes, and users will receive an email from the FDIC that\nBES access has been granted. "}], "answer": "Access to the Banker Engagement Site is provided within approximately 15 minutes after privileges are granted. Users should check their junk mail folders if no email is received after being granted access to the BES. BES Administrators should navigate to the People Tab, select the icon to manage permissions for the desired Contributor, and then select the desired permissions for each relevant area in the Manage Permissions overlay.", "output": "Answer: Users can expect to receive access to the Banker Engagement Site (BES) within 15 minutes after privileges are granted [1].", "intermediate": ["[Retrieval]", "Answer: Users can expect to receive access to the Banker Engagement Site (BES) within 15 minutes after privileges are granted."]}], "args": [], "total_cost": 0.0, "azure_filter_fail": ""}